/* ---------------- [F] industries: never truncate the SEO deks ----------------
   The U3 tile dek carries a 4-line -webkit-line-clamp as a "safety net", written
   on the measurement that the copy fits in 2-3 lines. It does at >=761px, but the
   narrower measure below that pushes the longest three (Bars & nightclubs,
   Hotels, Grocery stores) to 5 lines, so the clamp bit and cut them MID-SENTENCE
   — verified in the 768 and 375 renders. These descriptions are the reason the
   section was extended, so the tile grows to its copy rather than the copy being
   cut to the tile. Placed after the U3 block so it wins the cascade (the later
   max-width:460 rule re-asserted a fixed aspect-ratio). */
@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  #industries .ind-feature-grid { grid-auto-rows: auto; }
  #industries .ind-feature-grid .category-card {
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    min-height: clamp(210px, 40vw, 268px);
  }
  #industries .ind-tile__desc {
    display: block;
    -webkit-line-clamp: none;
    overflow: visible;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 460px) {
  #industries .ind-feature-grid .category-card {
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    min-height: clamp(230px, 62vw, 300px);
  }
}

/* ============================================================================
   [G] PERF FIX (2026-07-22) — hero smoke drift: PAINT property -> COMPOSITOR
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE MEASURED CAUSE OF THE REPORTED PAGE LAG (two reporters, two machines).
   `.hero__smoke::before/::after` animated `background-position-x`, which is NOT
   a compositor-accelerated property, over `--ds-smoke`/`--ds-smoke-alt` — two
   feTurbulence fractalNoise SVG data-URIs. Every frame therefore invalidated
   and re-painted + re-rasterized both full-bleed layers, forever, from load.
   It is a COMPOSITOR/RASTER cost, not a main-thread cost, which is why the
   main thread stayed idle and rAF-based FPS counting could not see it.

   EVIDENCE (headed real Chrome, DPR2 1440x900, interleaved trials, ranges do
   not overlap), idle frame interval p50 at the hero:
       baseline                151 ms  [116.7-200]  ~6.6 fps
       transform rewrite      16.7 ms  [16.6-16.7]   60  fps
   Chrome trace events: baseline 19-35 DrawFrames/3s with 57-105 Paint,
   38-70 RasterTask, 19-35 Layerize. After: 167-181 DrawFrames/3s with
   ZERO Paint, ZERO RasterTask, ZERO Layerize.

   FIX: keep the tiled noise static; translate an over-wide pseudo-element on
   the compositor instead. Visual is preserved: each layer travels exactly one
   background tile (1200px / 1600px), so the repeat-x pattern lands on itself
   with no seam, and the layer is extended by that tile on both sides so no
   edge is ever exposed mid-cycle.
   DO NOT instead promote the container (`will-change`/`contain:paint` on
   .hero__smoke) — that was measured and did NOT help (12.0 fps), because the
   invalidation originates in the pseudo-elements' own backgrounds.
   Appended, not edited in place, so the original rules remain and this is
   reversible by deleting this block.
   ========================================================================== */
.hero__smoke { overflow: hidden; }
.hero__smoke::before {
  left: -1200px; right: -1200px;
  animation-name: ds-smoke-drift-gpu;
  will-change: transform;
}
.hero__smoke::after {
  left: -1600px; right: -1600px;
  animation-name: ds-smoke-drift-alt-gpu;
  will-change: transform;
}
@keyframes ds-smoke-drift-gpu     { from { transform: translate3d(0,0,0); } to { transform: translate3d(-1200px,0,0); } }
@keyframes ds-smoke-drift-alt-gpu { from { transform: translate3d(0,0,0); } to { transform: translate3d(1600px,0,0); } }

/* ---------------- [G] click-to-load map placeholder ----------------
   The Google Maps embed was the page's only external dependency (~2.5 MB
   across several Google hosts). It is now loaded on request, so the page
   makes ZERO off-origin requests until the visitor asks for the map.
   The placeholder fills the same box as the iframe so nothing shifts. */
.cta-map__placeholder {
  position: relative; overflow: hidden;
  width: 100%; height: 100%; min-height: 320px;
  display: grid; place-items: center;
  background:
    radial-gradient(120% 120% at 30% 20%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 10%, transparent), transparent 60%),
    var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border-radius: inherit;
}
/* faint street-grid suggestion — pure CSS, no image, no request */
.cta-map__grid {
  position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; opacity: .5;
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(var(--ds-hairline) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(90deg, var(--ds-hairline) 1px, transparent 1px);
  background-size: 46px 46px, 46px 46px;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(70% 70% at 50% 50%, #000, transparent 78%);
          mask-image: radial-gradient(70% 70% at 50% 50%, #000, transparent 78%);
}
.cta-map__body { position: relative; z-index: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: var(--ds-space-sm); text-align: center; padding: var(--ds-space-xl); }
.cta-map__pin { display: grid; place-items: center; width: 46px; height: 46px; border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 16%, transparent); color: var(--ds-accent); }
.cta-map__pin svg { width: 24px; height: 24px; }
.cta-map__addr { font-family: var(--ds-font-display); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); margin: 0; }
.cta-map__load { min-height: 44px; margin-top: var(--ds-space-2xs); }
.cta-map__alt { font-size: 14px; color: var(--ds-accent-ink); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .18em; }
.cta-map__alt:hover { color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); }

/* ==========================================================================
   INVENTORY BOARD — filter/sort state (site.js owns the toggling)
   Append-only, token-pure: no new tokens, no geometry or layout overrides.

   (a) The hide rule is LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic. .product-card--spec sets
       `display:flex` (line 4273) — an author rule that outranks the UA
       `[hidden]{display:none}` rule, so toggling the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE alone
       leaves a filtered-out card fully laid out and still counted as visible.
       This selector is (1,2,0) against that rule's (0,1,0), so it wins, and it
       is scoped to #inventory so no other .collection__grid is affected.
       Distinct from .tg-fcell.is-hidden above, which FADES gallery tiles in
       place and requires both classes — the two never collide.

   (b) The active chip needs a visible selected state, or the type filter
       changes the board with nothing to say which type is in force. It mirrors
       the pressed-pill treatment of .tg-filterbar button[aria-pressed="true"]
       but takes its ink from --ds-primary-deep rather than --ds-ink-on-dark:
       on this warm-espresso repaint --ds-ink-on-dark (#ffffff) over
       --ds-accent (#e69c4e) measures 2.28:1, a WCAG failure, while
       --ds-primary-deep (#17120f) over the same gold measures 8.17:1.
   ========================================================================== */
#inventory .collection__grid > .is-hidden { display: none; }
.tg-filterbar a[data-cat][aria-current="true"] {
  background: var(--ds-accent); border-color: var(--ds-accent);
  color: var(--ds-primary-deep); font-weight: 700;
}

/* ============================================================================
   [AC] INLINE-LINK + SECONDARY-BUTTON CONTRAST RELIGHT  (2026-07-29)
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   MEASURED, not theorised. verify-contrast.mjs (headless computed-style WCAG
   walker) reported 26 HARD FAILURES across about / inventory / resources /
   services. Two independent root causes:

   A) 17 of them (20 elements) are INLINE PROSE LINKS carrying no class. There
      is no global `a` colour rule anywhere in this file (`grep -cE '^a\s*\{'`
      returns 0), so they fell back to the UA default #0000ee and measured
      1.77-1.98:1 against a 4.5 body-text floor on this site's three dark bands.
      Every CLASSED link (.cross-link, .btn, .faq-item__a a, .anchored-toc__nav
      a, .bl-ix__it a, .doc-list__item, .svc-row__link, .category-card,
      .cta-map__alt, plus all header / nav / drawer / footer chrome, which lives
      OUTSIDE .section entirely) already sets its own colour and already PASSES.
      That is exactly why this is scoped to prose CONTAINERS *and* to
      `a:not([class])` instead of a bare global `a { color: ... }` — a global
      rule would repaint chrome and components that are clean today, and would
      also hit a future `<p><a class="btn">` inline CTA.

   B) 9 of them are the `.btn-secondary` OUTLINE. Line 67 sets
      `border: 1.5px solid var(--ds-line)` (#4a4a4a). Line 908 already relights
      that outline for `.section--dark` and line 909 for `.section--accent`, but
      nothing ever did so for `.section--warm` or the base `.section` — where it
      measured 1.93:1 and 1.77:1 against a 3.0 UI / large-text floor.

   MEASURED RATIOS behind the colour choice, on this file's own band grounds:
                          dark      warm      base    #process
                        #17120f   #221a15   #2b211b   #f2ece3
     --ds-accent-ink     8.84      8.14      7.47     1.79 FAIL
     --ds-ink-on-dark   18.59     17.12     15.72     1.17 FAIL
     --ds-primary        2.64      2.43      2.23     6.00 PASS
   So: amber (--ds-accent-ink) for links on the three DARK bands, oxblood
   (--ds-primary) for links on the ONE LIGHT band. #process is the site's single
   light interlude ([X] above paints it #f2ece3), so it needs the opposite
   foreground — and its .btn-secondary must KEEP the dark-on-cream default
   (--ds-line on #f2ece3 measures 7.55:1, already well over the 3.0 floor).

   The amber + underline + .18em offset idiom is NOT new: it is the same
   treatment `.faq-item__a a` (line 4628) and `.cta-map__alt` already use, so
   inline links now read consistently sitewide instead of two ways.

   SPECIFICITY NOTES (why the light-band guards win):
     dark link rule  .section :is(...) a:not([class])      = (0,3,1)
     light guard     :is(#process,.cta-formcard) a:not(...) = (1,1,1)  <- ID wins
     dark btn rule   .section:not(.section--accent) .btn-secondary = (0,3,0)
     light guard     :is(#process,.cta-formcard) .btn-secondary    = (1,1,0)  <- ID wins
   `.section--accent` is excluded from the button rule so line 909's correct
   deep-ink relight on an accent band is not clobbered.

   `.cta-formcard` is the site's only OTHER light ground (`background:#ffffff`,
   line 3843). It carries NO class-less link and NO .btn-secondary today
   (verified), so its guards are PRE-EMPTIVE — they close the identical hazard
   before an editor opens it, rather than fixing a live failure.

   NO button :hover rule is written here on purpose: line 68's hover already
   applies, and line 908 has meant dark-band secondaries keep their relit border
   through hover since it was written. Extending only `color` + `border-color`
   makes warm/base behave EXACTLY like dark does today, instead of inventing a
   third hover behaviour.

   Token-pure: every colour is a var(--ds-*); the only literals are structural.
   Additive only — nothing above is edited or deleted; deleting this block is a
   complete revert.
   ========================================================================== */

/* --- A. inline prose links, dark bands (UA #0000ee 1.77-1.98:1 -> amber 7.47-8.84:1) --- */
.section :is(p, li, dd, td, blockquote, .lede, .data-proof__sub) a:not([class]) {
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: .18em;
}
.section :is(p, li, dd, td, blockquote, .lede, .data-proof__sub) a:not([class]):hover {
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}

/* --- A2. the ONE light band: amber would drop to 1.79:1 on #f2ece3, so go oxblood (6.00:1) --- */
:is(#process, .cta-formcard) a:not([class]) { color: var(--ds-primary); }
:is(#process, .cta-formcard) a:not([class]):hover { color: var(--ds-primary-deep); }

/* --- B. .btn-secondary outline on --warm (1.93:1) + base .section (1.77:1) -> 17.12/15.72:1 --- */
.section:not(.section--accent) .btn-secondary {
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
  border-color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}
/* light grounds keep the proven dark-on-cream default (--ds-line = 7.55:1 on #f2ece3) */
:is(#process, .cta-formcard) .btn-secondary {
  color: var(--ds-primary-deep);
  border-color: var(--ds-line);
}

/* --- C. sticky mobile CTA tel link: sits OUTSIDE .section, so rule A cannot reach it.
   Long-standing UA-blue defect. --ds-glass-bg is rgba(26,26,26,.74), so the composite
   ground varies with whatever is scrolled behind it; --ds-ink-on-dark is the only
   foreground that holds over the whole range (18.59:1 over the glass alone, 7.53:1 in
   the worst case of the glass over pure white). The .btn-primary sibling is untouched
   because it carries a class. --- */
.mm-sticky-cta a:not([class]) {
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: .18em;
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AD] INVENTORY BOARD — three MEASURED presentation defects, found by reading
   the rendered page at 1440 after the deterministic gates were already green.
   Green gates are not the bar; these were all invisible to them.
   Token-pure, append-only. Scoped to #inventory so nothing else moves.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (a) FUSED PARAGRAPHS. The board intro is a .lede followed by a bare <p>.
       The global reset sets `p { margin: 0 }`, and .lede carries its own width
       but no bottom margin, so the two paragraphs rendered as one fused block
       with different measures — it read as a layout bug, not a paragraph break. */
#inventory .container > .lede + p,
#inventory .container > p + p { margin-top: var(--ds-space-md); }

/* (b) CENTRED FILTER RAIL. `.tg-filterbar` (structural.css:2049) ships
       `justify-content: center; margin: 0 auto` because it was authored for the
       team-gallery grid. This page is left-aligned editorial, so the chip rail
       floated to the middle while its own heading, lede and grid stayed left. */
#inventory .tg-filterbar { justify-content: flex-start; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; }

/* (c) CHEVRON-LESS SELECTS. `.ec-ctrl` (structural.css:2687) sets
       `appearance: none` but NOTHING in the sheet draws a replacement chevron —
       the only chevron rule, `.hero-finder__control::after` (:4581), is
       ancestor-scoped to the hero. So county / asking-price / sort all rendered
       identical to a text input with no affordance that they open. Drawn here as
       a rotated border wedge (geometry literals only; colour is a token).
       `:has()` is already used in this sheet at :3277 and :4537. */
#inventory .ec-field { position: relative; }
#inventory .ec-field:has(> select.ec-ctrl) > select.ec-ctrl { padding-right: 40px; }
#inventory .ec-field:has(> select.ec-ctrl)::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; right: 17px; top: calc(50% + 4px);
  width: 8px; height: 8px; pointer-events: none;
  border-right: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(45deg);
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AE] HERO COMMAND BAR — full-width federated search + suggestion panel
   (2026-07-29). Replaces the [U8] two-select .hero-finder aside on index.html
   ONLY. Append-only, token-pure: every colour is a var(--ds-*); the only
   literals are structural (px sizes matching the sheet's existing control
   scale, z-indices, breakpoints, gradient/mix percentages).

   WHY THE HERO GOES BACK TO ONE COLUMN, FOR FREE.
   [U8] keyed its whole 2-column hero grid on `:has(> .hero-finder)` (line
   4537). Deleting the aside un-matches that block, so with NO edit to it the
   container returns to block flow and `.hero--editorial h1` (line 3252)
   recovers its full clamp(40px, 5.4vw, 74px) / 17ch measure. MEASURED at 1440,
   headless computed style: h1 font-size 53.28px BEFORE -> 74px AFTER, width
   504px -> 682px (the 62ch .hero__inner measure is now the binding constraint,
   not the 14ch retune). Verified, not assumed.

   STACKING. `.hero--photo > .container` is z-index 2 (line 3101). `.hero__stats`
   is a LATER, UNPOSITIONED sibling, so a positioned `.cmdbar` with a z-index
   paints over it — which is the requirement: the suggestion panel must FLOAT
   over the credentials rail, never push it down. Hence z-index on .cmdbar (3)
   and on the panel (4), both inside the container's own stacking context.

   SURFACE. The bar is the locked --ds-glass-* trio (same material as the
   header). The PANEL is deliberately NOT that glass: a .74-alpha ground over a
   photograph gives a composite that varies with whatever is behind it, and the
   panel is the one surface here dense with small text. It uses a 97%
   --ds-bg-surface-2 mix instead — visually the same family, but the worst-case
   composite (over pure white) still measures ~19:1 for --ds-ink-on-dark, so
   its contrast is deterministic rather than photograph-dependent.
   NOTE the panel ships `hidden`, so the contrast walker cannot measure it; that
   is exactly why its ground is reasoned here rather than left to the gate.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

.cmdbar {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 3;
  width: 100%;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl);
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
}
.cmdbar__form { margin: 0; position: relative; }

/* visually-hidden label + live region. The sheet has no such utility (its only
   prior instances are inlined onto specific `thead`s at :510 and :1431), so the
   pattern is inlined here too rather than a new global utility being invented. */
.cmdbar__label,
.cmdbar__live {
  position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px;
  margin: -1px; padding: 0; overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0 0 0 0); clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; border: 0;
}

/* ---- the bar itself ------------------------------------------------------ */
.cmdbar__field {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  min-height: 64px;
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: var(--ds-glass-bg);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--ds-glass-blur);
          backdrop-filter: var(--ds-glass-blur);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card), var(--ds-inset-hi);
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out),
              box-shadow var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
.cmdbar__field:hover { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 42%, transparent); }
/* The ring lives on the FIELD, not the input — the input has no border of its
   own, so a ring on it would float inside the bar instead of describing it. */
.cmdbar__field:focus-within {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card), var(--ds-inset-hi), 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 34%, transparent);
}
.cmdbar__glyph { width: 22px; height: 22px; flex: 0 0 auto; color: var(--ds-accent); }

.cmdbar__input {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  appearance: none;
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  outline: none;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 17px;
  line-height: 1.35;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}
.cmdbar__input::placeholder { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); opacity: 1; }
/* the input already carries the field's ring via :focus-within */
.cmdbar__input:focus-visible { outline: none; }

/* the `/` shortcut affordance — a hint, never a control */
.cmdbar__kbd {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: none;
  min-width: 26px;
  padding: var(--ds-space-2xs) var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono);
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.4;
  text-align: center;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-on-dark) 7%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);
}
@media (min-width: 861px) { .cmdbar__kbd { display: block; } }
/* once the visitor is typing, the shortcut hint is noise */
.cmdbar__field:focus-within .cmdbar__kbd { visibility: hidden; }

.cmdbar__go { flex: 0 0 auto; min-height: 48px; white-space: nowrap; }

/* ---- the suggestion panel ------------------------------------------------ */
.cmdbar__panel {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 4;
  top: calc(100% + var(--ds-space-xs));
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  max-height: min(62vh, 520px);
  overflow-y: auto;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs) 0;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 97%, transparent);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--ds-glass-blur);
          backdrop-filter: var(--ds-glass-blur);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-lift);
}
.cmdbar__panel[hidden] { display: none; }
.cmdbar__panel.is-open {
  animation: ds-cmdbar-in var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out) both;
}
@keyframes ds-cmdbar-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-6px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}

.cmdbar__grouplabel {
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-2xs);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
}

.cmdbar__opt {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  cursor: pointer;
  border-left: 2px solid transparent;
}
.cmdbar__opt[aria-selected="true"],
.cmdbar__opt:hover {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 13%, transparent);
  border-left-color: var(--ds-accent);
}
.cmdbar__opt-t { flex: 0 1 auto; font-family: var(--ds-font-body); font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); }
.cmdbar__opt-m { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }
.cmdbar__opt-go { flex: 0 0 auto; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }

/* the location-honesty tag: a market we cover that holds NO stock today */
.cmdbar__opt-tag {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  padding: 2px var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: .04em;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  background: var(--ds-warning-soft);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-warning) 46%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
}

.cmdbar__none {
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}

/* ---- the free-text parse report ----------------------------------------- */
.cmdbar__note {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
}
.cmdbar__note[hidden] { display: none; }
.cmdbar__note-txt { flex: 1 1 260px; min-width: 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); }
.cmdbar__note-go { flex: 0 0 auto; min-height: 44px; }

/* ---- footer row: scope statement + the valuation conversion link --------- */
.cmdbar__foot {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-lg);
}
.cmdbar__scope { flex: 1 1 320px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }
.cmdbar__alt {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: .2em;
}
.cmdbar__alt:hover { color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); }

/* ---- narrow: the bar stacks, and nothing may overflow the viewport ------- */
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .cmdbar__field {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding: var(--ds-space-sm);
    row-gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  }
  .cmdbar__input { flex: 1 1 140px; font-size: 16px; }
  .cmdbar__go { flex: 1 0 100%; }
  .cmdbar__panel { max-height: min(56vh, 420px); }
  .cmdbar__opt { flex-wrap: wrap; row-gap: var(--ds-space-2xs); }
  .cmdbar__opt-m { flex: 1 1 100%; }
  .cmdbar__opt-go { display: none; }
}
/* same 44px hit-box treatment the [F] block already gives .hero-finder__alt */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .cmdbar__alt { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .cmdbar__field { transition: none; }
  .cmdbar__panel.is-open { animation: none; }
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AF] LOCATIONS — market tabs + the five-step qualifier (2026-07-29).
   locations.html ONLY. Append-only, token-pure: every colour is a var(--ds-*);
   the only literals are structural (px control sizes matching this sheet's
   existing scale, grid counts, breakpoints, mix percentages).

   ONE PARENT PAGE, FOURTEEN INNER TABS — no per-location pages, no /cities/
   tree. The structure mirrors the client's own live nav, which nests
   location -> licence type, so a tab body is a location x classification
   matrix and nothing else.

   ── THE MEASURED BORDER PROBLEM, AND WHY THESE RULES LOOK THE WAY THEY DO ──
   verify-contrast.mjs scores ANY element matching `.btn, button, a.btn,
   [class*=btn-]` for OUTLINE contrast at a 3.0:1 UI floor against its own
   composited ground. `.loc-tab` is a real <button role="tab">, so it is scored.
   Block [AC] above already recorded the ratios for this sheet's own default
   outline token on this build's bands:

       --ds-line (#4a4a4a)   on --warm (#221a15) = 1.93:1   FAIL
                             on base  (#2b211b) = 1.77:1   FAIL

   That is exactly the defect [AC] fixed for `.btn-secondary`, and copying
   `.tg-filterbar button` (line 2050, `border: 1px solid var(--ds-line)`) would
   have reintroduced it. inventory.html escapes it only because its filter rail
   carries `a.chip`, which the walker does not treat as a button.

   So the outline here is mixed off --ds-ink-soft (#c8c6c5) at 62% and left
   translucent, so it composites over whichever band it lands on. MEASURED
   composite ratios of that mix, computed against this file's own grounds:

       over --warm  (#221a15) = 4.90:1     over base    (#2b211b) = 4.68:1
       over --dark  (#17120f) = 5.03:1     over surface-2(#0e0a08) = 5.4:1

   All four clear 3.0 with real headroom, and the mix reads as a warm hairline
   rather than the near-white a solid --ds-ink-on-dark outline would give.
   The SELECTED tab switches to a solid --ds-accent outline (7.01:1 on its own
   --ds-accent-soft ground) because selection must be legible as state, not
   just as emphasis.

   ── THE HONESTY TREATMENT IS A STYLE, NOT JUST COPY ──
   Six of the thirteen markets hold ZERO live listings, and San Bernardino is the
   inverse case: it holds a real listing but is on neither the client's published
   market list nor the homepage coverage band. Both are surfaced in words by the
   page. `.loc-flag` gives that sentence its own --ds-warning-soft ground so it
   cannot be skim-read as decoration, and `.loc-tab__count[data-stock="0"]`
   drops the tab's own badge to --ds-ink-soft so no tab in the rail can be
   mistaken for one that has stock. MEASURED on --ds-warning-soft (#2e1c12):
   --ds-ink-on-dark 16.27:1, --ds-ink-soft 9.56:1.

   ── WHY THE PANELS USE [hidden] AND NOT A UTILITY CLASS ──
   `.loc-panel` / `.qz__step` set their own `display`, and an author `display`
   ALWAYS beats the UA's `[hidden] { display: none }`. The explicit
   `[hidden] { display: none }` rules below are therefore load-bearing, not
   defensive: without them every one of the fourteen panels paints at once.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ---- tab shell -------------------------------------------------------------- */
.loc-tabs { margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl); }

/* THE RAIL WRAPS WIDE AND SCROLLS NARROW — a MEASURED correction, not a
   preference. The first build of this block scrolled at every width, and the
   1440 render showed the consequence: the fourteenth pill sat ~750px past the
   right edge of a 1152px container with the last VISIBLE pill clipped mid-word
   and no affordance that anything followed it. On a page whose entire subject is
   WHERE WE WORK, burying five markets off-screen is the wrong trade — so above
   640px every market is on screen at once across two rows.
   At 640px and below it goes back to one scrolling row, where a horizontal swipe
   is the native gesture and fourteen stacked pills would be ~400px of chrome
   before the visitor reaches any content. Either way the overflow is owned HERE:
   document-level horizontal overflow is measured at 1440 AND 375 by
   verify-render-reality, and it fails the page. */
.loc-tabrail {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
  padding-bottom: var(--ds-space-sm);
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .loc-tabrail {
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
  }
  .loc-tabrail::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 6px; }
  .loc-tabrail::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--ds-line); border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill); }
}

.loc-tab {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 2px;
  min-height: 56px;                       /* >= the 40px tap floor at 375 */
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-md);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 70%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-soft) 62%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  cursor: pointer;
  text-align: left;
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out),
              background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
.loc-tab:hover { border-color: var(--ds-accent); }
.loc-tab:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.loc-tab[aria-selected="true"] {
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
}
.loc-tab__name { font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.2; white-space: nowrap; color: var(--ds-ink); }
.loc-tab__count {
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.2;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
/* the honesty rule, in the rail itself: a market with no stock never reads amber */
.loc-tab__count[data-stock="0"] { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }

/* ---- one market panel ------------------------------------------------------- */
.loc-panel {
  display: block;
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
  padding: clamp(20px, 3vw, 34px);
}
.loc-panel[hidden] { display: none; }      /* load-bearing — see the header note */
.loc-panel:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
.loc-panel__head { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg); }
.loc-panel__head h3 { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs); }
.loc-panel__dek { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ds-ink-soft); max-width: 62ch; }
/* The global reset zeroes paragraph margins (line 45), so the statewide panel's two
   deks rendered FUSED as one block — the same defect block [AD](a) fixed for the
   inventory intro. MEASURED on the 1440 render, not theorised. */
.loc-panel__dek + .loc-panel__dek { margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm); }
.loc-panel__foot {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  padding-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}

/* ---- the honesty flag ------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Block, NOT flex. A flex container would turn the sentence after the label into
   an ANONYMOUS flex item — untargetable by CSS, and unable to carry the measure
   this text needs. Inline-block label inside a block paragraph keeps the badge
   on the first line and lets the sentence wrap normally. */
.loc-flag {
  display: block;
  max-width: 78ch;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: var(--ds-warning-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--ds-warning);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}
.loc-flag__lbl {
  display: inline-block;
  margin-right: var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: .1em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}

/* ---- location x classification rows ----------------------------------------- */
.loc-types { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--ds-space-sm); }
.loc-type {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 46px minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 55%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
}
.loc-type__no {
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 46px;
  height: 38px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono);
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);
}
.loc-type__body { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; min-width: 0; }
.loc-type__name {
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.35;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: .18em;
  text-decoration-color: var(--ds-glass-border);
}
.loc-type__name:hover { color: var(--ds-accent-ink); text-decoration-color: var(--ds-accent); }
.loc-type__name:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.loc-type__stock { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }
.loc-type__go {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 0 var(--ds-space-sm);
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 700;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-underline-offset: .2em;
}
.loc-type__go:hover { color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark); }
.loc-type__go:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

@media (max-width: 720px) {
  .loc-type { grid-template-columns: 46px minmax(0, 1fr); row-gap: var(--ds-space-xs); }
  .loc-type__go { grid-column: 1 / -1; padding-left: 0; }
}

/* ---- the five-step qualifier ------------------------------------------------ */
.qz {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
  padding: clamp(20px, 3vw, 34px);
}
.qz__head { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg); }
.qz__progress {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .1em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
}
.qz__bar {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xs);
  height: 4px;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-soft) 24%, transparent);
  overflow: hidden;
}
.qz__fill {
  display: block;
  height: 100%;
  width: 20%;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  background: var(--ds-grad-brand);
  transition: width var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
.qz__step { display: block; }
.qz__step[hidden] { display: none; }       /* load-bearing — see the header note */
.qz__q { font-size: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 26px); line-height: 1.25; }
.qz__hint { margin-top: var(--ds-space-xs); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ds-ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; }
.qz__opts {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}
@media (max-width: 720px) { .qz__opts { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }
/* The option rows are the canonical `.ec-shipopt` radio card. Two overrides only,
   and both are forced: (1) its default ground is --ds-bg-surface, which is the
   EXACT ground `.qz` itself sits on, so unmodified the cards would be invisible
   against their own container; (2) `.ec-shipopt.is-sel` (line 2711) mixes its
   selected ground off `transparent`, and this block is later in source order at
   equal specificity, so the selected state has to be restated here or the base
   rule below would silently clobber it. MEASURED on the restated grounds:
   --ds-ink 13.1-16.1:1, --ds-ink-soft 7.70-9.48:1. */
.qz__opts .ec-shipopt {
  align-items: flex-start;
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-soft) 34%, transparent);
}
.qz__opts .ec-shipopt:hover { border-color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }
.qz__opts .ec-shipopt.is-sel {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 14%, var(--ds-bg-surface-2));
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
}
.qz__opts .ec-shipopt:focus-within { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
/* `.ec-so-t` / `.ec-so-d` (lines 2717-2718) carry no `display`, so they are INLINE
   and the title ran straight into the dek with no break — MEASURED on the 1440
   render as "Not sure yetLeave it to us…". Blocking them is what makes the
   margin-top below do anything at all. */
.qz__opts .ec-so-t { display: block; line-height: 1.3; }
.qz__opts .ec-so-d { display: block; line-height: 1.45; margin-top: 2px; }

/* The inference callout. It appears only after the visitor picks "Not sure", and
   it QUOTES the homepage industry tile rather than publishing a sector -> type
   table of its own: claim C21 is homepage-only in _content-requirements/_dedup-ledger.md,
   so this surface may suggest and point, never restate. */
.qz__infer {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
}
.qz__infer[hidden] { display: none; }

.qz__sum {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 70%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
.qz__sum b { color: var(--ds-ink); }

/* Two form fields side by side inside the qualifier; .ec-ftwo already does this,
   but it has no narrow reflow of its own, so give it one here rather than edit it. */
@media (max-width: 720px) { .qz .ec-ftwo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }

/* Chevron for the qualifier's own selects. Block [AD] drew one for the inventory
   board but scoped it to #inventory, and `.ec-ctrl` (line 2687) sets
   `appearance: none` with no replacement glyph anywhere else in the sheet — so an
   unscoped select renders identical to a text input with no affordance that it
   opens. Same rotated-border-wedge construction as [AD](c); geometry literals
   only, colour is a token. */
.qz .ec-field { position: relative; }
.qz .ec-field:has(> select.ec-ctrl) > select.ec-ctrl { padding-right: 40px; }
.qz .ec-field:has(> select.ec-ctrl)::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; right: 17px; top: calc(50% + 4px);
  width: 8px; height: 8px; pointer-events: none;
  border-right: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(45deg);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .loc-tab { transition: none; }
  .qz__fill { transition: none; }
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AG] LOCATIONS — two MEASURED defects found by driving the rendered page at
   375 after the deterministic gates were already green. Neither is a gate
   failure (verify-render-reality's tap floor is 40px and both cleared it) —
   they are quality defects the gate cannot express. Token-pure, append-only.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (a) DISABLED BACK LOOKS ENABLED. On step 1 the Back button carries the
       `disabled` attribute, but measured it computes IDENTICALLY to an enabled
       .btn-secondary — opacity 1, rgb(255,255,255) text, rgb(255,255,255)
       border, cursor:pointer. A control that is inert but advertises itself as
       pressable is a worse affordance than no control. Scoped to the stepper so
       no other disabled button on the site changes. */
.qz [data-qz-back][disabled],
.qz [data-qz-next][disabled] {
  opacity: .42;
  cursor: not-allowed;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
/* NB: do NOT set `border-color: var(--ds-line)` here. My first attempt did, and it
   reintroduced the exact #4a4a4a-on-dark-band defect block [AC] exists to fix —
   verify-contrast caught it immediately at 1.77:1 against a 3:1 floor. The walker
   reads the COMPUTED border-color property, not the composited result, so the .42
   opacity does not excuse it. Leaving the border to [AC]'s relight keeps it legible;
   opacity + cursor + the muted label carry the disabled read on their own. */

/* (b) SUB-44px TAP TARGET. `.loc-type__name` measured 149x41 at 375 — it clears
       the gate's 40px floor but misses the 44px WCAG 2.5.8 target size. It is a
       standalone navigation link, not inline prose (inline links are exempt), so
       the target applies. */
.loc-type__name {
  min-height: 44px;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AH] CONTACT — chevron for the enquiry-form selects.
   `.ec-ctrl` sets `appearance: none` (:2687) and the sheet draws a replacement
   chevron ONLY under #inventory (block [AD]c) and .qz (block [AF]). contact.html's
   rebuild added #q-industry and #q-type alongside #q-need, so THREE selects were
   rendering identical to text inputs with no affordance that they open.
   This is a verbatim re-scope of [AD](c) — no new class, geometry literals only,
   colour from a token. `id="quote"` exists only on contact.html, so it cannot leak
   to index.html's `.field` usage.
   The general fix belongs on `.ec-field` upstream; REPORTED as a skill gap.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
#quote .field { position: relative; }
#quote .field:has(> select.ec-ctrl) > select.ec-ctrl { padding-right: 40px; }
#quote .field:has(> select.ec-ctrl)::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; right: 17px; bottom: 18px;
  width: 8px; height: 8px; pointer-events: none;
  border-right: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  transform: rotate(45deg);
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   [AI] HERO COMMAND BAR — SUGGESTION-PANEL COMPACTION + PLACEMENT (2026-07-30)
   index.html ONLY (the only page carrying [data-cmdbar]). Behaviour half:
   site.js block [AI], null-guarded on the cmdbar root exactly as [AE] is.
   Append-only and token-pure: every colour and every gap resolves from a
   var(--ds-*); the only literals are structural — two breakpoints, the grid
   track count, the 1px row unit the balancer states its split in, the 6px
   animation offset (the mirror of [AE]'s own -6px at :5057), and the
   62vh/520px/56vh/420px numbers restated verbatim from :5041 and :5150 as
   var() fallbacks so a JS-off page renders byte-identically to today.

   ── THE MEASURED DEFECT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   At 1440x900 the bar's form ends at y 868 inside a hero that ends at y 1183,
   and a suggestion panel measures 179-495px in one column. Two SEPARATE
   things then bite:
     1. `.hero--photo { overflow: hidden }` (:3066) clips whatever hangs below
        the hero.
     2. `.hero--photo { isolation: isolate }` (:3065) traps the panel's
        z-index:4 inside the hero, so anything that DID escape the clip is
        painted over by `section.logo-wall--strip`.
   Hit-testing every rendered option centre with elementFromPoint across 28
   openings (4 scroll positions x 7 queries) measured 62 of 164 rows
   reachable at 1440, and only 8 of 28 openings with the panel fully inside
   the hero. On an unscrolled load the number was ZERO of 43.

   ── WHY NEITHER DEFECT IS "FIXED" HERE ───────────────────────────────────
   `isolation: isolate` is LOAD-BEARING: the entire hero stack (media -3,
   scrim -2, bloom -1, grain 0, smoke 1, container 2 — documented at
   :3053-:3062) depends on it, and removing it drops the photograph and its
   scrim behind the page background. `overflow: hidden` likewise contains the
   ken-burns overscan (`transform: scale(1.06)`, :3088) and the smoke
   dissolve. `position: fixed` was separately measured: it escapes the
   overflow but NOT the z-index trap, reaching only 4 of 8 rows.

   So this block never lets the panel leave the hero. It is COMPACTED so it
   needs less room, then PLACED on whichever side of the bar has room —
   always inside the hero's own clip box, which is exactly where the isolated
   stacking context already paints it correctly at z-index 4. Nothing has to
   escape, so nothing about isolation, overflow, the z-index stack, the chrome
   or any HTML is touched. MEASURED AFTER: 164/164 rows reachable at 1440,
   152/164 at 1024, 162/164 at 768, and 28/28 openings fully inside both the
   hero and the viewport at every breakpoint (375 included, where the baseline
   managed 8/28).

   `--cb-panel-max` is a RUNTIME MEASUREMENT CHANNEL written by site.js [AI],
   not a design token — hence the --cb- prefix, deliberately not --ds-.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* (a) THE CLAMP BECOMES MEASURABLE. The fallbacks are the exact values this
       sheet already used at :5041 and :5150, so with JS off — or before the
       first placement runs — the panel's clamp is unchanged. */
.cmdbar__panel { max-height: var(--cb-panel-max, min(62vh, 520px)); }
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .cmdbar__panel { max-height: var(--cb-panel-max, min(56vh, 420px)); }
}

/* (b) FLIP UP. The panel re-anchors above the bar, over the hero copy, when
       site.js [AI] measures no usable room below. `bottom: 100%` resolves
       against `.cmdbar__form` (:4960, position: relative), whose height is the
       bar itself, so the panel sits one --ds-space-xs above the field's top
       edge — the mirror of [AE]'s own `top: calc(100% + var(--ds-space-xs))`.
       Both selectors are (0,1,0) and this block is later in the sheet, so
       `top: auto` wins over :5038. */
.cmdbar__panel--up { top: auto; bottom: calc(100% + var(--ds-space-xs)); }

/* The open transition must fall the other way when the panel opens upward.
   GATED ON no-preference DELIBERATELY: the reduce block at :5160 sets
   `animation: none` at (0,2,0) and comes EARLIER in the sheet, so an ungated
   `animation-name` here — also (0,2,0) — would re-enable motion for a
   reduced-motion visitor. site.js [AE] also withholds `.is-open` under
   reduce (:1000); this is the second belt. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .cmdbar__panel--up.is-open { animation-name: ds-cmdbar-in-up; }
  @keyframes ds-cmdbar-in-up {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(6px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }
}

/* (c) TWO COLUMNS AT >=768px — the compaction that makes a placement possible
       at all. site.js [AE] already emits one div[role="group"] per group
       (:986), so the panel's DIRECT children are already the correct grid
       cells and a group label can NEVER be separated from its options.

       `:not([hidden])` IS REQUIRED, NOT DECORATION. `.cmdbar__panel[hidden]
       { display: none }` (:5052) is (0,2,0); a bare
       `.cmdbar__panel.is-cols { display: grid }` would also be (0,2,0), come
       later, and therefore RENDER THE PANEL WHILE THE CONTROL IS CLOSED.
       With the `:not()` the rule is (0,3,0) but cannot match a hidden panel,
       so `[hidden]` still hides it. Verified: closed panel computes
       display:none with .is-cols still on it.

       `row-gap: 0` is load-bearing too — the balancer in site.js [AI] states
       its split in whole 1px rows, and any row gap would desynchronise that
       arithmetic. `scrollbar-gutter: stable` is likewise structural, not
       cosmetic: on a platform with CLASSIC (non-overlay) scrollbars, a
       scrollbar appearing after the height cap lands would narrow both
       columns and grow every group past the span the balancer measured for
       it, overlapping them. Reserving the track unconditionally makes the
       measurement stable on every platform. */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols:not([hidden]) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    align-content: start;
    align-items: start;
    column-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
    row-gap: 0;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
  }

  /* The 1px row unit site.js [AI] states its CONTIGUOUS split in: groups
     1..k stack down column 1, k+1..n down column 2, k chosen to minimise the
     taller column. Contiguous is the whole point — DOM order then reads down
     column 1 and then down column 2, which is precisely the order [AE]'s
     arrow keys already walk. Plain row-major auto-placement instead shares
     row heights between the columns and wastes the shorter cell in every row:
     MEASURED at 1440 for the query "type", 446px row-major vs 311px
     contiguous. The class is added only when the balancer has actually
     written spans, so the CSS-only fallback (row-major, still correct, just
     taller) survives if the JS half never runs. */
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols.is-balanced { grid-auto-rows: 1px; }

  /* the no-match banner is prose, not a group: it spans both columns */
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols > .cmdbar__none { grid-column: 1 / -1; }

  /* ONE group — the empty-input "Start here" prompt, which is the most-seen
     state, and any single-group result — cannot be laid out as a 2-up OF
     groups, so that group spans the panel and splits its OWN options across
     two columns instead, with the label spanning so it still labels both.
     site.js [AI] assigns each option an explicit column and row so the split
     is COLUMN-MAJOR (options 1..k down the left, k+1..n down the right) and
     not the left-right-left zig-zag that row-major auto-placement would give.
     That keeps ONE reading convention across the whole control: every group,
     single or not, reads down its left column and then down its right. */
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols.is-onegroup > [role="group"] {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    align-content: start;
    align-items: start;
    column-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  }
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols.is-onegroup > [role="group"] > .cmdbar__grouplabel { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
}

/* Between 768 and 1099 a column is ~350-540px — too narrow for title +
   routing label + arrow on one baseline — so the row takes the shape the
   <=640 block (:5151) already gives it, by the same precedent. The arrow goes
   for the same reason it goes at :5153: it is aria-hidden decoration, and
   keeping it forces a THIRD line, because DOM order is title -> meta -> arrow
   and a `flex: 1 1 100%` meta pushes the arrow onto its own row. MEASURED:
   96px rows with the arrow kept, 68px without, 44px unwrapped at >=1100. */
@media (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1099px) {
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols .cmdbar__opt { flex-wrap: wrap; row-gap: var(--ds-space-2xs); }
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols .cmdbar__opt-m { flex: 1 1 100%; }
  .cmdbar__panel.is-cols .cmdbar__opt-go { display: none; }
}

/* ================================================================
   [AJ] SERVICES MEGA-MENU -> SLEEKER, AND CLEAR OF THE NAV (2026-07-30)
   Owner: "I like this version better but it is too tall, let's reduce the
   height a little bit, to make it look sleeker, and at the same time, when it
   opens, I want it to sit a little under end of the nav section, NOT
   overlapping the nav section."

   The picked design is KEPT WHOLE — the numbered 01..08 ordinal chips and all
   eight deks stay, because that is the version the owner prefers. This block
   changes GEOMETRY ONLY: no copy, no markup, no colour, no open mechanism.

   (1) THE OVERLAP. `.mm-mega` (:629) hardcodes `top: 68px`, but #site-header
       MEASURES 80px — stable at scrollY 0 / 200 / 600 / 1200. The header is
       position:sticky and gains a `.scrolled` class, but :578 changes only
       background / blur / shadow / border, and `.site-header__bar` (:579)
       carries an unconditional `padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-lg)`
       with NO media-query override anywhere in this sheet — so the header's
       height never changes, on scroll or across breakpoints. 68 against a real
       80 is why the panel currently opens 12px INSIDE the nav (measured
       navGap -12). `calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs))` = 88px lands it 8px BELOW
       the nav's bottom edge — the "a little under" the owner asked for.
       There is NO header-height token in tokens.css (checked), so 80px is
       written as the measured literal rather than inventing a token this
       sheet cannot honour.

   (2) THE HEIGHT — and this is the non-obvious part. The panel measured 486px
       at 1440 (498 at 1280). The lever is NOT the service list:
       `.mm-mega__feature` (:635) carries `min-height: 440px` and
       `.mm-mega__layout` (:632) is `align-items: stretch`, so THE PHOTO PANEL
       FLOORS THE WHOLE MENU at 440px. Trimming only the cards side cannot get
       below that floor — three separate card trims all bottomed out at exactly
       442px. So the feature min-height comes down to 356px FIRST, and only then
       do the card-side trims (grid gap 6 -> 4, card 76 -> 62, head 28 -> 24,
       foot 54 -> 44, chip 46 -> 38) actually register. Prototyped and rendered
       by the operator at 401px total, navGap +8. That ordering is the >= 1181px
       story ONLY — in the 1041-1180 band the driver is the CARDS CONTENT, not the
       photo panel, so there the card trims register on their own; see (3b).

   (3) SCOPE — why this is TWO min-width gates, and what lands in each. These
       selectors are id-scoped to #mm-mega-services so nothing else in the
       header is touched, but id specificity (1,1,0) BEATS a class rule (0,2,0)
       REGARDLESS of media query — the same order-vs-media-query trap block [L]
       documents. So each declaration has to be gated to exactly the band whose
       measurement justifies it. The additive chrome block already hand-tunes
       this panel at narrow widths:
         :796  @media (max-width: 1180px) .mm-mega__feature { min-height: 380px }
         :802  @media (max-width: 1040px) .mm-mega__feature { min-height: 220px }
       (single-column down there — :801 collapses the layout to `1fr`).

       (3a) FEATURE OVERRIDE -> >= 1181px ONLY. :635's 440px floor is the height
       driver only in that band, and BOTH operator measurements of it (1440 and
       1280 — :790's query changes width only, never height) sit inside it. Left
       unscoped, 356px would win at EVERY width and REPLACE that 220px, making
       the stacked tablet panel ~136px TALLER — the exact opposite of the
       directive — and with `.mm-mega__cards { overflow: auto }` under a
       `min(82vh, 640px)` cap (:800) it would eat the card list's room instead.

       (3b) CARD-SIDE COMPACTION -> >= 1041px, i.e. the WHOLE two-column layout.
       The first pass of this block gated the card trims at >= 1181px as well,
       on the stated ground that the 1041-1180 band "was NOT re-measured". That
       caution was right, but the band has NOW been measured, and it turns out to
       have a DIFFERENT height driver:
         1180px (identical at 1100 and 1060): panel 504 · feature 502
         (min-height 380) · cardsContent 502 · card 76 · grid 347 · gap 6 ·
         cards padding 26px 26px 22px   =>  THE CARDS CONTENT drives it.
       380 < 502, so down there the feature floor is IRRELEVANT — the ONLY thing
       that band was missing is exactly these card/grid/head/foot/chip trims,
       because its own rules leave that side at full size (:797 cards padding
       26/26/22, :654 gap 6, :655 card, :652 head, :668 foot, :659 chip 46). At
       504px it was TALLER than the 486px the owner called "too tall" at 1440 —
       the worst case in the sheet. Extending the card trims down to 1041 is the
       same fix pointed at the driver that is actually there, not a new guess.
       EXPECTED (the operator renders this; NOT verified in this block): cards
       content falls to roughly 4x62 + 3x4 + head 24 + foot 44 + padding 40 =
       ~368px, at which point the band's own pre-existing 380px feature floor
       becomes the driver and the panel lands near 380px (from 504px, ~-25%).
       That 380px floor is deliberately LEFT IN PLACE — it was not re-measured
       as a floor and it is what the band should rest on.

       (3c) WHY 1041 AND NOT LOWER. 1041 is the exact lower edge of the
       two-column layout. At <= 1040 the panel is a 620px-wide SINGLE column
       (:800-:803) with `max-height: min(82vh, 640px)` and `.mm-mega__cards
       { overflow: auto }`, and the feature floor drops to 220px. That band
       measured 640px = the cap, i.e. it is already scroll-bounded, and it has
       NOT been render-measured with the compaction applied — so it keeps status
       quo and the gate stops above it.
       The nav-clearance fix and the tap-target floor are deliberately NOT
       scoped — an overlapping panel and an undersized hit box are defects at
       every width, not just wide ones.

   (4) THE DEK ELLIPSIS is PRE-EXISTING and only PARTIALLY improved here.
       `.mm-mega__txt .s` (:667) is `white-space: nowrap` + `text-overflow:
       ellipsis`. The longest dek ("Person-to-person and premises-to-premises")
       renders 270px wide and NO dek box at 1280 is that wide, so the clipping
       at 1280 CANNOT be removed by layout alone. Shrinking the chip 46 -> 38
       gives the dek box 8px more measure and so reduces the clipping — that is
       ALL this block claims about it. `white-space: normal` was REJECTED:
       wrapping adds height, which directly contradicts the directive. No copy
       was changed.

   (5) TAP TARGET. `.mm-mega__all` measured 37px against this project's 40px
       floor. It is already `inline-flex; align-items: center` (:671), so a
       min-height grows the hit box without moving the label's baseline.

   APPEND-ONLY: :629 / :632 / :635 / :667 are left untouched — this block wins
   by cascade + id specificity, so DELETING THE BLOCK is a clean full revert. */

/* Nav clearance + hit-box floor: every width at which the mega renders. (The
   desktop nav, and with it this panel, leaves the DOM entirely at <= 768 —
   :811 sets `.site-nav { display: none }` — so no mobile scope is needed.) */
#mm-mega-services { top: calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs)); }
#mm-mega-services .mm-mega__all { min-height: 40px; }

/* (3b) CARD-SIDE compaction — the whole two-column layout, where CARDS CONTENT
   is the height driver (measured 502px of it at 1180/1100/1060). Stops at 1041,
   the lower edge of two columns; see (3c). */
@media (min-width: 1041px) {
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__cards   { padding: 22px 26px 18px; }
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__grid    { gap: 4px; }
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__card    { min-height: 62px; padding-block: 5px; }
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__head    { min-height: 24px; padding-bottom: 8px; }
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__foot    { min-height: 44px; padding-top: 12px; }
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__ico     { width: 38px; height: 38px; min-width: 38px; font-size: 12px; }
}

/* (3a) FEATURE floor — ONLY the band where :635's 440px photo-panel floor is the
   height driver. Never extended below 1181: :802's 220px must keep winning. */
@media (min-width: 1181px) {
  #mm-mega-services .mm-mega__feature { min-height: 356px; padding: 26px 28px; }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AK] PRIMARY CTA == THE MEGA "BROWSE INVENTORY" TREATMENT
   (owner: "let's have this color and effect for all primary CTA buttons")
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The style pointed at is `.mm-mega__feature .lnk` (:645-:648): brand-gradient
   fill, accent hairline, brand glow, and a trailing arrow that slides on hover.
   This block ports that look onto `.btn-primary` sitewide.

   (1) WHY THE GRADIENT IS NOT COPIED VERBATIM. `--ds-grad-brand` runs
   accent -> primary. White text measures 7.04:1 on the primary end but only
   2.28:1 on the accent end. `.btn-primary` computes to 15px / weight 600, i.e.
   NOT WCAG large text, so it owes 4.5:1 rather than 3:1 — and this project's
   verify-contrast gate samples RENDERED pixels on every page. The mega link gets
   away with the raw token today only because its panel ships hidden
   (opacity: 0; visibility: hidden), so the paint-sampled walker has never once
   reached it; that style has never actually been contrast-tested.
   So the light end is pulled back to the RICHEST amber that still clears the
   floor: a 40% accent-into-primary mix measures 4.63:1 (PASS), while 45%
   already measures 4.34:1 (FAIL). Every channel decreases monotonically from
   that end to primary, so luminance does too and 4.63:1 is the worst pixel
   anywhere in the fill. Angle (135deg), the accent border and the glow are
   unchanged, so the look survives as closely as the contrast floor allows.
   Written as color-mix over tokens, never as a literal.

   (2) WHY HOVER CANNOT REUSE THE REST GRADIENT. (Conclusion superseded — see
   (6) for the gradient hover actually gets now; the two measurements here still
   stand and are why it had to be a different pair.) :65's `background:`
   SHORTHAND resets background-image to none, so on hover the fill falls back to
   the flat --ds-primary-deep it has always used (18.59:1 with white).
   Re-declaring THIS block's REST gradient on hover is FORBIDDEN: it would put
   `filter: brightness(1.05)` on the amber end, which measures 4.25:1 and FAILS
   the gate. Contrast beats fidelity, so the amber end never appears in a hover
   fill. (Consequence, accepted: the fill change is a hard cut, because
   background-image is not interpolable and `filter` is not in :63's transition
   list.)

   (3) ARROW SCOPE — ANCHORS ONLY, NEVER A PHONE LINK. An arrow after
   "Call 800.799.9081" reads as navigation, which it is not, and a <button> that
   submits a form is not going anywhere either. Measured over the markup: of the
   91 static .btn-primary elements across the 13 HTML files, 80 are arrow-bearing
   anchors and 11 are not — 10 <button> (form submits plus the locations
   qualifier steps) and the single tel: anchor in contact.html.

   (4) justify-content IS PART OF THE PORT, NOT DECORATION. Three existing rules
   make a primary CTA full-width and then lean on `text-align: center` to centre
   its label — which only works while the button is a BLOCK box:
   `.product-card__cta` (:4307 display:block + width:100% + text-align:center —
   15 anchors, at EVERY width) and [AB]'s `.cta-row > .btn` /
   `.cta-formcard .form > .btn` (:4064-:4065 width:100% + text-align:center —
   26 anchors at <= 600px). text-align does not position FLEX items, so those 41
   labels would left-align the moment display becomes inline-flex, and because
   this selector is more specific than either rule the media query could not win
   it back. On a shrink-wrapped button there is no free space, so it is a no-op.

   APPEND-ONLY: :63 (.btn), :64 / :65 / :66 (.btn-primary + hover + focus) and
   :645-:648 (the mega link) are all left byte-for-byte alone. This block wins on
   source order plus specificity, so DELETING IT is a clean full revert. :64's
   flat `background: var(--ds-primary)` stays as the colour layer beneath
   background-image; :66's focus ring and :65's translateY lift are never
   redeclared here and so survive untouched.
   ========================================================================== */
.btn-primary {
  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 40%, var(--ds-primary)), var(--ds-primary));
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 60%, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-glow);
}
a.btn-primary:not([href^="tel:"]):not([href^="sms:"]) { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 9px; }
a.btn-primary:not([href^="tel:"]):not([href^="sms:"])::after { content: "\2192"; transition: transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease); }
.btn-primary:hover { box-shadow: var(--ds-glow); filter: brightness(1.05); }
a.btn-primary:not([href^="tel:"]):not([href^="sms:"]):hover::after { transform: translateX(4px); }

/* (6) HOVER GETS ITS OWN, DARKER GRADIENT PAIR. THE DEFECT THIS FIXES: at rest
   the CTA painted (1)'s gradient, but on hover the gradient DISAPPEARED and the
   button flattened to near-black — it read as "switched off" rather than as
   hovered. The cause is exactly (2)'s finding: :65's `background:` SHORTHAND
   resets background-image to none, so the entire gradient layer vanished the
   moment the pointer arrived and the fill became flat --ds-primary-deep.

   THE OBVIOUS FIX WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED, NOT SKIPPED. Re-declaring (1)'s
   REST gradient here would combine with the `filter: brightness(1.05)` on the
   rule above: the 40% accent mix brightens to 4.251:1 against white, under the
   4.5:1 that .btn-primary's 15px / weight 600 label owes (it is not WCAG large
   text), so it would have failed verify-contrast on every page.

   So hover runs a DIFFERENT pair, --ds-primary -> --ds-primary-deep: still a
   gradient, but every stop is darker than the rest stop it replaces, which is the
   natural "press" direction. Contrast therefore IMPROVES rather than degrades.
   White measures 7.04:1 on --ds-primary and 18.59:1 on --ds-primary-deep, and
   every channel decreases monotonically from the first to the second, so
   luminance does too — THE WORST PIXEL ANYWHERE IN THE HOVER FILL IS THE
   --ds-primary END, at 7.04:1, against 4.63:1 for rest's worst pixel. Brightened
   by that same 1.05 filter the --ds-primary end still measures 6.53:1, about 1.45x
   the floor, so the filter cannot push this pair under the gate.

   CASCADE. This selector is (0,2,0), tying :65 and winning on source order, so
   background-image survives while :65's background-color stays underneath as the
   opaque fallback layer. It LOSES to :4774 at (0,3,1), so the inventory active
   chip keeps background-image: none and is untouched by this rule — the same
   reason (1)'s gradient was already inert there, see (5).

   NOT FIXED HERE, DELIBERATELY: per (2) the rest -> hover fill change is still a
   hard cut. It is now LESS noticeable, not more: the swap is gradient -> gradient
   at the same 135deg, with the rest fill's dark stop (--ds-primary) reappearing as
   the hover fill's light stop, so it reads as the same fill rolled darker instead
   of as the fill being switched off. */
.btn-primary:hover { background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--ds-primary), var(--ds-primary-deep)); }

/* (5) THE FILTER BAR REUSES .btn-primary AS A CHIP, SO THE ACCENT HAIRLINE HAD
   TO GO DARK THERE. Measured regression from (1)'s border. inventory.html's
   active category chip (`a.btn.btn-primary[data-cat][aria-current="true"]` — the
   ONLY .btn-primary site-wide carrying aria-current) is not a normal CTA: rule
   :4773-:4776 fills it with --ds-accent and inks it with --ds-primary-deep
   (correct dark-on-amber), and :4774 ALSO re-declares
   `border-color: var(--ds-accent)`. So (1)'s hairline painted accent ON accent
   and verify-contrast measured it at 1:1 against a 3:1 button-outline floor — a
   HARD FAIL that could not exist before this block, because :63's `border: 0`
   left no border to grade. A 70% primary-deep-into-accent mix measures 4.54:1 on
   that same amber fill, clearing the outline floor by about 1.5x. (1)'s gradient
   was already inert here — :4774's `background` SHORTHAND resets
   background-image to none — so the border was the only broken layer.

   WIDTH IS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT 1px, ONLY THE COLOUR MOVES. site.js:448-449
   toggles aria-current as the visitor switches category, so dropping to
   `border: 0` in the active state would resize the chip by 2px on every switch.

   SELECTOR WEIGHT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT VERBOSITY. The short form
   `.btn-primary[aria-current="true"]` is (0,2,0) and would LOSE to :4774's
   border-color at (0,3,1) — the fix would never paint. This selector is (0,4,1),
   so it wins on SPECIFICITY rather than merely on being later in the file. */
.tg-filterbar a.btn-primary[data-cat][aria-current="true"] { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 70%, var(--ds-accent)); }


/* ============================================================================
   [AL] LICENSING DROPDOWN -> "FEATURE SPLIT" SHELF, HEIGHT-MATCHED TO THE MEGA
        (2026-07-30)
   Owner picked Direction D and asked for it to be "the same height with what we
   have with the mega menu so it will look balanced and intentional".

   THE THREE DEFECTS THIS REPLACES (all operator-measured in headless Chrome):
     1. A 268x701 portrait WALL -- nine 77px links in one narrow column, titles
        wrapping to two lines.
     2. It OVERLAPPED THE NAV by ~19px. Worse than the mega's, because [AJ]'s
        clearance fix was scoped to #mm-mega-services only.
     3. It was CUT OFF with no escape: `max-height: none; overflow-y: visible`,
        so at 1440x700 it ran 63px past the viewport and the bottom links were
        unreachable -- not scrollable, gone. ANY window under 763px tall lost
        content.

   WHY THE CAP IS ON THE `> ul` AND NEVER ON THE PANEL: `.mm-panel::after` (:611)
   is the hover BRIDGE and it lives OUTSIDE the panel's box. `overflow-y` on the
   panel would clip it and the menu would become unreachable by mouse. Capping
   the ul caps the panel (panel height = ul + padding + border) while leaving the
   bridge in visible overflow.

   NAV CLEARANCE uses [AJ]'s exact idiom, `calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs))`.
   HONEST NOTE: the header now measures 82.25px, not 80px -- block [AK] added a
   1px border to .btn-primary and the header CTA is the header's height driver --
   so this yields +5.75px of real clearance, not +8. BOTH panels share that same
   stale 80px literal DELIBERATELY, so they sit on one line and move together
   when it is corrected. Matching the mega is the whole point of this block.
   ============================================================================ */
/* =============================================================================
 * DIRECTION D — "FEATURE SPLIT"  ·  #mm-dd-licensing ONLY
 * A wide, short three-zone shelf: one promoted classification tile (Type 47)
 * + two labelled ledger columns. Echoes the services mega's feature+list
 * rhythm and shares its exact top edge, so the header reads as ONE system.
 * ============================================================================= */

/* ── 1 · THE PANEL — wide short shelf, CLEAR of the nav ──────────────────────
   FIXED, like the mega (:629), because a 920px panel cannot hang off a 120px
   <li> offset-parent — the sheet's own comment at :626-628 documents exactly
   this. `top` on a fixed box is viewport-relative, so the clearance is exact
   and independent of the <li>'s offset inside the bar; 88px is the [AJ]
   literal (:5852), which puts this shelf on the SAME top edge as the mega.

   The right edge is pinned to the header container's own content edge instead
   of being viewport-centred, so the shelf ENDS where the nav ends (flush with
   the "Talk to a broker" CTA) at every width. Bonus: no translateX is needed,
   so :610/:614's transform — the open mechanism — is left completely alone. */
#mm-dd-licensing {
  position: fixed;
  top: calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs));           /* = 88px, same edge as the mega */
  left: auto;                                     /* releases .mm-dropdown's left: 0 */
  right: max(var(--ds-space-lg),
             calc((100vw - var(--ds-container-max)) / 2 + var(--ds-space-lg)));
  min-width: 0;                                   /* releases .mm-dropdown's 268px */
  width: min(920px, calc(100vw - 2 * var(--ds-space-lg)));
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
}
/* The hover-BRIDGE (:611) is deliberately NOT restyled and NOT clipped: no
   `overflow` is set on the panel, so ::after still spans y 76-90 across the
   whole 920px width — verified live: a pointer parked at y=78 or y=84 holds the
   panel open indefinitely. Only the 58-76px band is dead, and site.js's 240ms
   HOVER_CLOSE_DELAY + `panel.pointerenter → cancelClose` (site.js:139-144)
   covers crossing it. The mega's equivalent dead band is 58-88 (its own
   `overflow: hidden` clips its bridge), so this is strictly better than parity. */

/* ── 2 · THE SHELF'S TWO ZONES ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   preview pane | the nine-item ledger.
   RETRACKED 2026-07-30 by block [AM]. This rule keeps the grid SHELL — the gaps,
   the focus-ring padding and the no-cutoff cap below — but it no longer declares
   its own tracks. The `grid-template-columns: minmax(0,268px) minmax(0,1fr)
   minmax(0,1fr)` and `grid-template-rows: auto repeat(3, 1fr)` that used to sit
   here WERE the promotion: a 268px feature column for Type 47 plus a group-label
   row. [AM] states the replacement (a 2-column x 5-row ledger carrying all nine)
   and makes the PANEL the outer grid, preview | ul. See [AM] §1-§2. */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul {
  display: grid;
  column-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  row-gap: 0;
  align-items: stretch;
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs);                    /* room for the 3+2px focus ring */
  /* GUARANTEED no-cutoff backstop. The shelf FITS unaided down to a ~460px-tall
     viewport (measured), so this only engages below that — but when it does, no
     item is ever cut off, it scrolls. Capped on the UL and NOT on the panel, so
     the ::after hover-bridge is never clipped and .mm-panel__bloom still paints.
     Terms: viewport - header(80) - the 8px nav gap - a 24px bottom gutter
            - the panel's own 8px padding (x2) - the ul's 8px padding (x2)
            - the panel's 1px border (x2). */
  max-height: calc(100vh - 80px - var(--ds-space-xs) - var(--ds-space-lg)
                   - (2 * var(--ds-space-xs)) - (2 * var(--ds-space-xs)) - 2px);
  overflow-y: auto;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  scrollbar-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 55%, transparent) transparent;
}

/* every <li> is a column so its link FILLS the row it was dealt — that is what
   makes the two ledgers read as even rows instead of top-hugged blocks */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; }
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li > a { flex: 1; }

/* ── 3 · THE FEATURE TILE — REMOVED 2026-07-30, SEE BLOCK [AM] ───────────────
   THE PROMOTION LIVED HERE, and it is the one thing the owner asked to go: "I
   still want the Type 47 in the list and use this area as a larger preview."
   Deleted, exactly two things:
     (a) nine explicit `grid-column` / `grid-row` placements that dealt DOM item
         2 (Type 47) a tall tile in column 1 and flowed the other eight as a 2x4
         ledger in columns 2-3;
     (b) ~105 lines styling that tile — its own material, its own 186px "47"
         watermark (`> a::before`), its own 38px arrow (`> a::after`), its own
         `.t`/`.d` type scale and its own hover treatment.
   Type 47 was never missing from the DOM list; it was promoted OUT of it
   visually. It is now an ordinary ledger item in its existing DOM position, and
   the 268px column this tile occupied is a PREVIEW PANE (`.mm-dd-preview`),
   owned by block [AM] at the end of this file and driven by site.js [AM].
   NOTHING ELSE IN [AL] MOVED: §1 (the panel, its nav clearance, and the
   capped-ul / uncapped-panel no-cutoff rule), §5 (the focus ring) and the HEIGHT
   MATCH below are byte-for-byte what they were. */

/* ── 4 · THE LEDGER ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ALSO REMOVED 2026-07-30: the two GENERATED GROUP LABELS that headed items 1
   and 6 ("Licences, permits & transfers" / "Coverage & resources"). Two reasons,
   both hard:
     1. They no longer describe anything — [AM] flows the nine BY ROW across two
        columns, so item 6 is not the head of a column any more.
     2. They do not fit the HEIGHT MATCH. At >=1181px the ul's inner box is 367px;
        two ~26px label rows would leave 315px for five item rows = 63px a row,
        and a ledger link's padding + a two-line dek needs ~69px. The 40px tap
        floor and the 401px match cannot both survive them.
   The taxonomy they carried is not lost: DOM order still states it, and the
   preview pane reads each item out in full, one at a time. */

/* ledger hairlines: every row except the first. [CC] the ledger is now EIGHT items
   flowing two across, so row 1 is items 1-2 and every item from 3 on opens a row that
   needs the line. Written as a range rather than an enumeration so it cannot go stale
   again the way the old 3..9 list did when the count changed — the previous version
   still named item 9 after the ledger had dropped to eight. */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n + 3) {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
}

#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a {
  min-height: 40px;                               /* tap-target floor */
  gap: 1px;
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-sm);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  /* TOP-aligned, not centred: the link fills its row (flex:1), and rows carry
     one- and two-line deks, so centring threw the two columns' first-row titles
     8px out of register with each other (measured). Top-aligning puts every
     title in both ledgers on one baseline. */
  justify-content: flex-start;
  transition: background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a .t {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-size: 14.5px;
  line-height: 1.25;
}
/* arrow reveal — the .mm-mega__card idiom (:664) */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a .t::after {
  content: "\2192";
  content: "\2192" / "";                          /* decorative — see the note above */
  flex: none;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateX(-4px);
  transition: opacity var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease), transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a .d {
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.35;
}
/* replace :622's reflowing padding-left jump with a transform */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a:hover,
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a:focus-visible {
  padding-left: var(--ds-space-sm);
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
  transform: translateX(2px);
}
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a:hover .t::after,
#mm-dd-licensing > ul > li:nth-child(n) > a:focus-visible .t::after {
  opacity: 1;
  transform: translateX(0);
}

/* ── 5 · KEYBOARD FOCUS — unmistakable on every one of the 9 items ─────────── */
#mm-dd-licensing a:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* ── 6 · NARROW DESKTOP BAND (769-1023px) ────────────────────────────────────
   The desktop nav exists down to 769px (:811 removes it at <=768), so the shelf
   is trimmed there rather than left to guess. The four rules that trimmed the
   FEATURE TILE in this band were deleted with the tile itself; block [AM]
   narrows the preview pane and steps its type down in the same band. */
@media (max-width: 1023px) {
  #mm-dd-licensing > ul { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); column-gap: var(--ds-space-sm); }
}

/* ---- HEIGHT MATCH TO THE SERVICES MEGA MENU --------------------------------
   The mega is NOT one number; it changes by band (operator-measured):
       >= 1181px  ->  401px
       1041-1180  ->  418px
   ONE custom property carries it so there is a single edit point.
   *** THIS VALUE MIRRORS THE MEGA'S CONTENT-DRIVEN HEIGHT. If the mega's
   content changes, THIS PROPERTY MUST BE UPDATED WITH IT. *** No min-height is
   set on the mega itself -- it is already verified green and is not touched.

   The min-height goes on the `> ul`, not the panel: the ul is the grid, its rows
   are `1fr` under `align-items: stretch`, so the extra height distributes into
   the ledger rows instead of pooling as dead space under them. Panel height =
   ul + the panel's 8px padding x2 + its 1px border x2, so the ul target is the
   panel target minus those, expressed in tokens rather than as a magic 18px.

   Not applied below 1041: there the mega becomes a 620px single column capped at
   min(82vh, 640px) with its own internal scroll, and matching 640px would make a
   DROPDOWN as tall as a full mega panel. The shelf keeps its own layout there. */
@media (min-width: 1041px) {
  #mm-dd-licensing { --mm-panel-h: 418px; }
  #mm-dd-licensing > ul {
    min-height: calc(var(--mm-panel-h) - (2 * var(--ds-space-xs)) - 2px);
  }
}
@media (min-width: 1181px) {
  #mm-dd-licensing { --mm-panel-h: 401px; }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AM] LICENSING DROPDOWN -> NINE IN THE LIST, AND A LARGE PREVIEW PANE
        (2026-07-30)
   Owner, pointing at the shipped Licensing dropdown:
     "I still want the Type 47 in the list and use this area as a larger preview."

   TWO CHANGES, AND ONLY TWO.
     1. THE PROMOTION IS GONE. Type 47 was always in the DOM list; [AL] dealt it
        a tall FEATURE TILE in column 1 and flowed the other eight as a 2x4
        ledger. [AL] §3 and its nine explicit placements are deleted (a note is
        left in their place saying so). All NINE items are now ordinary ledger
        items, in their existing DOM order, Type 47 second.
     2. THE AREA IT VACATED IS A PREVIEW PANE. `.mm-dd-preview` — ONE <a>, added
        identically to all 11 chrome files — occupies exactly the tile's old box
        (268px wide, full panel height) and shows the hovered or keyboard-focused
        item LARGER than the list does. site.js block [AM] fills it.

   ── WHY 2 COLUMNS x 5 ROWS AND NOT 3x3 ──────────────────────────────────────
   Nine has no even 2-column rectangle, so the arrangement was picked on the
   shelf's MEASURED geometry, not on taste:

     · The panel is 920px wide at BOTH 1440 and 1280 — `min(920px, 100vw - 48px)`,
       and at 1280 the right offset resolves to 64px, so it never clamps. Content
       box = 920 - 2 (border) - 16 (padding) = 902px. The preview takes 268 and
       the column-gap 16, leaving the ul 618px, whose own content box is 602px,
       which at column-gap 16 gives two ledger columns of (602-16)/2 = 293px.
     · 293px is the EXACT ledger-column width [AL] already ships and that is
       already verified to hold every title on one line with the deks wrapping to
       at most two. "Titles unwrapped at 1440 and 1280" is therefore a carried-
       over measurement here, not a fresh bet on a font metric.
     · THREE columns cannot do that. The longest title ("Type 47 — On-Sale,
       Eating Place") is what sets the 293px figure; the other two groups still
       need roughly 253 and 180, so three real columns want a ~1075px panel —
       wider than the 993px available at 1041px, which is the BOTTOM of the band
       the height match covers. It would wrap exactly where it must not.
     · Five rows x two columns is ten cells for nine items, and the tenth is not
       left as a hole: item 9 (Licensing FAQs) SPANS both columns as a full-width
       closing row. Five even rows, no ragged corner.
     · Row height at >=1181: the ul's inner box is 367px / 5 = 73.4px a row. A
       ledger link is 8+18.1+1+33.8+8 = 68.9px at its worst (two-line dek), so
       every row clears both its content and the 40px tap floor.

   ── HEIGHT MATCH: UNTOUCHED, STILL ONE PROPERTY ─────────────────────────────
   `--mm-panel-h` still lives on `#mm-dd-licensing` and its `min-height` still
   sits on the `> ul`. NEITHER MOVED. The panel becomes the outer grid here, and
   an auto row is sized by the tallest item's contribution — which is the ul's
   383px min-height (401 - 2*8 padding - 2*1 border). The pane STRETCHES into
   that row rather than driving it: its own content tops out near 255px, and it
   carries the same viewport max-height as a backstop. So the panel still
   measures 401px at >=1181 and 418px at 1041-1180, from the same single edit
   point.

   ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED ────────────────────────────────────────
   The nav clearance (`top: calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs))`), the no-cutoff
   guarantee (`max-height` + `overflow-y` on the `> ul`, NEVER on the panel, so
   `.mm-panel::after` — the hover bridge at :611 — is never clipped), and the
   open mechanism at :614. `display: grid` on the panel is safe for both: the
   bridge AND `.mm-panel__bloom` are `position: absolute`, so they are out of
   flow and are NOT grid items. The mega menu is not referenced at all.
   ============================================================================ */

/* ── 1 · THE PANEL IS NOW THE OUTER TWO-ZONE GRID ────────────────────────────
   Both zones are placed EXPLICITLY, so the pane can sit on the LEFT while
   staying LAST in source order — the nine real links come first in the DOM. */
#mm-dd-licensing {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 268px) minmax(0, 1fr);
  column-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  align-items: stretch;
}
#mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
#mm-dd-licensing > ul             { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; min-width: 0; }

/* ── 2 · THE LEDGER — ALL NINE, FIVE EVEN ROWS ───────────────────────────────
   `1fr` and not `minmax(0, 1fr)`: a row must never be sized SMALLER than its
   item, or a two-line dek would overflow its own cell instead of the ul doing
   what [AL] built it to do and scrolling. */
#mm-dd-licensing > ul {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  /* [CC] FOUR rows, not five. The ledger was authored for nine items with the ninth
     spanning both columns to close it. Removing the three individual licence-type rows
     and replacing them with one leaves EIGHT — which fills 2x4 exactly, with no odd item
     to close and no empty fifth row. The nth-child(9) span is removed with them: at eight
     items it matches nothing, and leaving a rule that silently stops applying is how the
     next person inherits a mystery. */
  grid-template-rows: repeat(4, 1fr);
  grid-auto-flow: row;
}

/* ── 3 · THE PREVIEW PANE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   EVERY RULE HERE IS SCOPED THROUGH THE ID ON PURPOSE. The pane is an <a> inside
   `.mm-dropdown`, so `.mm-dropdown a` (:620) and `.mm-dropdown a:hover` (:625)
   both match it and would otherwise win on specificity, handing the pane the
   list-link padding, the soft hover ground and the padding-left hover jump.

   IT IS `aria-hidden="true"` AND `tabindex="-1"` IN THE MARKUP, AND IT CARRIES
   NO `role="menuitem"`. It duplicates content that is already in the list, so
   leaving it in the a11y tree would announce every item twice and double the
   menu's reported item count. All nine real menuitems stay in the list, visible,
   focusable, and >= 40px. `tabindex="-1"` is load-bearing too: a focusable node
   inside an aria-hidden subtree is itself an a11y defect.

   The material is [AL] §3's, carried over verbatim so the shelf still reads as
   one system — minus the promotion. The layer stack is held in a custom property
   because the hover rule has to restate `background` in full (see §4). */
#mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview {
  --mm-prev-bg:
    var(--ds-grain),
    var(--ds-glow-wash),
    linear-gradient(158deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 13%, transparent) 0%,
                            transparent 58%),
    linear-gradient(0deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 82%, transparent),
                          color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 82%, transparent)),
    var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  gap: var(--ds-space-2xs);
  min-width: 0;
  min-height: 0;
  padding: var(--ds-space-md);
  isolation: isolate;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-decoration: none;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 30%, transparent);
  background: var(--mm-prev-bg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-rest);
  /* Backstop only. The ul's cap ([AL] §2) is what actually governs the shelf;
     this simply guarantees the PANE can never become the thing that pushes the
     panel past the viewport on a very short window. It is on the pane, not on
     the panel — putting any overflow on the panel would clip the hover bridge. */
  max-height: calc(100vh - 80px - var(--ds-space-xs) - var(--ds-space-lg)
                   - (2 * var(--ds-space-xs)) - 2px);
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              box-shadow var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease);
}

/* THE WATERMARK.
   NUMBERED LICENCES SHOW THEIR OWN CODE — 21 / 47 / 48 — parsed by site.js [AM]
   out of the item's own title text. Never authored here, never synthesised.

   EVERY OTHER ITEM SHOWS "§", AND NEVER A NUMBER. Conditional Use Permits,
   Transfer timeline & escrow, Statewide coverage, Knowledge base, Guides and
   Licensing FAQs are regulatory TOPICS and resources, not numbered ABC licence
   types, and inventing a code for them would be a false claim on a licensing
   site. "§" is the standard typographic mark for a section of a code; it is a
   single glyph, so the pane's geometry does not shift between the two cases; and
   — this is the actual reason it beat the alternatives — it CANNOT be misread as
   a licence code. A big initial letter was considered and rejected for exactly
   that: a 186px "C" sitting in the slot where "47" sits reads as a class code. */
#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__mark {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;                                    /* over the pane's bg, under its text */
  top: -26px;
  right: -14px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: 186px;
  line-height: .72;
  letter-spacing: -.05em;
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 22%, transparent);
  pointer-events: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
  user-select: none;
  transition: color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              transform var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
/* one glyph instead of two digits: pull it back in and let it run a touch larger
   so it carries the same optical weight as a two-digit code */
#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__mark[data-kind="neutral"] {
  top: -22px;
  right: 6px;
  font-size: 196px;
  letter-spacing: 0;
}

/* LARGER THAN THE LIST — that is the whole ask.
   List: title 14.5px, dek 12.5px, arrow 15px inline.
   Pane: title 26px,   dek 15px,   arrow in a 44px disc. */
#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__t {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: 26px;
  line-height: 1.12;
  letter-spacing: -.015em;
  /* NOT `balance` — balancing breaks "Type 47 — On-Sale, Eating Place" at the
     "On-Sale" hyphen. Greedy fill reads correctly. (Carried from [AL] §3.) */
  text-wrap: pretty;
  color: var(--ds-ink-heading);
}
#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__d {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 400;
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
/* the affordance that this pane is a link target: a glyph, no words, so no claim
   is invented. 44px, up from the tile's 38px — it is also the pane's mouse cue. */
#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__go {
  align-self: flex-start;
  flex: none;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
  width: 44px;
  height: 44px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 17px;
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
  background-image: var(--ds-grad-brand);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 55%, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-glow);
  transition: transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              filter var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}

/* ── 4 · THE PANE'S OWN HOVER — it is a real link to the previewed item ──────
   `background` has to be RESTATED, not patched: `.mm-dropdown a:hover` (:625)
   declares the `background` SHORTHAND, which resets background-image to `none`.
   Overriding background-color alone would win that one property and still lose
   the whole layer stack. Hence `--mm-prev-bg` — one definition, two uses. */
#mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover {
  background: var(--mm-prev-bg);
  padding-left: var(--ds-space-md);               /* kill :625's padding-left jump */
  text-decoration: none;
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 62%, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-card);
}
#mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover .mm-dd-preview__mark {
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 34%, transparent);
  transform: translateY(2px);
}
#mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover .mm-dd-preview__go {
  transform: translateX(3px);
  filter: brightness(1.06);
}

/* ── 5 · REDUCED MOTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The pane's content is swapped on hover AND on keyboard focus. A visitor who
   asked for less motion gets the swap with no transition and no transform — the
   information still arrives, it just does not move. [AL] §5's focus ring is
   untouched here: it is an outline, not motion. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview,
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__mark,
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__go { transition: none; }
  #mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover .mm-dd-preview__mark,
  #mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover .mm-dd-preview__go { transform: none; }
}

/* ── 6 · NARROW DESKTOP BAND (769-1023px) ───────────────────────────────────
   Mirrors what [AL] §6 did for the feature tile, which it trimmed to 210px in
   this band. The pane takes that same 210px and its type steps down with it; the
   two ledger columns keep [AL] §6's tighter gap. NOTE the ledger's own titles
   were already tight below ~830px before this change (the ledger columns were
   the same width there); that band is unchanged, not newly regressed. */
@media (max-width: 1023px) {
  #mm-dd-licensing {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 210px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  }
  #mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview { padding: var(--ds-space-sm); }
  #mm-dd-licensing > .mm-dd-preview:hover { padding-left: var(--ds-space-sm); }
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__mark { top: -18px; right: -10px; font-size: 128px; }
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__mark[data-kind="neutral"] { top: -15px; right: 4px; font-size: 136px; }
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__t { font-size: 21px; }
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__d { font-size: 14px; }
  #mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview__go { width: 40px; height: 40px; margin-top: var(--ds-space-xs); }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AN] #about BAND — "STATEMENT & RAIL" + THE MANIFESTO/LINKS ROW
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner pick, 2026-07-31. Supersedes the U1 band (structural.css:4082-4138):
   a full-height statement panel beside a two-column photo rail, and the
   qualifying sentence and the three link cards SIDE BY SIDE in one row rather
   than stacked.

   APPEND-ONLY. Every rule is scoped to #about and this block is the last thing
   in the file, so it wins the cascade on source order. U1 is left intact —
   DELETING THIS WHOLE BLOCK IS A COMPLETE REVERT. Only index.html carries
   id="about" (verified), so no other page is affected.

   Measured on this build at 1440/1024/768/375 — 0 h-overflow, 0 broken images,
   0 console errors, nothing left unrevealed at any breakpoint:
     section height     1229 -> 1012 at 1440 (-217);  2029 -> 1444 at 375 (-585)
     copy/rail imbalance  294px -> 0
     worst image retina   1.03x -> 1.45x at 1440;  0.79x -> 1.64x at 375
   The band was SUB-RETINA on mobile before this. Retina here is
   min(naturalW/boxW, naturalH/boxH)/2 — NEVER width-only, because under
   object-fit:cover the more constrained axis binds. The gain is won by BOX
   ASSIGNMENT, not by scaling anything up: the weak 512x512 escrow-signing
   source is pinned to the compact square cell at every breakpoint while the
   1400x1400 and 1200x1594 sources take the large cells.

   The one-row rules are gated to `min-width: 981px` DELIBERATELY. Written as
   base rules plus a reset at 980, the reset's `max-width: 30ch` beat this
   block's own @560 cap on source order and produced 26px of h-overflow at 375.
   Gating by min-width leaves the phone layout stacked and untouched, with no
   reset to get wrong.

   Known-open, reported not fixed: at 768 the rail is capped at 620px and
   centred, so it sits inset from the copy and card columns. Re-cutting it to
   full column width would cost story-decanter's retina there (~1.1x), so it
   was left as a deliberate trade rather than changed silently.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ---- band rhythm ---------------------------------------------------------
   (1,1,0) — beats .section (0,1,0). A tighter band, not a cramped one. */
#about.section { padding-block: clamp(56px, 6.6vw, 96px); }

/* ---- the split: statement panel + rail, equal boxes ---------------------- */
#about .ab-split {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, .84fr);
  gap: clamp(32px, 3.9vw, 56px);
  align-items: stretch;          /* was center — this is what zeroes imbalance */
}

#about .ab-copy {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: center;       /* text stays optically centred in the panel */
  min-width: 0;
}
#about .ab-copy .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-sm); }
#about .ab-copy h2 {
  margin-top: 0;
  font-size: clamp(32px, 3.55vw, 50px);
  line-height: 1.06;
  letter-spacing: -.015em;
  max-width: 15ch;
}
#about .ab-copy > p:not(.eyebrow) {
  max-width: 46ch;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
  opacity: .85;
  line-height: 1.62;
}

/* ---- the rail ------------------------------------------------------------
   Two column lines, two row lines. Column 2 and row 2 are both pinned to the
   SAME compact measure, so the foot cell is a true square — the small source
   never gets a big box. */
#about .ab-cluster {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) clamp(150px, 12.4vw, 190px);
  grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) clamp(150px, 12.4vw, 190px);
  gap: clamp(10px, 1vw, 14px);
  aspect-ratio: auto;            /* kills the U1 1/1 (and its 16/10 @980) */
  height: clamp(360px, 28vw, 420px);
  max-width: none;
  margin-inline: 0;
}
#about .ab-cluster figure {
  margin: 0;
  min-height: 0;
  min-width: 0;
  aspect-ratio: auto;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
}
#about .ab-cluster img { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }

/* explicit plate assignment — DOM order is decanter, escrow, cheers */
#about .ab-cluster__tall               { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }  /* 1400x1400 -> large plate */
#about .ab-cluster figure:nth-of-type(3) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; }         /* 1200x1594 -> upper cell */
#about .ab-cluster figure:nth-of-type(2) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 2; }         /* 512x512  -> compact square */

/* ---- the qualifying line ------------------------------------------------- */
#about .ab-manifesto {
  max-width: 46ch;               /* two lines instead of three — real height saved */
  margin-top: clamp(36px, 3.4vw, 50px);
  line-height: 1.22;
}
#about .ab-manifesto .accent { color: var(--ds-accent); }

/* ---- three pointers ------------------------------------------------------ */
#about .doc-list.ab-links {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: clamp(10px, 1vw, 14px);
  margin-top: clamp(32px, 3.1vw, 44px);
}
#about .doc-list__item {
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  padding: 18px 20px;
}
#about .doc-list__icon { width: 38px; height: 38px; }
#about .doc-list__icon svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
#about .doc-list__dek { font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.45; }

#about .ab-cta { justify-content: center; margin-top: clamp(28px, 2.6vw, 38px); }

/* =========================== responsive ================================== */

/* stacked: the rail centres under the statement and keeps its shape */
@media (max-width: 980px) {
  #about .ab-split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px); }
  #about .ab-copy { justify-content: flex-start; }
  #about .ab-copy h2 { max-width: 18ch; }
  #about .ab-copy > p:not(.eyebrow) { max-width: 64ch; }
  #about .ab-cluster {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 620px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    height: clamp(320px, 47vw, 400px);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) clamp(140px, 22vw, 190px);
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) clamp(140px, 22vw, 190px);
  }
  #about .ab-manifesto { max-width: 30ch; }
}

@media (max-width: 860px) {
  #about .doc-list.ab-links { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  /* the archetype drops __meta onto its own full-width line below 860 — right
     for a "PDF · 2 MB" meta, wrong for a one-glyph chevron. Keep it inline. */
  #about .doc-list__item { flex-wrap: nowrap; align-items: center; }
  #about .doc-list__meta { width: auto; }
}

/* phone: the rail folds to a wide plate over a paired foot row. The small
   source lands in a ~158px square here — its BEST box of any breakpoint. */
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  #about .ab-cluster {
    height: auto;
    max-width: 420px;
    gap: 10px;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: clamp(170px, 52vw, 230px) minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  #about .ab-cluster figure { aspect-ratio: auto; }   /* kills U1's @560 4/3 */
  #about .ab-cluster__tall               { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 1; }
  #about .ab-cluster figure:nth-of-type(3) { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; }
  #about .ab-cluster figure:nth-of-type(2) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 2; aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; }
  #about .ab-manifesto { max-width: 22ch; }
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #about .doc-list__meta { transition: none; }
  #about .doc-list__item:hover .doc-list__meta { transform: none; }
}

/* =============================================================================
 * [AN-b] — MANIFESTO + LINK CARDS SHARE ONE ROW. Cards become a STACKED LIST.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Same container-grid mechanism as [AN-a], but the three cards run as a single
 * column of full-width rows in the right cell instead of three narrow columns.
 * Each card then gets ~530px of width, so its dek sits on one or two lines
 * rather than towering, and the icon/type stay at their normal scale.
 *
 * Gated to `min-width: 981px` for the same reason as [AN-a]: below that the
 * layout is ABC's, untouched, so there is no reset block to get wrong.
 * ==========================================================================*/
@media (min-width: 981px) {
  #about .container {
    display: grid;
    /* EXACTLY the columns .ab-split uses (1fr / .84fr, gap clamp(32px,3.9vw,56px)),
       so the whole band resolves onto TWO column lines: the card stack starts
       where the copy starts, and the sentence starts where the photo rail
       starts. Measured before this snap the cards began at x=744 against the
       rail's x=796 — a 52px drift that read as a third edge. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, .84fr);
    column-gap: clamp(32px, 3.9vw, 56px);
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* ROWS ARE EXPLICIT, and that is load-bearing. Grid auto-placement is SPARSE
     by default: once the sentence is placed in column 2 of row 2, the cursor
     cannot travel backwards to column 1 of that same row, so a card stack asking
     only for `grid-column: 1` gets pushed down to row 3. That is not a
     side-by-side row at all — it silently became three stacked rows and added
     216px (1012 -> 1228 at 1440) while both elements still reported their
     correct columns. Naming the row is what actually puts them beside each other. */
  #about .ab-split { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 1; }
  #about .ab-cta   { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 3; }

  /* SWAPPED (owner, 2026-07-31): cards take the wide LEFT column under the copy,
     the sentence takes the narrow RIGHT column under the rail. */
  #about .ab-manifesto {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 2;
    max-width: none;
    margin-top: clamp(30px, 3vw, 44px);
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* one column, three rows — each card spans the full left cell */
  #about .doc-list.ab-links {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 2;
    margin-top: clamp(30px, 3vw, 44px);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(9px, 0.9vw, 13px);
  }
  #about .doc-list__item { align-items: center; padding: 15px 18px; }
  #about .doc-list__dek { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.45; }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AO] #services — DEPTH-OF-FIELD FOCUS ON THE SERVICE CARDS
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner request, 2026-08-06, from a Behance/Pinterest reference ("Minimal
   Typography Carousel", Fazle Rabbi Sarkar) in which the focused card is sharp
   and elevated while its neighbours sit blurred behind it.

   OWNER'S TWO BINDING DECISIONS, both honoured here:
     1. KEEP THE GRID. All 8 services stay visible and crawlable. The reference
        is a one-at-a-time carousel; this takes its depth-of-field LANGUAGE and
        applies it as an interaction instead. Not a carousel.
     2. MOTION ONLY, PALETTE UNTOUCHED. No colour, card shape, imagery or link
        colour changes. Only how the cards BEHAVE.

   REST STATE IS UNCHANGED. Nothing here paints until a pointer is over a card
   or a card's link takes keyboard focus. Verified: every card computes
   filter:none / opacity:1 / transform:none at rest, and section height is
   1097/1076/1384/2322 at 1440/1024/768/375 — identical to before this block.

   APPEND-ONLY and scoped to #services .svc-rows > .svc-row. `.svc-rows` and
   `.svc-row` exist ONLY on index.html (services.html's #services uses
   .cap-breakdown__grid / .capability-card), so no other page is touched.
   DELETING THIS BLOCK IS A COMPLETE REVERT.

   ---- THREE CASCADE FACTS MEASURED, NOT READ ----
   (each contradicts what structural.css looks like it says)

   1. THE -6px HOVER LIFT IS ALREADY DEAD ON THESE CARDS. `.svc-rows` carries
      `.wow-stagger`, and `.wow-stagger.in-view > * { transform:none }` (:942) is
      (0,2,0) — the same specificity as `.wow-lift:hover` (:936) — and comes
      LATER, so it wins. Measured: hovered .svc-row computed transform === "none";
      removing .in-view restores matrix(1,0,0,1,0,-5.32). So there was no lift to
      "preserve": writing translateY(-6px) here would have RESURRECTED a lift the
      shipped page does not have. The forward read is carried by .wow-lift's
      box-shadow, which does still fire.
   2. NO CARD MAY CARRY A TRANSFORM. getBoundingClientRect reflects transforms,
      so any perceptible scale on a sibling moves its box (measured: scale(.985)
      shifted a sibling from [732,220,564,159] to [736,221,556,156]). The owner's
      "slight scale-down" is therefore DROPPED — it is mutually exclusive with a
      non-reflowing grid, and under blur(3px) a 1.5% shrink is invisible anyway.
      The blur is the depth-of-field signal; the scale was garnish.
   3. TRANSITION LONGHANDS, NOT THE SHORTHAND. The shorthand also resets
      transition-delay and, at this rule's (1,2,0), would outrank every shipped
      .wow-stagger rule on these cards forever — silently zeroing any per-card
      delay added later. Longhands leave transition-delay alone (verified: still
      "0s, 0s").

   ---- THE BUG THIS BLOCK'S SELECTORS EXIST TO AVOID ----
   Guarding each arm with only its own state (:not(:hover) on the hover arm,
   :not(:focus-within) on the focus arm) makes the two arms blur EACH OTHER'S
   active card. Measured with keyboard focus on card 1 and the pointer on card 5:
   8 of 8 cards blurred, INCLUDING the focused one with its amber focus ring
   blurred away — reachable by the most ordinary path there is, tab to a card
   then reach for the mouse. Both arms therefore exclude BOTH states.
   After the fix: 6 of 8 blurred, both active cards sharp.

   Touch: the whole effect is gated behind (hover:hover) and (pointer:fine),
   hover AND focus rules alike — on a coarse pointer a tap leaves :hover stuck
   AND leaves focus on the tapped link, so an ungated focus rule would strand 7
   cards blurred by a second route. Verified: touch context reports
   hover:none / pointer:coarse and 0 siblings blurred.
   Reduced motion: the sibling filter is cancelled outright rather than merely
   un-transitioned — structural.css:979 already forces transition:none there, so
   an un-cancelled blur would hard-cut on every pointer move, which is exactly
   the abrupt change the preference exists to suppress.
   ========================================================================== */

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   TOUCH GATE — the whole effect lives inside this query and nowhere else.
   On a coarse pointer :hover sticks after a tap, which would strand 7 cards
   permanently blurred. Both :hover AND :focus-within rules are inside the gate:
   on a touch device a tap also leaves focus on the tapped link, so a
   :focus-within rule left outside would reproduce the exact same stuck state by
   a different route. Below this query the section behaves precisely as shipped.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {

  /* Transition. The first two entries are the shipped .wow-stagger values,
     restated so the reveal animation is bit-for-bit unchanged. Appended:
     `filter` (the effect itself) at 240ms — deliberately much faster than the
     640ms reveal so the blur tracks the pointer instead of lagging it — and
     `box-shadow`, which .wow-lift always meant to transition (structural.css:935)
     but lost to .wow-stagger; restoring it is what makes the sharp card read as
     rising forward now that no transform is available to do it. */
  /* LONGHANDS, not the `transition` shorthand — deliberately. The shorthand
     also resets transition-delay to 0s, and at this rule's (1,2,0) it would
     outrank every shipped .wow-stagger-family rule on these cards forever. That
     costs nothing TODAY (.svc-rows carries no .bl-grid, so the nth-child
     stagger delays at structural.css:2876-2880 never apply here) but it would
     SILENTLY zero any per-card delay added later. Longhands leave
     transition-delay untouched, so the trap never gets planted.

     `opacity` and `transform` restate .wow-stagger's shipped values verbatim
     (var(--ds-dur-slow) = 640ms, var(--ds-ease-out)) so the scroll-reveal is
     bit-for-bit unchanged. `filter` at 240ms is the effect itself — deliberately
     much faster than the 640ms reveal so the blur tracks the pointer instead of
     lagging it, which also keeps the expensive re-raster window short.

     `box-shadow` at 240ms is an OWNER-APPROVED ADDITION (2026-08-06), not an
     oversight — do not "clean it up". It was raised explicitly as going beyond a
     pure sibling recede (both verification lenses flagged it) and the owner chose
     to keep it. Rationale: .wow-lift (structural.css:935) always intended to
     transition box-shadow but lost the shorthand to .wow-stagger, so the lift
     shadow currently SNAPS. With no transform available on these cards (see
     cascade fact 1 in this block's header), the shadow is the sharp card's only
     "rising forward" cue, and fading it is what makes the card read as lifting
     rather than blinking. Measured live: rest
     rgba(10,4,4,.42) 0 4px 14px + rgba(10,4,4,.3) 0 14px 38px  ->  hover
     rgba(10,4,4,.5) 0 10px 26px + rgba(10,4,4,.4) 0 26px 60px. It moves no
     measurement; rest state is unchanged. */
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
    transition-property: opacity, transform, filter, box-shadow;
    transition-duration: var(--ds-dur-slow), var(--ds-dur-slow), 240ms, 240ms;
    transition-timing-function: var(--ds-ease-out), var(--ds-ease-out), var(--ds-ease), var(--ds-ease);
  }

  /* THE RECEDE.
     :has() rather than `.svc-rows:hover .svc-row:not(:hover)` on purpose — the
     grid box includes its 32px gutters, so a `.svc-rows:hover` key would fire
     while the pointer is merely BETWEEN cards and flash all 8 cards blurred on
     every traverse. :has(> .svc-row:hover) only engages once a card is really
     under the pointer, so there is no state in which all 8 are blurred.

     Both blur and dim ride inside ONE property. filter's own opacity() function
     is used instead of the `opacity` property so that (a) only a single
     property animates, and (b) the `opacity` property is left completely alone
     — it is load-bearing for the .wow-stagger reveal, and computed opacity
     stays exactly "1" on every card in every state.

     blur(3px) is the whole depth-of-field read at this card size (13.5px body
     copy, 150px thumbnail); opacity(.62) pushes the sibling back without
     touching a single colour value. Palette, card shape, imagery and the amber
     link colour are all untouched — the cards only BEHAVE differently.

     Specificity (1,5,0): #services + .svc-rows + :has(.svc-row:hover) + .svc-row
     + :not(:hover). Nothing shipped comes close. */
  /* :not(:hover):not(:focus-within) on BOTH arms — the cross-state fix.
     With only :not(:hover) on the hover arm and only :not(:focus-within) on the
     focus arm, the two arms blur EACH OTHER'S active card: a card that is
     focused but not hovered matches the hover arm, and a card that is hovered
     but not focused matches the focus arm. Measured with the pointer on one
     card and keyboard focus on another: blurredCount 8 of 8 — including the
     focused card, with its amber focus ring blurred away. Reachable by the most
     ordinary path there is: tab to a card, then reach for the mouse.
     Excluding both states on both arms costs one extra sharp card in the rare
     simultaneous hover+focus case, which is the correct read since both are
     genuinely active. Verified: blurredCount 6, both active cards filter none. */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)         > .svc-row:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within)  > .svc-row:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: blur(3px) opacity(.62);
  }

  /* KEYBOARD PARITY is the second selector above, not an afterthought: tabbing
     to a card's .svc-row__link makes that card :focus-within, which drives the
     identical filter on the other seven. A keyboard user gets the same
     affordance as a mouse user, with the same 240ms timing.

     The focused card itself is given no declaration here — it stays sharp for
     the same reason the hovered card does, and its native :focus-visible
     outline (structural.css:4170) is left intact and unblurred. */

  /* PERFORMANCE — no `will-change`.
     A blur must be re-rastered every frame whether or not the layer is
     pre-promoted, so will-change:filter buys almost nothing here, while
     8 permanently promoted cards would hold 8 backing stores for a band that is
     static most of the time. The cost is instead controlled by keeping the
     radius small (3px), animating exactly ONE property, never animating a
     layout- or transform-affecting property alongside it, and holding the
     window to 240ms. Only the 7 non-hovered cards are ever filtered; the
     hovered card is never rasterised through a filter at all. */
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   REDUCED MOTION.
   structural.css:977-980 already covers the hovered card's transform, and
   line 978 (`.wow-reveal, .wow-stagger > * { transform: none !important }`)
   already covers the SIBLINGS' transform too — measured: with a scale(.985)
   sibling rule live, computed sibling transform under reduce was still "none".
   Neither of those touches `filter`, which is this sheet's entire payload, so
   it is cancelled explicitly here.

   The blur is dropped rather than merely un-transitioned: under reduce,
   .wow-lift's `transition: none !important` (line 979) makes the blur snap on
   instantly, and 7 cards hard-cutting between sharp and blurred on every
   pointer move is precisely the kind of abrupt visual change the preference
   exists to suppress. The affordance is not lost — the hovered card's
   box-shadow lift is not in structural.css's reduce kill-list and still fires,
   which matches how every other hover on this site degrades.

   Same selector, same (1,5,0), later in source than the rule it cancels, so it
   wins on order. Deliberately at top level: the rule it overrides only exists
   inside the (hover:hover) query, so this needs no gate of its own.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  /* :not(:hover):not(:focus-within) on BOTH arms — the cross-state fix.
     With only :not(:hover) on the hover arm and only :not(:focus-within) on the
     focus arm, the two arms blur EACH OTHER'S active card: a card that is
     focused but not hovered matches the hover arm, and a card that is hovered
     but not focused matches the focus arm. Measured with the pointer on one
     card and keyboard focus on another: blurredCount 8 of 8 — including the
     focused card, with its amber focus ring blurred away. Reachable by the most
     ordinary path there is: tab to a card, then reach for the mouse.
     Excluding both states on both arms costs one extra sharp card in the rare
     simultaneous hover+focus case, which is the correct read since both are
     genuinely active. Verified: blurredCount 6, both active cards filter none. */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)         > .svc-row:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within)  > .svc-row:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: none;
  }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AP] #services — DEPTH CAROUSEL (supersedes [AO]'s hover effect)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner request, 2026-08-06, from the Behance "Minimal Typography Carousel"
   reference (Fazle Rabbi Sarkar, gallery 238268219): ONE card centred and sharp,
   its neighbours blurred, scaled down and tucked behind it, advancing
   horizontally. The owner asked for this twice; an intermediate attempt kept the
   grid and applied a hover-only effect ([AO]) — that was the wrong reading and
   is superseded here.

   [AO] IS CANCELLED, NOT DELETED. In a carousel the centred card is already the
   subject, so [AO]'s hover-recede would fight it — hovering a neighbour would
   blur the actual subject. This block cancels [AO]'s filter at higher
   specificity and owns the blur itself. Behaviour: design-system/site.js [AP].

   REVERT LADDER: delete [AP] (css+js) -> back to the grid + [AO] hover effect.
   Delete [AP] and [AO] -> back to the plain shipped grid. Backups:
   _backups/structural.css.pre-AP-2026-08-06 · _backups/site.js.pre-AP-2026-08-06

   ---- WHAT IS PRESERVED ----
   Palette, card colours, card shape (wide image-left/text-right), imagery and
   the amber link colour are untouched. ALL EIGHT services stay in the DOM and in
   the accessibility tree with their heading, dek and link text intact — verified,
   because a carousel is the classic way to silently delete content. Measured:
   cards 8, contentOk true, hiddenCards 0 at 1440 and 375.

   ---- MECHANISM ----
   .svc-rows becomes a horizontal scroll-snap track (overflow-x:auto,
   scroll-snap-type:x mandatory, scroll-snap-stop:always). Native scrolling is
   deliberate: swipe, drag, keyboard scrolling and a real scrollbar all come free
   and cannot desync from an internal counter the way a transform-based slider
   can. JS only injects the controls and marks which card is centred.

   ---- THE NO-JS CORRECTION (measured, and it inverted the original design) ----
   The first version declared the RECEDED state as the CSS default, with a
   comment claiming the band would then "degrade to a plain, evenly-treated
   horizontal scroller". It does not. The receded state IS the blurred one, and
   .is-active is only ever set by script — so with scripting off every card stays
   blurred with nothing able to sharpen them. Measured with
   javaScriptEnabled:false: 8 of 8 blurred, 0 visible. "Evenly treated" was true;
   "plain" was not.
   Fixed by hanging every depth rule off `.svc-car-on`, a class site.js [AP] adds
   to the track. No script -> no class -> no blur, and the band falls back to a
   plain scroll-snap scroller with all eight cards sharp and readable. The LAYOUT
   properties stay UNCONDITIONAL so that fallback still works.
   Re-measured: JS off -> 0 blurred / 8 sharp / track still scrollable;
   JS on -> 7 blurred / 1 sharp.

   ---- MEASURED, all gates green at 1440 and 375 ----
   section height 1097 -> 820 at 1440, 1384 -> 653 at 768, 2322 -> 736 at 375
   PAGE overflow 0 at every breakpoint (the track scrolls, the document does not)
   exactly one sharp card · 2 real <button>s with accessible names ·
   next/prev genuinely advance and scroll the track · every card link reachable
   by Tab AND scrolled into view when focused · under prefers-reduced-motion the
   scroll is not smooth and no card is blurred · 0 console errors.
   ========================================================================== */

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. THE TRACK  —  .svc-rows becomes a horizontal scroll-snap scroller.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Overrides structural.css [P]:3702
     .svc-rows { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; ... }   (0,1,0)
   with (1,1,0). Also beats the [P] 560px query's grid-template-columns:1fr.

   Native scrolling on purpose (the brief's approach): touch swipe, pointer
   drag, wheel, and keyboard scrolling all come from the platform, cannot
   desync from a hand-rolled state machine, and degrade to a plain horizontal
   scroller if opt-CAR.js never runs.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows {
  /* --- tunables ------------------------------------------------------------
     --car-w is the SUBJECT card's width. It is consumed in exactly two places
     (the card's flex-basis and the ::before/::after spacer that lets the first
     and last card reach the centre) and BOTH resolve their percentage against
     the same box — the track's content box — because the track carries no
     inline padding. Centering is therefore one formula, not two that can drift.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  --car-w:          min(760px, 66%);
  --car-media:      250px;
  --car-gap:        var(--ds-space-lg);
  --car-minh:       250px;
  --car-h3:         26px;
  --car-p:          15px;

  /* --car-lead is the fraction of the FULL centering offset that the leading /
     trailing spacers use. At 1 the first card sits dead centre at rest, which
     leaves a wide empty plate on its left and a lopsided band — measured 256px
     of nothing at 1440 while the right side held a peeking neighbour. At .45
     the two END cards sit slightly inboard of centre and always keep a
     neighbour in frame, while EVERY interior card still snaps to dead centre
     (there is ample scroll room either side of them). The subject reads centred
     in every state a user navigates to; only the two extremes lean in. */
  --car-lead:       .45;

  /* OWNER, 2026-08-06: "do not blur the other cards". Blur is 0 at every
     breakpoint — the depth now reads from DIM + SCALE + the inward shift alone,
     which is enough separation without softening the photography. Kept as a
     token rather than deleting the blur() from the filter so the depth is one
     tunable knob and `filter` stays a single animated property. */
  --car-blur-near:  0px;
  --car-blur-far:   0px;
  --car-dim-near:   .55;
  --car-dim-far:    .32;
  --car-scale-near: .88;
  --car-scale-far:  .82;
  --car-shift:      8%;
  --car-dur:        420ms;

  position: relative;            /* REQUIRED: makes the track the offsetParent of
                                    every card, so opt-CAR.js can centre a card
                                    with layout geometry (offsetLeft/offsetWidth)
                                    instead of getBoundingClientRect — rects are
                                    transform-affected and the neighbours ARE
                                    transformed, which would mis-centre them. */
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  align-items: stretch;          /* every card takes the tallest card's height,
                                    so the media column is never a short stub */
  gap: var(--car-gap);
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl);

  /* vertical room for the subject card's shadow — overflow-y is clipped (see
     below), so without this the lift shadow would be sheared off */
  padding-block: var(--ds-space-xl);
  padding-inline: 0;             /* centering comes from the spacers, NOT padding */

  overflow-x: auto;
  overflow-y: hidden;            /* a scroller may not have a stray vertical bar */
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain; /* swiping past the last card must not chain
                                     into the page / browser back-gesture */

  /* NO `scroll-behavior: smooth` HERE, DELIBERATELY.
     A CSS-level smooth scroll also applies to the browser's own
     scroll-an-element-into-view on FOCUS, which would leave a tabbed-to card
     mid-flight and briefly off-screen. Smoothness is applied per-call in
     opt-CAR.js (prev/next only), and is dropped under reduced motion. This is
     also why `scroll-behavior` computes to `auto` in every state. */

  /* the real scrollbar is KEPT (thin + on-palette) — it is a genuine motor
     affordance for users who drag scrollbars rather than swipe */
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  scrollbar-color: var(--ds-line) transparent;
}

/* The two lead-in / lead-out spacers. Content pseudo-elements, so they add
   nothing to the DOM and nothing to the accessibility tree.
   The negative inline margin cancels the flex `gap` that would otherwise sit
   between a spacer and the end card — without it the lead can never actually
   reach 0, and on a 375px screen that stray 16px was eating the whole of the
   neighbour's peek. */
#services .svc-rows::before,
#services .svc-rows::after {
  content: "";
  flex: 0 0 max(0px, calc((100% - var(--car-w)) / 2 * var(--car-lead)));
  align-self: stretch;
  pointer-events: none;
}
#services .svc-rows::before { margin-inline-end: calc(-1 * var(--car-gap)); }
#services .svc-rows::after  { margin-inline-start: calc(-1 * var(--car-gap)); }

#services .svc-rows::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 6px; }
#services .svc-rows::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: transparent; }
#services .svc-rows::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. THE CARD  —  keeps the shipped WIDE horizontal shape (photo left, copy
   right). It is not turned into a portrait social slide.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Overrides structural.css [P]:3704 `.svc-row { grid-template-columns:150px 1fr }`
   (0,1,0) and the 560px query's 116px variant (0,1,0), both with (1,2,0).
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
  flex: 0 0 var(--car-w);
  max-width: 100%;
  min-height: var(--car-minh);
  grid-template-columns: var(--car-media) 1fr;
  scroll-snap-align: center;
  scroll-snap-stop: always;      /* a fast flick advances one card, not five */

  transform-origin: 50% 50%;

  transition:
    filter    var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out),
    transform var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out),
    box-shadow var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out);
}

/* THE DEPTH — gated behind .svc-car-on, which opt-CAR.js adds to the track.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   NO-JS CORRECTION (measured, 2026-08-06). The first version declared the
   RECEDED state as the base, with a comment claiming the band would then
   "degrade to a plain, evenly-treated horizontal scroller". It does not: the
   receded state IS blurred, so with scripting off every card stays blurred and
   nothing can ever sharpen them, because .is-active is only ever set by JS.
   Measured with javaScriptEnabled:false — 8 of 8 cards blurred, 0 visible.
   "Evenly treated" was true; "plain" was not.

   Gating the depth on a class the script adds inverts it correctly: no JS means
   no blur, no scale, and the band falls back to a plain horizontal scroll-snap
   scroller with all eight cards sharp and readable — which the native track
   supports with no script at all. The LAYOUT properties above stay
   unconditional precisely so that fallback still works.

   Blur + dim ride inside ONE property (filter) rather than using the `opacity`
   property, for the same reason block [AO] does it: `opacity` is load-bearing
   for the .wow-stagger reveal on these exact cards and must compute to "1". */
#services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row {
  filter: blur(var(--car-blur-far)) opacity(var(--car-dim-far));
  transform: scale(var(--car-scale-far));
  z-index: 0;                    /* flex items honour z-index at position:static */
}

/* IMMEDIATE NEIGHBOURS — less blur, less shrink, and slid TOWARD the subject so
   their inner edge tucks UNDER it (the reference's partial occlusion).
   --car-dir is written by opt-CAR.js as +1 for the card on the LEFT of the
   subject and -1 for the card on the RIGHT, so the shift is always inward.
   Inward-only matters: an outward shift would grow the track's scrollable
   overflow. It cannot grow it here. */
#services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row.is-near {
  filter: blur(var(--car-blur-near)) opacity(var(--car-dim-near));
  transform: scale(var(--car-scale-near)) translateX(calc(var(--car-dir, 0) * var(--car-shift)));
  z-index: 1;
}

/* THE SUBJECT — sharp, full size, forward, with the deepest shadow so it reads
   as sitting in front of its neighbours rather than merely next to them.
   Only box-shadow is restated; no colour token is redefined. */
#services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row.is-active {
  filter: none;
  transform: none;
  z-index: 2;
  /* SHADOW SIZED TO THE CLIP BUDGET (owner: "reduce the drop shadow so it will
     not look cropped"). The track is overflow-y:hidden, so anything extending
     past its padding-block is cut with a hard horizontal edge — which is what
     read as "cropped". MEASURED before this change: the outer layer was
     0 44px 90px, extending ~134px below the card against only 40px of budget,
     so it was clipped by 94px; even the inner 0 18px 40px overshot by 18px.
     Budget is padding-block: 40px at >=900px and 24px below that, so both
     layers are sized to fit the SMALLER budget and are safe at every
     breakpoint: 8+14 = 22px and 4+10 = 14px of reach. */
  box-shadow:
    0 4px 10px rgba(10, 4, 4, .5),
    0 8px 14px rgba(10, 4, 4, .34);
}

/* THE BLUR MUST NEVER LAND ON THE ELEMENT THAT HAS KEYBOARD FOCUS.
   opt-CAR.js already promotes a focused card to .is-active, so this is the
   belt-and-braces guarantee that holds even if the script is mid-update or
   never ran: (1,3,0), later in source than .is-near, so it wins outright. */
#services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row:focus-within {
  filter: none;
  transform: none;
  z-index: 3;
}

/* The card interior. The card is now the BAND'S SUBJECT at roughly 2.5x its
   former width, so the type is re-scaled to that box — the 18px/13.5px pair was
   sized for a 564px two-up tile and reads as a caption inside a 760px subject.
   Scale only: not one colour, token, word or link is changed, and the SAME
   sizes apply to all eight cards so nothing reflows when the subject changes. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body {
  padding: var(--ds-space-lg) var(--ds-space-lg) var(--ds-space-lg) 0;
}
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body h3 {
  font-size: var(--car-h3);
  line-height: 1.18;
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
}
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body p {
  font-size: var(--car-p);
  line-height: 1.6;
  max-width: 46ch;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. CANCELLING BLOCK [AO]  (structural.css:6898 + its reduced-motion twin)
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AO] is a HOVER depth-of-field: hovering ANY card blurs the other seven.
   Inside a carousel that is actively wrong — the centre card is already the
   subject, so hovering a receded neighbour would blur the SUBJECT. [AO] must
   not be allowed to decide blur at all; this sheet owns it.

   [AO]'s two selectors are
     #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)
     #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row:not(:focus-within):not(:hover)
   = (1,6,0)  [ #services | .svc-rows + :has(.svc-row + :hover) + .svc-row +
                :not(:hover) + :not(:focus-within) = 6 class-level components ].

   Cancellation strategy: re-declare `filter` on BOTH of [AO]'s selectors, with
   one extra class-level component each, so every card [AO] could reach is
   instead assigned THIS sheet's carousel value. Three arms cover the whole set
   [AO] matches — subject / near / far — so there is no card left for [AO] to
   claim. Nothing is left to source order alone.

   Specificity of the arms below: (1,7,0) for the .is-active and .is-near arms,
   (1,8,0) for the far arm. Both beat (1,6,0) outright.

   The hovered card itself is never matched by [AO] (both arms exclude :hover),
   so it keeps whatever this sheet's base rules give it — i.e. hovering a
   neighbour does NOT sharpen it and does NOT blur the subject. That is the
   intended carousel behaviour: position, not the pointer, decides the subject.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {

  /* [AO] must not touch the subject */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-active:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-active:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: none;
  }

  /* [AO] must not touch the immediate neighbours */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-near:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-near:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: blur(var(--car-blur-near)) opacity(var(--car-dim-near));
  }

  /* [AO] must not touch the far cards */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: blur(var(--car-blur-far)) opacity(var(--car-dim-far));
  }

  /* [AO]:6861 also rewrites the transition shorthand on these cards
     ( #services .svc-rows > .svc-row, (1,2,0) ). Same specificity, later file,
     so this wins on order and the carousel owns its own timing. */
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
    transition:
      filter    var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out),
      transform var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out),
      box-shadow var(--car-dur) var(--ds-ease-out);
  }
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. CONTROLS  (injected by opt-CAR.js — there is no HTML file to edit)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-car__ctrls {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}

#services .svc-car__btn {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 48px;
  height: 48px;              /* >= 44px tap target at every breakpoint */
  padding: 0;
  /* CONTRAST FIX. --ds-line (#4a4a4a) on this band's #2b211b is 1.77:1 and
     verify-contrast requires 3:1 for a control outline — the SAME defect block
     [AC] fixed for .btn-secondary, whose outline used the same token at the same
     1.77:1. Using --ds-accent-ink (#eba459 = 7.47:1) rather than [AC]'s
     --ds-ink-on-dark (#e8e2dc = 12.23:1) because these are icon buttons whose
     glyph is already accent — matching the ring to the glyph keeps them one
     object and on-palette, and 7.47:1 clears the 3:1 bar with room to spare. */
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-accent-ink);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 22px;
  line-height: 1;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              background var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease);
}
#services .svc-car__btn:hover:not(:disabled) {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
}
#services .svc-car__btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
#services .svc-car__btn:disabled {
  opacity: .34;
  cursor: default;
}

#services .svc-car__count {
  margin-left: var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

/* the polite live region. Visually hidden, NEVER display:none / aria-hidden —
   it has to stay in the accessibility tree to be announced at all.
   1x1 box so the reveal probe (which only flags invisible boxes > 8x8) and the
   clipped-text probe (which only inspects h3 / p / a) both ignore it. */
#services .svc-car__live {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: -1px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* the track is focusable (it is a scrollable region — WCAG 2.1.1) */
#services .svc-rows:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  outline-offset: 4px;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. BREAKPOINTS — the card stays a WIDE horizontal card at every width; only
   its width, its media column and the blur radii scale.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-w: min(660px, 68%);
    --car-media: 230px;
    --car-minh: 240px;
    --car-h3: 23px;
    --car-p: 14.5px;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 899px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-w: 80%;
    --car-media: 200px;
    --car-minh: 230px;
    --car-h3: 21px;
    --car-p: 14px;
    --car-blur-near: 0px;
    --car-blur-far: 0px;
    padding-block: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
}

@media (max-width: 560px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-w: 86%;
    --car-media: 100px;
    --car-minh: 210px;
    --car-h3: 19px;
    --car-p: 13.5px;
    --car-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
    --car-blur-near: 0px;
    --car-blur-far: 0px;
    --car-scale-near: .90;
    --car-scale-far: .86;
    --car-shift: 5%;
    /* lead 0 on phones: there is only ~23px of slack per side at 375, so
       spending any of it on a left inset leaves nothing for the neighbour and
       the depth stops reading (measured: a 13px sliver). At 0 the subject sits
       flush with the band's own type column and ALL the slack goes to the
       neighbour peeking in on the right. */
    --car-lead: 0;
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body {
    padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-md) 0;
  }
  /* back to the SHIPPED 1.5 leading on phones. The narrower measure inside a
     carousel card costs lines, every line makes the card taller, and the media
     column is object-fit:cover so its HEIGHT is what binds the three 512x512
     sources' effective resolution. Shorter card = sharper photo. */
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body p { line-height: 1.5; }
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. REDUCED MOTION
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The carousel must still WORK under reduce — it just must not animate:
     - no smooth scrolling  (opt-CAR.js passes behavior:'auto'; and this sheet
       never set scroll-behavior:smooth, so it computes 'auto' in every state)
     - no blur at all, and no blur/scale TRANSITION
   Depth is then carried by a static dim (filter's opacity() function, no blur
   token in the value) plus the subject's shadow. The site's own reduce policy
   already kills transforms on these cards
   (structural.css:978 `.wow-stagger > * { transform: none !important }`), so no
   scale is asserted here — fighting that with !important would be re-adding
   exactly what the preference asks to remove.

   [AO]'s reduced-motion twin (structural.css:6957, `filter: none`, (1,6,0))
   would otherwise un-dim the receded cards on hover under reduce, so the same
   three-arm cancellation is repeated here at (1,7,0) / (1,8,0).
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {

  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
    transition: none !important;
    filter: opacity(var(--car-dim-far));
    transform: none;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row.is-near { filter: opacity(var(--car-dim-near)); transform: none; }
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row.is-active,
  #services .svc-rows.svc-car-on > .svc-row:focus-within { filter: none; transform: none; }

  #services .svc-car__btn { transition: none !important; }

  /* cancel [AO]'s reduced-motion `filter: none` on all three arms */
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-active:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-active:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: none;
  }
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-near:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-near:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: opacity(var(--car-dim-near));
  }
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: opacity(var(--car-dim-far));
  }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AQ] TWO OWNER FIXES, 2026-08-06
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Append-only; deleting this block reverts both. Independent of each other and
   of [AP] — kept together only because they landed in the same pass.
   ========================================================================== */

/* --- 1. #licensing CTA — centre it -----------------------------------------
   Owner: "let's make sure this CTA button is center-aligned". `.cta-row` is a
   flex row whose base rule (structural.css:69) sets no justify-content, so the
   button sat flush left — MEASURED 412px left of its container's centre
   (button 144-473 inside a 120-1320 container). `.closing-cta .cta-row` already
   solves the same thing at :104; this is that rule applied to this band.
   NOTE the id: this CTA lives in #licensing, NOT in #industries. The two
   sections are adjacent and the industries band ends at index.html:572 while
   the CTA is at :596 — worth stating because the obvious guess is wrong. */
#licensing .cta-row { justify-content: center; }

/* --- 2. #services carousel — fade the track edges --------------------------
   Owner: "let's have this edges in this section look fading instead of having
   this hard crop edges". The track is overflow-x:auto, so the cards entering
   and leaving are sliced by a hard vertical edge at the scroll port's bounds.
   A horizontal mask feathers both edges so cards dissolve instead of being cut.

   The gradient is `to right` only, so it is fully opaque at every vertical
   position — it cannot eat the active card's shadow, which is already sized to
   the vertical clip budget by [AP]'s shadow fix.

   Applied to .svc-rows WITHOUT the .svc-car-on gate on purpose: the hard edge
   is a property of the scroll port, so it exists with scripting off too, and
   the fallback scroller deserves the same treatment.
   -webkit- prefix retained for Safari, which still needs it for mask-image. */
#services .svc-rows {
  --car-fade: 56px;
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
    transparent 0, #000 var(--car-fade),
    #000 calc(100% - var(--car-fade)), transparent 100%);
          mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
    transparent 0, #000 var(--car-fade),
    #000 calc(100% - var(--car-fade)), transparent 100%);
}
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  /* Only ~23px of slack per side at 375 — a 56px feather would eat into the
     subject itself, so the fade is tightened to match. */
  #services .svc-rows { --car-fade: 24px; }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AR] #industries — FIT THE BAND IN ONE VIEWPORT (owner, 2026-08-06)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "let's reduce sizes of the card or re-layout just so it can fit
   everything in the same viewport."
   MEASURED BEFORE: sectionH 1534px against a 900px viewport — overflows by 634.
   3 columns x 4 rows of 268px, plus 116px padding at each end.

   THE TILING LAW IS PRESERVED, not broken. The original block states it
   explicitly ("zero gaps, no lone card"): 8 tiles = feature 2x2 (4 cells) +
   6 singles (6) + one 2x1 wide (2) = 12 cells. Twelve is exactly 4 cols x 3
   rows, so moving 3 -> 4 columns drops a whole row and STILL tiles perfectly
   with no empty cell. That is why 4 was chosen and not 5 (15 cells = 3 holes).

   Budget at 1440x900: 900 - 144 padding - ~215 header = ~540 for the grid.
   3 rows + 2 gaps at 168px = 536. Rows are clamped so the band also behaves at
   taller viewports and never collapses below the design's own floor — the
   shipped sheet already uses a 150px floor at its own small breakpoint
   (structural.css:3684), so 168 is inside the design's existing range, not a
   new cramped value invented here.
   Append-only; deleting this block restores the 3-column band exactly.
   ========================================================================== */

#industries.section { padding-block: clamp(56px, 5vw, 72px); }

#industries .ind-feature-grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  grid-auto-rows: clamp(150px, 11.7vw, 178px);
}

/* The feature keeps its 2x2 emphasis — it is the whole point of the bento —
   and the wide tile keeps its 2x1, so the 12-cell law holds at 4 columns. */
#industries .ind-feature-grid__feature { grid-column: 1 / span 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }
#industries .ind-feature-grid__wide { grid-column: span 2; }

/* Narrower cells (276px at 1440 vs 373px before) need the caption to step down
   or the dek runs past its tile. Sized against the measured cell, not guessed. */
#industries .ind-feature-grid .category-card__label { font-size: 19px; }
#industries .ind-feature-grid__feature .category-card__label { font-size: clamp(22px, 2.2vw, 28px); }
#industries .ind-feature-grid .ind-tile__desc { font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.4; }
#industries .ind-feature-grid__feature .ind-tile__desc { font-size: 14px; }

@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  /* Below the wide desktop the 4-up cells get too narrow for a dek, so fall
     back to the shipped 2-column arrangement rather than inventing a third. */
  #industries .ind-feature-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); grid-auto-rows: clamp(150px, 32vw, 210px); }
  #industries .ind-feature-grid__feature { grid-column: auto; grid-row: auto; }
  #industries .ind-feature-grid__wide { grid-column: span 2; }
}

/* --- [AR] cont. — SCRIM, because shorter tiles moved the caption into the light
   The 4-up cells are 178px tall instead of 268px, so the caption block occupies
   a much larger share of the tile and rides UP into the transparent end of the
   shipped scrim (structural.css:1156 fades out by 78%). MEASURED by sampling the
   real rendered pixels behind each label and computing contrast against the
   white label text: mean was comfortable everywhere (8-18:1) but the WORST
   PIXELS failed on the two brightest photos —
       Bars & nightclubs  worst 2.40:1   (neon bar)
       Hotels             worst 2.33:1   (bright city sky)
   against a 3:1 floor for large text. verify-contrast passed the page because it
   treats text-over-photo as low-confidence and samples means, not worst pixels —
   so this needed a direct pixel sample to catch, and the mean would have hidden it.
   Scoped to #industries so no other .category-card scrim changes. */
#industries .category-card__overlay {
  background: linear-gradient(to top,
    var(--ds-scrim) 0%,
    var(--ds-scrim) 46%,
    rgba(10, 4, 4, .74) 64%,
    rgba(10, 4, 4, .36) 82%,
    transparent 100%);
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AS] #services — THE FRAMED PRINT (owner-selected layout, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked this from a six-option fan-out built in
   logs/labs/liquorlicense-services-2026-07-31/ (PLATE, SPLIT, GLASS, EDITORIAL,
   GALLERY, SHELF). All six passed probe-carousel.mjs 14/14 at 1440 and 375, so
   the deterministic gate did NOT discriminate between them. The decision was
   made on the two things no gate measures: pixel density and real-paint contrast.

   WHY THIS ONE — it is the only option that BUYS image resolution instead of
   spending it. Three of the eight service photos are only 512x512
   (escrow-signing, zoning-blueprints, compliance-gavel) and the cards are
   uniform, so the weakest source caps the layout for ALL of them. The mat
   shrinks the printed box, which is the frame's whole point and also a
   resolution win:

     option        print box   effective DPR on the three 512px sources
     GALLERY       212x194     2.42   <- this block
     [AP] shipped  250x248     2.05
     PLATE         318x318     1.61
     SHELF         366x229     1.40
     SPLIT         379x298     1.35
     GLASS         718x378     0.71   (also failed contrast: 4.22:1, needs 4.5)
     EDITORIAL     758x378     0.68

   MEASUREMENT TRAP, recorded because it was hit and corrected during selection:
   use offsetWidth/offsetHeight, NOT getBoundingClientRect. The three 512px
   photos sit on cards 2/4/5, which are never the subject at rest, so a rect
   measurement returns the depth transform's scaled-down box (--car-scale-near
   .88 / --car-scale-far .82) and OVERSTATES sharpness by ~20%.

   EVIDENCE — pc-GALLERY-1440.json / pc-GALLERY-375.json: 8 cards, contentOk,
   0 hidden, 0 page overflow, exactly one subject, controls advance, all 8 links
   keyboard-reachable and scrolled into view, 0 console errors, 0 failed
   requests. Contrast against the real composited paint stack: worst 9.37:1
   across 24 text runs, 0 failures; caption numeral 5.73:1 across all 8 cards.

   RELATIONSHIP TO [AP] (:6980) — [AP] STAYS and still owns the carousel
   behaviour, the three depth states, the prev/next controls and the centering
   spacers. This block is a custom-property retune plus layout on top of it.
   --car-w is deliberately NOT touched, so the band footprint the owner signed
   off is unchanged at every breakpoint. No colour, token, heading, dek or link
   label changes. No jpg on disk is touched — all reframing is object-fit:cover.
   ============================================================================ */

/* =============================================================================
 * "The framed print" — promoted from opt-GALLERY.css (lab 2026-07-31), 2026-08-07
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE FRAME IS THE IDEA. Deliberately the opposite of full-bleed: the photo is
 * pulled IN, off every edge, and set behind a mat with a plaque under it, so the
 * card stops being a tile with a picture stuck to its left side and becomes a
 * PLATE HOLDING A FRAMED PRINT.
 *
 *   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 *   │   ┌────────────────────┐                                 │
 *   │   │ ┌────────────────┐ │                                 │
 *   │   │ │ ┌────────────┐ │ │   Buy a liquor licence          │
 *   │   │ │ │            │ │ │   Off-market Type 47, 48 and    │
 *   │   │ │ │   PRINT    │ │ │   21 licences, vetted for …     │
 *   │   │ │ │            │ │ │                                 │
 *   │   │ │ └────────────┘ │ │   FIND INVENTORY →              │
 *   │   │ └────────────────┘ │                                 │
 *   │   │         0 1        │                                 │  ← the plaque
 *   │   └────────────────────┘                                 │
 *   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 *      ▲ plate inset 20   ▲ french line 7 out   ▲ mat reveal 18 / 18 / 36,
 *                           from the aperture     bottom-weighted as a mat is cut
 *
 * Four things carry the read, and all four are physical, not decorative:
 *   1. THE MAT IS A DIFFERENT MATERIAL from the plate. --ds-bg-warm (#221a15)
 *      board on the --ds-bg-surface-2 (#0e0a08) card, carrying --ds-grain so it
 *      reads as paper stock rather than another flat panel.
 *   2. THE FRENCH LINE. A hairline rectangle ruled on the board 7px out from the
 *      aperture — the one detail that separates "cut and ruled mat board" from
 *      "the photo has a margin". Drawn on `.svc-row__media::before`, which the
 *      shipped sheet leaves unused.
 *   3. THE PRINT SITS BEHIND THE OPENING. The mat's window casts a soft lip down
 *      onto the photo (an INSET shadow — see the trap-4 note below) and a gold
 *      fillet rings the aperture. Recessed, not pasted on.
 *   4. THE PLAQUE. `.svc-row__n` — the "01".."08" index that ships
 *      `display:none` (structural.css:3722) — is restored as the engraved plate
 *      number centred in the mat's bottom reveal. It lives INSIDE
 *      `.svc-row__media` in the markup, which is exactly where a plaque belongs,
 *      so no escape trick is needed to place it.
 *
 * The copy is a wall label set beside the frame: centred against it, not
 * top-aligned. Centring is only safe because §7 reserves the dek at its longest
 * measure, which makes all eight bodies exactly one height — see §7 for why that
 * matters far more in a carousel than it would in a grid.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HOW THIS OVERRIDES [AP] (structural.css:6980-7493) — the 5 traps, handled
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. SPECIFICITY. The geometry is a custom-property retune on
 *    `#services .svc-rows` (1,1,0) — the same selector [AP]:7033 and [AQ]:7528
 *    use, later in source ⇒ wins. Everything needing a real declaration is
 *    written at the specificity [AP]/[P] already use or higher:
 *      card                 #services .svc-rows > .svc-row              (1,2,0)
 *      media / body / img   #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__…(1,3,0/1)
 *      index                #services .svc-rows > .svc-row .svc-row__n  (1,3,0)
 *    all beating [P]'s (0,1,0) / (0,1,1) outright, and matching [AP]'s own
 *    (1,3,0) so ORDER decides. Nothing here relies on a lower-specificity
 *    override silently working — that is the documented 2026-07-30 trap.
 * 2. `opacity` IS LOAD-BEARING on `.svc-row` (`.wow-stagger > *`:941 animates the
 *    opacity PROPERTY and it must end at 1). NOTHING here touches opacity on the
 *    card, and the depth dim stays inside [AP]'s `filter: opacity()`.
 *    `.wow-stagger > *` matches the <article> only, never its descendants.
 * 3. `transform` IS OWNED by the three depth arms (base / .is-near / .is-active).
 *    No transform is declared on `.svc-row` anywhere in this sheet.
 * 4. THE SHADOW CLIP BUDGET — measured, and this option deliberately spends ~none
 *    of it. The track is `overflow-y:hidden` with 40px (24px < 900) of
 *    padding-block, so a card-level shadow may reach at most ~22px; [AP] already
 *    sized `.is-active`'s two layers to 22px and 14px and this sheet does not
 *    touch them. The MAT's own depth is carried by an INSET shadow (the opening's
 *    cast lip) plus `--ds-inset-hi`, both of which reach 0px outward, and one
 *    `--ds-shadow-rest` (0 1px 2px = 3px of reach) which is contained twice over:
 *    the mat is inset --gal-plate (20px) from the card's edges AND the card is
 *    `overflow: hidden` ([P]:3710). A matted print with a big drop shadow is
 *    exactly how this option could have re-broken the sheared-edge defect; it
 *    does not, because the shadow that sells the depth points INWARD.
 * 5. NO-JS FALLBACK. Every rule below is LAYOUT and is UNCONDITIONAL — not one is
 *    gated behind `.svc-car-on`. With scripting off the band is still the plain
 *    sharp scroll-snap scroller, now with the matted card.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * IMAGE RESOLUTION — this option BUYS headroom instead of spending it (brief §3)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The mat SHRINKS the rendered photo, which is the whole point of the frame and
 * is also, unusually, a resolution WIN. Shipped [AP] paints the photo edge to
 * edge across a 250x250 media box (512/250 = 2.05x of device-pixel headroom on
 * the three capped sources). Here the column is WIDER at 290px but the print
 * inside it is 212x194, so escrow-signing / zoning-blueprints / compliance-gavel
 * (512x512) render at 512/212 = 2.42x — 18% MORE headroom than shipped, not
 * less. All eight print boxes are identical, so the number is the same for every
 * card. Measured off the built page, not estimated. No jpg on disk is touched;
 * `object-fit: cover` is left exactly as shipped, so all reframing is
 * non-destructive.
 * ============================================================================= */


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. THE TRACK — retune [AP]'s tunables + declare the mat geometry.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * (1,1,0), same as [AP]:7033 / [AQ]:7528, later in source ⇒ wins.
 * --car-w is deliberately NOT changed: the centering spacers
 * (#services .svc-rows::before/::after) derive their flex-basis from it, so the
 * band's footprint stays exactly what the owner signed off at every breakpoint.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows {
  /* The media COLUMN grows (250 -> 290) while the PRINT inside it shrinks
     (250 -> 212). The extra 40px is not more photo, it is more mat. */
  --car-media: 290px;

  /* Card floor. The frame, not the copy, sets the card's height here — the
     reserved body is 150px and the plate is 292, so the extra height is bought
     deliberately to give the print a near-square aperture — 212x194 measured —
     instead of the letterbox slot a copy-fitted card produces.
     MEASURED, and this is the number that took the most iterations:
       252 + top-aligned copy  -> 91px of empty plate under "FIND INVENTORY".
                                  A hole in the card's bottom-right quadrant.
       248 + top-aligned copy  -> 63px. Better; still visibly one-sided, because
                                  top-aligning puts ALL of the slack in one place.
       292 + centred copy      -> the slack is split above and below the label,
                                  and the print gets 46px of the height back.
     The shipped [AP] card leaves ~78px under the same link, so this is tighter
     than what ships even at the taller card height. */
  --car-minh:  292px;

  /* The copy column is ~468px here, the same measure [AP] tuned 26px against.
     25px is what keeps all eight headings on ONE line at this width, which is a
     load-bearing fact rather than a taste call: --gal-h3-lines below reserves
     ONE line here, so a heading that wrapped would exceed its own reservation
     and be the single card that hops. Verified — all eight measure one line at
     >=1100px. Scale only: not one colour, token, word or link label changes. */
  --car-h3:    25px;
  --car-p:     15px;

  /* ---- THE MAT, in four numbers ----------------------------------------
     --gal-plate  the mat panel's inset from the CARD's edges. This is the
                  "plate" margin — the card ground you can see all the way
                  around the frame. It is also what keeps the mat's small drop
                  shadow (3px) nowhere near any clipping edge.
     --gal-mat-x  mat reveal left and right of the print
     --gal-mat-t  mat reveal above the print
     --gal-mat-b  mat reveal below the print. DELIBERATELY the largest: a mat is
                  cut bottom-weighted so the print reads optically centred, and
                  the extra reveal is where the plaque is engraved.
     The print opening is derived from these — never hard-coded — so the mat
     cannot drift out of proportion when a breakpoint retunes one of them. */
  --gal-plate: 20px;
  --gal-mat-x: 18px;
  --gal-mat-t: 18px;
  --gal-mat-b: 36px;

  /* --gal-fillet is the FRENCH LINE: the hairline rectangle ruled on the mat a
     few millimetres out from the aperture. It is the single detail that makes a
     mat read as cut-and-ruled board rather than as a coloured margin, and it
     costs nothing — one pseudo-element, no box-model effect, no reach. */
  --gal-fillet: 7px;

  /* THE DEK IS RESERVED at its longest measure (--gal-p-lines) rather than
     allowed to size to its own text. This is what makes centring the label safe.
     MEASURED: the eight deks run 2 or 3 lines at this width, so an unreserved
     centred label would sit 12px higher on the 3-line cards than on the 2-line
     ones. In a GRID that is invisible variance between neighbours. In a CAROUSEL
     the cards occupy the SAME position one after another, so it becomes a 12px
     hop of the heading every single time you press next — the layout equivalent
     of a jump cut. Reserving the dek makes all eight bodies exactly 150px, so
     the heading, the link and the label's centre line are pinned for all eight.
     `min-height`, not `height`: a longer dek still gets a fourth line. See §7. */
  --gal-p-lh:    1.6;
  --gal-p-lines: 3;

  /* The heading is reserved the same way and for the same reason. MEASURED at
     768, where "Escrow and transaction guidance" is the one heading that wraps:
     card 07's h3 sat 12.4px higher than the other seven (h3Top 31.6 vs 44.0)
     and hopped on every press of next. Reserving the dek alone does not catch
     this — the heading is the OTHER variable-height element in the label, and
     the fix has to cover both or it covers neither.
     1 line here (all eight headings measured as one line at >=1100px), 2 at
     <=899 where one of them wraps. --gal-h3-lh mirrors [AP]:7234's 1.18 so the
     reservation is computed from the leading the text actually uses. */
  --gal-h3-lh:    1.18;
  --gal-h3-lines: 1;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2. THE CARD — kill the inter-column gap; the mat's own margin is the gutter.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [P]:3707 `.svc-row { gap: var(--ds-space-md) }` (0,1,0) would stack 16px of
 * grid gap ON TOP of the mat's 20px right margin, giving 36px between frame and
 * copy against 24px between copy and the card's right edge — a lopsided plate.
 * With gap:0 the frame sits --gal-plate from the copy and --gal-plate from the
 * card's left/top/bottom: one inset, used four times. (1,2,0) beats (0,1,0).
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
  gap: 0;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3. THE MAT PANEL  (`.svc-row__media` stops being the photo and becomes board)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Overrides [P]:3712-3715 (0,1,0) with (1,3,0).
 * `position: relative`, `isolation: isolate` and `overflow: hidden` are NOT
 * re-declared — all three are still wanted and are inherited from [P]:3713.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media {
  margin: var(--gal-plate);

  /* board, not panel: a warmer, lighter stock than the card, with the shipped
     grain over it so it reads as paper rather than a second flat surface.
     --ds-bg-warm (#221a15) sits between the card (#0e0a08) and the band
     (#2b211b), so the mat separates from BOTH without inventing a colour. */
  background-color: var(--ds-bg-warm);
  background-image: var(--ds-grain);

  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);

  /* --ds-inset-hi is a lit top lip (reach 0). --ds-shadow-rest is 0 1px 2px =
     3px of outward reach, sitting inside a 20px inset inside a card that is
     itself `overflow: hidden` — see the trap-4 note in the header. */
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-rest);

  /* a grid item's default min-width:auto lets a wide min-content push the
     column past its declared track size; 0 pins the frame to --car-media. */
  min-width: 0;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 4. THE PRINT — inset inside the mat's aperture
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [P]:3716 pins the <img> `position:absolute; inset:0; width:100%; height:100%`.
 * An absolutely positioned box resolves its insets against its containing
 * block's PADDING box, so padding on the mat would NOT move the photo — the
 * inset has to be written on the image itself.
 *
 * THE REPLACED-ELEMENT TRAP, measured and caught here, because it produced a
 * card that LOOKED matted and passed every probe:
 *   The obvious spelling is `inset: t x b; width: auto; height: auto` — four
 *   insets, let the box fall out of them. That is correct for a normal element
 *   and WRONG for a replaced one. CSS 2.1 §10.3.8 resolves `width:auto` on an
 *   absolutely positioned REPLACED element to its INTRINSIC width (not to
 *   containing-block − left − right), which then over-constrains the equation
 *   and `right` is ignored outright; `height:auto` then follows the intrinsic
 *   aspect ratio and `bottom` is ignored too. MEASURED with that spelling:
 *   the mat panel was 240 wide and the photo rendered 237.98 — clamped by the
 *   global `img { max-width: 100% }` (structural.css:38) rather than by my
 *   inset — so the mat reveal was 18px on the left and ZERO on the right, and
 *   the 512x512 sources rendered 195x195 and overflowed the panel's bottom by
 *   38px into the plaque, hidden only by [P]'s `overflow: hidden`. Every gate
 *   in probe-carousel stayed green through all of that: not one of them looks
 *   at whether the photo is where the layout says it is.
 * Fixed by stating the box explicitly. Percentages resolve against the same
 * padding box the insets do, so the mat is still one set of four numbers, and
 * `object-fit: cover` (left exactly as shipped) does the reframing — no jpg is
 * touched and nothing is distorted. (1,3,1) beats [P]'s (0,1,1).
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media img {
  top:    var(--gal-mat-t);
  left:   var(--gal-mat-x);
  right:  auto;
  bottom: auto;
  width:  calc(100% - var(--gal-mat-x) * 2);
  height: calc(100% - var(--gal-mat-t) - var(--gal-mat-b));
  /* a print has square corners; the softening belongs to the mat and the card */
  border-radius: 0;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 5. THE APERTURE — the mat's window, drawn over the print's edge
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [P]:3718 uses this pseudo-element for a foot gradient whose ONLY job was to
 * stop the photo meeting the dark card on a hard edge. That meeting no longer
 * happens — the photo now meets a mat — so the pseudo-element is re-tasked to
 * the aperture instead of being left to wash the bottom of the print for a
 * reason that has gone away.
 *
 * Both shadows are INSET, so this whole treatment has 0px of outward reach and
 * cannot participate in the sheared-shadow defect:
 *   - the 1px ring is the gold fillet line around the opening (--ds-glass-border
 *     is the shipped amber-at-18% token, not a new colour)
 *   - the top lip is the mat's own thickness casting onto the print behind it
 * Re-anchored to the print's box with the same four numbers as the <img>, so
 * the ring can never drift off the photo's edge. (1,3,1) beats [P]'s (0,1,1).
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media::after {
  inset: var(--gal-mat-t) var(--gal-mat-x) var(--gal-mat-b);
  background: none;
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 0 0 1px var(--ds-glass-border),
    inset 0 3px 8px -3px var(--ds-scrim);
  border-radius: 0;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 5b. THE FRENCH LINE — the mat's ruled hairline, --gal-fillet out from the
 * aperture on all four sides.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `.svc-row__media::before` is UNUSED by the shipped sheet ([P] only defines
 * ::after at :3718), so this adds a detail without taking one away and without
 * touching the DOM or the accessibility tree.
 * Paint order inside the mat's stacking context ([P]:3713 `isolation: isolate`)
 * is ::before → <img> → ::after, all position:absolute, so the line sits under
 * the print's stacking level — which is moot, because it is ruled ENTIRELY on
 * the mat: --gal-fillet (7) is smaller than every mat reveal (18/18/36), so the
 * rectangle never touches the photo and never reaches the plaque.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset:
    calc(var(--gal-mat-t) - var(--gal-fillet))
    calc(var(--gal-mat-x) - var(--gal-fillet))
    calc(var(--gal-mat-b) - var(--gal-fillet));
  /* --ds-hairline (white at 9%) and NOT --ds-glass-border (the amber at 18%
     used for the aperture ring). Both were built and read side by side at 2x:
     amber-on-warm-board has less LUMINANCE separation from --ds-bg-warm than
     white does at half the alpha, so the gilt version was the fainter line of
     the two — the opposite of the intuition. White also keeps the ONE warm line
     in the assembly at the aperture, where the gilt fillet belongs. */
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  pointer-events: none;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 6. THE PLAQUE — `.svc-row__n` restored, engraved in the mat's bottom reveal
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The index is `display:none` at [P]:3722 and is `aria-hidden="true"` in the
 * markup, so it is decorative by construction — restoring it adds nothing to the
 * accessibility tree and removes nothing from it. It already lives INSIDE
 * `.svc-row__media`, which is precisely where a plate number belongs, so unlike
 * every other use of this span it needs no trick to get where it is going.
 * `pointer-events: none` keeps it from ever intercepting a click.
 * Positioned against the mat's padding box (the mat is [P]:3713
 * `position:relative`), spanning exactly the print's width so the plaque is
 * centred on the print rather than on the panel. (1,3,0) beats [P]'s (0,1,0).
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row .svc-row__n {
  display: block;
  position: absolute;
  left: var(--gal-mat-x);
  right: var(--gal-mat-x);
  bottom: 0;
  height: var(--gal-mat-b);
  z-index: 2;

  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 11px;
  line-height: var(--gal-mat-b);   /* = the band height ⇒ optically centred in it */
  letter-spacing: .28em;
  text-indent: .28em;              /* cancels the trailing letter-space so two
                                      digits sit dead centre, not 1.5px left */
  text-align: center;
  font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);     /* 5.73:1 on the mat, all 8 cards — measured 2026-08-07 */

  pointer-events: none;
  user-select: none;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 7. THE COPY — a wall label, centred against the frame
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Keeps [P]:3724's `align-self: center` and drops [AP]:7230's vertical padding
 * (re-declared at the same (1,3,0), later in source): with the label centred,
 * the vertical padding was doing nothing except decide where the block's
 * unusable slack sat.
 *
 * WHY CENTRED — and why that is a decision, not a default. TOP-aligning the
 * label to the print's top edge was built and measured first. It pins all eight
 * headings to one line, which is correct, but it also parks 100% of the card's
 * vertical slack in one place: 63px of empty plate in the bottom-right quadrant,
 * under "FIND INVENTORY" (91px before the dek was reserved). A framed print with
 * a hole punched next to it is not a gallery wall. Centring splits that slack
 * above and below the label, which is what a wall label beside a picture
 * actually looks like.
 *
 * The reason centring is normally the WRONG call here — a carousel swapping
 * different-height bodies through the same position makes the heading hop — is
 * removed at source by the reserved dek below, not tolerated. All eight bodies
 * measure exactly 150px, so "centred" resolves to the same y on every card.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body {
  align-self: center;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: 0 var(--ds-space-lg) 0 0;
}

/* THE RESERVED LABEL. Both variable-height elements in the label — the heading
   and the dek — get a floor, so the block is one height on all eight cards and
   "centred" resolves to one y.
   `min-height`, not `height`: if a future breakpoint or a longer string needs
   another line it still gets one, and the card grows rather than clipping a
   word. [AP]:7233/7238 own these elements' line-heights at (1,3,0) and :7445
   restates the dek's at 1.5 inside the 560px query; a reservation computed from
   a different number than the text actually uses would be wrong at exactly the
   width that matters, so both line-heights are taken over here as tokens and
   the 560px value is restated with them. Same (1,3,0), later in source ⇒ these
   win at every width. `font-size` and `max-width` are left exactly as [AP] set
   them — the type scale and the measure are not this rule's business. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body h3 {
  line-height: var(--gal-h3-lh);
  min-height: calc(var(--car-h3) * var(--gal-h3-lh) * var(--gal-h3-lines));
}
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body p {
  line-height: var(--gal-p-lh);
  min-height: calc(var(--car-p) * var(--gal-p-lh) * var(--gal-p-lines));
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 8. BREAKPOINTS — the SAME properties, in the SAME queries as [AP]:7394-7446.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * --car-w is left exactly as [AP] tuned it at every width. Only the mat scales.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-media:  260px;
    --car-minh:   262px;
    --car-h3:     23px;
    --car-p:      14.5px;
    --gal-plate:  18px;
    --gal-mat-x:  16px;
    --gal-mat-t:  16px;
    --gal-mat-b:  32px;
    --gal-fillet: 6px;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 899px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-media:  240px;
    --car-minh:   244px;
    --car-h3:     21px;
    --car-p:      14px;
    --gal-plate:  16px;
    --gal-mat-x:  14px;
    --gal-mat-t:  14px;
    --gal-mat-b:  30px;
    --gal-fillet: 6px;
    /* "Escrow and transaction guidance" wraps to two lines in the ~310px
       measure this width leaves; reserving 2 lines on ALL eight keeps the
       label block one height. Measured at 768 — see the token's note. */
    --gal-h3-lines: 2;
  }
}

/* PHONES — the frame ROTATES rather than being abandoned.
   [AP] drops the media column to 100px at this width. A 100px column would
   leave a ~64px print inside a mat, which is not a framed print, it is a
   thumbnail with a border. So the card stacks instead: the framed print takes
   the top of the plate at full width, the wall label sits under it, and the mat,
   the aperture, the fillet and the plaque all survive at a size where they can
   still be read. Layout only — still unconditional, still no-JS safe. */
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  #services .svc-rows {
    --car-h3:     19px;
    --car-p:      13.5px;
    --gal-plate:  16px;
    --gal-mat-x:  14px;
    --gal-mat-t:  14px;
    --gal-mat-b:  30px;
    --gal-fillet: 6px;
    /* the stacked card sets its own height from its content; without this
       [AP]'s 210px floor is simply inert, but stating 0 keeps the intent
       explicit rather than accidental */
    --car-minh:  0px;

    /* [AP]:7445 goes back to 1.5 leading on phones (a narrower measure costs
       lines, every line makes the card taller, and a taller card is a taller
       media box). Restated here because §7 took ownership of this declaration.
       The dek is NOT reserved at this width: stacked, the frame is pinned to
       the TOP of the card and the label is pinned under it (align-self:start
       below), so nothing above the dek can move and there is no hop to remove —
       reserving would only pad the short deks for a problem that does not exist
       at this width. */
    --gal-p-lh:    1.5;
    --gal-p-lines: 0;
    --gal-h3-lines: 0;
  }

  /* `auto 1fr`, not two auto rows: the track is align-items:stretch, so the
     tallest card sets every card's height, and with two auto rows that surplus
     would be split between the frame and the label — stretching the mat away
     from its aspect ratio. `1fr` gives all of it to the label. */
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
  }

  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media {
    margin: var(--gal-plate) var(--gal-plate) 0;
    /* 5:4 keeps the print generous without pushing the card past a phone
       screen. object-fit:cover means this is a reframe, never a distortion. */
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 4;
    /* belt and braces against the stretch described above */
    align-self: start;
  }

  /* Stacked, the label sits UNDER the frame, so it is pinned there rather than
     centred: the track is align-items:stretch, so a short card inherits the
     tallest card's height, and centring inside the `1fr` row would float that
     card's copy away from the frame it belongs to. */
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body {
    align-self: start;
    padding: var(--ds-space-md);
  }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AT] #inventory — COVERFLOW (owner request, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "for this section I would like you to use this carousel style" +
   "ensure all card/container corners matches its roundness and text + images
   are complete inside each card."

   SCOPE — anchored on `#inventory.collection`, NOT `#inventory`.
   id="inventory" exists on BOTH index.html AND inventory.html and both use
   .collection__grid; only the homepage band also carries .collection.
   inventory.html is the full browsable board and MUST stay a grid. A bare
   #inventory selector would have turned the client's inventory PAGE into a
   carousel.

   GATED — probe-cover.mjs, 17/17 at 1440 and 375. Both owner criteria are
   asserted by the probe, not eyeballed:
     CORNERS  all 6 cards cardR 16 / border 1 / mediaR 15 / expected 15 -> match
     NO CLIP  all 6 cards wOver 0 and hOver 0 on card, body AND media
   Plus: 6 cards present with full content, 0 page overflow, exactly one
   subject, named prev/next that advance, all 6 CTAs keyboard-reachable AND
   brought to the front on focus, 0 console errors, 0 failed requests, and with
   scripting off nothing is rotated or dimmed.

   THE RADIUS FIX THIS CARRIES. The shipped card was a latent mismatch:
   .card:72 is --ds-r-lg (10px) while .product-card--spec .product-card__media
   :4275 is border-radius: 0, faking a rounded top purely through the card's
   overflow:hidden :4273. That fake holds in a flat grid and BREAKS in 3D,
   because a transformed subtree escapes the ancestor clip. Here one token
   (--cov-r) sets the card and the media derives its top corners from it minus
   the 1px border — the correct nested-radius relationship.

   Behaviour ships in site.js block [AT].
   ============================================================================ */

/* =============================================================================
 * opt-COVER.css — #inventory as a COVERFLOW  ·  owner reference, 2026-08-07
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Owner: "for this section I would like you to use this carousel style" +
 * "ensure all card/container corners matches its roundness and text + images
 * are complete inside each card."
 *
 * The reference is classic coverflow: one card square-on and full size at the
 * centre, its neighbours hinged away from the viewer in 3D, scaled back and
 * tucked UNDER it, fanning out to both edges.
 *
 *              ┌──┐  ╱────╲   ┌────────┐   ╱────╲  ┌──┐
 *              │  │ ╱      ╲  │        │  ╱      ╲ │  │
 *              │-3│╱   -1   ╲ │    0   │ ╱   +1   ╲│+3│
 *              └──┘╲        ╱ │        │ ╲        ╱└──┘
 *                   ╲──────╱  └────────┘  ╲──────╱
 *               back        hinged   FRONT   hinged        back
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SCOPING — this must NOT touch inventory.html
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `id="inventory"` exists on BOTH index.html AND inventory.html, and both use
 * `.collection__grid`. inventory.html is the full browsable board (9 licences)
 * and MUST stay a grid. The two sections differ:
 *     index.html      <section class="section collection section--warm" id="inventory">
 *     inventory.html  <section class="section section--warm wow-reveal" id="inventory">
 * Only the homepage band carries `.collection`, so every selector below is
 * anchored on `#inventory.collection` and is inert on inventory.html.
 * Verified by grep before a line was written — a bare `#inventory .collection__grid`
 * would have silently turned the client's inventory PAGE into a carousel.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE TWO OWNER ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA, handled explicitly
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. CORNERS MATCH. The shipped card is a radius mismatch waiting to show:
 *    `.card`:72 is --ds-r-lg (10px) but `.product-card--spec .product-card__media`
 *    :4275 is `border-radius: 0`, faking a rounded top purely by the card's
 *    `overflow:hidden` :4273. That fake works in a flat grid and is exactly the
 *    kind of thing 3D breaks. Here ONE token, --cov-r, sets the card, and the
 *    media gets that radius MINUS the card's 1px border on its top two corners —
 *    the correct nested-radius relationship, so the photo's curve sits parallel
 *    to the card's curve instead of fighting it. The CTA keeps --ds-r-pill.
 * 2. NOTHING CLIPS. `.product-card--spec`:4273 is `overflow:hidden`, so anything
 *    that does not fit is silently cut rather than visibly broken — the failure
 *    mode is invisible. Countered three ways: the card is never given a fixed
 *    height (content sets it), the spec rows may wrap rather than being cut, and
 *    probe-cover.mjs asserts every text run and every image is inside its card's
 *    padding box on all 6 cards. See §5.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE TRAPS (the same five that govern [AP]/[AS], re-derived for this band)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. SPECIFICITY. `.collection__grid` is (0,1,0); :4042 and :4314 retune it at
 *    (1,1,0). Every rule here is (1,2,0) for the track and (1,3,0)+ for the
 *    card, so nothing depends on a lower-specificity override quietly working.
 * 2. `opacity` IS LOAD-BEARING. The track is `.collection__grid.wow-stagger`,
 *    so the CARDS are the staggered children of :941 `.wow-stagger > *`, which
 *    animates the opacity PROPERTY to 1. Setting `opacity` on a card would
 *    freeze it invisible. Every dim below therefore rides inside
 *    `filter: opacity()`, never `opacity`.
 * 3. `transform` IS OWNED HERE. `.wow-lift:hover`:936 sets
 *    `transform: translateY(-6px)` at (0,2,0). Our per-offset transforms are
 *    (1,4,0) and always apply, so that hover would simply never fire — losing a
 *    live affordance silently. It is deliberately re-provided in §4 by folding a
 *    lift INTO the front card's own transform, so the card still responds.
 * 4. THE 3D + CLIP INTERACTION. `perspective` lives on the TRACK and the
 *    rotation on its DIRECT children — one level of 3D, so no `preserve-3d` is
 *    needed and the track's own `overflow` cannot flatten it. The fan is
 *    deliberately allowed to be cut by the track's edges: that is what the
 *    reference does, and it is why the PAGE never gains a horizontal scrollbar.
 * 5. NO-JS FALLBACK. Every 3D rule hangs off `.cov-on`, which opt-COVER.js adds.
 *    With scripting off the band is a plain, sharp, readable horizontal
 *    scroller with all six cards square-on — no rotation, no dim, nothing
 *    hidden. LAYOUT is unconditional; only DEPTH is gated.
 * ============================================================================= */


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. THE TRACK — grid becomes a perspective scroller
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Native scroll-snap, so swipe, drag, keyboard and the scrollbar are all browser
 * behaviour and cost nothing to support. `grid-template-columns: none` is stated
 * explicitly to undo :186 / :4314 / :4042 rather than relying on `display:flex`
 * making them inert.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid {
  /* ---- the one radius that governs the whole component ---- */
  --cov-r:       16px;

  /* ---- geometry ---- */
  --cov-w:       340px;   /* card width — 6 cards, so the fan reads at 1440 */

  /* ★ THE TUCK — the single number that makes this a STACK rather than a row.
     A negative inline margin, so each card's LAYOUT footprint is narrower than
     the card itself and its neighbours genuinely sit on top of it.
     WHY IT MUST BE LAYOUT AND NOT A TRANSFORM: the first build pulled
     neighbours in with translateX(74px) alone and they still showed a visible
     GAP. Measured why — at full 340px pitch the neighbour is first scaled
     (.88 -> 299px), then foreshortened by rotateY(42deg) (299 x cos42 = 222px),
     then shrunk again by perspective at translateZ(-120) (x 1700/1820 = 207px).
     Its half-width collapses 170 -> 104, which OPENS 66px of gap that a 74px
     nudge can barely close. Transform-based overlap fights three shrinking
     effects at once; a negative margin sets the pitch directly and none of them
     touch it. Pitch = --cov-w - 2 x --cov-tuck = 340 - 110 = 230px. */
  --cov-tuck:     55px;

  /* Centring-spacer share, same idea as [AP]:7060 — but 1, not [AP]'s .45.
     MEASURED: at .5 the track's midpoint at scrollLeft 0 fell nearer card 02
     than card 01, so the band opened with the SECOND licence square-on and the
     first already hinged away. A full share makes the spacer exactly
     (track - card) / 2, which centres card 01 at scroll origin. The + tuck term
     gives back the half-tuck the first card's negative margin eats, so card 01
     can still reach dead centre instead of resting 55px short of it. */
  --cov-lead:     1;

  /* ---- the hinge ----
     Shallower than the first build. At 42deg the neighbour was mostly edge and
     its content unreadable; the reference's neighbours are clearly legible
     cards seen at an angle, not slivers. The stacking now comes from the tuck,
     so the rotation only has to sell the turn. */
  --cov-rot1:    28deg;
  --cov-rot2:    44deg;
  --cov-rot3:    52deg;
  --cov-scale1:  .87;
  --cov-scale2:  .76;
  --cov-scale3:  .68;
  --cov-z1:      -80px;
  --cov-z2:     -180px;
  --cov-z3:     -260px;
  /* outward pull for the far cards: linear pitch alone throws them too wide, so
     ranks 2 and 3 are drawn back IN toward the stack */
  --cov-pull2:   110px;
  --cov-pull3:   190px;
  /* Dim rides in filter: opacity() — NEVER the opacity property (trap 2).
     PUSHED HARDER THAN THE REFERENCE, deliberately. The reference stacks
     PHOTOGRAPHS, so overlapping them is visually clean. These cards carry a
     kicker, a name, four label/value rows and a button, so a brightly-lit back
     card shows a second set of "Licence type / Location / Price & terms"
     alongside the one you are meant to be reading and the stack turns into
     noise. Receding the back ranks harder is what keeps the subject the only
     card being READ while the others still read as physical layers. */
  --cov-dim1:    .55;
  --cov-dim2:    .30;
  --cov-dim3:    .16;
  --cov-dur:     460ms;

  /* ---- the drop shadow that sells the stacking ----
     Budgeted against the track's padding-block below, because overflow-y is
     hidden and anything reaching further is sheared off with a hard edge. */
  --cov-shadow-front:
    0 26px 50px -18px rgba(0, 0, 0, .78),
    0 10px 20px -10px rgba(0, 0, 0, .62);
  --cov-shadow-back:
    0 18px 34px -14px rgba(0, 0, 0, .70),
    0 6px 14px -8px rgba(0, 0, 0, .55);

  display: flex;
  grid-template-columns: none;
  gap: 0;
  align-items: center;

  /* the vanishing point sits slightly above centre so the fan reads as looking
     down the row rather than up at it — matches the reference's eyeline */
  perspective: 1700px;
  perspective-origin: 50% 44%;

  overflow-x: auto;
  overflow-y: hidden;
  scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  scrollbar-width: none;
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;

  /* Room for the drop shadow. overflow-y:hidden shears anything past this with
     a hard horizontal edge — exactly as [AP]:7048 documented for the services
     band. The front shadow reaches 26+50 = ~76px at its softest, so 92px keeps
     the whole falloff inside the box instead of cutting it into a visible line. */
  padding-block: 92px;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

/* centring spacers — derived from --cov-w so changing the card width moves the
   centring automatically instead of drifting (the [AP]:7060 lesson).
   The + --cov-tuck term returns the half-tuck the first and last cards' negative
   margins eat, so both ends can still reach dead centre. */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid::before,
#inventory.collection .collection__grid::after {
  content: "";
  flex: 0 0 max(0px, calc((100% - var(--cov-w)) / 2 * var(--cov-lead) + var(--cov-tuck)));
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2. THE CARD — sizing, and the corner that has to match
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * No height is declared anywhere: the tallest card sets the row and every card
 * stretches to it, so no card can ever be shorter than its own content. That is
 * the structural half of "text is complete inside each card"; §5 is the rest.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card {
  flex: 0 0 var(--cov-w);
  /* ★ THE TUCK. This is what makes the cards a stack instead of a row: each
     card's layout footprint is --cov-w minus twice this, so its neighbours
     genuinely sit on top of it rather than beside it. scroll-snap-align uses
     the BORDER box (margins are not part of the snap area), so snapping still
     centres the whole 340px card and the scroll maths is unaffected. */
  margin-inline: calc(var(--cov-tuck) * -1);
  scroll-snap-align: center;
  scroll-snap-stop: always;

  /* ONE radius for the card. --ds-r-xl is 14px; 16px is stated as a component
     token rather than borrowing a scale step, because the media's inner radius
     is derived FROM it and the pair has to stay in lockstep. */
  border-radius: var(--cov-r);

  transform-origin: 50% 50%;
  transition:
    transform var(--cov-dur) cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1),
    filter    var(--cov-dur) cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1),
    box-shadow var(--cov-dur) cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1);
  will-change: transform;
}

/* THE MEDIA'S CORNERS. Nested-radius maths: the card's border is 1px (.card:72),
   so the inner element's radius must be the outer radius MINUS that border for
   the two curves to stay parallel. Stated on the element instead of relying on
   the parent's overflow clip — a transformed subtree can escape that clip, which
   is precisely how a 3D card ends up with one square corner. */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card > .product-card__media {
  border-radius: calc(var(--cov-r) - 1px) calc(var(--cov-r) - 1px) 0 0;
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
}

/* the image fills its rounded frame; object-fit is left as shipped so no jpg is
   touched and nothing is distorted */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card > .product-card__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3. NO-JS BASELINE — flat, sharp, readable
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Everything above is unconditional layout. Without opt-COVER.js no `.cov-on`
 * is added, so none of §4 applies: six square-on cards in a horizontal
 * scroll-snap row. Nothing is rotated, dimmed or hidden. (trap 5)
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 4. THE FAN — depth states, gated on .cov-on
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `data-off` is the SIGNED distance from the subject, written by the script:
 * 0 is the front card, negative is to its left, positive to its right. A card
 * left of centre hinges its RIGHT edge toward the viewer (+rotateY); a card to
 * the right hinges its LEFT edge toward the viewer (-rotateY). The translateX
 * pulls each neighbour back under the card in front of it so the row overlaps
 * instead of merely shrinking.
 *
 * Shadow budget: the track is overflow-y:hidden with 46px of padding-block, so
 * the front card's shadow is sized to 28px of reach and stays inside it.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* EVERY card carries a real drop shadow, not just the subject. That is what
   makes a stack read as physically layered sheets rather than as a row of flat
   panels — in the reference the shadow under each card is what tells you it is
   ON TOP of the next one. Declared on the base arm so no card can ever be
   shadowless, then strengthened for the subject. */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card {
  /* anything past rank 3 sits with rank 3 */
  transform:
    rotateY(0deg)
    scale(var(--cov-scale3))
    translateZ(var(--cov-z3));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim3));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 1;
}

/* ---- the subject: square on, full size, in front ---- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="0"] {
  transform: rotateY(0deg) scale(1) translateZ(0);
  filter: none;
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-front);
  z-index: 30;
}

/* the lift that .wow-lift:hover can no longer deliver (trap 3), folded into the
   subject's own transform so hover still does something on the card in front */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="0"]:hover {
  transform: rotateY(0deg) scale(1) translateZ(26px);
}

/* ---- rank 1: tucked under the subject, hinged toward the viewer ---- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-1"] {
  transform: rotateY(var(--cov-rot1)) scale(var(--cov-scale1)) translateZ(var(--cov-z1));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim1));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 20;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="1"] {
  transform: rotateY(calc(var(--cov-rot1) * -1)) scale(var(--cov-scale1)) translateZ(var(--cov-z1));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim1));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 20;
}

/* ---- rank 2: pulled back IN toward the stack, or the fan throws too wide ---- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-2"] {
  transform: translateX(var(--cov-pull2)) rotateY(var(--cov-rot2)) scale(var(--cov-scale2)) translateZ(var(--cov-z2));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim2));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 12;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="2"] {
  transform: translateX(calc(var(--cov-pull2) * -1)) rotateY(calc(var(--cov-rot2) * -1)) scale(var(--cov-scale2)) translateZ(var(--cov-z2));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim2));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 12;
}

/* ---- rank 3: the last sliver at each edge ---- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-3"] {
  transform: translateX(var(--cov-pull3)) rotateY(var(--cov-rot3)) scale(var(--cov-scale3)) translateZ(var(--cov-z3));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim3));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 6;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="3"] {
  transform: translateX(calc(var(--cov-pull3) * -1)) rotateY(calc(var(--cov-rot3) * -1)) scale(var(--cov-scale3)) translateZ(var(--cov-z3));
  filter: opacity(var(--cov-dim3));
  box-shadow: var(--cov-shadow-back);
  z-index: 6;
}

/* A hinged card is a photograph of a card, not a control: its links must not be
   clickable while it is turned away, or the user clicks a target they cannot
   read. Keyboard focus is UNAFFECTED — pointer-events does not remove anything
   from the tab order, and the script scrolls a focused card to the front. */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card:not([data-off="0"]) {
  pointer-events: none;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card:not([data-off="0"]):focus-within {
  pointer-events: auto;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 5. NOTHING CLIPS — the owner's second criterion, at the type level
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `.product-card--spec`:4273 is `overflow:hidden`, so an overflow here is CUT,
 * not visible. These rules remove the ways the content could exceed the card at
 * 340px instead of relying on the clip to hide the evidence.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* a flex/grid child's default min-width:auto refuses to shrink below its
   min-content, which is what pushes a long county name out of the card */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card > .product-card__body {
  min-width: 0;
}

/* the spec table is a two-column flex row; both cells need permission to shrink,
   and the value needs permission to wrap rather than to be sheared */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .specs__row {
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  align-items: baseline;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .specs__row dt {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 0 1 auto;
}
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .specs__row dd {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 0 1 auto;
  text-align: right;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

/* the name may wrap to two lines rather than be cut at the card's edge */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .product-card__name {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

/* the CTA is a pill and stays one: it must not be squeezed narrower than its
   own label, and it keeps the shipped --ds-r-pill so the card's one square
   corner-family (the pill) is intentional rather than an oversight */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .product-card__cta {
  width: 100%;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  white-space: nowrap;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 6. THE CONTROLS — injected by opt-COVER.js
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Same visual family as [AP]'s services controls so the page has one carousel
 * vocabulary rather than two. 44px hit area, which is the tap-target floor.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.collection .cov-nav {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}
#inventory.collection .cov-nav__btn {
  inline-size: 44px;
  block-size: 44px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 1;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease), color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease);
}
#inventory.collection .cov-nav__btn:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }
#inventory.collection .cov-nav__btn:disabled { opacity: .38; cursor: default; }
#inventory.collection .cov-nav__count {
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .18em;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 7. REDUCED MOTION — the fan is a position, not an animation
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The 3D arrangement is what the layout IS, so it stays; what goes is the
 * movement between states and the smooth scroll. :977 already forces
 * `.wow-stagger > *` to opacity 1 under this query, so the reveal is handled.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid { scroll-behavior: auto; }
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card { transition: none; }
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 8. BREAKPOINTS — the fan tightens, then flattens
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Below 900 the hinge angle and the overlap come down: at a narrow viewport a
 * 42deg card is mostly edge and stops being readable. On phones the fan is
 * abandoned outright for a plain one-up scroller — a 3D fan on a 375px screen
 * shows one card and two slivers, which is worse than a card.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid {
    --cov-w:       310px;
    --cov-tuck:     52px;
    --cov-rot1:    26deg;
    --cov-rot2:    40deg;
    --cov-pull2:   96px;
    --cov-pull3:  166px;
    --cov-z1:      -70px;
    --cov-z2:     -160px;
    perspective: 1500px;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 899px) {
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid {
    --cov-w:       290px;
    --cov-tuck:     48px;
    --cov-rot1:    22deg;
    --cov-rot2:    34deg;
    --cov-pull2:   84px;
    --cov-pull3:  146px;
    --cov-scale1:  .9;
    --cov-scale2:  .82;
    --cov-z1:      -60px;
    --cov-z2:     -130px;
    padding-block: 72px;
    perspective: 1300px;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 640px) {
  /* FLAT. One card at a time, square-on, full size — the fan is dropped rather
     than shrunk into unreadability. Still the same scroll-snap track, so the
     controls, the counter and the keyboard behaviour are unchanged.
     --cov-tuck goes to 0: cards must NOT overlap when there is only one in
     frame, or the neighbour's edge crowds the card you are reading. */
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid {
    --cov-w: min(86vw, 340px);
    --cov-tuck: 0px;
    padding-block: 40px;
    perspective: none;
  }
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card,
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="0"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-1"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="1"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-2"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="2"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="-3"],
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card[data-off="3"] {
    transform: none;
    margin-inline: calc(var(--ds-space-sm) / 2);
  }
  /* only the subject stays at full strength; the rest recede by dim alone */
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid.cov-on > .product-card:not([data-off="0"]) {
    filter: opacity(.55);
  }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AU] VIEWPORT FIT — every band sized to the screen it is read on (owner, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "let's ensure all sections' elements are within viewport allocated to
   each section. This way we can avoid too much scrolling and feed information to
   user/viewer easily and more efficient."

   THE BUDGET IS 818px, not 900. The header is sticky and floats over the top of
   every band, so a section that "fits 900" still loses its last 82px behind it.

   MEASURED BEFORE: 12 of the homepage's 14 sections overflowed; the page was
   13,728px, or 15.3 viewports of scrolling.

   TWO HALVES:
     1. GLOBAL — --ds-section-py in tokens.css went clamp(64px, 8.5vw, 116px) ->
        clamp(48px, 5vw, 76px). At 1440 that is 232px -> 144px per band: the old
        token spent 28% of the entire budget on padding alone. Returned 88px to
        every band on all 13 pages and fixed services, licensing, process,
        coverage and testimonials outright. That change lives in tokens.css.
     2. PER-SECTION — the seven sheets below, for the bands still over after (1).

   RESULT AT 1440x900 (measured, per section, before -> after):
     hero        1059 -> 807   fits
     about        993 -> 813   fits
     industries   863 -> 809   fits
     resources    963 -> 812   fits
     guides       951 -> 804   fits
     contact      980 -> 810   fits
     inventory   1245 -> 958   DOES NOT FIT — documented floor, see below

   WHY #inventory STOPS AT 958. It is the owner-approved coverflow ([AT]). Its
   958 is 112 section padding + 158 head (eyebrow, h2, lede — all client copy)
   + 638 track (36 top gutter + 522 card + 80 bottom gutter, the gutters being
   the measured minimum that does not shear the card shadow against the track's
   overflow-y:hidden) + 51 control row. Reaching 818 would mean removing ~140px
   from a 522px card that is already 190 media + 330 body, where the body is four
   MANDATED spec rows plus the CTA. That is content surgery, not layout tuning,
   and it would undo the stacked-deck look. Reported over budget rather than hit
   by damaging the band.

   NOT ALL BREAKPOINTS FIT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. These are tuned for the
   desktop reading position (1440x900) where "one band per screen" is the goal.
   Phones scroll by nature: #guides is 1854px at 375 and #resources 1590px, and
   forcing those into 735px would mean deleting content. The per-section notes
   below record each sheet's own measured behaviour at 1024/768/375.

   ⚠ ONE BUG CAUGHT IN REVIEW, recorded so it is not reintroduced: the
   #resources sheet originally carried a bare @media (max-width: 1120px). At
   (1,1,0) that outranked the shipped (0,1,0) rules at EVERY width below 1120,
   not just the intended 1025-1120 window, and CUT a panel heading at 375px
   (scrollWidth 177 vs clientWidth 150 inside an overflow:hidden card). The
   section's own scrollHeight check missed it because the damage was horizontal,
   and page h-overflow read 0px precisely BECAUSE the card was clipping. It now
   carries a min-width bound. See the note at that rule.
   ============================================================================ */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AU.hero]  — from lab sec-hero.css
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ============================================================================
   sec-hero.css — VIEWPORT-FIT RETUNE for section.hero.hero--photo.hero--editorial
   Budget 818px (900 viewport - 82px sticky header). Baseline 1059px at 1440x900.

   WHAT ACTUALLY OWNS THE HEIGHT (read, not assumed — every hit for the hero in
   structural.css was walked, last-matching-block wins):

     .section                    :48    padding-block: var(--ds-section-py)   -> 72px at 1440
     [K]  .hero--photo           :3069  min-height: clamp(560px,82vh,840px)   -> 738px at 900
     [K]  .hero--photo           :3071  padding-bottom: calc(section-py + smoke*.55)
     [L]  .hero--editorial       :3245  align-items: end
     [L]  .hero--editorial.hero--photo :3249 padding-bottom: calc(smoke-h*.46 + 40px)
     [L]  .hero--editorial .hero__inner :3251 max-width: 62ch
     [L]  .hero--editorial h1    :3252  max-width 17ch / clamp(40px,5.4vw,74px) / lh 1.02
     [AE] .cmdbar                :4955  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl)  (40px)
     [L]  .hero__stats           :3275  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl)  (40px)

   TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE BRIEF, BOTH MEASURED:

   (1) The bottom padding is NOT structural.css:3071. [L]'s
       `.hero--editorial.hero--photo` (0,2,0) at :3249 is later AND stronger than
       [K]'s `.hero--photo` (0,1,0) at :3071, so :3071 is dead on this hero.
       Measured padding-bottom 164.2px == 270*.46 + 40 == the [L] calc exactly.

   (2) min-height is NOT the binding constraint at a 900px viewport. It computes
       to 738px; the hero measures 1059px because its CONTENT is 72 + 822.9 + 164.2.
       Capping min-height alone would have moved nothing. The hero is content-driven.

   So the height comes from four real places, and this sheet spends all four:
       302px  h1 (4 lines x 75.48)  <- the single biggest consumer
       164px  padding-bottom (smoke clearance)
       164px  the two 40px --ds-space-xl rail margins + the section padding-top
        94px  lede + cta-row + gaps

   NO TYPE IS SHRUNK. The h1 keeps clamp(40px,5.4vw,74px) and lh 1.02; the lede,
   eyebrow, stat numerals and cmdbar all keep their shipped sizes.
   ========================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. HEADLINE MEASURE — the 241px lives here. 4 lines -> 2, same 74px type.

   [AE] already recorded that the h1's own 17ch cap is INERT: the binding
   constraint is `.hero--editorial .hero__inner { max-width: 62ch }` (682.4px),
   and 17ch at 74px is 849px, i.e. wider than the box it sits in. So the shipped
   headline is not ragged to a 17ch measure at all — it is ragged to 62ch, and it
   breaks badly: line 1 "Buy, sell and", line 2 the single orphan word
   "transfer", then the inline-block .accent dropping whole to lines 3-4.

   Opening the inner to 88ch and giving the h1 a REAL 20ch cap lets the headline
   set as two full lines (measured 150.9px vs 301.9px, -151px) with the accent
   span reading as one deliberate second line. The lede is unaffected in kind: it
   returns to its own authored 52ch (682 -> 715px) and still sets 3 lines. Only
   the h1 takes the extra width; eyebrow, cta-row, cmdbar and stat rail are
   content-sized or self-capped.

   Specificity: the rules being beaten are (0,2,0) `.hero--editorial .hero__inner`
   and `.hero--editorial h1`. These are (0,3,0) and later in source order.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.hero.hero--editorial .hero__inner { max-width: 88ch; }
.hero.hero--editorial h1           { max-width: 20ch; }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. THE DISSOLVE AND ITS CLEARANCE, RESCALED TOGETHER.

   padding-bottom exists only to keep the copy out of the smoke's dense zone, so
   it is derived from --ds-smoke-h. At a 900px viewport --ds-smoke-h resolves to
   30vh = 270px, and the clearance it buys is 164px — 20% of the whole budget
   spent on empty air below the stat rail.

   Both are re-derived from ONE local variable at 22vh (198px at 900), keeping
   [L]'s .46 ratio, so the stat rail lands at the same PROPORTIONAL depth in the
   dissolve that it does today (39% of the smoke's height before, 42% after —
   derived from the gradient stops at :3117, not pixel-sampled). What IS
   pixel-measured is the consequence: the rail's worst solid-glyph contrast over
   the composited plate is 9.28:1 on the numerals and 11.62:1 on the labels.
   The dissolve stays a dissolve — 198px is still above the token's own 190px
   floor — it is simply no longer a third of the band.

   --vf-smoke-h is declared on the section and inherits down to .hero__smoke, so
   the gradient and the clearance can never drift apart.

   Specificity: `.hero.hero--editorial.hero--photo` (0,3,0) beats [L]'s (0,2,0)
   at :3249 and [K]'s (0,1,0) at :3071. `.hero.hero--photo .hero__smoke` (0,3,0)
   beats [K]'s `.hero__smoke` (0,1,0) at :3113.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.hero.hero--editorial.hero--photo {
  --vf-smoke-h: clamp(160px, 22vh, 260px);
  padding-bottom: calc(var(--vf-smoke-h) * .46 + var(--ds-space-lg));
}
.hero.hero--photo .hero__smoke { height: var(--vf-smoke-h); }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. SECTION PADDING-TOP — capped, never raised.

   --ds-section-py is a clamp on vw: 72px at 1440 but only 51.2px at 1024. A flat
   token here would ADD height at 1024, so this takes the MIN of the shipped value
   and 64px: 72 -> 64 at 1440, 51.2 -> 51.2 (untouched) at 1024.

   The floor on this is the sticky header. The hero is the first band, so at
   scroll 0 the 82px header sits ABOVE it in flow and 64px is clean breathing
   room below it; it is not clearance the header eats.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.hero.hero--editorial.hero--photo {
  padding-top: min(var(--ds-section-py), var(--ds-space-2xl));
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. THE TWO 40px RAIL MARGINS -> 24px.

   .cmdbar and .hero__stats each open with a full --ds-space-xl (40px). Stacked
   under a 4-element copy block that already carries its own 16px flow gap, that
   reads as drift rather than rhythm. --ds-space-lg (24px) still separates the
   three zones clearly — copy / search / credentials — and the hairline rule above
   the stat rail is doing most of that separating work anyway.

   The stat rail's own padding-top goes 16 -> 12 so the numerals sit tight to
   their rule rather than floating off it.

   Specificity: `.cmdbar` is (0,1,0) at :4955 — beaten by (0,3,0). `.hero__stats`
   is (0,1,0) at :3271 — beaten by (0,2,0)+.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.hero.hero--editorial .cmdbar      { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg); }
.hero.hero--editorial .hero__stats {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  padding-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. CTA ROW — 24px off the lede instead of 40px.

   `.cta-row` (:69) carries margin-top --ds-space-lg (24px) AND sits in
   .hero__inner's 16px flow gap, so the real gap between the lede and the buttons
   is 40px — wider than the gap between the headline and the lede (32px), which
   inverts the reading order. --ds-space-md restores the hierarchy at 32px.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.hero.hero--editorial .hero__inner .cta-row { margin-top: var(--ds-space-md); }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. min-height GUARD (desktop only).

   Not load-bearing at 900 — it computes to 738px and the content is taller — but
   on a short desktop window (< ~642px tall) the clamp's 560px FLOOR would exceed
   the budget on its own. Capped to the real budget there.

   SCOPED TO >= 769px ON PURPOSE. [K]'s mobile override at :3158 is
   `.hero--photo` (0,1,0) inside @media (max-width:768px); an unconditional
   (0,2,0) rule here would beat it and silently rewrite the phone hero. This is
   the same order-vs-media-query trap [L.2] documents at :3350.

   82px is the measured sticky-header height at >= 769px (77px at 375, which this
   rule does not touch).
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 769px) {
  .hero.hero--photo {
    min-height: min(clamp(560px, 82vh, 840px), calc(100vh - 82px));
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. OBJECT-POSITION — deliberately NOT changed.

   [L.1] set `object-position: 50% 50%` because the plate is 1.25:1 into a wider
   box, so cover crops VERTICALLY. This retune removes 253px of BOX height, which
