/* ============================================================================
   [AT-ind] #industries — SECOND-PASS VIEWPORT FIT (lab, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Continues [AR] (structural.css:7545). It does NOT re-litigate [AR]: the
   4-column, 12-cell bento IS the layout and THE TILING LAW IS UNTOUCHED at
   every width — feature 2x2 (4 cells) + 6 singles (6) + wide 2x1 (2) = 12,
   which is exactly 4 cols x 3 rows and exactly 3 cols x 4 rows, no empty cell.

   MEASURED BEFORE (1440x900, sticky header 82px -> budget 818px):
       section        863.06px   (+45.06 over budget)
         padding       72 + 72 = 144.00
         container            719.06
           eyebrow  20.14 + 12mb =  32.14
           h2                       47.52
           lede     16mt + 62    =  78.00
           grid     24mt + 537.41 = 561.41   (3 rows x 168.48 + 2 gaps x 16)
       AFTER          809.45px   (-8.55 under budget)

   Nothing here changes a font-size, a colour, a radius, a gap, a row count or a
   column count at 1440. No text is clamped, shortened or hidden. The 45px comes
   out of two places only: the band's own padding, and a few px off each row.
   Deleting this file restores [AR] exactly.
   ========================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. PADDING  72 -> 56  (saves 32.00px at 1440)

   [AR]:7567 sets clamp(56px, 5vw, 72px). The 5vw term reaches the 72px cap at
   1440, so the widest viewport — the one with the least room to spare after the
   82px sticky header — is the one paying the MOST padding. That is backwards.

   56px is not a value invented here: it is [AR]'S OWN FLOOR for this same band.
   Because 5vw crosses 56px at 1120px wide, [AR] already ships 56px at every
   width at or below 1120 — tablet, mobile, everything. Pinning 56px flat
   therefore changes NOTHING at or below 1120px (identical computed value) and
   only stops the desktop end inflating to 72. The design system's own section
   floor (--ds-section-py, tokens.css:118) is 48px, so 56 sits above the floor
   the whole site already uses, not below it.

   SPECIFICITY: #industries.section is (0,1,1,0) — it MATCHES [AR]:7567 exactly
   and this sheet is linked after structural.css, so it wins on source order.
   A weaker selector (e.g. `.section` or `#industries`) would silently lose. */
#industries.section { padding-block: 56px; }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. ROW HEIGHT  168.48 -> 161.28  (saves 21.60px across the 3 rows at 1440)

   [AR]:7570 sets grid-auto-rows: clamp(150px, 11.7vw, 178px) -> 168.48px at
   1440. 11.2vw -> 161.28px. That is still comfortably above [AR]'s own 150px
   floor, which [AR] took from the shipped sheet (structural.css:3684), so this
   stays inside the range the design already uses rather than inventing a
   cramped one.

   WHY THE ROWS CAN TAKE IT — measured on the real render, not assumed. The
   binding tile is whichever single-height cell carries the tallest caption:
       Bars & nightclubs / Grocery stores   label 21.8 + gap 4 + 4-line dek 70
                                            = 95.8px caption
   on a 16px bottom inset = 111.8px of the cell. At 161.28px that still leaves
   49.5px of clear photo above the caption, and — the point — THE CAPTION BOX
   ITSELF IS UNCHANGED. No dek re-wraps, so the -webkit-line-clamp:4 safety net
   at structural.css:4218 is never reached and no sentence is cut. Verified
   scrollHeight <= clientHeight on every card, every dek and every label.

   The 2x2 feature is not changed in kind, only in size: it spans two of the new
   rows (2 x 161.28 + 16 gap = 338.56px), its caption is only 75.4px, so it
   keeps 247px of clear photo and its role as the band's anchor.

   The 162px cap (was 178) keeps the band inside budget on wider desktops too:
   at >=1447px the vw term would otherwise re-inflate the rows and push the
   section back over 818 — MEASURED at 1920x900: 892px before, 812px after.
   Below 1447px the cap is inert; 11.2vw wins and 1440 is unaffected by it.

   SCOPED to min-width:1100px, i.e. exactly the regime where [AR]'s 4-column
   grid applies. Unscoped, this rule would out-cascade [AR]'s max-width:1099
   block (media queries add no specificity) and silently squash the 2-column
   tablet tiles from 210px to their 150px floor — a real regression I measured
   and backed out of.
   SPECIFICITY: #industries .ind-feature-grid is (0,1,1,0), matching [AR]:7569. */
@media (min-width: 1100px) {
  #industries .ind-feature-grid { grid-auto-rows: clamp(150px, 11.2vw, 162px); }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. 1001-1099px — RESTORE THE SHIPPED 3-COLUMN ARRANGEMENT
   Independent of the 1440 fix above; delete this stanza and 1440 is unaffected.

   THE BUG THIS REPAIRS. [AR]:7588 falls back to 2 columns for everything below
   1100px. At 2 columns the 12-cell law cannot close: the feature drops to a
   single cell, so the band is 7 singles + one 2-wide = 9 cells in a 2-column
   grid = 5 rows with ONE EMPTY CELL at row 4 col 2 — the exact "lone card"
   failure the original block ([R], structural.css:3659) was written to prevent.
   MEASURED at 1024x900: 5 rows x 210px -> section 1404px, +586 over budget.

   3 columns is not a third arrangement invented here — it is the arrangement
   this band shipped with ([R] + [U3], structural.css:3669), and 12 cells is
   exactly 3 cols x 4 rows: feature 2x2 at r1-2/c1-2, six singles filling c3 and
   r3, and the 2-wide closing r4/c2-3. Zero gaps, no lone card. grid-auto-rows
   reuses [R]'s own 15.5vw term (structural.css:3672) rather than a new number.

   MEASURED AFTER at 1024x900: 4 rows x 158.72px -> section 973px. Still over
   the 818 budget and honestly reported as such (see the note below), but 431px
   better and correctly tiled. Bounded to 1001px so the <=1000px rules ([F],
   structural.css:4656, which unclamps the deks and lets the tiles grow to their
   copy) are untouched. */
@media (min-width: 1001px) and (max-width: 1099px) {
  #industries .ind-feature-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    grid-auto-rows: clamp(150px, 15.5vw, 190px);
  }
  #industries .ind-feature-grid__feature { grid-column: 1 / span 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }
  #industries .ind-feature-grid__wide    { grid-column: span 2; }
