/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. THE TRACK'S SHADOW GUTTER — 92/92 was a guess; 36/80 is a measurement
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [AT] budgets padding-block: 92px a side purely so overflow-y:hidden cannot
 * shear the drop shadow, sizing it off the CSS arithmetic ("26+50 = ~76px").
 * That arithmetic is wrong in both directions, because Chrome's blur support is
 * not blur/2 and because the offset is asymmetric. Rendered both shadow stacks
 * on a flat white ground and scanned the PNG for the first non-white pixel:
 *
 *     --cov-shadow-front        up 28   DOWN 80      (scale 1)
 *     --cov-shadow-front        up 33   DOWN 86      (+ the hover translateZ(26))
 *     --cov-shadow-back         up 17   DOWN 53
 *
 * So the shadow never paints more than 33px ABOVE the card even at its hover
 * peak — 92px up was 59px of pure dead air — while 92px DOWN was only 6px of
 * headroom, not the 16px the comment assumed.
 *
 * 36px top clears the hover peak (33) with 3px to spare.
 * 80px bottom clears the resting reach exactly, and clips only the hover
 * state's last 6px, which is the 1/255 tail of the falloff (at a 2/255
 * threshold the hover reach is 77px, still inside 80).
 * Net: 184px of gutter becomes 116px. The shadow is untouched.
 *
 * SCOPED to min-width 900px ON PURPOSE. [AT]:8678 and :8700 already retune this
 * property to 72px and 40px inside max-width queries. Media queries carry no
 * specificity, so an unscoped rule in THIS sheet would beat both on source
 * order and silently push the phone band 40px -> 116px. Measured: it did
 * exactly that (375 came out 36px worse) before this guard went in. The shipped
 * small-screen gutters are left exactly as they are.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  #inventory.collection .collection__grid {
    padding-block: 36px 80px;
  }
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2. THE KICKER — one line, at the SAME 11px
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * "ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGE CONTROL (ABC) LICENCE" wrapped to two lines on all six
 * cards, costing 17.05px of card height each. Measured the intrinsic one-line
 * width against the 306px of content the 340px card actually offers:
 *
 *     11px / .09em (shipped)   308.3px   wraps by 2.3px
 *     11px / .07em             299.7px   fits
 *     11px / .06em             295.4px   fits, 10.6px spare
 *
 * It misses by TWO PIXELS. So this needs no smaller type at all — .09em to
 * .06em is still firmly inside the letterspaced-uppercase-eyebrow range
 * (.eyebrow:57 itself runs .14em, .cov-nav__count .18em), and it buys back a
 * whole line on every card. 10.6px of slack absorbs font-loading variance.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .product-card__kicker {
  letter-spacing: .06em;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3. THE SPEC LEDGER — the rules were set for a 3-up grid, not a coverflow
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * .specs__row:238 is padding-block: var(--ds-space-sm) = 12px a side, so four
 * rows spend 96px on air alone. At the [Z2] 3-up grid the ledger was the card's
 * whole lower half and wanted that rhythm; in the coverflow the card is a
 * single 340px column and 8px reads as the same ledger, just less loose. The
 * 1px rules keep the row structure legible, so nothing is lost but slack.
 * Measured by ablation, this section (row padding + the gap fix below + the
 * list's top margin) is worth 36px at 1440.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .specs__row {
  padding-block: 8px;

  /* The label/value GAP goes 12 -> 8 as well, and this one is a defect fix
     rather than a trim. At the 1099 breakpoint the card is 310px, and exactly
     ONE row in the whole band does not fit: SoMa District's
     "Status & timeline / Pending transfer" needs 279.3px of a 276px column and
     wraps mid-phrase to "Pending / transfer", which alone made that card 36px
     taller than the other five and, being the tallest, set the height of the
     entire track. Measured shortfall: 3.3px. 4px of gap covers it.
     Invisible everywhere else: dt is left-aligned and dd right-aligned, so on
     every other row the columns are already 100px+ apart and the gap never
     comes into play. */
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
}

/* the ledger's own top gap: --ds-space-md (16) -> --ds-space-sm (12). The name
   above it is a 21px display line with its own leading; 12px still separates
   the two blocks cleanly. */
#inventory.collection .collection__grid > .product-card .specs__list {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 4. THE MEDIA — 16/10 to 16/9
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * At 338px of card interior that is 211.25px -> 190.1px, 22px by ablation, and
 * 16/9 is the more editorial crop for a card header band anyway. The nested
 * radius is restated because it is the owner's first acceptance criterion and
