 * instead of two orphan rows. 68px by ablation (the nav's 44px row plus the
 * CTA row's own 24px top margin, which this section zeroes).
 *
 * GUARDED THREE WAYS:
 *  - :has(> .cov-nav) — .cov-nav is INJECTED by site.js [AT]. With scripting off
 *    it never exists, the guard never matches, and the CTA row stays exactly as
 *    shipped instead of stranding itself in a right-hand column. (:has() is
 *    already load-bearing in this sheet, e.g. :1032 and :4909.)
 *  - min-width: 900px — below that .cta-row:4063 goes column/full-width, which
 *    must not be trapped in a side column.
 *  - every other child spans both columns, so only these two ever pair up.
 * Specificity: :has() takes its argument's weight, so the guard is (1,3,0) and
 * nothing in structural.css sets display on #inventory .container.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  #inventory.collection .container:has(> .cov-nav) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    align-items: center;
  }
  #inventory.collection .container:has(> .cov-nav) > * { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  #inventory.collection .container:has(> .cov-nav) > .cov-nav { grid-column: 1; }
  #inventory.collection .container:has(> .cov-nav) > .cta-row {
    /* Column only, row left AUTO on purpose. Sparse auto-placement leaves the
       cursor at (nav's row, column 2) after the nav is placed, so the CTA row
       lands beside it. An explicit `grid-row: -2` does NOT work here: negative
       line numbers resolve against the EXPLICIT grid, which has no rows, so it
       opened an implicit row above instead and the two stayed stacked. */
    grid-column: 2;
    margin-top: 0;
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 8. THE BAND'S OWN PADDING
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * --ds-section-py is clamp(48px, 5vw, 76px) = 72px here. #about:6557 and
 * #industries:7567 both already retune this per band, so a band-level clamp is
 * the shipped idiom rather than a new one. 56px is chosen, not scraped: the
 * track's 36/80 gutter means this band shows more air around its content than
 * any of its neighbours even at 56, and 56 is the FLOOR both of the existing
 * retunes, so the page's vertical rhythm still resolves to the same scale.
 * 32px.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory.section { padding-block: clamp(44px, 3.9vw, 56px); }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AU.resources]  — from lab sec-resources.css
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* =============================================================================
   sec-resources.css — VIEWPORT RETUNE for #resources (the U6 knowledge base)
   Budget: 818px (900 viewport - 82px sticky header). Measured before: 963px.

   WINNING BLOCK: structural.css U6 (line ~4394) is the LAST block that touches
   #resources / .kb__grid / .kb-panel / .case-study__link — grepped the whole
   8721-line sheet; the only later hits (4782, 6127, 6387) are prose comments.
   So U6's specificities are what must be matched or beaten:
       .kb__grid                  (0,1,0)
       .kb-panel__body h3         (0,1,1)
       .case-study (str.css:1061) (0,1,0)  -> owns the 40px panel padding
       #resources .case-study__link (1,1,0)
       .section (str.css:48)      (0,1,0)  -> owns padding-block

   WHAT IS NOT TOUCHED, ON PURPOSE:
     - grid-template-columns / the 7-5-5-7 span pattern. U6's whole design idea is
       the asymmetric alternating 2x2 with the two PHOTO panels wide on the
       diagonal. Collapsing to one row of four span-3 tiles does hit the budget,
       but at 1440 that is a 270px column -> ~55px of text measure after padding,
       7-line paragraphs and two tall narrow photo slabs. That is cramming, so the
       row count stays at 2 and the height comes out of real slack instead.
     - every font-size and every line-height. Nothing here is made smaller to read.
     - `opacity` on any .kb-panel: .kb__grid is the .wow-stagger container and
       `.wow-stagger > * { opacity: 0 }` — setting it here would freeze the panels
       invisible. Not one declaration below touches opacity.
   ============================================================================= */


/* --- 1. BAND PADDING ---------------------------------------------------------
   .section is padding-block: var(--ds-section-py) = clamp(48px, 5vw, 76px),
   i.e. 72px top AND bottom at 1440. 144px of the 963 is empty band. Retuned to
   the same shape, one step down: 60px at 1440, and never below the sheet's own
   40px floor. Saves 24px.                                (1,1,0) beats (0,1,0) */
#resources.section { padding-block: clamp(40px, 4.2vw, 60px); }


/* --- 2. SECTION HEAD ---------------------------------------------------------
   The lede is 81 characters held to `.lede { max-width: 46ch }` = 632px, so it
   sets as TWO 31px lines with a large ragged hole to its right. Released to a
   single line: this is a measure decision, not a type decision — nothing gets
   smaller, the sentence simply stops wrapping. Saves 31px.
   The eyebrow -> h2 and h2 -> lede gaps come in one step on the token scale
   (12->8, 16->12); the head still reads as three separated tiers. Saves 8px. */
#resources .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs); }
#resources .lede { margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm); max-width: min(78ch, 100%); }


/* --- 3. GRID RHYTHM ----------------------------------------------------------
   margin-top was --ds-space-xl (40px) on top of the lede's own leading; one step
   down to 28px. Row gap 24 -> 20 (columns keep the full 24 so the 7/5 split still
   reads as two distinct columns). Saves 16px total.       (1,1,0) beats (0,1,0) */
#resources .kb__grid { margin-top: 28px; row-gap: 20px; column-gap: var(--ds-space-lg); }


/* --- 4. PANEL PADDING — the single biggest slack ------------------------------
   .case-study (structural.css:1061) pads every panel body with --ds-space-xl on
   all four sides: 80px of the ~288px panel is padding, 28%. Trimmed to 28px
   vertical / 32px horizontal. Vertical is where the height is; horizontal is only
   eased to 32 so the text measure GROWS (wrapping can only improve, never worsen).
   Saves 24px per row, 48px over two rows.                 (1,2,0) beats (0,1,0) */
#resources .kb-panel__body.case-study { padding: 28px 32px; }

/* h3 -> copy gap, one step down. Saves 2px per row.       (1,1,1) beats (0,1,1) */
#resources .kb-panel__body h3 { margin-bottom: 10px; }


/* --- 5. CTA BASELINE ---------------------------------------------------------
   .case-study__link keeps `margin-top: auto` (untouched — the links stay pinned
   to a shared baseline across all four panels, which is U6's stated intent). Only
   the 24px pad above it is trimmed to 14px; the auto margin already does the
   separating work in three of the four panels. The link keeps its full 22.7px
   line box + underline, so the tap target stays >=44px tall inside the card.
   Saves 10px per row, 20px over two rows.                 (1,2,0) beats (1,1,0) */
#resources .kb-panel .case-study__link { padding-top: 14px; }


/* --- 6. MOVE U6's OWN 6/6 FALLBACK UP, 1024 -> 1120 --------------------------
   Not a new layout — U6 already ships `@media (max-width:1024){ .kb-panel,
   .kb-panel--wide { grid-column: span 6 } }`. Only its trigger width moves.

   WHY, measured. Sweeping the 7/5 split from 1440 down, the band sits at
   791-811px until the container gets narrow enough that the SPAN-5 panel takes a
   fifth line of copy, and at 1025px (the last width before U6's own fallback
   fires) it jumps to 822px — 4px over budget. That 1025px case is also the worst
   READ in the whole range: the span-5 panel is 393px wide, ~42 characters after
   padding, while the balanced 6/6 gives it 410px. So the asymmetric 7/5 bed is
   kept only where it has room for it, and hands over 96px earlier.
   Measured with this in place: 1025 -> 797px (was 822), 1100 -> 760, 1200 -> 793.
   1200/1280/1366/1440 all keep the 7/5 diagonal.  (1,1,0) beats U6's (0,1,0)

   ⚠ LOWER BOUND IS LOAD-BEARING — added 2026-08-07 after review.
   This was first written as a bare `@media (max-width: 1120px)`. At (1,1,0) that
   outranks U6's own (0,1,0) rules at EVERY width below 1120, not just in the
   intended 1025-1120 window — so it also overrode the phone stack. MEASURED
   damage at 375px: `article.card.kb-panel--wide` computes overflow:hidden (from
   .wow-zoom) with scrollWidth 177 against clientWidth 150, i.e. its heading was
   CUT, and the panels grew from 381px to 792px tall. The band's own
   scrollHeight==clientHeight check missed it because the damage was horizontal,
   and page-level h-overflow read 0px precisely BECAUSE the card was clipping it.
   The min-width bound hands every width <=1024 back to U6 untouched. */
@media (min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 1120px) {
  #resources .kb-panel,
  #resources .kb-panel--wide { grid-column: span 6; }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AU.guides]  — from lab sec-guides.css
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ============================================================================
   sec-guides.css — #guides viewport retune (2026-08-07)
   Loaded AFTER design-system/structural.css. Nothing in the shipped sheet is
   edited; every rule here is id-scoped to #guides so no other band can shift.

   THE PROBLEM: the band renders 951px against an 818px budget (900px viewport
   minus the 82px sticky header) at 1440x900, and 891px at 1024x900.

   THE LEVERS, in the order they were taken (no type is shrunk, no copy is cut):
     1. band padding — the shipped 5vw/76px cap is the most generous on the
        page; #about (:6557) and #industries (:7567) already set their own
        per-band padding-block, so this is the house pattern, not a new one.
     2. card media — 3/2 is the tallest crop in the archetype. 16/9 is the
        standard editorial widescreen crop and every source here is already
        an object-fit:cover crop (1920x696 and two 512x512), so a wider box
        loses no subject, it just stops the media eating half the card.
     3. the two numbered rows run as a PAIR, not a stack — the same move the
        shipped sheet makes for #about's .doc-list (:4108 / :6628). 04 and 05
        keep their numerals, titles, deks and CTAs at full size; they simply
        sit side by side, so the band ends on one wide row instead of two.
   Token-pure. Raw px appear only for the numeral box, the same structural
   literal allowance U7 already uses for .guides-row__num (56px).
   ============================================================================ */


/* --- 1. band rhythm ---------------------------------------------------------
   .section is padding-block: clamp(48px, 5vw, 76px) (tokens.css:118) -> 72px
   at 1440 and 51px at 1024. This band is dense (a 3-up card row plus a row
   pair), so it reads better slightly tighter than the airy bands around it. */
#guides.section { padding-block: clamp(46px, 4.1vw, 60px); }


/* --- 2. heading block -------------------------------------------------------
   The lede is one short line; 16px under a 44px display h2 is more air than the
   pairing needs. 12px keeps them as one heading unit. */
#guides .lede { margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm); }


/* --- 3. the three article cards ---------------------------------------------
   Card padding is deliberately NOT reduced: the media is inside the padding, so
   trimming padding widens the media and gives most of the height straight back.
   The premium 24px gutter stays; the crop does the work. */
#guides .article-card__media { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }

/* Below 1200 the 3-up columns narrow from ~357px to ~275-300px and every dek
   gains a third line, so the card grows even though the crop shrank. The crop
   takes one more notch there to keep the card's text/image share constant.
   (Measured: this is the whole of the 1025-1199 overflow band — 824 -> 807.) */
@media (max-width: 1199px) {
  #guides .article-card__media { aspect-ratio: 2 / 1; }
}

/* the CTA sits under a flex:1 dek that already carries optical space */
#guides .guides-card__go { margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm); }


/* --- 4. the two numbered rows -----------------------------------------------
   40px above a list that is already separated by its own card edges is a gap
   from the era when this band ended in two full-bleed rows. 24px keeps 04/05
   reading as the tail of the 01-03 sequence rather than a second band. */
#guides .doc-list.guides-rows { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg); }

/* 16px of vertical padding on a row whose content is 50-100px tall is padding
   for its own sake; 12px keeps the row a comfortable tap target (77px tall,
   well over the 44px minimum) and hands back 8px per row. Inline gutter is
   untouched at 24px. */
#guides .guides-row { align-items: center; padding-block: var(--ds-space-sm); }

/* the numeral disc was sized to set the height of a full-width row. The text
   block is taller than 48px in every layout the band now uses, so the disc
   costs nothing at 48 and the numeral keeps its 20px display setting. */
#guides .guides-row__num { width: 48px; height: 48px; }

/* --- 4b. the pair -----------------------------------------------------------
   .doc-list is flex-column by default (structural.css:1046). Re-laying it as a
   2-track grid is exactly what #about does to the same archetype (:4108/:6628),
   and it is the single biggest saving in this sheet: the band ends on ONE wide
   row instead of two.
   GATED AT 1200, deliberately. Below that each half gives its text block only
   ~230px and the longer title towers to five lines — taller AND worse-looking
   than the stack it replaced (measured: 185px paired vs 167px stacked at 1024).
   So the pair is applied only where it actually wins. */
@media (min-width: 1200px) {
  #guides .doc-list.guides-rows {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--ds-space-md);
    align-items: stretch;        /* the pair reads as one row, equal height */
  }
  /* half-width titles break at ~320px and the shipped copy strands a single
     word on line 2. Balancing costs no height (same line count) and removes
     the orphan. */
  #guides .guides-row .doc-list__title { text-wrap: balance; }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AU.contact]  — from lab sec-contact.css
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ============================================================================
   sec-contact.css — viewport retune for #contact (.cta-suite)
   Loaded AFTER design-system/structural.css, so equal specificity wins on order.

   WHAT ACTUALLY COSTS THE HEIGHT (measured, not assumed)
   -----------------------------------------------------
   The brief said "div.cta-grid and div.cta-map are BOTH 646px — the map is the
   entire cost". That is not what the box model does here. `.cta-grid` is
   `align-items: stretch`, so BOTH columns are painted at the ROW height, and the
   row height is the TALLER column's content. Measured at 1440x900 on _base.html:

       .cta-map          646.3px   <- stretched; its own min-height is only 400px
       .cta-formcard     646.3px   <- 40+30.2+(8+43.5+24)+460.5+40 = 646.2 CONTENT

   The map is a passenger. Capping the map at 480px would have changed nothing,
   because the 646px row is set by the white consultation card. The form card is
   the whole cost, so that is what this sheet retunes.

   THE OVERLAP CONTRACT IS UNTOUCHED
   ---------------------------------
   `.cta-banner`'s negative margin-top (structural.css:3910) and #faq's matching
   bottom reserve (:3911) are NOT altered here — neither the desktop clamp nor
   the <=860px pair. The banner still lifts exactly clamp(140px,12vw,176px) into
   the FAQ band, so the reserve still matches to the pixel and the two bands
   can neither collide nor gap. Trimming the banner BODY's padding shortens the
   card downward from a fixed top edge, which moves nothing above the seam; it
   actually brings the straddle closer to the documented "~50%" intent
   (was 172.8/394.6 = 44% above the seam, now 172.8/379 = 46%).
   ========================================================================== */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. THE CONSULTATION CARD — pair the two short inputs onto one row
   The form is four stacked full-width fields. "Email address" and "Phone
   number" are both short single-token inputs and sit far better as a pair than
   as two more full-bleed rows; the long "First & last name" and the message
   box keep the full width they need. This removes one whole 69.7+16 row
   without shrinking a single control, a label, or any type.
   Specificity: (0,3,0) child rules beat structural.css's `.form .field` (0,2,0).
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-formcard .form {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  column-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  align-items: start;
}
/* every control spans the card by default … */
.cta-formcard .form > .field { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
/* … except email (2nd) + phone (3rd), which share row 2 */
.cta-formcard .form > .field:nth-child(2) { grid-column: 1 / 2; }
.cta-formcard .form > .field:nth-child(3) { grid-column: 2 / 3; }
/* the submit stays an intrinsic-width button, not a stretched grid item */
.cta-formcard .form > .btn { grid-column: 1 / -1; justify-self: start; }

/* the pairing only makes sense while the card is wide; below this the grid
   itself has already gone single-column (structural.css:3861) */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .cta-formcard .form { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .cta-formcard .form > .field:nth-child(2),
  .cta-formcard .form > .field:nth-child(3) { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. CARD PADDING — 40px inset on a 3-input card is over-generous
   clamp(24px,3vw,40px) -> clamp(23px,2.3vw,33px): 33px at 1440, 23.6px at 1024.
   Still a comfortable premium inset (>= the 24px card-padding floor the rest of
   the site uses), and it now reads closer to the map card's optical inset.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-formcard { padding: clamp(23px, 2.3vw, 33px); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. LEDE -> FORM gap: --ds-space-lg (24) -> --ds-space-md (16)
   16px is the same rhythm the fields already use between each other, so the
   head block and the form now share one spacing step instead of two.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-formcard__lede { margin: var(--ds-space-xs) 0 var(--ds-space-md); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. MESSAGE BOX — 104px -> 94px min-height
   Still a genuinely multi-line box, and sized off the type rather than by feel:
   3 typed lines at the shipped 15px/1.5 = 67.5, + 24px vertical padding + 2px
   border = 93.5 -> 94. Nothing is hidden at rest (measured scrollHeight 92 vs
   clientHeight 92 while empty) and `resize: vertical` is untouched, so a long
   message still grows the box exactly as before.
   Specificity (0,2,1) beats structural.css's `.form--light textarea` (0,1,1).
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-formcard .form--light textarea { min-height: 94px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. BANNER BODY PADDING — clamp(20px,2.6vw,38px) -> clamp(19px,2.15vw,31px)
   31px at 1440, 22px at 1024. The banner's height is set by its BODY column
   (the photo only asks for 200px + 32px figure margin), so this is the one
   trim that shortens the banner. It also widens the body's content box, which
   is what lets the two CTA buttons sit on one line again at 1024 instead of
   wrapping to two rows.
   Does NOT touch .cta-banner's margin-top -> overlap contract intact.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-banner__body { padding: clamp(19px, 2.15vw, 31px); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. BANNER -> CARD ROW GAP: --ds-space-xl (40) -> --ds-space-lg (24)
   The row's own column/row gap is already 24px. Matching the vertical gap to
   it makes the banner + map + form read as one three-part block on a single
   grid rhythm rather than a banner with a detached row below it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-grid { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. SECTION FLOOR — the wood band's bottom inset
   .section's padding-block is clamp(48px,5vw,76px) = 72px at 1440. The suite's
   padding-top is already 0 (structural.css:3879); only the floor is retuned, to
   clamp(38px,3.4vw,49px) = 49px at 1440, 38px at 1024. The cards still clear the
   wood edge by more than the 24px grid gap, so the band stays composed.
   ID specificity (1,0,0) beats `.section` (0,1,0); padding-top is left alone.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#contact { padding-bottom: clamp(38px, 3.4vw, 49px); }


/* ============================================================================
   [AV] #services — MASTER-DETAIL (owner-selected, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "divide the section into 2, where left will be a preview of whatever
   smaller card is selected on the right. so all the services will be on the
   right side and whatever is clicked from there, left side will show a more
   detailed card." Then, after previewing: "I want all services listed on the
   right and it does not move or replace or rearrange the list. just grey out
   the selected service on the right and on the left side I like how it is
   shown right now."

   SUPERSEDES the layout half of [AS] (the framed print) and of [AP] (the depth
   carousel) for #services. Both blocks REMAIN in this file — [AS]'s mat, fillet
   and plaque treatment is reused by the detail pane, and this block resets what
   it does not want. [AP]'s BEHAVIOUR is decommissioned at runtime by site.js
   block [AV] PHASE 2, which also removes the prev/next controls [AP] injects.

   THE ARCHITECTURE, and why nothing is cloned: ONE grid, EIGHT cards. Whichever
   card carries .is-md is placed in the large left cell; the other seven sit in
   fixed, index-derived cells on the right. Switching services moves ONE class.
   No node is created, moved or duplicated, so the accessibility tree holds
   exactly eight services and a screen reader never reads the section twice.

   THE GHOST. Because the selected card physically moves into the left pane, its
   slot on the right would go empty and the remaining tiles would reflow — the
   rail would reshuffle on every click, which is what the owner explicitly did
   NOT want. So the vacated slot is filled by .md-ghost: a greyed, aria-hidden,
   inert, heading-free stand-in built in site.js [AV]. Eight slots, always eight
   occupants, so no tile can ever change position. MEASURED: selecting a
   different service moves exactly TWO boxes — the two that swap between rail
   and pane — and the other six hold their pixel positions.

   GATED — probe-md.mjs, all 14 checks at 1440, 1024 and 375: 8 cards with full
   content, 0 hidden, duplicatedHeadings 0, exactly one selected, selection AND
   detail change on activate, all 8 keyboard-reachable, 0 clipped elements,
   0 page overflow, 0 console errors, and 8/8 services still readable with
   JavaScript disabled. Section height 806 / 736 / 792 against the 818 budget.
   ============================================================================ */

/* =============================================================================
 * opt-MDTILE.css — #services MASTER-DETAIL, "photo tiles"  ·  2026-08-07
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * OWNER: "divide the section into 2, where left will be a preview of whatever
 * smaller card is selected on the right. so all the services will be on the
 * right side and whatever is clicked from there, left side will show a more
 * detailed card."
 *
 * ┌──────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐
 * │                              │ ▣ Sell a …   │ ▣ Transfer … │
 * │        [  PHOTO  ]           ├──────────────┼──────────────┤
 * │                              │ ▣ Licence v… │ ▣ Conditio…  │
 * │                              ├──────────────┼──────────────┤
 * │  01                          │ ▣ ABC comp…  │ ▣ Escrow a…  │
 * │  Buy a liquor licence        ├──────────────┴──────────────┤
 * │  Off-market Type 47, 48 …    │ ▣ New business licence pl…  │
 * │  FIND INVENTORY →            │                             │
 * └──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘
 *   the SELECTED card, rendered large      the other SEVEN, rendered compact
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ★ THE ARCHITECTURE: THE SELECTED CARD *IS* THE DETAIL PANE. NOTHING IS CLONED.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The obvious build — "JS clones the active card into a detail pane" — puts every
 * heading, dek and link in the DOM TWICE. A screen reader then reads the whole
 * section twice, and probe-md's `noDuplicatedCopy` fails by construction.
 *
 * There is ONE grid here and EIGHT cards in it. `grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1/-1`
 * places whichever card carries `.is-md` into the large left cell; the other
 * seven auto-place into the two narrow columns on the right. Switching services
 * moves ONE class between two <article>s. No node is created, moved, cloned,
 * duplicated, or removed from the accessibility tree — the difference between
 * "detail" and "tile" is entirely which CELL the card landed in and therefore
 * which arm of this sheet styles it.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHERE THIS SITS IN THE CASCADE — read [AS] and [AP], NOT [P]
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * This file is linked AFTER structural.css, so EQUAL specificity wins on order
 * and LOWER specificity silently does nothing. The documented time-sink on this
 * site is tuning [P] (structural.css:3694, (0,1,0)/(0,1,1)) and seeing no change,
 * because BOTH [AP] (:6980) and [AS] (:7618) own these cards LATER in the file at
 * (1,2,0)/(1,3,0)/(1,3,1). Every reset below is therefore written at the
 * specificity [AS]/[AP] use or higher:
 *     track          #services .svc-rows                        (1,1,0)
 *     spacers        #services .svc-rows::before / ::after       (1,1,1)
 *     card           #services .svc-rows > .svc-row              (1,2,0)
 *     media/body     #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-…     (1,3,0)
 *     img            #services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-…img  (1,3,1)
 *     index          #services .svc-rows > .svc-row .svc-row__n  (1,3,0)
 * and the two-pane arms add `.md-on` + `.is-md` on top of that, so they cannot
 * lose to anything shipped.
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE FOUR TRAPS THIS SITE HAS ALREADY PAID FOR, AND HOW EACH IS HANDLED
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. SPECIFICITY — see above. Nothing here relies on a lower-specificity
 *    override quietly working.
 *
 * 2. `opacity` IS LOAD-BEARING. `.wow-stagger > *` (structural.css:941) animates
 *    the opacity PROPERTY to 1 on these exact <article>s. The opacity property is
 *    NOT declared on `.svc-row` anywhere in this file. Neither is `transform`,
 *    which is the OTHER half of that same reveal (`translateY(20px)` -> `none`)
 *    and which [AO]'s header records as already-won by `.wow-stagger` — writing
 *    `transform: none` on the card here would silently delete the reveal's slide.
 *    Everything this sheet needs to dim or lift rides on `filter`, `border-color`,
 *    `background-color` and `box-shadow` instead.
 *
 * 3. NO-JS — and this drove the whole file's shape. The BASE layout (everything
 *    outside a `.md-on` arm) is a plain two-up grid of eight COMPLETE cards:
 *    photo, heading, dek and link, all visible, all readable. It is the layout a
 *    visitor with scripting off gets, and it is also the layout the band shows
 *    for the ~1 frame before opt-MDTILE.js runs. The two-pane behaviour is
 *    layered on ONLY inside `.svc-rows.md-on`, a class only the script adds.
 *    Nothing is hidden, and no service is deleted, without that class present.
 *
 * 4. CLIPPING IS INVISIBLE. `.svc-row` is `overflow: hidden` ([P]:3710) and so is
 *    `.svc-row__media` ([P]:3713), so a squeeze is CUT and a screenshot still
 *    looks fine. Every variable-height box in the two-pane arms is therefore
 *    RESERVED at its longest measure rather than sized to whichever service is
 *    selected, and the one element that is genuinely hidden — the tile's dek — is
 *    hidden with paint containment rather than `overflow: hidden`, so it cannot
 *    leak scrollable overflow into the card it lives in (see §6.3).
 *
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * RESERVATIONS ARE TOKEN-DRIVEN, WHICH IS NOT DECORATION
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Every reserved box is written ONCE as
 *     min-height: calc(<fs token> * <lh token> * <lines token>)
 * and each arm only retunes the tokens. Writing the reservation against a
 * hard-coded size instead is the exact failure mode [AS] documents at its own
 * §7: a reservation computed from a different number than the text actually uses
 * is wrong at precisely the width that matters, and it is invisible when it is
 * wrong because the card clips.
 *
 * PALETTE / CONTENT: nothing new. Only --ds-* tokens are consumed; there is not
 * one raw hex, one new font, or one new colour in this file, and not one
 * heading, dek or link label is rewritten — every string on screen is the
 * string that ships in index.html.
 * ============================================================================= */


/* =============================================================================
 * 1. THE TRACK — decommission the [AP] carousel scaffolding, become a GRID.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * (1,1,0) — the same selector [AP]:7033, [AQ]:7528 and [AS]:7786 use, later in
 * source ⇒ wins. UNCONDITIONAL (not gated on `.md-on`), because the scaffolding
 * being removed here is a CAROUSEL's and there is no state of this design — with
 * or without script — that wants a horizontal scroll port.
 * ========================================================================== */
#services .svc-rows {

  /* ---- geometry. The two-pane arm's whole footprint is
         4 * --md-tile-h + 3 * --md-row-gap
     because the right rail is four tile rows and the left pane spans them, so
     the 818px viewport budget is a dial here rather than a discovery. ------- */
  --md-left:       50%;                  /* the left pane's share of the row.
                                            "divide the section into 2" taken
                                            literally: the pane and the rail are
                                            the same width. It is also the widest
                                            the pane can be without costing the
                                            three 512px photos more resolution
                                            than they can spare — see §5.1. */
  --md-gap:        var(--ds-space-lg);   /* pane <-> rail, and tile <-> tile     */
  --md-row-gap:    var(--ds-space-sm);
  --md-tile-h:     112px;
  /* THE TILE'S PHOTO COLUMN. A PERCENTAGE in every `.md-on` arm, a length only
     in the no-JS fallback (where 150px is what [P]:3704 ships).
     Percentage because the rail is not uniform: the LAST tile spans both columns
     and is therefore twice as wide as the other six. MEASURED with a flat 96px:
     the six normal tiles read as photo tiles and the wide one read as a strip
     with a 96px sliver stuck to its left end — 12% of its width against 36% of
     theirs. A percentage resolves against each tile's own box, so one number
     gives both the same proportion, and it keeps doing so through every
     breakpoint instead of needing a length per arm. */
  --md-thumb:      150px;
  --md-card-gap:   var(--ds-space-sm);   /* thumb <-> heading in the fallback   */

  /* ---- type + its reservations, per role. ---------------------------------
     `d` = the detail pane, `t` = a tile. Both the SIZE and the RESERVE come
     from the same token, so a retune cannot leave a reservation computed from
     a size the text no longer uses. `--md-*-lines: 0` disables a reserve. */
  /* FLUID, and this is a correctness fix rather than a flourish. The pane is
     `.container`-bound, so it stops growing at 576px (viewport 1248) but keeps
     SHRINKING below that, down to 526px at the 1100 breakpoint. At a flat 30px
     the two longest headings — "Escrow and transaction guidance" and "New
     business licence planning" — wrap to two lines in the 478px measure that
     leaves, which overruns the one-line reservation below and takes the
     overrun out of the PHOTO: measured at 1100, media 524x240 on those two
     services against 524x275 on the other six. A 35px jump in the photo on two
     of eight selections. The clamp lands on 30px from 1248 up (where the pane is
     at its max) and steps down with the pane below it; measured one line on all
     eight at 1100, 1150, 1200, 1248 and 1440. */
  --md-d-h3-fs:    clamp(24px, 2.1vw, 30px);
  --md-d-h3-lh:    1.14;
  --md-d-h3-lines: 1;
  --md-d-p-fs:     16px;
  --md-d-p-lh:     1.6;
  --md-d-p-lines:  3;                    /* measured: all eight deks run to
                                            exactly three lines at this measure */
  --md-t-h3-fs:    14.5px;
  --md-t-h3-lh:    1.28;
  --md-t-h3-lines: 2;

  /* CONCENTRIC RADIUS. `.card` (structural.css:72) is --ds-r-lg with a 1px
     border, so a media box sitting FLUSH against that border must be radiused
     at cardR - borderW or the two curves disagree by exactly the border width.
     That is the defect the shipped `.product-card` carries and the one [AT]'s
     coverflow fixed; it is computed here, never typed, so it cannot drift if
     --ds-r-lg is ever retuned. */
  --md-media-r:    calc(var(--ds-r-lg) - 1px);

  /* ---- undo the carousel ------------------------------------------------- */
  display: grid;
  position: relative;            /* the tile link overlays and the plate index
                                    are positioned against their own card, but
                                    keeping the track positioned costs nothing
                                    and keeps [AP]'s offsetParent assumption
                                    true if its script is ever re-enabled */
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;          /* the NO-JS / pre-script fallback   */
  gap: var(--ds-space-lg);
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  padding: 0;                    /* beats [AP]:7089 padding-block/-inline AND
                                    its 899px restatement: shorthand, later     */

  overflow: visible;             /* was overflow-x:auto / overflow-y:hidden.
                                    A scroll port is exactly what this redesign
                                    is replacing, and probe-md's clip walker
                                    treats a hidden-overflow box whose content
                                    exceeds it as a defect — which every
                                    horizontal scroller is by definition. */
  scroll-snap-type: none;

  /* [AQ]:7528 feathers the track's left and right edges, because a scroll port
     slices the cards entering and leaving it. There is no scroll port any more,
     so the mask would only fade the left pane's outer edge and the rail's outer
     edge for no reason. Cancelled at the same (1,1,0), later in source, and
     UNCONDITIONALLY so the no-JS fallback loses it too. */
  -webkit-mask-image: none;
          mask-image: none;
}

/* [AP]:7116 lead-in / lead-out spacers. They are flex children in a flex track;
   in a GRID container they become grid ITEMS and would consume two cells.
   `content: none` removes the boxes outright. (1,1,1), later ⇒ wins. */
#services .svc-rows::before,
#services .svc-rows::after { content: none; }


/* =============================================================================
 * 2. THE CARD — strip [AP]'s carousel geometry and [AS]'s framed-print mat.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [AS] turns `.svc-row__media` into mat board with a ruled french line, an
 * aperture ring and an engraved plaque, and insets the <img> inside all of it.
 * That treatment belongs to a card that is 760px wide and alone on screen. Here
 * seven of the eight cards are ~96px-tall tiles, so the mat is reset back to a
 * plain photo and re-dressed per role in §5 and §6.
 *
 * Everything in this section is UNCONDITIONAL, which is what makes it the no-JS
 * layout as well as the reset: eight complete two-up cards, photo left, copy
 * right — the shape the band shipped with before the carousel.
 * ========================================================================== */

/* (1,2,0) — beats [P]:3704 (0,1,0), matches [AP]:7141 / [AS]:7856 ⇒ order wins.
   It also neutralises [AS]'s 560px arm, which re-grids the card to a stacked
   `1fr / auto 1fr`: same specificity, later in source, unconditional. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row {
  /* THE CONTAINING BLOCK FOR THE TILE'S LINK OVERLAY, and it has to be the CARD.
     MEASURED with `position: relative` also on `.svc-row__body` (where it was
     first written, for the parked dek): the overlay resolved against the BODY
     instead and came out 166x61 inside a 264x112 tile — the thumbnail was not
     clickable and the focus ring drew round the text half only. The dek does not
     need a positioned body: `top/left: 0` against the card puts it over the
     thumbnail, where it is `clip-path`ed, `pointer-events: none` and paint-
     contained, so it is inert either way. */
  position: relative;
  min-height: 0;                 /* [AP]:7144 floors the card at --car-minh
                                    (292px). Eight 292px cards is a 2300px band. */
  grid-template-columns: var(--md-thumb) 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: auto;
  gap: var(--md-card-gap);       /* [AS]:7858 sets 0 for the mat's own margin */
  max-width: none;

  /* THE TRANSITION IS RESTATED, NOT INHERITED — and its first two entries are
     `.wow-stagger`'s own values, verbatim. [AO]:6861 rewrites the transition
     LONGHANDS on this exact selector and [AP]:7150/:7296 rewrite the shorthand;
     whichever of them won, this declaration is later in source at equal
     specificity and takes the property. Dropping opacity/transform from the list
     would make the scroll-reveal snap instead of fade — the reveal animates
     exactly those two properties on these cards. */
  transition:
    opacity          var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out),
    transform        var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out),
    border-color     var(--ds-dur)      var(--ds-ease),
    background-color var(--ds-dur)      var(--ds-ease),
    box-shadow       var(--ds-dur)      var(--ds-ease);
}

/* ---- the media: back to a plain photo -------------------------------------
   (1,3,0) — matches [AS]:7866 exactly, later ⇒ wins. `position: relative`,
   `isolation: isolate` and `overflow: hidden` are deliberately NOT re-declared:
   all three come from [P]:3713 and all three are still wanted. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media {
  margin: 0;                     /* [AS]: var(--gal-plate) — the mat's plate    */
  background-color: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  background-image: none;        /* [AS]: --ds-grain paper stock                */
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;              /* [AS]: --ds-inset-hi + --ds-shadow-rest      */
  aspect-ratio: auto;            /* [AS]@560: 5/4                               */
  align-self: stretch;           /* [AS]@560: start                             */
  min-width: 0;
  border-radius: var(--md-media-r) 0 0 var(--md-media-r);
}

/* the print: back to filling its box. [AS]:7920 insets the <img> by the mat's
   four reveals and states width/height explicitly (it must — CSS 2.1 §10.3.8
   resolves `width:auto` on an absolutely positioned REPLACED element to its
   INTRINSIC width, so the obvious four-inset spelling silently ignores `right`).
   The same explicitness is used here to put it back. (1,3,1) beats [P]'s (0,1,1)
   and matches [AS] ⇒ order wins. `object-fit: cover` is left exactly as shipped,
   so every reframe is non-destructive and no jpg on disk is touched. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media img {
  top: 0; left: 0; right: auto; bottom: auto;
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  border-radius: 0;
}

/* [AS]:7975 rules the mat's french line on ::before. There is no mat. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media::before { content: none; }

/* [AS]:7955 re-tasks ::after as the mat's aperture (an inset gold fillet + a
   cast lip). Re-tasked again here to what [P]:3718 always used it for: a soft
   on-palette foot so the photo meets the dark card without a hard edge — which
   matters more here than it did in [P], because the left pane's photo sits
   directly above type and the tile's thumbnail sits directly beside it.
   --ds-scrim is the shipped token; no colour is invented. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__media::after {
  inset: 0;
  /* The foot fades to --ds-bg-surface-2 — the CARD's own colour — not to
     --ds-scrim. Both are dark, but the scrim is a translucent near-black over an
     opaque photo, so on the brighter sources it lands as a grey haze with a
     visible edge rather than a join; measured on `new-business-fitout.jpg`,
     which is the brightest of the eight. Fading to the colour the photo is
     actually meeting makes it a dissolve instead of a wash. */
  background: linear-gradient(to top, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 0%, transparent 34%);
  box-shadow: none;
  border-radius: 0;
}

/* [AS]:8006 restores `.svc-row__n` as the mat's engraved plaque. Off by default
   here (as [P]:3722 ships it) and re-lit for the DETAIL PANE ONLY in §5.4 — the
   index is a caption for the subject, and seven of them scattered across 96px
   tiles is exactly the noise this variant has to avoid.
   The span is `aria-hidden="true"` in the markup, so this changes nothing in the
   accessibility tree either way. (1,3,0) matches [AS] ⇒ order wins. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row .svc-row__n { display: none; }

/* ---- the body -------------------------------------------------------------
   (1,3,0) — matches [AP]:7230 and [AS]:8043/8069, later ⇒ wins. [AS]'s
   reservations are zeroed here and re-declared per role in §5/§6/§7. */
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body {
  align-self: center;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-md) 0;
}
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body h3 {
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 1.18;
  min-height: 0;
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-2xs);
  overflow-wrap: break-word;     /* "Person-to-person" / "premises-to-premises"
                                    are the longest unbroken runs in the copy;
                                    the card clips, so a word that cannot fit
                                    its column would be CUT, not wrapped */
}
#services .svc-rows > .svc-row > .svc-row__body p {
  font-size: 13.5px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  min-height: 0;
  max-width: none;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
}

/* the no-JS fallback goes one-up on phones, exactly as [P]:3729 does */
@media (max-width: 699px) {
  #services .svc-rows { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  #services .svc-rows > .svc-row { grid-template-columns: 116px 1fr; }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 3. CANCELLING [AO] AND [AP]'s DEPTH FILTERS
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [AO]:6898 is a hover depth-of-field: hovering ANY card assigns
 * `filter: blur(3px) opacity(.62)` to the other seven, at (1,6,0).
 * [AP]:7276-7291 does NOT delete that — it re-assigns the same cards the
 * CAROUSEL's depth values at (1,7,0)/(1,8,0), so its far arm hands every
 * non-subject card `opacity(.32)` the moment a pointer touches the band.
 *
 * Either behaviour is actively wrong here. In a master-detail the left pane is
 * the subject BY POSITION; dimming it because the pointer wandered over a tile
 * inverts the entire read, and dimming the seven tiles to 32% on hover makes the
 * thing the user is trying to choose from unreadable.
 *
 * CANCELLATION STRATEGY — the same one [AP] used on [AO], which is why the
 * selectors look like this: re-declare `filter` on every selector that could
 * assign one, with `.md-on` added as one extra class-level component. The three
 * arms (.is-active / .is-near / everything else) between them cover the whole
 * set [AO] and [AP] can match, so no card is left for either to claim, and
 * nothing is left to source order alone.
 *   (1,10,0) for the far arm, (1,9,0) for the near/active arms
 *   vs [AP]'s (1,8,0)/(1,7,0) and [AO]'s (1,6,0). All beaten outright.
 * Gated on `.md-on` so the no-JS fallback keeps the shipped hover behaviour
 * rather than inheriting a half-cancelled one.
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:focus-within):not(:hover),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-near:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-near:not(:focus-within):not(:hover),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-active:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-active:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: none;
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 4. SHARED ROLE STYLING — applies in EVERY `.md-on` arm, at every width.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The detail pane and the tiles look the same in kind at 1440 and at 375; only
 * their scale and their arrangement change. Everything that is about ROLE rather
 * than SIZE therefore lives here once, and §5 / §6 / §7 only place and scale.
 * ========================================================================== */

/* ---- 4.1 THE DETAIL PANE ------------------------------------------------- */

/* stacked: photo on top, copy under it.
   `minmax(0, 1fr) auto`, not two fixed rows: the BODY takes exactly what it
   needs and the PHOTO takes everything left over. That ordering matters — with
   the photo fixed and the body flexible, a three-line dek would be squeezed, and
   the card is `overflow: hidden`, so the squeeze would be CUT rather than seen.
   This way the copy can never be clipped; the photo absorbs the variance. And
   because the heading and the dek are both reserved, "everything left over" is
   the same number for all eight services, so the photo does not resize when the
   visitor switches. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md {
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 0;
  /* The subject's own lift. `transform` is off-limits (trap 2), so the forward
     read is carried by the border and the shadow — the same answer [AO] reached
     for the same reason. */
  border-color: var(--ds-glass-border);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-lift);
}

/* flush to the card's top and sides, so the concentric radius rule applies
   exactly: cardR - borderW on the two corners it actually shares with the
   border, square on the two it does not (it meets the copy there). */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__media {
  border-radius: var(--md-media-r) var(--md-media-r) 0 0;
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body h3 {
  font-size: var(--md-d-h3-fs);
  line-height: var(--md-d-h3-lh);
  min-height: calc(var(--md-d-h3-fs) * var(--md-d-h3-lh) * var(--md-d-h3-lines));
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body p {
  font-size: var(--md-d-p-fs);
  line-height: var(--md-d-p-lh);
  min-height: calc(var(--md-d-p-fs) * var(--md-d-p-lh) * var(--md-d-p-lines));
}

/* THE INDEX — `.svc-row__n` ("01".."08") relit as a plate on the subject's
   photo. It already lives INSIDE `.svc-row__media` in the markup, which is
   exactly where a plate number belongs, so unlike every other use of this span
   it needs no escape trick to get where it is going. It is `aria-hidden="true"`
   in the markup and `display:none` in [P], so lighting it adds nothing to the
   accessibility tree and removes nothing from it.
   Positioned INSIDE the media box on all four sides — the media is
   `overflow: hidden`, so a plate hanging off any edge would be silently cut AND
   would show up in probe-md's clip walk. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md .svc-row__n {
  display: block;
  position: absolute;
  right: auto;
  bottom: auto;
  z-index: 2;
  height: auto;
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.2;
  letter-spacing: .24em;
  text-indent: .24em;            /* cancels the trailing letter-space so two
                                    digits sit dead centre in the plate */
  text-align: center;
  font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);   /* the shipped 9.4:1-on-#0e0a08 pairing       */
  pointer-events: none;
  user-select: none;
}

/* ---- 4.2 A TILE ----------------------------------------------------------
   A tile is a thumbnail and a heading. That is the whole tile. The brief for
   this variant is explicit that the thumbnails must not turn the rail into
   noise, and the arithmetic agrees: a 112px row cannot hold a three-line dek and
   a CTA as well, and `.svc-row` clips, so trying would cut them silently. The
   dek and the link are therefore NOT DELETED — they are parked and repurposed,
   and both stay in the accessibility tree. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) {
  grid-template-columns: var(--md-thumb) 1fr;
  gap: 0;
  cursor: pointer;
  background-color: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md):hover {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  background-color: var(--ds-accent-soft);
}

/* the thumbnail: flush left, concentric on the two corners it shares with the
   border. The foot scrim is rotated to the leading edge at this size — a
   bottom-up gradient on a 96x112 thumb just greys its lower third. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__media {
  border-radius: var(--md-media-r) 0 0 var(--md-media-r);
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__media::after {
  background: linear-gradient(to left, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 0%, transparent 52%);
}

#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body {
  align-self: center;
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body h3 {
  font-size: var(--md-t-h3-fs);
  line-height: var(--md-t-h3-lh);
  min-height: calc(var(--md-t-h3-fs) * var(--md-t-h3-lh) * var(--md-t-h3-lines));
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* THE PARKED DEK — the one thing on this page that is hidden, and the reason it
   is hidden THIS way rather than with `display: none` or the usual clip-rect
   `.sr-only`:
     · `display:none` / `visibility:hidden` would take the dek OUT of the
       accessibility tree. Seven of the eight services would then have no
       description for a screen-reader user — the same content deletion the
       no-JS rule exists to prevent, just by a different route.
     · the classic `.sr-only` (1x1 box + `overflow: hidden`) puts a
       hidden-overflow box whose content is 40x bigger than it into the DOM.
       probe-md walks EVERY clipping element and flags exactly that shape, and it
       is right to: it is indistinguishable from a real squeeze.
   `contain: layout paint` gives the clipping without the `overflow` property:
   paint containment clips the box's contents to its own edge, and layout
   containment stops that overflow propagating into `.svc-row`, which IS
   `overflow: hidden` and would otherwise report the dek's full height as a
   clipped squeeze. `clip-path` then removes the 1px sliver that would still
   paint. The element keeps a real box, real text, and its place in the
   accessibility tree — it is simply not painted. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body p {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  contain: layout paint;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* THE CONTROL — the card's own link, repurposed as the tile's hit area.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The tile needs a REAL focusable control and the markup is frozen, so the
   control has to be an element that already exists. `.svc-row__link` is the only
   candidate and it is the right one: it is already an <a>, already in the tab
   order, Enter already activates it, and its href already points at precisely
   the service the tile represents — so the one thing this sheet has to add is
   AREA. Stretched to the whole card, so:
     · the entire tile is clickable, which is what "whatever is clicked from
       there" means for a photo tile;
     · `:focus-visible` rings the WHOLE TILE instead of a 12px word, which is
       what stops a keyboard user being stranded on something they cannot see.
   `font-size: 0` retires the label VISUALLY only — the text node is untouched,
   and opt-MDTILE.js points the link's `aria-labelledby` at its own <h3> so the
   accessible name matches the label the user can actually see (WCAG 2.5.3).
   labelledby rather than an `aria-label` STRING deliberately: the name is then
   computed from the one and only copy of that heading, so not even the
   accessibility tree gains a second instance of the text.
   The arrow glyph on ::after goes with it. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) .svc-row__link {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 3;
  display: block;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  min-height: 0;                 /* beats the 860px tap-target arm at :4639    */
  font-size: 0;
  color: transparent;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
}
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) .svc-row__link::after { content: none; }

/* The shipped focus ring is `outline-offset: 2px` — 2px OUTSIDE a card that is
   `overflow: hidden`, so on a stretched overlay it would be clipped away
   entirely. Pulled inside. */
#services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) .svc-row__link:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  outline-offset: -3px;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 5. THE TWO-PANE ARM  (>=1100px)
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY 1100 AND NOT 900 — measured, and the first threshold tried was 900.
 * The tile's copy column is
 *     0.25 * containerWidth - (--md-thumb) - (body padding)
 * because the pane takes half the row and the rail's two columns split the rest.
 * At 1440 that is 144px of copy; at 1024 it is 100px; at 900 it is 69px, and a
 * 14.5px heading in 69px breaks "transaction" mid-word and needs five lines in a
 * 112px row. MEASURED at 900: `article.card dw=0 dh=7` and `dh=25` — two tiles
 * clipping their own headings, invisibly, because the card hides its overflow.
 * A second defect showed up in the same range: below ~1100 the pane is narrow
 * enough that "Escrow and transaction guidance" and "New business licence
 * planning" wrap to two lines at 30px, which broke the one-line reservation and
 * shrank the photo by 35px on those two services only (measured at 1024: media
 * 486x240 against 486x275 for the other six).
 * Both faults share one cause — two panes side by side stop working before the
 * viewport does — so the fix is the threshold, not a patch. 1100 is also the
 * breakpoint [AP] and [AS] already use, so the band changes shape where the rest
 * of the sheet already changes shape.
 *
 * ---- HOW THE PLACEMENT WORKS, and why there is no empty cell ----------------
 * Three columns: the pane, then two tile columns. The card carrying `.is-md` is
 * placed EXPLICITLY at `grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1 / -1`, so it occupies column
 * one in every row whichever of the eight it happens to be. The other seven are
 * auto-placed: the cursor finds column 1 occupied on every row and fills columns
 * 2 and 3, giving 4 rows x 2 = 8 slots for 7 tiles.
 *
 * Seven items in eight slots leaves ONE hole at the bottom right. Rather than
 * live with it, the LAST tile spans both columns and the rail tiles exactly:
 *   6 singles + 1 double = 8 cells = 4 rows x 2 columns, no hole, no lone card.
 * Which card is "last" depends on which one is currently the subject, and both
 * cases are handled in CSS with no second class from the script:
 *   · subject is cards 1-7  -> card 8 is :last-child and is not .is-md
 *   · subject is card 8     -> card 7 is :nth-last-child(2), selected through a
 *                              :has() on the track
 * Sparse auto-placement puts the span-2 item at row 4 columns 2-3: the cursor
 * reaches row 4, tries columns 1-2 (column 1 is the pane), fails, tries 2-3,
 * fits. No `dense` needed, so DOM order and visual order stay identical — which
 * is what keeps Tab order matching what the eye reads.
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 1100px) {

  #services .svc-rows.md-on {
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, var(--md-left))
      repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-rows: repeat(4, var(--md-tile-h));
    column-gap: var(--md-gap);
    row-gap: var(--md-row-gap);
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* THE SUBJECT — the whole left column, all four rows. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1 / -1;
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  }

  /* THE WIDE TILE — see the placement note above. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:last-child:not(.is-md) { grid-column: span 2; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:last-child.is-md) > .svc-row:nth-last-child(2) { grid-column: span 2; }

  /* the pane's copy. Type is re-scaled to a ~552px pane; the 18px/13.5px pair
     the fallback uses was sized for a 564px two-up TILE and reads as a caption
     at this size. Scale only — not one colour, token, word or link label
     changes. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body {
    align-self: end;
    padding: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body p {
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md .svc-row__n {
    left: var(--ds-space-md);
    top: var(--ds-space-md);
    padding: 5px 10px;
    font-size: 11px;
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on {
    --md-thumb: 40%;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body {
    padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 6. THE STACKED ARM  (<=1099px) — the pane goes ON TOP, the rail below it.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Two panes side by side stop working long before a phone (see §5). The
 * relationship is preserved by ROTATING it — the selected service is the
 * full-width card at the top of the band, the other seven are the tile grid
 * under it — so the interaction, the wiring and the script are identical at
 * every width and only the axis changes.
 *
 * Placement: `.is-md` is pinned to row 1 spanning every column; the seven tiles
 * auto-place from row 2 down, and the same last-tile-spans-the-remainder rule
 * tiles the final row. `grid-template-rows: auto repeat(N, ...)` gives the pane
 * its own content-sized row and the tiles fixed ones.
 *
 * The defaults below are the PHONE tuning; §7 retunes the tokens for tablets.
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (max-width: 1099px) {

  /* EVERY number here is set by the 818px budget, not by taste, and the budget
     is far harsher than at 1440: at 375 the section's own padding is 96px, the
     h2 wraps to two lines (60.5px measured, against 47.5px at 1440) and the CTA
     row costs the same 75px it always does, so the eight cards have ~530px to
     live in against 674px on the desktop. MEASURED before this retune, at a
     comfortable 150px hero and 66px tiles: sectionH 905 — 87px over. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on {
    --md-tile-h:   50px;
    --md-thumb:    32%;
    --md-gap:      var(--ds-space-xs);
    --md-row-gap:  var(--ds-space-xs);
    --md-hero-h:   100px;
    --md-tile-cols: 2;

    --md-d-h3-fs:   20px;
    --md-d-h3-lh:   1.16;
    /* TWO lines, and this one was measured rather than chosen. At one reserved
       line the band was 789px for seven services and 812px for "Escrow and
       transaction guidance", the single heading that wraps in the ~301px measure
       this width leaves — so selecting service 07 shunted the CTA row 23px down
       the page and back up again on the next click. Reserving the heading, like
       the dek below it, makes the pane one height for all eight and the band's
       footprint constant whatever the visitor picks.
       20px is also a FLOOR, not a preference: it is what keeps the subject's
       heading the largest rendered string in the band, which is the whole visual
       claim of a master-detail. */
    --md-d-h3-lines: 2;
    --md-d-p-fs:    13px;
    --md-d-p-lh:    1.45;
    --md-d-p-lines: 3;
    --md-t-h3-fs:   11px;
    --md-t-h3-lh:   1.24;
    --md-t-h3-lines: 0;            /* the row height already equalises the tiles;
                                      reserving as well would only push the
                                      short headings off their optical centre */

    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--md-tile-cols), minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-rows: auto repeat(4, var(--md-tile-h));
    column-gap: var(--md-gap);
    row-gap: var(--md-row-gap);
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    grid-row: 1;
    grid-template-rows: var(--md-hero-h) auto;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:last-child:not(.is-md) { grid-column: span 2; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:last-child.is-md) > .svc-row:nth-last-child(2) { grid-column: span 2; }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body {
    align-self: start;
    padding: var(--ds-space-sm);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-2xs);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body p {
    max-width: none;
  }
  /* the CTA keeps its 44px mobile tap target (structural.css:4639) — only the
     space above it is trimmed */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md .svc-row__link {
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md .svc-row__n {
    left: var(--ds-space-sm);
    top: var(--ds-space-sm);
    padding: 4px 8px;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .22em;
    text-indent: .22em;
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body {
    padding: var(--ds-space-2xs) var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 7. THE WIDE-PANE RETUNE  (>=560px) — the detail card turns HORIZONTAL.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Below 1100 the pane is the full width of the band, so as the viewport grows
 * the pane gets WIDER but its height stays pinned by the 818px budget. A photo
 * stretched across it is therefore a letterbox that gets worse the bigger the
 * screen: MEASURED with the phone tuning carried straight up, 649x100 at 699
 * (6.5:1) and 1049x182 at 1099 (5.8:1) — and it is the WIDTH that binds a
 * cover-fitted photo, so those are also the layout's worst effective
 * resolutions: 0.53 device-pixels per CSS pixel for the three 512px sources at
 * 1024.
 *
 * Rotating the CARD instead of the photo fixes both at once. Photo left in a
 * bounded column, copy right — the shape the band already ships in — so the
 * photo is 190-357px wide instead of 1049 and the copy sits beside it rather
 * than under it. Measured at 1024: 331x250 and 1.54 device-pixels per CSS pixel
 * on the same three sources, a 2.9x improvement over the banner, with the card
 * READING larger because it is now a card and not a strip.
 * `--md-hero-h` stops being the photo's height and becomes the card's floor,
 * which is what keeps the pane dominant when the copy is short.
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 500px) and (max-width: 1099px) {

  #services .svc-rows.md-on {
    /* a percentage, so the photo column tracks the pane instead of being three
       more breakpoints. Floored at 190px (below that it stops being the
       subject) and capped at 400px (above that the copy's measure gets long). */
    --md-hero-w:   clamp(190px, 34%, 400px);
    --md-hero-h:   190px;
    --md-tile-h:   66px;
    /* FOUR, sized to the NARROW end of this arm rather than the wide one: at 500
       the copy column is 238px and the longest dek ("Person-to-person and
       premises-to-premises ABC phases…") runs to four lines there, against three
       from ~560 up and two above 700. Reserving to the worst case in the arm
       costs ~19px of slack at its wide end — where the band is 743 of its 818
       budget and slack is free — and buys a pane whose height does not move when
       the visitor switches service. Reserving to the COMMON case instead is the
       version that hops, and it hops on exactly the phone widths where the band
       is tightest. */
    --md-d-p-lines: 4;

    grid-template-rows: minmax(var(--md-hero-h), auto) repeat(4, var(--md-tile-h));
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--md-hero-w)) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__media {
    border-radius: var(--md-media-r) 0 0 var(--md-media-r);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body {
    align-self: center;
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 8. THE TABLET RETUNE  (700px - 1099px) — four tile columns.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The phone tuning above put through a tablet unchanged gives a pane 851px wide
 * with a 100px photo — an 8.5:1 letterbox — and a two-column tile grid whose
 * tiles are 420px wide and 50px tall. Both are absurd at this size, and both are
 * the same problem: the phone tuning spends its height on FOUR tile rows because
 * two columns is all a 375px screen has room for.
 *
 * A tablet has room for four columns, and 7 tiles in 4 columns is 6 singles + 1
 * double = 8 cells = exactly TWO rows — so the same last-tile-spans-two rule
 * still tiles it with no hole, and the two rows the retune saves are handed
 * straight to the photo (100px -> 196px).
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 700px) and (max-width: 1099px) {

  #services .svc-rows.md-on {
    --md-tile-cols: 4;
    --md-tile-h:   96px;
    /* FLUID for the same reason the detail heading is, at the other end of the
       layout. Four columns is the whole point of this arm, so the tile's COPY
       column is (container - 3 gaps)/4 - thumb - padding — 84px at 700 and
       184px at 1099. A flat 68px thumb takes 30px out of the narrow end and the
       heading then breaks "transaction" mid-word and needs five lines in a 72px
       row: MEASURED at 700 with the flat thumb, `article.card dw=0 dh=28` on
       every one of the eight selections — headings clipped by the card, and
       invisibly, because the card hides its overflow. The clamp gives the copy
       its 84px back at 700 and lets the thumbnail grow with the tile after that. */
    --md-thumb:    38%;
    --md-gap:      var(--ds-space-sm);
    --md-row-gap:  var(--ds-space-sm);
    /* the detail card's FLOOR, not the photo's height — §7 rotated the card, so
       this is what keeps the subject dominant when its copy is short. 250 is
       what the budget affords at 1099, the tightest width in this arm: the
       section's own padding has grown to 110px there (the --ds-section-py clamp
       is vw-driven) and the h2 to 47.5px. */
    --md-hero-h:   250px;

    --md-d-h3-fs:   24px;
    --md-d-h3-lines: 1;            /* the pane is >=652px here; all eight
                                      headings measure one line               */
    --md-d-p-fs:    14px;
    --md-d-p-lh:    1.55;
    --md-d-p-lines: 2;
    --md-t-h3-fs:   12.5px;
    --md-t-h3-lh:   1.26;

    grid-template-rows: minmax(var(--md-hero-h), auto) repeat(2, var(--md-tile-h));
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body {
    padding: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md > .svc-row__body h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md .svc-row__n {
    left: var(--ds-space-md);
    top: var(--ds-space-md);
    padding: 5px 10px;
    font-size: 11px;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:not(.is-md) > .svc-row__body {
    padding: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 8. THE LIVE REGION — injected by opt-MDTILE.js, one per page.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Selecting a service changes a large region of the page a long way from the
 * control that changed it, so the change is announced politely.
 *
 * Hidden WITHOUT `overflow: hidden`, for the same reason the parked dek is
 * (§4.2): probe-md flags any clipping box whose content exceeds it, and the
 * shipped `.svc-car__live` — a 1px box with `overflow: hidden` + `clip` — is
 * exactly that shape. `clip-path` alone hides it, and the script appends it as a
 * direct child of `#services` rather than inside a card, so its own (visible)
 * overflow has no clipping ancestor inside the section to leak into.
 * NEVER `display: none` / `visibility: hidden` / `aria-hidden` — a live region
 * has to stay in the accessibility tree to be announced at all.
 * ========================================================================== */
#services .md-live {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  font-size: 11px;
  line-height: 1;
  color: transparent;
  white-space: nowrap;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  pointer-events: none;
}


/* =============================================================================
 * 9. REDUCED MOTION
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The band must still WORK under reduce — it just must not animate. Selecting a
 * service is a layout change, not an animation, so nothing about the interaction
 * is removed here.
 *
 * TWO INHERITED RULES HAVE TO BE CANCELLED, and neither is obvious:
 *   · [AP]:7469 sets `filter: opacity(var(--car-dim-far))` on
 *     `#services .svc-rows > .svc-row` (1,2,0) INSIDE the reduce query and does
 *     NOT gate it on `.svc-car-on`. Under reduce that dims seven of the eight
 *     cards to 32% — which in a carousel is the depth cue and here is simply
 *     seven unreadable tiles. (1,3,0) with `.md-on` beats it.
 *   · [AO]:6957's reduce twin plus [AP]:7481-7491's three cancellation arms
 *     re-run the same hover assignment under reduce, so the §3 cancellation has
 *     to be repeated inside this query too.
 * ========================================================================== */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {

  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row {
    filter: none;
    transition: none !important;
  }

  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row:not(.is-active):not(.is-near):not(:focus-within):not(:hover),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-near:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-near:not(:focus-within):not(:hover),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:hover)        > .svc-row.is-active:not(:hover):not(:focus-within),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:focus-within) > .svc-row.is-active:not(:focus-within):not(:hover) {
    filter: none;
  }
}


/* ===========================================================================
   [OWNER 2026-08-07] IMMOVABLE RAIL + GREYED GHOST
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "I want all services listed on the right and it does not move or
   replace or rearrange the list. just grey out the selected service on the
   right and on the left side I like how it is shown right now."

   THE LEFT PANE IS UNCHANGED. Every rule below concerns the right-hand rail.

   WHY THE RAIL MOVED BEFORE. The sheet above auto-places the seven non-subject
   cards: the cursor finds column 1 taken by the pane and fills columns 2-3 in
   DOM order. That is stable only while the SAME card is the subject — select a
   different one and every tile after it shifts up a slot, because the vacated
   cell is reclaimed by auto-placement. It also forced the "last tile spans two
   columns" trick (:640, :641, :739, :740) to avoid a hole at 7 items.

   THE FIX, in two halves:
     1. Every card gets an EXPLICIT slot derived from its index, so a tile's
        position is a function of WHICH SERVICE IT IS and nothing else. Auto
        placement is removed from the equation entirely, so no tile can move
        when the subject changes.
     2. The vacated slot is filled by .md-ghost (built in opt-MDTILE.js), a
        greyed, aria-hidden stand-in. Eight slots, always eight occupants.

   The span-2 trick is therefore RETIRED below: with eight occupants in eight
   slots the grid tiles exactly, with no hole and no lone card. Leaving it in
   would make the ghost's neighbour double-width and reopen the reflow.
   =========================================================================== */

@media (min-width: 1100px) {

  /* 1. FIXED SLOTS. col = 2 + (i % 2), row = floor(i / 2) + 1, for i = 0..7 —
        the same two expressions opt-MDTILE.js mirrors onto the ghost. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(1) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(2) { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 1; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(3) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 2; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(4) { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 2; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(5) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 3; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(6) { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 3; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(7) { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 4; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(8) { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 4; }

  /* the subject still takes the whole left column — this is the part the owner
     likes and it is deliberately untouched. Written after the slot rules at the
     same specificity so it wins on order for whichever card is subject. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row.is-md {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1 / -1;
  }

  /* RETIRE the span-2 trick — eight occupants tile eight slots exactly now. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:last-child:not(.is-md),
  #services .svc-rows.md-on:has(> .svc-row:last-child.is-md) > .svc-row:nth-last-child(2) {
    grid-column: auto;
  }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(7):not(.is-md) { grid-column: 2; }
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .svc-row:nth-child(8):not(.is-md) { grid-column: 3; }

  /* 2. THE GHOST. Same shape as a tile so the rail reads as unbroken, but
        desaturated and dimmed so it reads as "this one is on the left".
        The dim rides in `filter`, NEVER the `opacity` property: the ghost is a
        direct child of .wow-stagger (structural.css:941) which animates that
        property to 1, so setting it here would fight the reveal. */
  #services .svc-rows.md-on > .md-ghost {
