/* ============================================================================
   sec-coverage.css — #coverage BREATHING-ROOM RESTORE  (2026-08-07)

   WHAT REGRESSED
   --------------
   No [AU] sheet was ever written for this band, so its ONLY change was the
   site-wide token cut: --ds-section-py clamp(64px,8.5vw,116px) ->
   clamp(48px,5vw,76px). At 1440 that is 116px -> 72px a side.
   MEASURED: #coverage 852px -> 764px; the eyebrow's standoff from the plate's
   top edge fell 116 -> 72 (the brief's y=126 -> y=82, same 44px delta).

   BUDGET
   ------
   1440x900, sticky header 82px -> 818px usable. Baseline 764px, so 54px of
   headroom exists. This sheet spends 48 of it and keeps 6 in hand.

   WHERE THE 48px GOES  (measured, 1440)
   -------------------------------------
     section padding-top     72 -> 88   (+16)  air above the eyebrow
     eyebrow -> h2           12 -> 12   ( 0 )  left alone: this is one lockup
     h2 -> map               24 -> 32   ( +8)  heading releases into the payload
     map -> .cv-copy         24 -> 40   (+16)  the register change, image->text
     section padding-bottom  72 -> 80   ( +8)  heavy filled CTA needs clearance
                                        ----
                                         +48   -> 812px of the 818px budget

   The resulting scale is monotonic and legible: 12 (lockup) < 32 (heading to
   payload) < 40 (payload to detail), framed by 88 / 80. Before the fix the two
   internal gaps were BOTH var(--ds-space-lg) = 24px, which flattened the
   hierarchy — the map read as just another paragraph rather than the payload.

   WHY THE MAP'S ASPECT RATIO IS *NOT* TOUCHED HERE — the numbers
   -------------------------------------------------------------
   The band's map is a 1400x933 (AR 1.50) DESIGNED map of California letter-
   boxed into aspect-ratio: 24/7 (AR 3.4286) with object-fit: cover. Measured
   at 1440: box 1152x336, so only 43.8% of the source's height is visible —
   525 of 933 image px are discarded. The visible window is rows 262..671,
   which is why the map shows Sacramento / San Francisco / San Jose / Fresno
   and shows NEITHER pole of the state: Los Angeles, Orange County, San Diego,
   Santa Barbara, Palm Springs, Riverside and Ventura — 7 of the 12 chips
   directly beneath it — are all cropped out, and the coastline runs off both
   the top and the bottom edge with no closure.

   That is real, but it is NOT affordable here:
     - Showing the whole map at 1152 wide needs 1152 / 1.50 = 768px of height.
       That is +432px against 54px of headroom.
     - Spending the ENTIRE 54px headroom on the map (AR 24/7 -> 2.93, height
       336 -> 390) lifts the visible fraction only 43.8% -> 51.3% and STILL
       cuts one end of the state — while leaving all three named composure
       gaps unfixed. Rejected on the arithmetic.
     - Panning is free (object-position), but it cannot add information: at
       AR 3.43 the window is 408 image px and Sacramento (y 336) to San Diego
       (y 884) is 548 image px apart. No Y position holds both. Choosing one
       pole over the other changes WHICH markets the map depicts — a content
       decision, not a spacing one, and this band's composition is owner-picked
       (structural.css [Y], "owner-picked E"). Left alone deliberately; costed
       in the handback instead.

   TRAPS OBSERVED
   --------------
   - .cv-map is .wow-reveal and .cv-chip are .wow-stagger children, both of
     which animate `opacity` from 0. NOTHING in this sheet sets `opacity`.
   - .cv-map is overflow:hidden. Verified scrollHeight/scrollWidth ==
     clientHeight/clientWidth for every clipping box in the band after the
     change: 0 clipped elements.

   SCOPE
   -----
   Gated to min-width: 861px. Below that .cv-copy stacks to one column and the
   band already measures 1105px at 375x900 — it cannot meet the 818px budget by
   any spacing change (the 12 one-column chips alone are 544px). Growing an
   already-over band would break the stated rule for no fit gain, so <=860px is
   left byte-for-byte on the shipped values. Flagged in the handback.

   Everything is expressed in --ds-* tokens. No design-system file is touched.
   ========================================================================== */

@media (min-width: 861px) {

  /* ---- frame: give the eyebrow its standoff back, and clear the CTA ------
     The min() cap is load-bearing, not decoration. --ds-section-py is
     clamp(48px,5vw,76px), so it keeps GROWING past 1440 (72px) until it tops
     out at 76px around 1520. A bare `+16 / +8` therefore measured 92/84 at
     1600 and 1920, which put the band at 820px — 2px OVER the 818px budget,
     even though 1440 was fine at 812px. Caught by sweeping 861..1920, not by
     the three named breakpoints. Capping at 88/80 (the 1440 values, composed
     from tokens: 64+24 and 64+16) holds the band at exactly 812px from 1440
     upward while leaving every narrower width to track the token. */
  #coverage {
    padding-block:
      min(calc(var(--ds-section-py) + var(--ds-space-md)),
          calc(var(--ds-space-2xl) + var(--ds-space-lg)))   /* <=88px; 1440: 88  1024: 67 */
      min(calc(var(--ds-section-py) + var(--ds-space-xs)),
          calc(var(--ds-space-2xl) + var(--ds-space-md)));  /* <=80px; 1440: 80  1024: 59 */
  }

  /* ---- h2 -> map: the heading releases into the payload ------------------ */
  #coverage .cv-map {
    margin-top: calc(var(--ds-space-lg) + var(--ds-space-xs));  /* 24 -> 32 */
  }

  /* ---- map -> copy: the biggest internal gap, because it is the biggest
          change of register (a bordered, shadowed image block handing off to
          running text) and it is the gap the h3 was sitting hard against ---- */
  #coverage .cv-copy {
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl);                             /* 24 -> 40 */
  }

}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [AW.logowall]  — from lab sec-logowall.css
   NOT A REGRESSION — this band measured 392px both before and after
   everything this session. The real fault was original: all 16 marquee
   images were capped at max-height 74.88px with object-fit:contain, so they
   rendered identical HEIGHTS but widths of 334px (Silverlake) vs 99px (AMF)
   — a 3.37x spread. Capping height on logos with different aspect ratios is
   exactly what unbalances a logo wall. Now normalised on apparent visual
   weight: measured spread narrows to 198/289/138/218 = 2.09x.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ============================================================================
   sec-logowall.css — LOGO WALL (.logo-wall / .logo-wall--strip / .logo-marquee)
   Optical-balance pass. Scoped ENTIRELY to .logo-wall; touches nothing else.

   THE DEFECT (measured on _base.html @1440x900, before this sheet):
     every marquee <img> is capped by ONE shared rule
       .logo-marquee .logo-cell img { max-height: clamp(44px,5.2vw,78px) }
     so all four render at exactly 74.88px tall and their widths fall out of
     their raw aspect ratios:
       raleys      420x172  r=2.442  ->  182.8 x 74.9   area 13,683
       silverlake  420x 94  r=4.468  ->  334.5 x 74.9   area 25,038
       amf         306x232  r=1.319  ->   98.7 x 74.9   area  7,391
       firenza     420x158  r=2.658  ->  199.0 x 74.9   area 14,896
     WIDTH SPREAD 3.39x, AREA SPREAD 3.39x. Capping HEIGHT on marks whose
     aspects run 1.3 -> 4.5 does not normalise them, it normalises the one
     dimension nobody perceives. The wide wordmark reads 3.4x the visual mass
     of the squat badge even though both are "the same size".

   THE FIX — cap AREA, not height.
     For a mark of aspect r, an equal-box-area fit is  h = sqrt(A/r).
     Expressed as a multiplier off a shared reference height, that is
       k = sqrt(r_geo / r)      r_geo = geometric mean of the four aspects
                                      = (2.442*4.468*1.319*2.658)^(1/4) = 2.487
       raleys 1.009   silverlake 0.746   amf 1.373   firenza 0.967
     Then an optical trim on top of the pure geometry, because equal area is
     not equal weight: a solid high-coverage badge reads heavier than an open
     script at the same area, and a lockup that spends part of its box on a
     tagline or a second line reads smaller than its box. Measured ink
     coverage (alpha > 32, whole canvas):
       amf 80.1%   firenza 50.6%   raleys 46.5%   silverlake 20.1%
     Trims, each eye-checked against a rendered A/B at 1440 — five weightings
     were built, screenshotted and looked at, not reasoned about:
       amf        -7%   solid hexagon, densest mark in the set
       silverlake +6%   sparsest mark, and a two-line lockup
       firenza    +3%   its box includes the "PIZZA LIKE..." tagline strip
       raleys     -2%   boldest script, was reading a touch heavy
     Geometric mean of the final k is 1.000 — the row's overall scale is
     preserved, the marks are only redistributed within it.

     HONEST COST OF THE TRIMS. amf and silverlake are the narrowest and widest
     marks, so trimming them DOES give width spread back:
       pure geometry (no trim) -> width spread 1.85x, area spread 1.02x
       final (trimmed)         -> width spread 2.09x, area spread 1.29x
     That 0.24x was spent deliberately. At pure geometry the AMF badge renders
     112.3px tall against a 61.5px Silverlake and dominates the row — the
     metric was better and the band looked worse. 2.09x is the eye-verified
     setting, and it is still a 38% reduction on the 3.39x we started from.

     Implemented as a per-logo max-height only, via a src attribute selector —
     width and height stay `auto` and object-fit stays `contain`, so NO aspect
     ratio is touched anywhere. Nothing is stretched; each mark is simply
     fitted to its own honest optical size.

   BUDGET: this band measured 392px against an 818px budget (0.48x) — it is the
   one band on the page with real vertical headroom, so the composure spent
   here costs the viewport goal nothing. See the note at the foot of this file.
   ========================================================================== */

.logo-wall--strip {
  /* the optical reference height. Everything below is a multiple of it.
     Raised off the old clamp(44px,5.2vw,78px) because the whole row was
     running small and the band has ~426px of unused budget to spend.
     The 54px floor is what keeps the two-line Silverlake lockup legible at
     375 (0.79 x 54 = 42.7px tall, vs 44px before — effectively unchanged). */
  --lw-cap: clamp(54px, 5.8vw, 82px);

  /* end-feather width, driven off vw with px bounds rather than the old flat
     7% so it neither under-feathers a wide screen nor eats a phone viewport.
     1440 -> 172.8px, 1024 -> 122.9px, 375 -> 56px (floor).
     Depth chosen by rendering three settings at 1440 AND 375 and looking:
     40px/10vw still left a hot red Raley's fragment at the left edge;
     64px/14vw ate 34% of the 375 viewport for no visible gain. */
  --lw-feather: clamp(56px, 12vw, 190px);

  /* air around the strip. Symmetric above and below so the marquee reads as
     one plate rather than a rule that drifted upward. */
  --lw-air: clamp(var(--ds-space-lg), 2.4vw, var(--ds-space-xl));
}

/* ---- 1 · per-logo optical weight -----------------------------------------
   k = sqrt(r_geo / r), then the ink-density trim described above.
   Selectors key off src so the markup stays frozen. */
.logo-wall .logo-cell img[src*="logo-raleys"]  { --lw-k: 0.99; }  /* geo 1.009  -2% bold script  */
.logo-wall .logo-cell img[src*="silverlake"]   { --lw-k: 0.79; }  /* geo 0.746  +6% sparse/2-line*/
.logo-wall .logo-cell img[src*="logo-amf"]     { --lw-k: 1.28; }  /* geo 1.373  -7% solid badge  */
.logo-wall .logo-cell img[src*="logo-firenza"] { --lw-k: 1.00; }  /* geo 0.967  +3% tagline strip*/

/* one rule, one variable — the cap is now per-mark instead of shared.
   width/height stay auto and object-fit stays contain: aspect ratios are
   never touched, so no mark is distorted. */
.logo-wall--strip .logo-cell img,
.logo-marquee .logo-cell img {
  max-height: calc(var(--lw-cap) * var(--lw-k, 1));
  width: auto;
  height: auto;
  object-fit: contain;
}
/* Only the MARQUEE gets max-width released. Its cells are flex:none/width:auto
   inside a width:max-content track, so nothing can overflow the layout and the
   height cap above is left as the single, exact constraint. The non-marquee
   .logo-strip fallback (equal-share flex cells) keeps the inherited
   max-width:100% so a wide wordmark can still never burst its cell there. */
.logo-marquee .logo-cell img { max-width: none; }

/* the row now has four different heights, so centring is what aligns them.
   (It already was `center`; restated so this sheet is self-contained and the
   alignment cannot be lost to source order.) */
.logo-marquee .logo-strip { align-items: center; }

/* ---- 2 · seamless ends ----------------------------------------------------
   The 7%/93% feather left an entering logo ~half-visible at full opacity —
   the loudest unfinished tell in the band. Deeper feather + a soft mid-stop
   so the ramp eases instead of running linearly to a hard edge. Purely a
   mask change: the tiling maths (4 copies, translateX(-25%)) is untouched. */
.logo-wall .logo-marquee {
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
      transparent 0,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, .18) calc(var(--lw-feather) * .45),
      #000 var(--lw-feather),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--lw-feather)),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, .18) calc(100% - var(--lw-feather) * .45),
      transparent 100%);
          mask-image: linear-gradient(to right,
      transparent 0,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, .18) calc(var(--lw-feather) * .45),
      #000 var(--lw-feather),
      #000 calc(100% - var(--lw-feather)),
      rgba(0, 0, 0, .18) calc(100% - var(--lw-feather) * .45),
      transparent 100%);
}

/* ---- 3 · vertical rhythm --------------------------------------------------
   Before: 24px between a ~44px display serif and the strip's top hairline, and
   16px between the bottom hairline and the caption — asymmetric, and tight
   above. Symmetric --lw-air puts equal breath on both sides of the plate. */
.logo-wall--strip .logo-marquee { margin-top: var(--lw-air); }
.logo-wall--strip .logo-strip__caption { margin-top: var(--lw-air); }

/* ============================================================================
   FIT NOTE — this sheet ADDS height to the band, deliberately.
   .logo-wall--strip has always carried its OWN padding-block,
   clamp(34px,4vw,58px); it never read --ds-section-py, so this session's
   section-py tightening did not touch it and cannot be given back here.
   The band measured 392px against an 818px budget. The taller AMF badge plus
   the symmetric air spend part of that 426px of slack. Measured after-heights
   are in the audit output; the band stays comfortably under budget at every
   breakpoint, so composure and fit do not conflict in this band.
   ========================================================================== */


