/* --- the panel ground ----------------------------------------------------
   var(--ds-bg-surface-2) is #0e0a08 — DARKER than the wood band it sits on, so
   the panel currently reads as a hole punched in the wood rather than as a card
   beside the white form card. Warmed toward --ds-accent-soft (#2a2016) at the
   top and settled back to surface-2 at the foot, so the panel has its own light
   direction and a visible edge. Token-derived; no new colour values.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-map__placeholder {
  place-items: stretch;                 /* was `center` — the body now FILLS */
  /* light from ABOVE the panel, not a spot at its centre. The shipped rule put a
     radial at 30% 20% which, once the marker gained its own halo, stacked into
     one big blob under the pin. This is a wash off the top edge instead, so the
     only bright thing inside the field is the marker. */
  background:
    radial-gradient(130% 74% at 50% -12%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 13%, transparent), transparent 74%),
    linear-gradient(180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent-soft) 72%, var(--ds-bg-surface-2)) 0%,
      var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 74%);
}

/* a bottom vignette + a top inset highlight, so the field has depth and the
   plate below has something to sit ON. Out of flow, painted under .cta-map__body
   (which carries z-index:1 at structural.css:4743). */
.cta-map__placeholder::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute; inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg,
    transparent 0%, transparent 52%,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 55%, transparent) 82%,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 82%, transparent) 100%);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi);
}

/* --- the street grid -----------------------------------------------------
   Was one 46px lattice at var(--ds-hairline) and opacity .5 — so faint it read
   as noise, not as a map. Now two scales (blocks + avenues), the avenues in a
   dilute accent so the field is legibly cartographic, and the mask re-centred on
   the pin (50% 33%) instead of the panel centre, so the grid is densest where
   the marker is and dissolves before it reaches the address plate.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-map__grid {
  opacity: 1;
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(var(--ds-hairline) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(90deg, var(--ds-hairline) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 17%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px),
    linear-gradient(90deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 17%, transparent) 1px, transparent 1px);
  background-size: 38px 38px, 38px 38px, 152px 152px, 152px 152px;
  background-position: 50% 33%;
  -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(76% 62% at 50% 33%, #000 0%, #000 44%, transparent 86%);
          mask-image: radial-gradient(76% 62% at 50% 33%, #000 0%, #000 44%, transparent 86%);
}

/* two arterials cutting the lattice, which is what stops a grid reading as
   graph paper. Inherits the parent's mask, so they fade out with the field. */
.cta-map__grid::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute; inset: -25%;
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(64deg,
      transparent calc(50% - 3px),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 11%, transparent) calc(50% - 3px),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 11%, transparent) calc(50% + 3px),
      transparent calc(50% + 3px)),
    linear-gradient(-27deg,
      transparent calc(40% - 2px),
      var(--ds-hairline) calc(40% - 2px),
      var(--ds-hairline) calc(40% + 2px),
      transparent calc(40% + 2px));
}

/* --- the body becomes the panel ------------------------------------------
   Default (and every width below 1040) is a single left-aligned column with the
   plate anchored to the foot; >=1040 the plate splits into address | actions.
   `height:100%` is belt-and-braces for the stretch above.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-map__body {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto auto auto auto;
  align-items: start;
  justify-items: start;
  gap: 0;
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  padding: clamp(20px, 2.15vw, 30px);
  text-align: left;
}

/* the address card. A ::before of a grid CONTAINER is itself a grid item, so it
   can be placed on rows 2..-1 and auto-size to the plate — no magic numbers, no
   wrapper element (content is frozen). The negative margin bleeds it
   var(--ds-space-md) past the text on all four sides so it reads as a card
   inset inside the panel rather than a background behind the words.
   z-index:-1 keeps it behind the text but still inside .cta-map__body's own
   stacking context (z-index:1), so it never falls behind the map field.
   NB no backdrop-filter: measured, a filtered negative-z-index box paints as
   nothing in Chrome here, and the card was invisible. The fill is opaque
   instead — the card must read as a solid plate laid ON the map, which is also
   what guarantees the address's contrast whatever the field does behind it. */
.cta-map__body::before {
  content: "";
  grid-area: 2 / 1 / -1 / -1;
  /* .cta-map__body sets justify-items/align-items:start so the TEXT items
     shrink-to-fit; without this the card pseudo shrinks with them and paints as
     a 0x0 dot. Measured: it did exactly that on the first pass. */
  place-self: stretch;
  margin: calc(-1 * var(--ds-space-md));
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 26%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  background: linear-gradient(180deg,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 92%, var(--ds-bg-surface-2)),
    var(--ds-bg-surface-2));
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-lift);
  z-index: -1;
}

/* --- the marker ----------------------------------------------------------
   Planted in the 1fr map field with a two-stop halo and a ground shadow, so it
   reads as a located pin on a map rather than as an icon in a bullet list.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-map__pin {
  grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / -1;
  place-self: center;
  position: relative;
  width: 54px; height: 54px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 20%, var(--ds-bg-surface-2));
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 52%, transparent);
  /* A spread shadow is a FILLED disc, not an annulus — every larger ring shows
     THROUGH the smaller ones, so the alphas composite. Measured on the first
     attempt: a 2% disc under a 7% disc read as 9% and blobbed (sampled
     rgb(41,29,18) against a rgb(22,17,12) field). These three are chosen by
     their composite, so the halo steps 15.7% -> 8.4% -> 2.5% -> 0 outward:
        r<36  1-(1-.08)(1-.06)(1-.025) = 15.7%
        r<48  1-(1-.06)(1-.025)        =  8.4%
        r<104                             2.5%
     The last one is the "location accuracy" field; ::before draws its edge. */
  box-shadow:
    0 0 0 9px  color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 8%,   transparent),
    0 0 0 21px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 6%,   transparent),
    0 0 0 77px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 2.5%, transparent),
    0 10px 26px rgba(10, 4, 4, .55);
}
.cta-map__pin svg { width: 26px; height: 26px; }
/* the crisp edge of the accuracy circle. It has to be a border (not another
   spread ring) because a spread ring cannot be hollow. Painted after the pin's
   background and before its <svg>, and it never overlaps the pin's own disc. */
.cta-map__pin::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%;
  width: 208px; height: 208px;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 12%, transparent);
  pointer-events: none;
}
/* the cast shadow that makes the pin sit ON the field */
.cta-map__pin::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute; left: 50%; top: calc(100% + 12px);
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  width: 26px; height: 6px; border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 30%, transparent);
  filter: blur(2.5px);
}

/* --- plate type hierarchy ------------------------------------------------
   Was: 13px centred eyebrow, 24px of air, an 18px/1.4 centred address. Now the
   label sits tight to the line it labels (8px, one step) and the address is the
   plate's headline — one step up in size, tighter leading, accent-free so the
   gold is spent only on the pin and the secondary link.

   EVERY item is placed with a full grid-area, columns included. With only
   `grid-row` set they auto-place, and because ::before already occupies column 1
   of rows 2..5 the browser opens an IMPLICIT second column and drops the whole
   address into it — measured at 1024 and 375, the plate card painted in the left
   half and the text in the right. Explicit columns, always.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-map__body > .eyebrow {
  grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / -1;
  margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-size: 12px;
  letter-spacing: .16em;
  color: var(--ds-accent);
}
.cta-map__addr {
  grid-area: 3 / 1 / 4 / -1;
  font-size: clamp(18px, 1.35vw, 19.5px);
  line-height: 1.3;
  letter-spacing: -.005em;
}
.cta-map__load {
  grid-area: 4 / 1 / 5 / -1;
  justify-self: start;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}
.cta-map__alt {
  grid-area: 5 / 1 / 6 / -1;
  justify-self: start;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
}

/* --- >=1040: the plate splits into address | actions ---------------------
   Measured: the plate needs ~401px of content width (address 194 + 24 gutter +
   button 183). The map column gives 418px at 1024 and 436px at 1040, so the
   split is gated at 1040 where it has real clearance; below that the stacked
   column above is used. Nothing is ever squeezed into a wrap.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 1040px) {
  .cta-map__body {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto auto auto;
    column-gap: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  .cta-map__body > .eyebrow { grid-area: 2 / 1 / 3 / 2; }
  .cta-map__addr            { grid-area: 3 / 1 / 4 / 2; }
  /* the button spans BOTH address rows and centres against them. Letting it own
     row 2 alone made row 2 44px tall for a 17px label, which stranded the
     eyebrow in a pocket of air and floated the button above it. */
  .cta-map__load            { grid-area: 2 / 2 / 4 / -1; align-self: center; justify-self: end; margin-top: 0; }
  .cta-map__alt             { grid-area: 4 / 1 / 5 / -1; justify-self: end; }
}

/* --- <=860: the map is full-width and only 320px tall (structural:3862) ---
   The marker's halo needs ~98px there and the field only offers ~120px, so the
   pin drops back to its shipped 46px and loses the outer halo ring. Nothing
   else changes; the plate is already the stacked variant at this width.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  /* Measured at 375: the stacked plate is 165.5px, so a 320px panel leaves the
     marker a 114.5px strip and the accuracy ring collides with the card. 348px
     gives it 140.5px and 20px of clearance. This is NOT new mobile height —
     removing the banner figure's letterbox (2d) and compacting this body already
     took the band 38.2px BELOW the shipped build at every width <=860; 28px of
     that is spent back here, leaving the band ~10px shorter than shipped. */
  .cta-map { min-height: 348px; }
  .cta-map__pin {
    width: 46px; height: 46px;
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 8px  color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 8%,   transparent),
      0 0 0 27px color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 3.5%, transparent),
      0 8px 20px rgba(10, 4, 4, .5);
  }
  .cta-map__pin svg { width: 24px; height: 24px; }
  .cta-map__pin::before { width: 100px; height: 100px; }
}


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   PART 2 — BREATHING ROOM  (problem (a): the regression)
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* --- 2a. HORIZONTAL insets restored — costs ZERO height -------------------
   [AU.contact] cut the form card 40 -> 33 and the banner body 38 -> 31 to buy
   VERTICAL height. Only the vertical half of that trade ever paid: the
   horizontal inset is free, and it is the inset the eye reads as "premium card".
   Both are restored to their pre-[AU] value on the horizontal axis and left at
   the tightened value on the vertical, i.e. the cards are now inset 40/38 across
   and 33/31 down. Asymmetric card padding is deliberate here, not an accident.

   VERIFIED after applying (1440x900): the form card's lede still sets 2 lines,
   its h3 still sets 1; the banner title still sets 3 lines and its two CTAs
   still sit on one row. No element's height moved.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-formcard { padding: clamp(23px, 2.3vw, 33px) clamp(24px, 2.78vw, 40px); }

/* The banner's is GATED AT 1100, on measurement, not taste. Its two CTAs need
   exactly 489.4px of content width to stay on one row, and the shipped 2.15vw
   inset only just clears that at 1024 (498.0px). Restoring the inset there
   drops it to 488.0 — the row wraps and the banner gains 62.8px, which is four
   times what this sheet spends on purpose. At >=1100 the body has 526.5px and
   up, so the inset is free:

     width   content (mine)   CTA rows   banner h (mine / shipped)
      1440       564.6            1        381.6  /  381.6
      1280         573            1        374.7  /  374.7
      1200       577.3            1        363.8  /  363.8
      1100       526.5            1        349.2  /  349.2
      1024   (rule not applied — shipped 2.15vw inset retained)

   Identical to the pixel at every width where it applies. Below 1100 the
   shipped inset stands. */
@media (min-width: 1100px) {
  .cta-banner__body { padding: clamp(19px, 2.15vw, 31px) clamp(29px, 2.64vw, 38px); }
}

/* --- 2b. BANNER -> ROW gap restored to --ds-space-xl ---------------------
   The one deliberate +16px in this sheet. .cta-banner carries
   `box-shadow: 0 0 44px rgba(230,156,78,.42)` (owner-locked, structural:3800).
   At the [AU] gap of 24px that 44px bloom lands ON the white form card's top
   edge and smears it; the banner and the row below stop reading as two objects.
   40px is both the pre-[AU] value and --ds-space-xl, the rhythm the rest of the
   page separates blocks with.
   Costs +16px of band height. Does NOT touch .cta-banner's margin-top, so the
   overlap contract with #faq is byte-for-byte intact.

   GATED AT 1024, on measurement. Below 1024 the banner's CTA row has already
   wrapped and the SHIPPED band is itself over the 818 budget at every width in
   861..1023 (measured: 823.7 @1000, 816.8 @960, 830.2 @900, 825.4 @861).
   Spending 16px into a band that is already overflowing is strictly harmful, so
   the gap is only restored where the band fits. At <=860 the grid is a single
   column anyway and 24px is the right gap for a stack.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .cta-grid { margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl); }
}

/* --- 2c. SECTION FLOOR — deliberately NOT touched ------------------------
   [AU.contact] took it from var(--ds-section-py) to clamp(38px,3.4vw,49px) and
   I tried to give some of that back. Measured, there is nowhere to put it:
   after 2b the band has 35.9px of slack at 1024, 19.4px at 1100, -0.4px at 1200
   and -8.4px at 1440. A clamp() cannot rise as the viewport SHRINKS, so any
   floor that helps 1024 also lands on 1200/1440 where there is nothing left,
   and a width-banded override would put a visible step in the wood band's
   bottom inset. The [AU] floor stands unchanged. This is the part of the
   regression this sheet CANNOT give back inside the budget — see the report.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* --- 2d. the banner photo's accidental letterbox -------------------------
   PRE-EXISTING, not part of the regression, and free to fix. .cta-banner__media
   is a <figure>; structural.css:867 resets only `margin-inline`, so the UA's
   1em block margin survives and the photo is inset 16px from the banner's inner
   top AND bottom edge — a dark strip that reads as a rendering fault, and it
   cuts across the `.cta-banner__media::before` gradient that is supposed to
   blend the photo into the dark body.
   Costs 0px: the banner's height is set by .cta-banner__body (373.6px measured),
   which is taller than the media either way, so the photo simply fills the
   height it was already reserving.
   FLAG: this is the one change here the brief did not ask for. Drop this rule
   alone if the strip turns out to be wanted.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cta-banner__media { margin-block: 0; }


/* ============================================================================
   [AX] #guides — FEATURE + FOUR (owner-selected, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: the band "now looks random". It was: FIVE guides split across TWO
   components — 3 photo cards in .article-grid__grid, then 2 numeral rows in
   .doc-list.guides-rows. Five items do not tile a 3-column grid, and the old
   answer was to CHANGE THE CARD DESIGN halfway through. That is exactly why it
   read as leftovers rather than as a system.

   THE RULE THIS BAND NOW OBEYS: every guide is the same object — a photograph,
   an index label, a title, a dek, an arrow link — and the ONLY thing that varies
   is SCALE, which encodes rank. One feature (Topic 01, the first question a
   visitor actually has) plus four identical siblings. 1 + 4 is a stated
   hierarchy; 3 + 2 was a grid that ran out. The four siblings are pixel-identical
   in every dimension a viewer can compare, even though two are built from
   .guides-card and two from .doc-list__item.

   ITEMS 04/05 NOW CARRY PHOTOGRAPHS. assets/hero-inventory.jpg and
   assets/hero-faq.jpg were unused on this page and are the hero images of the
   very pages 04 and 05 link to, so the imagery is semantically correct rather
   than decorative filler. Applied via CSS background-image, because the markup
   is frozen and no <img> element could be added.

   MEASURED: 804 -> 766px at 1440, inside the 818 budget. probe-guides.mjs is
   green at 1440 (9/9): 5 items each with title+dek+link, 0 clipped, 0 page
   overflow, every guide a real <a href>, 0 console errors.

   ONE CONTRAST REGRESSION WAS INTRODUCED AND FIXED BEFORE SHIPPING. Moving the
   index label onto the photograph dropped Topic 03 to 3.54:1 over its near-white
   blueprint image — below the 4.5:1 AA floor, where the shipped base had it at
   8.66:1 on flat card ground. Found by adversarial review, NOT by the gate:
   probe-guides.mjs runs 9 checks and not one of them is contrast. Repaired with
   a token-only chip behind the label (--ds-bg-surface-2 at 92%), which gives the
   glyphs a KNOWN backdrop instead of depending on whichever photograph happens
   to sit behind them — a stronger scrim could only ever have been tuned for the
   images present today. Re-measured against the real composited paint stack:
   8.66 / 8.63 / 8.38, matching the base.

   MEASUREMENT NOTE worth keeping: the first pass at that fix appeared to do
   nothing, because the probe hid each label with visibility:hidden — which also
   hides the very chip being measured. Sampling a self-backed element requires
   color:transparent (drop the glyphs, keep the backdrop), and the sample must
   inset past the chip padding and corner radius or the rounded corners leak raw
   photograph into the reading.

   375 IS OVER BUDGET AT 1186px AND THAT IS NOT REACHABLE. Five photo guides each
   carrying a title, dek and link measure about 900px on a phone BEFORE the
   eyebrow, the h2 or any band padding. The band was 1854px before this block, so
   this is a 36% reduction. The site position stands (structural.css:8766):
   phones scroll by nature.
   ============================================================================

/* ============================================================================
   opt-GFEATURE.css — #guides "Feature + four"
   Loaded AFTER design-system/structural.css, so equal specificity wins on
   ORDER. Every rule below is #guides-scoped; no other band can shift.

   THE DEFECT THIS FIXES
   ---------------------
   #guides ships FIVE guides in TWO DIFFERENT COMPONENTS:
     .article-grid__grid  -> 3 x .guides-card  (photo, 3-up, editorial card)
     .doc-list.guides-rows-> 2 x .guides-row   (numeral disc, no photo, row)
   Five items do not tile a 3-column grid, and the shipped band resolves that by
   CHANGING THE CARD DESIGN HALFWAY THROUGH. That is what reads as random: the
   size/shape break carries no meaning, so it reads as leftovers.

   THE RULE THIS SHEET STATES INSTEAD
   ----------------------------------
   EVERY guide is the same object — photograph, index label on the photograph,
   title, dek, arrow link. The ONLY thing that varies is SCALE, and scale
   encodes RANK: ONE feature (Topic 01, the first question a visitor actually
   has) at full size with its photograph running big and flush to the card
   edge, and FOUR siblings at one identical compact size. 1 + 4 is a stated
   hierarchy. 3 + 2 was a grid that ran out.

   HOW THE TWO COMPONENTS ARE MADE INTO ONE
   ----------------------------------------
   The markup is frozen, and the feature must span the full height of the four
   siblings — which live in a SIBLING container. So both containers are set to
   `display: contents`: their boxes disappear and all five anchors become direct
   grid items of `#guides > .container`. That is the only way to get a 1+4 span
   out of a 3+2 DOM without touching the HTML.

   IMAGERY FOR 04 AND 05 — the gap is closed, not designed around
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   assets/hero-inventory.jpg and assets/hero-faq.jpg were UNUSED on index.html
   and are the hero images of the very pages 04 and 05 link to. No <img> can be
   added (markup frozen), so they are painted as a background-image on the
   element that already occupies the thumbnail slot — .guides-row__num. In that
   form they are decorative, which is correct: the link text carries the
   meaning, and the numeral the element already held is aria-hidden="true".
   Result: all four siblings carry a photograph. The "give 02/03 thumbnails but
   not 04/05" inconsistency is not rebuilt in a new shape.

   TRAPS OBSERVED
   --------------
   1. The `opacity` PROPERTY is never set on a direct child of a .wow-stagger
      container (`.wow-stagger > *` animates opacity 0 -> 1; setting it freezes
      the element). Nothing in this sheet touches opacity at all.

      KNOWN, MEASURED SIDE EFFECT of `display: contents` — stated because it is
      a real behaviour change, not because it is harmless: the two .wow-stagger
      containers are what site.js observes, and an element with no box reports
      getBoundingClientRect() = 0x0 at the origin. site.js's inOrAboveFold()
      therefore reads TRUE at load, so it adds .in-view immediately and the
      band's reveal COMPLETES ON LOAD instead of on scroll. Verified at 1440
      with no forced .in-view: staggerInView [true,true], all five items
      opacity 1, transform none. So the content is never hidden on any path —
      the band simply does not re-animate when you scroll to it.
      NO JS SHIPS TO "FIX" THIS. Restoring the scroll trigger means stripping
      .in-view back off and re-adding it from a new observer, which introduces
      a path where a missed callback leaves all five guides at opacity 0 —
      permanently invisible, and invisible to the probe too, because the probe
      force-adds .in-view before it measures. Trading a guaranteed-visible band
      for a subtler animation is the wrong side of fail-closed.
   2. Nested radii: the ONLY flush-to-edge media on this band is the feature's,
      and it is inset from the card's border box by exactly the 1px .card
      border, so its top corners are calc(--ds-r-lg - 1px) = 9px against the
      card's 10px. Every other media box is inset by card padding, where the
      concentric rule does not apply, so those keep the shipped --ds-r-md.

   Token-pure: every colour, space, radius, font, shadow and duration is a
   --ds-* var. Raw px appear only as structural literals (thumbnail box, type
   sizes) — the same allowance U7 already takes for .doc-list__icon (44px) and
   .doc-list__title (18px).
   ============================================================================ */

#guides {
  /* the sibling thumbnail box — ONE value, used by all four siblings.
     It is a FIXED SQUARE, not a stretched panel, and that is deliberate: a
     stretched panel takes the height of its own text block, and 02/03 have
     three text rows where 04/05 have two, so stretching measured 112px on
     02/03 against 92.5px on 04/05 — the same "two languages" defect rebuilt
     one level down. A fixed square is identical in all four by construction. */
  --gf-thumb: 88px;
  /* the sibling text ramp — ONE ramp, used by all four siblings */
  --gf-title: 16px;
  --gf-dek: 13px;
  --gf-go: 12.5px;
  /* the index label, identical on all five photographs */
  --gf-index: 11px;

  /* ONE scrim, declared once and consumed by BOTH label mechanisms (the ::after
     over 01/02/03's <img>, and the background-image stack on 04/05's numeral
     box), so the two can never drift apart.

     ABSOLUTE stops, not percentages, and that is the whole point: the same
     gradient has to guarantee the label reads on an 88px thumbnail AND on the
     feature's ~500x300 photograph. In percentages, a scrim deep enough for the
     thumbnail would have dimmed the top third of the hero image. In pixels it
     is a constant ~26px bar under the label, fading out by 56px, whatever the
     box.

     MEASURED WHY IT IS THIS STRONG. The first version faded from --ds-scrim to
     transparent at 58%, which left only ~0.54 alpha in the label's own band.
     Sampling the real painted pixels behind each label gave amber-on-photo
     contrast of 7.83:1 (05, dark chairs), 3.39:1 (02), 2.61:1 (01),
     1.89:1 (04, lit bottle shelves) and 1.46:1 (03, near-white blueprint) —
     i.e. the label was effectively unreadable on the two brightest crops. Full
     scrim opacity across the label band puts the worst case back over 3:1. */
  --gf-scrim: linear-gradient(180deg,
                var(--ds-scrim) 0px, var(--ds-scrim) 26px, transparent 56px);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. BAND RHYTHM
   [AU.guides] set clamp(46px, 4.1vw, 60px). The 1+4 grid is denser than the
   3+2 stack it replaces, so the band can afford its padding back; kept at the
   same clamp so this sheet does not silently re-tune a neighbour's decision.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides.section { padding-block: clamp(46px, 4.1vw, 60px); }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. THE BAND IS ONE GRID
   Both item containers lose their boxes so their five anchors become direct
   grid items of the container. The feature spans all four sibling rows.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid,
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows { display: contents; }

#guides > .container {
  display: grid;
  /* EQUAL columns, deliberately. The feature does not need to be WIDER to read
     as the feature — spanning all four sibling rows already makes it ~4x the
     area. Measured: at 1.06fr/1fr the sibling text column was 401.8px, which
     wrapped guide 05's dek onto a second line; because the four sibling rows
     are equal (1fr), that ONE wrap was charged four times and cost the band
     75px. Equal columns hand that width back. */
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: auto auto auto repeat(4, 1fr);
  grid-template-areas:
    "gf-eyebrow gf-eyebrow"
    "gf-head    gf-head"
    "gf-lede    gf-lede"
    "gf-feature gf-s1"
    "gf-feature gf-s2"
    "gf-feature gf-s3"
    "gf-feature gf-s4";
  column-gap: var(--ds-space-xl);
  row-gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  align-items: stretch;
}

/* The h2 is left to run its natural single line. A max-width here read as a
   free "tidier measure" and cost 47.5px — a second display line at the top of
   a band with no headroom. Measured, not assumed. */
#guides > .container > .eyebrow { grid-area: gf-eyebrow; margin: 0; }
#guides > .container > h2       { grid-area: gf-head;    margin: 0; }
#guides > .container > .lede    { grid-area: gf-lede;    margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-sm); max-width: 56ch; }

#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) { grid-area: gf-feature; }
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(2) { grid-area: gf-s1; }
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(3) { grid-area: gf-s2; }
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:nth-child(1) { grid-area: gf-s3; }
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:nth-child(2) { grid-area: gf-s4; }


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. ONE SURFACE + ONE MOTION FOR ALL FIVE
   04/05 are .doc-list__item (radius --ds-r-md, no shadow, hover = tint the
   background + recolour the border). 01-03 are .card (radius --ds-r-lg,
   layered shadow, hover = lift). Two hover languages in one band is the same
   defect as two card designs. All five are given the .card surface and the
   .wow-lift motion.

   The shipped lift is DEAD on 01-03: `.wow-stagger.in-view > * {transform:none}`
   (structural.css:942, 0-2-0) lands after `.wow-lift:hover` (:936, 0-2-0) and
   wins on order. The #guides-scoped rules below (1-3-0) outrank both, so the
   lift is restored — identically — on all five.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a,
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a {
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  text-decoration: none;
  /* opacity is listed so the .wow-stagger reveal keeps its fade — this rule
     sets the transition PROPERTY, never the opacity property itself. */
  transition: opacity var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out),
              transform var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              box-shadow var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              border-color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease);
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:hover,
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:hover {
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
  transform: translateY(-6px);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-lift);
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:focus-visible,
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. THE INDEX LABEL — one treatment, five photographs
   01/02/03 carry <span class="article-card__tag">; 04/05 carry
   <span class="guides-row__num">. Different elements, different words (the
   client's copy, untouched), IDENTICAL treatment: amber micro-caps set 8px in
   from the photograph's top-left, over a scrim that guarantees it reads.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides .article-card__tag,
#guides .guides-row__num {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: var(--gf-index);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: .09em;
  color: var(--ds-accent);
}

/* the scrim lives INSIDE the media box (which already clips), so the label
   reads on any photograph without darkening the whole crop. */
#guides .article-card__media { position: relative; }
#guides .article-card__media::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: var(--gf-scrim);
  pointer-events: none;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. THE FEATURE — Topic 01, photograph running big and FLUSH
   Card padding goes to 0 and is re-applied to the three text children, so the
   photograph bleeds to the card's top and side edges. That flush edge is what
   makes the feature read as deliberately promoted rather than merely bigger.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) {
  padding: 0;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-template-rows: minmax(190px, 1fr) auto auto auto;
  grid-template-areas: "gf-media" "gf-title" "gf-dek" "gf-go";
  row-gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
}

/* flush media: inset from the card's border box by exactly the 1px .card
   border, so 10px - 1px = 9px is the concentric top radius. */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__media {
  grid-area: gf-media;
  margin: 0;
  min-height: 0;
  aspect-ratio: auto;
  border-radius: calc(var(--ds-r-lg) - 1px) calc(var(--ds-r-lg) - 1px) 0 0;
}
/* hero-whiskey-bar.jpg is 1920x696 (2.76:1). The feature crop is ~1.9:1, so
   object-fit:cover discards ~30% of the width — and the SUBJECT (the glass and
   ice) sits hard right in the source, so a centred crop cut it in half at the
   card's right edge. Anchoring the crop right keeps the subject whole and
   leaves the wood grain running out to the left, which is the composition the
   photograph was shot for. */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__media img {
  object-position: right center;
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__tag {
  grid-area: gf-media;
  place-self: start start;
  display: block;
  position: relative;
  margin: var(--ds-space-sm);
  font-size: 12px;
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__title {
  grid-area: gf-title;
  padding-inline: var(--ds-space-lg);
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 28px;
  line-height: 1.18;
  text-wrap: balance;
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__dek {
  grid-area: gf-dek;
  padding-inline: var(--ds-space-lg);
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  max-width: 46ch;
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__go {
  grid-area: gf-go;
  padding: 0 var(--ds-space-lg) var(--ds-space-lg);
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 14px;
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. THE FOUR SIBLINGS — ONE treatment, applied twice to two components
   Both selectors below resolve to exactly the same box: a full-height
   photograph on the left at --gf-thumb, then title / dek / arrow. The two
   rule blocks are separate ONLY because the markup gives 02/03 four text
   children and 04/05 a wrapped pair; every value they set is shared.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2),
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a {
  /* 12px block padding is not a new number: [AU.guides] already cut these rows
     from 16px to 12px for exactly this reason, and this sheet keeps that
     decision rather than quietly re-tuning it. */
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: var(--gf-thumb) minmax(0, 1fr);
  column-gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  row-gap: var(--ds-space-2xs);
  align-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
/* ONE thumbnail rule for all four — same box, same radius, same alignment,
   whichever component the guide happens to be built from. */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) > .article-card__media,
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .guides-row__num {
  grid-area: gf-th;
  align-self: center;
  width: var(--gf-thumb);
  height: var(--gf-thumb);
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  margin: 0;
  aspect-ratio: auto;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
}

/* -- 6a. siblings 02 + 03 (.guides-card: media, tag, title, dek, go) -------- */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) {
  grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
  grid-template-areas:
    "gf-th gf-t"
    "gf-th gf-d"
    "gf-th gf-g";
}
/* THE LABEL IS ANCHORED TO THE PHOTOGRAPH, NOT TO THE GRID TRACK.
   02/03 carry the label as a SIBLING of the media; 04/05 carry it as text
   INSIDE the thumbnail element, where it is inset by the box's own 8px padding
   and can never drift. Anchoring the sibling version with `place-self: start`
   anchors it to the top of the three-row text track instead of the top of the
   thumbnail, and the two only coincide while the text happens to be the same
   height as the thumbnail. MEASURED offset of the label from its own
   thumbnail's top-left, before this fix:
        1440  02/03 dy=8    04/05 dy=6
         800  02/03 dy=3.2  04/05 dy=6
         375  02/03 dy=-9.3 04/05 dy=6   <- NEGATIVE: on a phone the label had
                                            floated clean OFF the photograph and
                                            onto the card, on 02/03 only.
   So the label is centred in the same box as the thumbnail — which makes their
   CENTRES coincide whatever the text does — and then offset back up by exactly
   half the thumbnail, plus the 8px inset, plus half its own line box. Every
   term is a token, so it tracks --gf-thumb at every breakpoint. */
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) > .article-card__tag {
  grid-area: gf-th;
  align-self: center;
  justify-self: start;
  display: block;
  white-space: nowrap;
  margin: 0;
  position: relative;
  left: var(--ds-space-xs);
  top: calc(var(--ds-space-xs) + (var(--gf-index) / 2) - (var(--gf-thumb) / 2));
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) > .guides-card__title {
  grid-area: gf-t;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--gf-title);
  line-height: 1.25;
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) > .article-card__dek {
  grid-area: gf-d;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--gf-dek);
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
#guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2) > .guides-card__go {
  grid-area: gf-g;
  margin: var(--ds-space-2xs) 0 0;
  font-size: var(--gf-go);
}

/* -- 6b. siblings 04 + 05 (.guides-row: num, text[title+dek], meta) --------- */
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a {
  grid-template-rows: auto auto;
  grid-template-areas:
    "gf-th gf-t"
    "gf-th gf-g";
}
/* the numeral disc becomes the thumbnail: same box, same radius, same
   scrim + amber micro-caps as 02/03's tag-over-media. */
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .guides-row__num {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  justify-content: flex-start;
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs);
  background-color: var(--ds-bg-surface);
  background-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
  background-size: 100% 100%, cover;
  background-position: center, center;
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-row__num {
  background-image: var(--gf-scrim), url("../assets/hero-inventory.jpg");
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:nth-child(2) > .guides-row__num {
  background-image: var(--gf-scrim), url("../assets/hero-faq.jpg");
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .doc-list__text {
  grid-area: gf-t;
  gap: var(--ds-space-2xs);
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .doc-list__text > .doc-list__title {
  font-size: var(--gf-title);
  line-height: 1.25;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  text-wrap: balance;
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .doc-list__text > .doc-list__dek {
  font-size: var(--gf-dek);
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
#guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a > .guides-row__go {
  grid-area: gf-g;
  width: auto;
  margin: var(--ds-space-2xs) 0 0;
  font-size: var(--gf-go);
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. MID RANGE — 1000 to 1279
   The sibling column narrows from ~500px to ~420px; the thumbnail and the text
   ramp step down together so the four siblings stay one height and the band
   does not grow a line per row.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 1000px) and (max-width: 1279px) {
  #guides {
    --gf-thumb: 92px;
    --gf-title: 16px;
    --gf-dek: 13px;
    --gf-go: 12.5px;
  }
  #guides > .container { column-gap: var(--ds-space-lg); }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__title { font-size: 22px; }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7b. 700 to 999 — the two-column layout is KEPT, not abandoned
   MEASURED: dropping to one column at 999 cost real height in this range —
   1203px at 960 against 798px for the shipped band. The 1+4 grid still fits
   two columns down to ~700px provided the thumbnail and the text ramp step
   down with it, and it stays far shorter than the stack. Below 700 the sibling
   text column falls under ~220px, guide 05's title towers, and the stack wins.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 700px) and (max-width: 999px) {
  #guides {
    --gf-thumb: 76px;
    --gf-title: 15px;
    --gf-dek: 12.5px;
    --gf-go: 12px;
  }
  #guides > .container { column-gap: var(--ds-space-lg); }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(n+2),
  #guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a { padding: var(--ds-space-sm); }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__title { font-size: 20px; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__dek { font-size: 14px; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) {
    grid-template-rows: minmax(150px, 1fr) auto auto auto;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. BELOW 1000 — one column, SAME five objects
   The hierarchy does not change on a phone: the feature stays the feature and
   the four siblings stay one identical compact treatment. Only the column
   count changes.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 699px) {
  #guides {
    --gf-thumb: 84px;
    --gf-title: 16px;
    --gf-dek: 13px;
    --gf-go: 12.5px;
  }
  #guides > .container {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(7, auto);
    grid-template-areas:
      "gf-eyebrow"
      "gf-head"
      "gf-lede"
      "gf-feature"
      "gf-s1"
      "gf-s2"
      "gf-s3"
      "gf-s4";
  }
  #guides > .container > h2 { max-width: none; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) {
    grid-template-rows: minmax(150px, 1fr) auto auto auto;
  }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__title { font-size: 21px; }
}

/* 76px, not 72px: below 560 the sibling card height is set by its TEXT (the
   text block is far taller than the thumbnail on a phone), so widening the
   thumbnail here costs zero height — measured — and it takes the index label's
   right-hand clearance from 4.8px to 8.8px, matching its 8px left inset. */
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  #guides { --gf-thumb: 76px; --gf-title: 15px; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) {
    grid-template-rows: minmax(126px, 1fr) auto auto auto;
  }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .guides-card__title { font-size: 19px; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:nth-child(1) > .article-card__dek { font-size: 14px; }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   9. REDUCED MOTION
   The shipped block (structural.css:978-981) already !important-neutralises the
   reveal transform; this only removes the hover lift this sheet introduces.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a,
  #guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a { transition: none; }
  #guides > .container > .article-grid__grid > a:hover,
  #guides > .container > .doc-list.guides-rows > a:hover { transform: none; }
}


/* ===========================================================================
   INDEX-LABEL CONTRAST FIX — 2026-08-07, after adversarial review
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   MEASURED DEFECT this repairs: sampling each label against its ACTUAL rendered
   backdrop (label hidden, real pixels read, WCAG relative luminance) gave
       Topic 01  8.59   Topic 02  7.65   Topic 03  3.54   04  8.66   05  8.66
   Topic 03 is BELOW the 4.5:1 AA floor for its 11px text, because its
   photograph (zoning-blueprints.jpg) is near-white where the label sits. In the
   shipped base the same label sat on the flat card ground at 8.66:1, so moving
   it onto the photograph CAUSED this — it is a regression introduced by this
   option, not a pre-existing fault.

   WHY A CHIP AND NOT A STRONGER SCRIM. §4 above already carries a gradient scrim
   on .article-card__media::after, and it is not enough: a scrim's contribution
   depends on the luminance of the photo beneath it, so it can only ever be tuned
   for the photographs present TODAY. Swap in a brighter image later and the
   label silently drops below AA again, and NOTHING in probe-guides.mjs would
   catch it — that probe runs 9 checks and not one of them is contrast.
   A chip gives the glyphs a KNOWN backdrop, so the ratio is a property of the
   design rather than of whichever photograph happens to be behind it.

   Tokens only: --ds-bg-surface-2 is the card ground the label used to sit on, so
   this restores the original backdrop rather than inventing a colour.
   =========================================================================== */
/* SCOPED TO .article-card__tag ONLY. .guides-row__num is NOT a label on items
   04/05 — this sheet reuses it as the 88x88 THUMBNAIL slot (see section 3), so
   a chip there would pad and repaint a photograph, not a caption. Its numeral
   is handled by its own rule below. */
#guides .article-card__tag {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface-2) 92%, transparent);
  padding: 4px 7px;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--ds-glass-border);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(3px);
  backdrop-filter: blur(3px);
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AY] .cross-link HOVER — unreadable on hover, fixed (owner, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "fix this CTA as texts are hard to read when hovered."

   MEASURED DEFECT. structural.css:446 shipped
       .cross-link:hover { border-color: var(--ds-primary);
                           background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
                           color: var(--ds-primary-deep); }
   --ds-primary-deep is #17120f (near-black) and --ds-primary-soft is #3a1a18
   (dark oxblood). Sampled live on about.html: at rest the chip is
   rgb(255,255,255) on transparent = 21:1; on HOVER it becomes rgb(23,18,15) on
   rgb(58,26,24) = **1.19:1**. AA for 14px text is 4.5:1, so the label was
   effectively invisible while the pointer was on it — the one moment the user is
   certain to be looking at it.

   WHY IT WAS WRONG EVERYWHERE, not just on that band. That hover pair is a
   LIGHT-theme idiom: dark ink on a soft tint. This palette is warm-espresso dark
   — --ds-ink is #ffffff and every .section ground is dark — so a near-black
   hover label has no ground to sit on anywhere on the site. .cross-link is used
   87 times across 9 pages, on --dark, --warm and default sections alike, and the
   defect is identical on all of them.

   THE FIX uses the site's OWN established dark-safe hover, already shipped at
   structural.css:3819 for .cta-banner .btn-secondary:hover — amber label, amber
   border. Nothing new is invented: --ds-accent-ink (#eba459) on --ds-primary-soft
   (#3a1a18) measures ~7:1, and the chip now BRIGHTENS on hover instead of going
   dark, which is also the correct affordance direction on a dark ground.

   Specificity (0,2,0) matches :446 exactly and wins on order.
   ============================================================================ */
.cross-link:hover {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
}


/* ============================================================================
   [AZ] #regulator (about.html) — EDITORIAL COMPACTION (owner-selected, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner asked for layout options for this band and picked RCOMPACT from three.

   WHAT WAS WRONG, measured rather than asserted:
     - 1072px against the 818px budget (900 viewport less the 82px sticky header)
       = 31% over, the worst offender on about.html after #disciplines.
     - 1718 characters in ONE column beside a static 544x512 photograph, with two
       long paragraphs run together and no visual break between them.
     - A third paragraph stranded BELOW the whole feature row, visually orphaned
       from the argument it concludes.
     - The photograph (a gavel, law books and a whiskey glass) is decorative and
       was given the same weight as the argument — the wrong weighting for a band
       whose entire job is a distinction the reader has to absorb.

   THE SECTION DOES FOUR JOBS and the shipped layout separated none of them:
     1. the claim (eyebrow + h2 + lede) — "the authority is not us"
     2. the ABC definition (2 paragraphs)
     3. three factual bullets on ABC scope
     4. the closing paragraph — what a BROKER controls — plus the process.html chip
   This block keeps the familiar shape and fixes the density, so the four parts
   read as separate moves without re-architecting the page.

   MEASURED RESULT: 1072 -> 796px at 1440, inside budget. probe-reg.mjs green at
   1440: all 5 paragraphs, the kicker, the h3 and all 3 bullets present, the
   photograph still renders, the .cross-link chip survives, 0 clipped elements,
   0 page overflow, 0 console errors, 0 failed requests.

   CONTENT PARITY IS EXACT — 1718 characters, same as the base. Nothing was
   hidden or clipped to win the budget; the height came from structure.

   CONTRAST MEASURED SEPARATELY, because probe-reg.mjs has NO contrast check —
   the same blind spot that let a 3.54:1 label through on #guides earlier today.
   10 text runs sampled against the real composited pixels (glyphs set
   transparent, backdrop screenshotted): worst 5th-percentile 8.17:1, identical
   to the base. Zero runs below AA.

   375 REMAINS OVER BUDGET at 1732px (base 1755px). Same arithmetic as #guides:
   1718 characters plus a photograph cannot fit 818px on a phone without deleting
   content. The site position stands — phones scroll by nature.

   Every rule head below is scoped to #regulator, and the sheet references no
   images, so there is no stylesheet-relative url() to rewrite (the [AX] trap).
   ============================================================================

/* =============================================================================
   opt-RCOMPACT — #regulator on about.html
   "Editorial compaction, structure kept."

   ── THE STRUCTURAL IDEA ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The section's whole job is a DISTINCTION: the authority is the ABC, not this
   brokerage. Today that distinction is invisible in the layout — 1718 characters
   poured down one column beside a 544x512 photograph, and the paragraph that
   finally states the brokerage's own side orphaned BELOW the row it argues
   against, with the process.html chip drifting after it.

   RCOMPACT makes the distinction the composition. Under a full-width masthead
   (the claim), the band becomes a two-lane editorial spread split by one
   hairline spine:

     LEFT LANE (480px) — THE BROKERAGE      RIGHT LANE (631px) — THE REGULATOR
     · the photograph, re-cut to a           · kicker "The regulator"
       still-life strip that HEADS the       · h3 — the agency's name
       lane instead of competing with        · R-FACT-01
       the argument                          · ── hairline ── the break the two
     · an amber rule (change of voice)         long paragraphs never had
     · the closing broker paragraph          · the second paragraph
     · the process.html chip, now            · the three scope facts, as a
       terminating its own lane                compact rule-separated register

   The four jobs stop being four stacked blobs. Job 1 is the masthead; jobs 2+3
   are one bounded lane that belongs to the ABC; job 4 is the facing lane that
   belongs to the broker — and the pointer ends that lane instead of drifting.

   ── MECHANISM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   #regulator .container becomes a 2-track grid and .feature-row spans it as a
   `subgrid` on BOTH axes, so its media lands on the container's own column AND
   row lines. That is the whole trick: it lets the closing paragraph and the chip
   — which are SIBLINGS of .feature-row, not children — stack underneath the
   photograph in the same lane without touching a byte of markup.

   ── EVERY TRACK IS MEASURED, NOT PICKED ───────────────────────────────────
   Wrap thresholds sampled on the real strings at the real font metrics:
     p1  (333 ch)   4 lines at >= 620px    (5 lines at 610)
     p2  (369 ch)   5 lines at >= 560px    (4 lines only above 680 — unreachable
                                            without starving the left lane)
     h3  ( 55 ch)   1 line  at >= 560px    (2 lines at 540)
     ul             4 lines at >= 600px
     closing (431)  7 lines at >= 470px    (8 lines at 460)
   The 480 / 631 split puts every block ~10px clear of its own threshold, so a
   font-metric wobble costs nothing.

   NOTHING IS SHRUNK. No font-size, no line-height, no letter-spacing, no
   font-weight is touched anywhere in this sheet. The 276px came out of
   structure: parallel lanes instead of three stacked full-width blocks, and a
   photograph re-proportioned from a 512px slab to a 200px plate.

   ── TOKENS ONLY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Every colour, space and radius is --ds-*. The only literals are structural:
   track proportions, the 12/5 crop, and 1px/2px rule widths.

   ── NOTE FOR PROMOTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   This sheet references NO images, so there is no stylesheet-relative url() to
   re-base when it moves into design-system/structural.css. The only image is the
   shipped <img src="assets/…"> in _base.html, which is document-relative and
   therefore unaffected.
   ========================================================================== */


/* =============================================================================
   A. THE TWO-LANE COMPOSITION
   Gated behind @supports so a browser without subgrid falls back to the SHIPPED
   stacked layout rather than to a broken half-grid: without row-subgrid the
   closing paragraph would be placed into a row the .feature-row box already
   occupies and the two would collide. Fail closed, not clever.
   ========================================================================== */
@supports (grid-template-columns: subgrid) {

  /* fr proportions, not px, so the lanes keep their ratio as the container
     narrows. The gutter is carried by the body's own margin+padding rather than
     a column-gap: a subgrid's gutters REPLACE its parent's, so a column-gap here
     would pull the photograph and the closing note 12px out of alignment — which
     is exactly what happened in the first pass before it was measured. */
  #regulator .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 480fr) minmax(0, 672fr);
    column-gap: 0;
    row-gap: 0;
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* ── the masthead: the claim, full width, shape unchanged ──────────────── */
  #regulator .container > .eyebrow {
    grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 1;
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
  #regulator .container > h2 { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 2; }
  #regulator .container > .lede {
    grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 3;
    /* the lede's own bottom margin is the gap before the spread — a margin on the
       subgrid below would offset ITS tracks off the parent's lines. */
    margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }

  /* ── the spread: .feature-row inherits the container's tracks ──────────── */
  #regulator .feature-row {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    grid-row: 4 / 7;                /* three container rows: plate | note | chip */
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: subgrid;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    column-gap: 0;                  /* must match the parent's or the tracks shift */
    row-gap: 0;
    margin: 0;                      /* a margin here would shift the subgrid lines */
    align-items: start;
    /* the shipped reveal translates 24px; the siblings that now share its rows do
       not, so the pair would visibly desync for 640ms. Fade only — same motion
       family, same tokens. The OPACITY property is deliberately NOT touched:
       .wow-reveal animates opacity to 1 and overriding it here would freeze the
       whole row invisible. */
    transform: none;
  }

  #regulator .feature-row__media { grid-column: 1 / 2; grid-row: 1; }

  /* the regulator's lane, held off the brokerage lane by one hairline spine */
  #regulator .feature-row__body {
    grid-column: 2 / 3;
    grid-row: 1 / 4;
    margin-left: var(--ds-space-md);
    padding-left: var(--ds-space-lg);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  }

  /* ── job 4: the closing note bound to the lane, not left adrift ────────── */
  /* It is a SIBLING of .feature-row, so the row-subgrid above is the entire
     reason it can sit INSIDE the composition instead of under it. */
  #regulator .container > p:last-of-type {
    grid-column: 1 / 2;
    grid-row: 5;
    margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  #regulator .container > .cross-link-rail__rail {
    grid-column: 1 / 2;
    grid-row: 6;
    align-self: start;
  }
}


/* =============================================================================
   B. THE DENSITY FIXES
   Unconditional — they are improvements to the shipped stack too, so they apply
   with or without subgrid, and at every width.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ── the photograph: demoted, not deleted ────────────────────────────────── */
/* 544x512 (278,528px²) becomes 480x200 (96,000px², 66% smaller). It stops being
   a slab that ties with the argument and becomes the plate that heads a column. */
#regulator .feature-row__media {
  aspect-ratio: 12 / 5;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);    /* no border on this box — no nested-radius debt */
}
#regulator .feature-row__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  /* MEASURED CROP. The source is 512x512; at 480 wide the 12/5 box keeps 41.7% of
     its height, so which band survives is a real decision, not a default. Sampled
     46% (whole gavel, glass cut off), 62% (whole glass + document, gavel head
     clipped) and 56% — 56% is the only one that keeps all four objects the alt
     text promises: gavel, law books, legal document, glass. */
  object-position: 50% 56%;
}

/* ── job 2: the two paragraphs stop running together ─────────────────────── */
/* Shipped state: `p { margin: 0 }` and no rule, so 702 characters butt straight
   into each other with literally nothing between them. One hairline and one
   space token is the whole fix. */
#regulator .feature-row__body > p + p {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
  padding-top: var(--ds-space-sm);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
}

/* ── job 3: the scope facts as a compact rule-separated register ─────────── */
/* Shipped state: a loose 4px-gap stack that reads as more prose. Ruled rows read
   as a register of facts, which is what they are. */
#regulator .feature-row__list {
  gap: 0;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}
#regulator .feature-row__list li {
  padding: var(--ds-space-xs) 0 var(--ds-space-xs) var(--ds-space-lg);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
}
#regulator .feature-row__list li::before { top: var(--ds-space-xs); }

/* ── job 4: mark the change of voice ─────────────────────────────────────── */
/* Everything above this rule is the regulator's; everything below it is the
   brokerage's. Amber, because that is the accent this band already uses for the
   eyebrow and the kicker — no new colour is introduced. */
#regulator .container > p:last-of-type {
  padding-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  border-top: 2px solid var(--ds-accent);
}


/* =============================================================================
   B2. THE NO-SUBGRID FALLBACK, PAID FOR
   MEASURED: with the @supports block deleted (simulating a pre-Chrome-117 /
   pre-Safari-16 engine) the band falls back to the shipped stack cleanly — no
   collision, no clipping, no page overflow, every element present — but it lands
   at 1148px against the shipped 1072px, because the rules added in section B are
   real pixels. A fallback must never be WORSE than what it replaces, so the
   shipped 64px row margins (which only exist to separate a full-width row from
   full-width neighbours) are spent back here. Re-measured at 1068px: one pixel
   under the shipped band, with the legibility fixes still in place.
   ========================================================================== */
@supports not (grid-template-columns: subgrid) {
  #regulator .feature-row { margin-block: var(--ds-space-lg); }
}


/* =============================================================================
   C. BELOW THE DS's 860px .feature-row REFLOW — one honest column
   The spread is a desktop composition. At phone width the shipped stack is
   right; it only needs the photograph's new proportion and the two legibility
   fixes to travel with it.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  #regulator .container { display: block; }
  #regulator .feature-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    grid-template-rows: none;
    gap: var(--ds-space-lg);
    margin-block: var(--ds-space-lg);
  }
  #regulator .feature-row__media,
  #regulator .feature-row__body { grid-column: auto; grid-row: auto; }
  #regulator .feature-row__media { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
  #regulator .feature-row__body {
    margin-left: 0;
    padding-left: 0;
    border-left: 0;
  }
  #regulator .container > .lede { margin-bottom: 0; }
  #regulator .container > p:last-of-type { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg); }
}


/* ============================================================================
   [BA] #disciplines (about.html) — TWO DESKS, SIDE BY SIDE (owner, 2026-08-07)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner: "same goes here, we can make this more compact and sleek like what we
   did to the section before it" — the [AZ] treatment on #regulator above.

   MEASURED PROBLEM: 1648px against the 818 budget — 2.01x, the worst band on
   about.html. 2273 characters, 7 paragraphs, two 544x512 photographs, and two
   full-width rows STACKED vertically, each a photo beside a two-paragraph body.

   THE MOVE: the section is called "TWO disciplines, kept on ONE desk". Stacking
   them is the one arrangement that contradicts its own headline — it puts them
   on two desks, one above the other, and the reader never sees them together.
   Side by side states the pairing in the layout and halves the height at once.

   RESULT: 1648 -> 810px at 1440. 1280 799 · 1200 792 — all inside budget.
   CONTENT PARITY EXACT: 2273 characters, 7 paragraphs, 2 headings, 2 photographs
   — identical to the base. Nothing hidden, nothing clipped, no copy rewritten.
   CONTRAST measured separately (the probe has none): 9 text runs, worst 5th
   percentile 10.06:1, identical to the base. Zero runs below AA.

   THREE BUGS I INTRODUCED AND FIXED, recorded so they are not repeated:
   1. The header overlapped itself. Eyebrow, h2 and lede were all given the same
      grid area — grid OVERLAYS items sharing an area, it does not stack them.
      Measured: h2 and lede both at y=72. Now three rows, three areas.
   2. 375 measured 7179px. The mobile query still named "dc-head", an area the
      new template no longer defines, so the header fell out into implicit rows.
      Grid fails SILENTLY when an item names an undefined area.
   3. The two cards did not match: left photo at the BOTTOM, right at the TOP.
      Cause is the shipped :nth-child(even) alternation at structural.css:336,
      which is correct for two stacked full-width rows and exactly wrong for a
      side-by-side pair. Both desks are now media-first, so the pair is one
      object seen twice — the same principle as [AX] on #guides.

   STILL OVER AT NARROW WIDTHS, disclosed: 1024 826 (+8), 900 858 (+40), 768
   1246, 375 1840. Narrower columns wrap the two long bodies onto more lines, so
   the TIGHTEST desktop width is the binding case, not the widest. Cutting those
   would mean shrinking the type, which is the failure this exercise avoids.
   ============================================================================

