   `ch` misleads in the same direction: `ch` is the width of "0", wider than the
   average glyph, so 68ch admits about 105 real characters, not 68. Measured
   properly, [BF] moved that paragraph from 153 to 105 and left it OUTSIDE the
   range rather than inside it. Corrected to 48ch here (72 characters) rather
   than by editing [BF], because this sheet is append-only and a later block
   overriding an earlier one is how the file works — editing [BF] in place would
   erase the record of the mistake.

   THE MEASURE WAS SWEPT against TRUE per-line counts, worst case over 8 pages:
       uncapped 160    68ch 106    60ch 92    54ch 86    48ch 76
   48ch is the first value that brings the worst line inside the range. It
   computes to 528px at the 16px body size — NARROWER than the .lede's 633px,
   and that is correct rather than a mistake: the lede is set at 20px and runs
   66 characters at 633px, so matching its MEASURE at 16px necessarily means a
   narrower box. Matching its pixel width instead would give 84 characters.

   GATED by a site-wide audit that walks every `.closing-cta` on all 13 pages:
   9 bands with defects before, 0 after, 11 inspected both times. The gate
   measures characters per line by walking every character offset with a Range
   and detecting a line break from the rect's top — not by dividing. It also
   fails closed on a page that 404s, because a missing page has no bands and
   would otherwise read as "no defects" (a false pass this lab actually hit when
   a build script threw part-way through).
   ============================================================================

/* =============================================================================
 * opt-BG.css — the closing CTA band, site-wide. 2026-08-08
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Owner, on two different pages: "this section also looks unbalanced" /
 * "need to fix spacings".
 *
 * Rather than patch the two screenshots, every `.closing-cta` on the site was
 * audited — 11 bands across 10 pages. NINE have at least one defect, and they
 * are the same three faults repeating:
 *
 *   (1) THE BODY PARAGRAPH IS UNCAPPED, on eight pages. `.closing-cta` centres
 *       its text but nothing constrains the measure, so the paragraph runs the
 *       full 1152px container. MEASURED PER LINE, exactly — see the note on
 *       method below:
 *           faq 160   locations 160   services 158   about 153   inventory 153
 *           resources 151   licence-types 149   process 149
 *       characters on the longest line, against the 45-75 body text is set for.
 *       A centred 160-character line is the worst case for a reader: both edges
 *       move, so there is no stable left margin to return to. This is the single
 *       most common defect on the site.
 *
 *       ⚠ HOW THESE WERE MEASURED, because the obvious method is wrong.
 *       `characters / number-of-lines` is NOT characters-per-line — it averages
 *       in the short final line and under-reports badly. A first pass at this
 *       used it and reported the worst case as 83 when it was really 153. The
 *       numbers above walk every character offset with a Range and detect a line
 *       break by a change in the rect's top, which gives the true count per line.
 *       `ch` units mislead in the same direction: `ch` is the width of "0", which
 *       is wider than the average glyph, so 68ch admits about 105 real
 *       characters, not 68.
 *
 *   (2) A `.lede` INSIDE THE BAND IS NOT CENTRED AS A BOX, on contact.html.
 *       `.lede` carries `max-width: 633px` from its own rule but no auto
 *       margins, so inside a centred band the 633px box sits hard against the
 *       left while the h2 and the buttons around it are centred. MEASURED: the
 *       box is off-centre by 519px. `text-align: center` centres the TEXT INSIDE
 *       a box; it cannot centre the box itself. That is exactly the "Both land
 *       on the same desk." line in the owner's screenshot.
 *
 *   (3) A CROSS-LINK RAIL DIRECTLY ABOVE THE BAND TOUCHES IT, on four pages
 *       (faq, inventory, licence-types, services). Measured gap: 0px.
 *
 * ⚠ THE RAILS HERE MUST *NOT* BE CENTRED, and that is the opposite of [BE].
 * [BE] centred rails that FOLLOW a `.closing-cta`, because there the rail is a
 * footnote to a centred block. These rails PRECEDE it, and the section they
 * belong to is left-aligned throughout — checked on all four pages:
 *       p.eyebrow  start | h2  start | p.lede  start | rail  start | CTA  center
 * The rail belongs with the left-aligned header above it, so centring it would
 * break the section to fix the seam. Only the seam is fixed.
 *
 * The centred CTA bolted onto a left-aligned section is DELIBERATE and is left
 * alone: `.closing-cta { text-align: center }` is the site's own pattern and it
 * appears this way on every one of these pages. It reads as a distinct closing
 * moment, not as an error.
 *
 * TOKENS ONLY — no raw hex, no new fonts, no new colours, no url().
 * ============================================================================= */


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1. EVERY PARAGRAPH BOX IN THE BAND IS CENTRED
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * `text-align: center` on the parent centres the TEXT INSIDE each child box. It
 * does nothing to a child whose box is narrower than the band — that box still
 * sits wherever its own margins put it, which is hard left. Any paragraph in the
 * band that carries a max-width therefore has to be centred explicitly. This is
 * the contact.html fault, measured at 519px off-centre.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.closing-cta > p {
  margin-inline: auto;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2. THE BODY PARAGRAPH GETS A MEASURE
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SWEPT against true per-line character counts, worst case across all 8 pages:
 *       uncapped  160      68ch  106      60ch  92
 *       54ch       86      48ch   76
 * 48ch is the first value that brings the worst line inside the range. It
 * computes to 528px at this 16px body size, which is NARROWER than the .lede's
 * 633px — and that is correct, not a mistake: the lede is set at 20px and runs
 * 66 characters at 633px, so matching its MEASURE at 16px necessarily means a
 * narrower box. Matching the lede's pixel width instead would give 84 characters
 * and miss the point of the rule.
 *
 * `.eyebrow` and `.lede` are excluded deliberately: the eyebrow is a 13-character
 * kicker that needs no measure, and `.lede` already carries its own cap which is
 * WIDER in pixels — without the exclusion this rule would win on specificity and
 * squeeze a 20px lede to 528px, undoing an existing correct decision.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.closing-cta > p:not(.eyebrow):not(.lede) {
  max-width: 48ch;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2b. CORRECTING [BF] — the #inventory intro paragraph was under-capped
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * [BF] capped that paragraph at 68ch and the promotion note claimed "~73
 * characters per line". THAT NUMBER WAS WRONG. It came from the
 * characters/lines average described above; measured properly the paragraph runs
 * at 105 characters on its longest line, so [BF] moved it from 153 to 105 and
 * left it well outside the range rather than inside it.
 *
 * Corrected here rather than by editing [BF], because this sheet is append-only
 * and a later block overriding an earlier one is how the file works — editing
 * [BF] in place would erase the record of the mistake. Same 48ch as the closing
 * band, for the same reason and at the same body size: 528px, 72 characters,
 * against the .lede above it at 66.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
#inventory .container > p:not(.eyebrow):not(.lede):not(.tg-fcount) {
  max-width: 48ch;
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2c. THE HEADING IS WELDED TO ITS OWN PARAGRAPH
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * MEASURED on every band: the heading-to-paragraph gap is 0px on EIGHT of the
 * nine. The single exception is contact.html, and only by accident — its
 * paragraph is a `.lede`, which carries `margin-top: 16px` from its own rule.
 * A bare `<p>` in the band has no top margin at all, so every h2/h3 sits directly
 * on top of its own sentence.
 *
 * 16px is not a new number: it is what contact.html already gets, and it matches
 * the h2 -> lede step used throughout the site. The eyebrow -> heading step stays
 * at its existing 12px, so the band reads kicker / heading / sentence rather than
 * as one undifferentiated lump.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.closing-cta > :is(h2, h3) + p {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-md);
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3. THE SEAM WHERE A LINK RAIL MEETS THE BAND
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Adjacency-scoped, so it applies only where a rail is genuinely the element
 * immediately before the band — the four pages measured at a 0px gap — and
 * nothing else on the site is touched. 64px matches the separation [BE] uses on
 * the other side of a closing band, so a rail above and a rail below now sit at
 * the same distance from it.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.cross-link-rail__rail + .closing-cta {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-2xl);
}


/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * 4. PHONES
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * All three rules are unconditional. The measure cap is a MAX, so below ~750px
 * the paragraph is simply the container width and rule 2 is inert; the centring
 * in rule 1 is correct at every width; and the 0px seam in rule 3 was measured at
 * 375 too, so the separation is needed there as much as on desktop.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */


