/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [BU] THE CLOSING CTA BAND, WHEN ITS CONTENT SITS IN A .container
        (owner, 2026-08-20 — "these should be centered stacked and observe
        proper line spacing")

   The 38 pages built today ([BP]-[BT]) wrap their closing band's content in a
   `.container`, which the original 13 pages do not. That one structural
   difference silently opted them out of TWO existing fixes:

   1. [BG] centres the band's paragraphs with `.closing-cta > p` — a DIRECT CHILD
      combinator. With a .container in between, the selector never matches.
      MEASURED on licence-type-47: the `.lede` is capped at 632.9px with
      `margin-left/right: 0`, putting BOTH its lines 259.6px LEFT of centre while
      the h2 above it is centred. This is the same defect [BG] itself documents:
      `text-align: center` centres text INSIDE a box, it cannot centre the BOX.
      The cure is `margin-inline: auto`, and it has to be reachable.

   2. [BE] centres a rail with `.closing-cta + .eyebrow + .cross-link-rail__rail`
      — a SIBLING chain. Today's rails are INSIDE the band, so that chain misses
      them too. And `text-align: center` is INERT here for a second reason:
      `.cross-link-rail__rail` is `display: flex`, so alignment is
      `justify-content`, not `text-align`. Computed was `justify-content: normal`.
      MEASURED: rail line 1 sat 151.4px and line 2 410.9px left of centre while
      reporting `text-align: center` — which is exactly why the box measurement
      looked fine and only a per-LINE measurement caught it.

   ⚠ SCOPE, counted before writing a line of this block. `.cross-link-rail__rail`
   appears 86 times across the site. In a 15-page sample covering every distinct
   page shape, 32 rails were found and only FOUR sit inside a `.closing-cta`.
   The other 28 are correctly LEFT-ALIGNED and must not move — including the
   guides', which live in `#related`, outside the band. Every selector below is
   therefore anchored to `.closing-cta`, which is what makes this safe. This is
   the rule [BE] exists because of: centring the class outright would have broken
   28 rails to fix 4.

   SPACING: the label measured a 0px gap from the buttons above it — it read as
   attached to the CTA row rather than as the heading of the rail below it. The
   band now spaces label and rail off the buttons, and wrapped rail lines get a
   real row-gap instead of the 12px inherited from the generic rail.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* 1 — centre the BOX, not just the text inside it */
.closing-cta > .container > p,
.closing-cta > .container > .lede {
  margin-inline: auto;
}

/* 2 — the rail is a flex container: alignment is justify-content */
.closing-cta .cross-link-rail__rail {
  justify-content: center;
  row-gap: var(--ds-space-md);
}
.closing-cta .cross-link-rail__label {
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-sm);
}

/* 3 — the rail block is its own thought, not a tail on the buttons */
.closing-cta .cross-link-rail {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl);
}


/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [BV] .article-header__inner — centre the CAPPED child, not just the container
        (owner audit, 2026-08-20: "check the other pages for similar centering
        issues")

   The audit of all distinct page shapes for the [BU] defect class found exactly
   ONE more instance, and it is on an ORIGINAL page rather than one built today.

   `.article-header__inner` (:257) already centres ITSELF — `max-width: 720px;
   margin-inline: auto; text-align: center`. What it never did is centre a child
   that carries its own cap. MEASURED on faq.html: the container is 720px with
   216px margins each side (correctly centred), its `.eyebrow` and `h2` are
   full-width and centre at offset 0, but its `.lede` is capped at 632.9px with
   `margin: 0` and therefore sits 43.6px LEFT of the heading above it.

   Identical root cause to [BU] and to [BG] before it: `text-align: center`
   centres text INSIDE a box; it cannot centre the BOX. Any capped block inside a
   centred band needs `margin-inline: auto` explicitly.

   SCOPE: `.article-header__inner` appears exactly TWICE site-wide, both on
   faq.html, and both contain a `.lede`. Counted before writing this rule. The
   `> *` form also covers the full-width eyebrow and h2, where auto margins are a
   no-op, so it is safe and it protects any capped child added later.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.article-header__inner > * { margin-inline: auto; }

/* ==========================================================================
   [BX] THE STATE TIER on locations.html
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One level ABOVE the fourteen market tabs. The client publishes six states,
   so the page needed an outer rail; California's panel still holds the
   hand-authored market matrix untouched inside it.

   Two things here are load-bearing:

   1. `.loc-panel--state` STRIPS the card treatment off `.loc-panel`. The
      California state panel wraps `.loc-tabs`, whose own `.loc-panel`
      children are cards — without this reset that renders a card inside a
      card. It sits after `.loc-panel` in the file, so equal specificity
      resolves the right way; `.loc-panel[hidden]` is (0,2,0) and still wins,
      which is what keeps panel switching working.

   2. The state rail is deliberately NOT the same pill as `.loc-tab`. A rail
      that looked identical to the one below it would read as one flat set of
      twenty options rather than two tiers.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.loc-states { margin-top: var(--ds-space-xl); }

.loc-staterail {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg);
}
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .loc-staterail {
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    padding-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
  }
  .loc-staterail::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 6px; }
  .loc-staterail::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--ds-line); border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill); }
}

.loc-state {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 3px;
  min-height: 56px;                       /* the same 40px tap floor as .loc-tab */
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  background: transparent;
  border: 0;
  border-bottom: 3px solid transparent;   /* underline, not pill — this is the outer tier */
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm) var(--ds-r-sm) 0 0;
  cursor: pointer;
  text-align: left;
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out),
              background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
.loc-state:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 60%, transparent); }
.loc-state:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.loc-state[aria-selected="true"] {
  border-bottom-color: var(--ds-accent);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent-soft) 55%, transparent);
}
.loc-state__name {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-size: 19px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.15;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
}
.loc-state__sub {
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.2;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
/* a state with nothing published never reads amber — same honesty rule as the market rail */
.loc-state__sub[data-thin] { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); font-style: italic; }

/* ---- the state panel is a container, not a card (see note 1 above) ---------- */
.loc-panel--state {
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
  padding: 0;
}

/* ---- the zero-stock statement, carried by every non-California state -------- */
.loc-empty {
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg);
  padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-lg);
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--ds-ink-soft);
  border-radius: 0 var(--ds-r-md) var(--ds-r-md) 0;
  max-width: 78ch;
}
.loc-empty b { color: var(--ds-ink); }
.loc-empty--thin { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-md); }

/* ---- counties and cities, side by side ------------------------------------- */
.loc-geo {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
  gap: var(--ds-space-md);
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-lg);
}
.loc-geo__col {
  min-width: 0;
  padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-lg);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
}
.loc-geo__col h4 {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-sm);
  padding-bottom: var(--ds-space-xs);
  font-size: 14px;
  letter-spacing: .08em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
}
.loc-geo__n {
  margin-left: auto;
  font-size: 13px;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.loc-geo__list {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));
  gap: 2px var(--ds-space-md);
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  max-height: 340px;
  overflow-y: auto;
  overscroll-behavior-y: contain;
}
.loc-geo__list li {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: var(--ds-space-xs);
  padding: 3px 0;
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.4;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  min-width: 0;
}
.loc-geo__name { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.loc-geo__flag {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  font-size: 10px;
  letter-spacing: .04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-sm);
  padding: 0 4px;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [BY] LOCATIONS CASCADE MENU
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Mirrors the client's own nav: a list of STATES on the left, and a right
   column that swaps to the active state's contents. All six states are peers
   — California sits in alphabetical position with no feature panel and no
   headline treatment.

   It reuses the .mm-mega shell (position, width, open/close transition, and
   the generic .mm-has-panel controller) and replaces only what sits inside
   .mm-mega__layout.

   Height: .mm-mega caps at min(76vh, 560px) with `overflow: hidden`. A
   cascade is naturally short — only ONE pane is ever in flow — so the panel
   sizes to the tallest pane (California, 7 rows) rather than to the sum of
   everything, which is the structural reason this shape fits where a single
   long grid did not. `min-height: 0` on the panes column keeps that true if a
   pane ever outgrows the cap.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* THREE columns now: the general location info as a card, the state rail, then that
   state's licence types. Owner asked for the type options per location to be in the
   menu and the general location info to sit left-most. */
/* FOUR columns: the state card, the state rail, that state's options, and what sits
   under the hovered option. The fourth is where the 50 market x type pages surface —
   Type 47 -> the ten markets that each have a Type 47 page, two hovers from the header. */
.mm-casc { grid-template-columns: 252px 206px 292px minmax(0, 1fr); }
@media (max-width: 1180px) { .mm-casc { grid-template-columns: 214px 184px 250px minmax(0, 1fr); } }

/* ---- column 1: the states ---------------------------------------------- */
.mm-casc__rail {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 2px;
  padding: 16px 12px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 55%, transparent);
  border-right: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  min-height: 0;
  overflow-y: auto;
}
/* Name OVER sub, not beside it. MEASURED at a 292px rail: "Pennsylvania" beside
   "No markets published" collided — flex:1 with min-width:0 lets the name grow into
   the sub, and neither had an ellipsis. Stacking removes the failure mode entirely
   instead of truncating a state's name. */
.mm-casc__state {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
  grid-template-areas: "name chev" "sub chev";
  align-items: center;
  column-gap: 10px;
  row-gap: 1px;
  width: 100%;
  padding: 9px 12px;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  background: transparent;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__state:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.mm-casc__state:hover { background: var(--ds-primary-soft); }
.mm-casc__state[aria-selected="true"] {
  background: var(--ds-accent-soft);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 45%, transparent);
}
.mm-casc__name {
  grid-area: name;
  min-width: 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.2;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
.mm-casc__state[aria-selected="true"] .mm-casc__name { color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }
.mm-casc__sub {
  grid-area: sub;
  min-width: 0;
  font-size: 11.5px;
  line-height: 1.25;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
/* a state with nothing published never reads amber — the rail's honesty rule */
.mm-casc__sub[data-thin] { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); font-style: italic; }
.mm-casc__chev {
  grid-area: chev;
  width: 15px;
  height: 15px;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  opacity: .55;
  transition: opacity var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease), transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__state[aria-selected="true"] .mm-casc__chev { opacity: 1; color: var(--ds-accent-ink); transform: translateX(2px); }

/* ---- column 2: the active state's contents ------------------------------ */
/* MEASURED: California's pane (7 rows + note) ran 572px into the 560px cap, leaving
   12px unreachable. Trimmed here rather than dropping a row. */
.mm-casc__panes { min-height: 0; overflow-y: auto; padding: 14px 20px 14px; }
.mm-casc__pane { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
.mm-casc__pane[hidden] { display: none; }

.mm-casc__row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 13px;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__row:hover, .mm-casc__row:focus-visible {
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
  border-color: var(--ds-line);
  transform: translateX(2px);
  text-decoration: none;
}
.mm-casc__row:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 1px; }
.mm-casc__mark {
  flex: none;
  width: 38px;
  height: 38px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 11%, transparent);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 24%, transparent);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.mm-casc__txt { min-width: 0; flex: 1; }
.mm-casc__txt .t {
  display: block;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 15.5px;
  line-height: 1.25;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
}
.mm-casc__row:hover .t, .mm-casc__row:focus-visible .t { color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }
.mm-casc__txt .s {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 2px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.3;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
/* the honest note: classifications we can NAME but have no page for, and the states
   that publish no markets at all. Deliberately not a link — it goes nowhere because
   there is nowhere for it to go. */
.mm-casc__note {
  margin: 8px 2px 0;
  padding-top: 9px;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  max-width: 62ch;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [BX-fix] THE STATE TIER'S GRID PLACEMENT
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   REGRESSION THIS REPAIRS, and why it was invisible in the earlier checks.

   structural.css:13264 places the market matrix with a DIRECT-CHILD selector:

       @media (min-width: 800px) {
         #markets > .container > .loc-tabs { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 4; }
       }

   `#markets > .container` is a two-column grid (1.32fr / 1fr) and `.loc-tabs`
   earned its full width only by being a direct child of it. Wrapping the matrix
   in `.loc-states` + `.loc-panel--state` for the state tier broke that chain:
   `.loc-tabs` stopped matching, fell back to auto placement, and rendered inside
   a single 633px column — MEASURED at 1440: rail 256px, panel 337px, the
   fourteen market tabs stacked one per row.

   Every gate I had ran on markup, JSON-LD, link targets and the MENU's own box.
   None of them looked at the PAGE's layout, which is why this survived a
   15/15 pass and only showed up on a screenshot. The lesson is in the log.

   `.loc-states` is now the direct child, so it takes the placement.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

@media (min-width: 800px) {
  #markets > .container > .loc-states { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 4; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [BZ] TYPE-PAGE POINT LISTS
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The 50 market x licence-type pages carry three bulleted blocks each — what
   the classification authorises, who it suits, and how we work it. Everything
   else those pages need already existed (.tp-note, .faq__list/.faq-item,
   .closing-cta, .cta-row, the hero), so this is the only new component.

   Sits in the `.tp-` namespace, which is the type-page vocabulary added by
   [BR]; additive-only, nothing already on the site is reachable by it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.tp-points {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--ds-space-sm);
  margin: var(--ds-space-lg) 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  max-width: 74ch;
}
.tp-points li {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 30px;
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
/* the marker is a rule, not a disc — it matches the hairline language the rest
   of the design system uses and stays legible on the dark bands */
.tp-points li::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: .72em;
  width: 16px;
  height: 2px;
  border-radius: 1px;
  background: var(--ds-accent);
}
.tp-points li > strong { color: var(--ds-ink); font-weight: 600; }
.section--dark .tp-points li { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }
.tp-points + .tp-note { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg); }

/* [BZ] cont. — the FAQ block on the 50 type pages is LEFT-aligned, not centred.
   .faq__list ships `margin-inline: auto` for faq.html, where [O] pairs it with a
   centred .faq__head so both axes agree. The type pages have a left-rail heading
   like every other band on them, so inheriting the centring reproduced exactly the
   defect [O] was written to fix: a centred list under a left-aligned heading, with
   a dead quadrant lower-left. Scoped to #faqs, which is this page kind's own id —
   faq.html uses #faq and is untouched. */
#faqs .faq__list { margin-inline: 0; max-width: 820px; }

/* ==========================================================================
   [BZ] cont. — THE LOCATION CARD, left-most column of the Locations mega
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The owner pointed at the Licensing dropdown's preview pane as the treatment.
   One deliberate difference: THAT pane is aria-hidden / tabindex="-1" and
   carries no role="menuitem", because it duplicates a row sitting beside it.
   This card duplicates nothing — it is the ONLY route to a state's services
   page — so it is a real, focusable menu item and must never inherit that
   hidden-from-AT treatment.

   All of the Licensing pane's styling is scoped through #mm-dd-licensing, so
   none of it reaches here; this is authored in the .mm-casc namespace.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.mm-casc__cards {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 0;
  padding: 16px;
  border-right: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 40%, transparent);
}
.mm-casc__card {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  gap: 4px;
  height: 100%;
  min-height: 190px;
  padding: 20px;
  overflow: hidden;
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
  text-decoration: none;
  isolation: isolate;
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              box-shadow var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__card[hidden] { display: none; }
.mm-casc__card:hover, .mm-casc__card:focus-visible {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card), var(--ds-glow);
  text-decoration: none;
}
.mm-casc__card:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

/* the state code as a watermark, the same device the Licensing pane uses for its
   licence number — big, low-contrast, clipped by the card */
.mm-casc__cardmark {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  top: -16px;
  right: -6px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 116px;
  line-height: .82;
  letter-spacing: -.05em;
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 16%, transparent);
  pointer-events: none;
  user-select: none;
}
.mm-casc__cardt {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 19px;
  line-height: 1.2;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  text-wrap: balance;
}
.mm-casc__card:hover .mm-casc__cardt { color: var(--ds-accent-ink); }
.mm-casc__cardd {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.4;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
.mm-casc__cardgo {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 34px;
  height: 34px;
  margin-top: 10px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  font-size: 15px;
  transition: background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease),
              transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__card:hover .mm-casc__cardgo {
  background: var(--ds-accent);
  color: var(--ds-bg-surface);
  transform: translateX(3px);
}
@media (max-width: 900px) { .mm-casc__cards { display: none; } }

/* [CA] the persistent route to the Locations page itself. The nav trigger is a
   <button> that opens the panel and cannot also navigate — the disclosure controller
   calls preventDefault in eight places, so making it an <a> would be a coin-flip on
   whether the click ever reaches the browser. This link sits under the card instead,
   visible whichever state is active. */
.mm-casc__all {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 12px;
  padding: 11px 14px;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.35;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: background var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease), color var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.mm-casc__all:hover, .mm-casc__all:focus-visible {
  background: var(--ds-accent); color: var(--ds-bg-surface); text-decoration: none;
}
.mm-casc__all:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.mm-casc__cards { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
.mm-casc__card { flex: 1; height: auto; }

/* ==========================================================================
   [CA] cont. — COLUMN 4 of the Locations cascade
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Hovering an option in column 3 reveals what sits under it. For California
   that is the real depth: a classification opens onto the ten market pages
   that carry it. For Arizona and Florida it is the published county and city
   lists — rendered as TEXT, not links, because the client publishes no page
   for an individual AZ or FL county, and a dead link is worse than a name.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.mm-casc__details {
  min-height: 0;
  overflow-y: auto;
  padding: 18px 22px 20px;
  border-left: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-bg-surface) 30%, transparent);
}
.mm-casc__detail[hidden] { display: none; }
.mm-casc__dfall {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  font-style: italic;
}
.mm-casc__dhead {
  margin: 0 0 4px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.25;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
}
.mm-casc__dnote {
  margin: 0 0 12px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
.mm-casc__dlist {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 1px 14px;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="1"] { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="3"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.mm-casc__dlist li { min-width: 0; }
.mm-casc__dlist a,
.mm-casc__dlist span {
  display: block;
  padding: 5px 10px;
  border-radius: 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.3;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
.mm-casc__dlist a { color: var(--ds-ink); text-decoration: none; }
.mm-casc__dlist a:hover, .mm-casc__dlist a:focus-visible {
  background: var(--ds-primary-soft); color: var(--ds-accent-ink); text-decoration: none;
}
.mm-casc__dlist a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 1px; }
/* a published place with no page of its own — deliberately not a link */
.mm-casc__dlist span { color: var(--ds-ink-soft); }
@media (max-width: 1000px) { .mm-casc__details { display: none; } }

/* [CA] cont. — every cascade column must be able to SCROLL, not push the panel past its cap.
   MEASURED: Florida's 66-county detail list overflowed the panel by 194px and 66 names were
   unreachable. `min-height: 0` alone is not enough — these are grid items whose row is sized
   by content, so each also needs an explicit max-height for its `overflow-y: auto` to engage.
   This is the same failure the Locations menu hit once before with .mm-mega__cards; the
   pattern is: min-height:0 lets it shrink, max-height makes it actually bounded. */
.mm-casc__rail,
.mm-casc__panes,
.mm-casc__details { max-height: min(76vh, 560px); }

/* ==========================================================================
   [CB] SERVICES SPLIT — the Brokerage band on the state services page
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Replaces a full-width .doc-list of eight stacked rows. MEASURED at 1440:
   the old band was 1410px tall and its prose occupied 633px of the 1200px
   container, leaving the right half empty for its whole height. This puts the
   eight services in that space and holds the copy to a 46ch measure.
   After: 582px, a 59% cut, with one extra route (All services) that the old
   band did not have.

   Its own `.svc-` namespace; nothing on the site is reachable by it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.svc-split {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
  gap: 0 var(--ds-space-xl);
  /* STRETCH, not start: the copy column is the taller of the two, and with `start` the
     card grid stopped at its natural ~450px and left ~350px of dead space beneath it.
     Stretching lets the grid fill the row so both columns end on the same line. */
  align-items: stretch;
}
@media (max-width: 900px) { .svc-split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--ds-space-lg); } }
.svc-split__copy .lede { max-width: 46ch; }
.svc-split__copy .btn { margin-top: var(--ds-space-md); }

.svc-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  /* four equal rows that share whatever height the copy column sets, so the cards grow
     into the space instead of the space sitting empty */
  grid-template-rows: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 8px;
  height: 100%;
}
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  /* stacked, the copy no longer sets the height — let the cards size to their content.
     The card overrides that go with this live AFTER .svc-card at the end of the block:
     placed here they sat BEFORE it and lost the cascade on equal specificity, so
     min-height never applied and the column stayed 64px. MEASURED at 375 before the move. */
  .svc-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; grid-template-rows: none; height: auto; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CB] SERVICE CARD — the Licensing preview pane's material, at grid scale
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner asked for the treatment used by `#mm-dd-licensing .mm-dd-preview`:
   a clipped numeral watermark, the layered warm ground, copy anchored to the
   bottom, and a gradient arrow as the affordance.

   The pane's own rules are ALL scoped through `#mm-dd-licensing`, so none of
   them reach here — the material is carried over deliberately rather than
   inherited, and re-tuned for eight cards in a grid instead of one 268px pane:
   watermark 186px -> 104px, title 26px -> 19px, arrow 44px -> 38px.

   TWO DIFFERENCES FROM THE PANE, BOTH DELIBERATE:
   1. The pane is `aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"` with no role, because it
      duplicates a list item beside it. These cards duplicate nothing — each is
      the only route to its service page — so they stay real, focusable links.
      Only the watermark and the arrow glyph are aria-hidden.
   2. The pane shows "§" for non-numbered items so a code is never invented.
      Here every card has a real sequence position, so the numeral is honest.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.svc-card {
  --svc-bg:
    var(--ds-grain),
    var(--ds-glow-wash),
    linear-gradient(158deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 13%, transparent) 0%,
                            transparent 58%),
    linear-gradient(0deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 82%, transparent),
                          color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-primary-deep) 82%, transparent)),
    var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  gap: var(--ds-space-2xs);
  min-width: 0;
  min-height: 0;
  padding: var(--ds-space-md);
  isolation: isolate;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-decoration: none;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 30%, transparent);
  background: var(--svc-bg);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-rest);
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              box-shadow var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease);
}
.svc-card:hover,
.svc-card:focus-visible {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 62%, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-inset-hi), var(--ds-shadow-rest), var(--ds-glow);
  text-decoration: none;
}
.svc-card:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--ds-accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

/* the numeral sits OVER the card's background and UNDER its text — the same
   z-index:-1 + isolation:isolate pairing the pane uses */
.svc-card__mark {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: -1;
  top: -18px;
  right: -8px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: 104px;
  line-height: .72;
  letter-spacing: -.05em;
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 22%, transparent);
  pointer-events: none;
  -webkit-user-select: none;
  user-select: none;
  transition: color var(--ds-dur) var(--ds-ease),
              transform var(--ds-dur-slow) var(--ds-ease-out);
}
.svc-card:hover .svc-card__mark {
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 32%, transparent);
  transform: translateY(3px);
}

.svc-card__t {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: 19px;
  line-height: 1.14;
  letter-spacing: -.015em;
  /* pretty, not balance — the pane's note applies here too: balancing breaks a
     hyphenated service name at the wrong point */
  text-wrap: pretty;
  color: var(--ds-ink-heading);
}
.svc-card__d {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-weight: 400;
  font-size: 13.5px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
/* a glyph, no words — the card is already obviously a link, and a label repeated
   eight times in one band is noise */
.svc-card__go {
  align-self: flex-start;
  flex: none;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-xs);
  width: 38px;
  height: 38px;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill);
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark);
  background-image: var(--ds-grad-brand);
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent) 55%, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-glow);
  transition: transform var(--ds-dur-fast) var(--ds-ease);
}
.svc-card:hover .svc-card__go { transform: translateX(3px); }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .svc-card__mark, .svc-card__go { transition: none; }
  .svc-card:hover .svc-card__mark { transform: none; }
  .svc-card:hover .svc-card__go { transform: none; }
}
/* stacked: the grid no longer stretches, so give the card a floor or the watermark
   has nothing to sit above. MUST stay after the base rules — placing it earlier
   lost the cascade on equal specificity once already this session. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .svc-card { min-height: 148px; }
  .svc-card__mark { font-size: 88px; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CD] SERVICE TILES — the "service list" band on services.html
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked Option D from the layout round (2026-09-02). Replaces eight
   .capability-card boxes laid out three-across in .cap-breakdown__grid with
   eight denser tiles four-across: two rows instead of three, and the number
   moves from its own stacked block into a ghost numeral behind the title, so
   it still anchors each tile without consuming vertical space.

   OWN NAMESPACE, DELIBERATELY. .capability-card is used 42 times site-wide and
   only 8 of those are this band — the other 26 are specimens on brand-card.html
   and design-system.html. Restyling the shared class would have moved all 42 to
   fix 8. Nothing outside this band is reachable by .svc-tile.

   Placed at the END of the file on purpose: equal-specificity rules earlier in
   this stylesheet would otherwise win, which has already cost a cascade once.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.svc-tiles {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 14px;
}

.svc-tile {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding: 20px 18px 18px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .10);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .028);
  text-decoration: none;
  color: inherit;
  transition: border-color .18s ease, transform .18s ease, background .18s ease;
}
.svc-tile:hover,
.svc-tile:focus-visible {
  border-color: rgba(230, 156, 78, .5);
  background: rgba(230, 156, 78, .05);
  transform: translateY(-3px);
}

/* the number, as a watermark rather than its own block */
.svc-tile__ghost {
  position: absolute;
  right: 8px;
  top: -14px;
  z-index: 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display, Georgia, serif);
  font-size: 74px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1;
  color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .045);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* every real child sits above the watermark */
.svc-tile__t,
.svc-tile__d,
.svc-tile__go { position: relative; z-index: 1; }

.svc-tile__t {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display, Georgia, serif);
  font-size: 16.5px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.24;
  letter-spacing: -.01em;
  color: var(--ds-ink-heading, #fff);
}
.svc-tile__d {
  margin: 7px 0 0;
  font-size: 13.5px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft, #c8c6c5);
}
/* pinned to the bottom so the eight link rows align across a ragged row */
.svc-tile__go {
  margin-top: auto;
  padding-top: 12px;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #eba459);
}
.svc-tile__go::after { content: " \2192"; }

@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  .svc-tiles { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  .svc-tiles { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .svc-tile__ghost { font-size: 62px; }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .svc-tile { transition: none; }
  .svc-tile:hover, .svc-tile:focus-visible { transform: none; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CE] SERVICE ACCORDION — the "Moving a licence between owners" band
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked Option A from the detail-layout round (2026-09-02). Replaces four
   full-width two-column rows with four <details> rows.

   MEASURED at 1440 before and after: the band was 1201px (first row top to last
   row bottom); closed it is 271px, a 77% cut. One row is open on load so the
   band is never a wall of closed bars.

   NATIVE <details>, NOT A JS WIDGET. It is keyboard-operable and screen-reader
   announced for free, it still works with JS disabled, and the copy inside stays
   in the DOM — so nothing here is hidden from indexing.

   ⚠ THE ANCHORS ARE THE REASON FOR THE JS IN [CE] site.js. There are ~1,285
   inbound links to #buy / #sell / #transfer / #valuation across the site (323 to
   #buy alone). Landing on a COLLAPSED row would have made every one of those
   links look broken, so the id lives on the <details> and the script opens the
   targeted row on load and on hashchange.

   Own namespace: .svc-acc-* had zero prior hits in css, html and js.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.svc-acc { border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .12); }

.svc-acc__row { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .10); }

.svc-acc__row > summary {
  list-style: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 46px minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  gap: 18px;
  align-items: baseline;
  padding: 18px 4px;
  transition: background .16s ease;
}
.svc-acc__row > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
.svc-acc__row > summary:hover { background: rgba(230, 156, 78, .05); }
.svc-acc__row > summary:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  outline-offset: -2px;
}

.svc-acc__n {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono, monospace);
  font-size: 12.5px;
  color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
}
.svc-acc__t {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display, Georgia, serif);
  font-size: 19px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.25;
  color: var(--ds-ink-heading, #fff);
}
/* a plus that becomes a cross when the row is open */
.svc-acc__sign {
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #eba459);
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 1;
  transition: transform .18s ease;
}
.svc-acc__row[open] > summary .svc-acc__sign { transform: rotate(45deg); }

.svc-acc__body {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 32px;
  padding: 2px 4px 26px 68px;
}
.svc-acc__body > * { min-width: 0; }
.svc-acc__body p { margin: 0 0 14px; }

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .svc-acc__body { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px; padding-left: 4px; }
  .svc-acc__row > summary { grid-template-columns: 38px minmax(0, 1fr) auto; gap: 12px; }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .svc-acc__row > summary { transition: none; }
  .svc-acc__sign { transition: none; }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [CE] MEDIA VARIANT — services 05-08 carry a photograph each.
   The band was 2486px, the tallest on the page, because each row rendered its
   image at 544x512 (one at 544x756). Collapsing to the accordion above keeps
   every one of those four photographs rather than discarding them: the image
   simply moves inside the open body at ~200px, and is only painted when the
   row is open.

   Three columns when there is room, stacking to one on a narrow screen.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.svc-acc__body--media {
  grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 24px;
  align-items: start;
}
.svc-acc__media {
  margin: 4px 0 0;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md, 6px);
  overflow: hidden;
  line-height: 0;
}
.svc-acc__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}

/* A placeholder must announce itself even when the row is CLOSED — otherwise
   collapsing the band would hide the disclosure along with the draft copy. */
.svc-acc__flag {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono, monospace);
  font-size: 10.5px;
  letter-spacing: .10em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-warning, #d9682f);
  border: 1px dashed rgba(217, 104, 47, .55);
  background: var(--ds-warning-soft, #2e1c12);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill, 999px);
  padding: 3px 9px;
  margin-left: 10px;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

@media (max-width: 1099px) {
  .svc-acc__body--media { grid-template-columns: 160px minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .svc-acc__body--media > :last-child { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
}
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .svc-acc__body--media { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .svc-acc__body--media > :last-child { grid-column: auto; }
  .svc-acc__media { max-width: 220px; }
  .svc-acc__flag { margin-left: 0; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CF] SERVICE SELECTOR — services 05-08, "the work that sits around the
   transaction". Owner picked Option D 2026-09-02.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   WHY THIS AND NOT THE ACCORDION IT REPLACES
   The four photographs become the NAVIGATION rather than decoration inside a
   row. MEASURED at 1440 against the 2486px the band was originally:

       original image rows      2486px
       media accordion (mine)    548px   -78%
       THIS                      437px   -82%   ← and it keeps all four images

   The accordion I had built was replaced on the numbers: this is shorter AND
   keeps more. The text-only variant that DISCARDED the photographs measured
   510px — worse than both — so "drop the images to save space" was never a
   trade worth making.

   ⚠ NO-JS BEHAVIOUR IS DELIBERATE. The panels are NOT hidden in the markup.
   With JS off all four render stacked: degraded, but every service readable and
   every anchor still lands. [CF] in site.js hides the inactive ones on init.
   Never add `hidden` to these panels in the HTML.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* MEASURED SPACING FIXES (2026-09-02, owner: "observe proper spacing between
   card and texts"). Three numbers, all read off the rendered page:

     lede -> cards          0px   the lede computes margin-bottom:0 and the
                                  selector sits in a bare wrapper div with no
                                  margin of its own, so the copy touched the cards
     caption -> card floor  1px on card 08 vs 9px on the others, because its
                                  "Illustrative scope" flag wraps to a second line
                                  and card 08 is what sets the row height
     h3 -> prose            0px   (fixed earlier the same day)

   The margin lives on .svc-sel so it holds whatever wrapper the band sits in. */
.svc-sel { margin-top: 34px; }

.svc-sel__thumbs {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 12px;
  margin: 0 0 30px;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.svc-sel__thumb {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0;
  text-align: left;
  color: inherit;
  font-family: inherit;
  background: none;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .12);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  overflow: hidden;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color .18s ease, background .18s ease;
}
.svc-sel__thumb img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 92px;
  object-fit: cover;
  opacity: .55;
  transition: opacity .18s ease;
}
.svc-sel__thumb:hover { border-color: rgba(230, 156, 78, .5); }
.svc-sel__thumb:hover img { opacity: .82; }
.svc-sel__thumb:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
.svc-sel__thumb[aria-selected="true"] {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  background: rgba(230, 156, 78, .06);
}
.svc-sel__thumb[aria-selected="true"] img { opacity: 1; }

/* 14px floor so a caption that wraps to two lines still clears the card edge —
   card 08 measured 1px before this. */
.svc-sel__cap { display: block; padding: 11px 12px 14px; }
.svc-sel__kick {
  display: block;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono, monospace);
  font-size: 10.5px;
  letter-spacing: .12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  margin-bottom: 3px;
}
.svc-sel__name {
  display: block;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display, Georgia, serif);
  font-size: 13.5px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.22;
  color: var(--ds-ink-heading, #fff);
}
/* the placeholder flag drops to its own line inside a caption rather than
   crowding the service name it sits beside */
.svc-sel__name .svc-acc__flag {
  display: inline-block;
  margin: 6px 0 0;
}

.svc-sel__panel {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 30px;
  align-items: start;
}
.svc-sel__panel > * { min-width: 0; }
.svc-sel__panel[hidden] { display: none; }

/* Typographic spacing inside the panel. The site's h3 and p both compute to
   margin 0 here, so heading and body copy were touching at 0px — measured, not
   guessed. These restore a normal rhythm without reaching outside the panel. */
.svc-sel__panel h3 {
  margin: 0 0 10px;
  font-size: 22px;
  line-height: 1.18;
}
.svc-sel__panel .feature-row__kicker { margin: 0 0 6px; }
.svc-sel__panel p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
.svc-sel__panel p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
.svc-sel__panel .sv-deep { margin-top: 4px; }
.svc-sel__panel .feature-row__list { margin: 0 0 18px; }
.svc-sel__panel .trust-note { margin-bottom: 12px; }

@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .svc-sel__thumbs { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .svc-sel__panel { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 22px; }
}
/* Deliberately NOT one column at 375. Stacking the four thumbnails cost 652px of
   pure navigation before the panel even started — measured. A 2x2 grid keeps the
   nav to roughly a third of that and the tap target is still ~160px wide. */
@media (max-width: 560px) {
  .svc-sel__thumbs { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
  .svc-sel__thumb img { height: 76px; }
  /* keep the same breathing room as desktop, scaled — the earlier value here
     silently overrode the 14px floor set above, because a later media block
     wins at equal specificity. */
  .svc-sel__cap { padding: 10px 10px 13px; }
  .svc-sel__name { font-size: 12.5px; }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .svc-sel__thumb, .svc-sel__thumb img { transition: none; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CH] WHERE BAND — two columns: the copy on the left, the markets on the right
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked a two-COLUMN layout for this band (2026-09-02). It was a heading
   and a 62ch lede occupying the left ~55% with the right half empty, and the
   thirteen markets flowing underneath as one run-on line of links separated by
   middots.

   SCOPED TO .where--split, NOT TO #where OR TO THE RAIL CLASS. Two counts made
   before writing a line of this:
     - `.cross-link-rail__rail` appears 83 times site-wide and only 25 of those
       sit inside a #where band. Restyling the class would have moved 58 rails
       elsewhere on the site to change 25.
     - one page (california-liquor-license-services.html) has a #where band with
       NO market list at all — it carries a CTA row instead. Targeting #where
       directly would have given that page an empty second column.
   The generator stamps .where--split only on the bands it actually converts, so
   both problems are structural rather than something to remember.

   The markets also stop being a run-on paragraph and become a real <ul>, which
   is what a list of thirteen links always was.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.mkt-list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 2px 24px;
}
.mkt-list > li { min-width: 0; }
.mkt-list a {
  display: block;
  padding: 7px 0;
  font-size: 14.5px;
  line-height: 1.35;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #eba459);
  text-decoration: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .08);
  transition: color .16s ease, padding-left .16s ease;
}
.mkt-list a:hover,
.mkt-list a:focus-visible {
  color: var(--ds-ink, #fff);
  padding-left: 6px;
}

@media (min-width: 900px) {
  .where--split > .container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 76px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* left column: the copy, in its own reading order */
  .where--split > .container > .eyebrow { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
  .where--split > .container > h2       { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; }
  .where--split > .container > .lede    { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 3; margin-bottom: 0; }
  /* right column: the markets, spanning the copy's full height */
  .where--split > .container > .mkt-list {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1 / span 3;
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
}

/* Below the split, one column of markets reads better than two narrow ones. */
@media (max-width: 620px) {
  .mkt-list { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CI] CALIFORNIA SERVICES PAGE — prose bands
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked, from the layout round on 2026-09-02:
     F  for the two LONG bands (Qualification 166 words, Consulting 199)
     E  for the three SHORT ones (Corporate 77, Classifications 15+5, Coverage 18)

   WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, measured on the live page before any of this:
   the five bands ran to 2998px, and the text column occupied 633px of a 1200px
   container — 47% of the width sat empty. The long paragraphs are inside
   <p class="lede">, a class meant for a SHORT intro, which is WHY they rendered
   at that measure. The missing pictures were the symptom; the misused class was
   the cause.

   MEASURED at 1440 for the picked pair:
     two long bands   1581px -> 1009px   -36%   (F)
     three short ones 1417px ->  525px   -63%   (E, as one card row)

   Own namespace. .ca-fig / .ca-cards had zero prior hits anywhere.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---- F: measured prose left, square figure right ---- */
.ca-fig > .container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  column-gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 60px);
  align-items: start;
}
.ca-fig__copy { grid-column: 1; max-width: 62ch; }
/* the site's h2 and p both compute to margin 0 in this band, so heading and body
   copy were touching at 0px — measured on the rendered page, not guessed. */
.ca-fig__copy > .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 10px; }
.ca-fig__copy > h2 { margin: 0 0 16px; }
.ca-fig__copy > p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
.ca-fig__copy > p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
.ca-fig__figure {
  grid-column: 2;
  grid-row: 1 / span 99;   /* span whatever the copy column produces */
  margin: 6px 0 0;
  align-self: start;
}
.ca-fig__figure img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;          /* same trap as .tp-hero__media img — see the note there */
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  object-fit: cover;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  display: block;
}
.ca-fig__figure figcaption {
  margin-top: 9px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-mono, monospace);
  font-size: 11.5px;
  letter-spacing: .10em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft, #c8c6c5);
  opacity: .75;
}

/* ---- E: the three short bands as one row of cards ---- */
.ca-cards__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}
.ca-card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .028);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .10);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
}
.ca-card__img { height: 126px; overflow: hidden; }
.ca-card__img img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; display: block; }
.ca-card__in { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; padding: 18px 18px 20px; }
.ca-card__in > h3 {
  margin: 0 0 10px;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display, Georgia, serif);
  font-size: 17px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.25;
}
.ca-card__in > p { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--ds-ink-soft, #c8c6c5); }
.ca-card__in > p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
/* the classification list keeps its own component styling; only the spacing is ours */
.ca-card__in .tp-points { margin: 0 0 12px; }
.ca-card__in .tp-note { font-size: 12.5px; }
.ca-card__foot { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 14px; }

@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .ca-fig > .container { grid-template-columns: 1fr; row-gap: 26px; }
  .ca-fig__copy, .ca-fig__figure { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
  .ca-fig__figure img { max-width: 320px; }
  .ca-cards__grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CJ] MARKET x TYPE PAGES — hero split + two-column bullet bands
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked Hero A + Sections B (2026-09-02) for this page family, and asked
   for it across all 50 market x type pages.

   WHAT WAS WRONG, measured on liquor-license-san-francisco-type-20.html:
     six stacked single-column sections and ZERO images in <main>
     hero 741px, its lede 715px of a 1200px container — 40% of the width empty
     the three middle bands share the IDENTICAL shape (eyebrow, heading, a 10-12
     word lede, a bullet list), which is what actually made the page read flat

   .tp-hero  copy left, market photograph right
   .tp-split heading + one-line lede left, the bullets right

   WHY .tp-split IS NOT APPLIED EVERYWHERE
   It needs bullets to put in the right column. On the ten Los Angeles and San
   Diego pages #fit/#how are 116-159 words of PROSE with no list at all — those
   already carry the [CI] figure layout, which is the right treatment for prose.
   Content decides which band gets which, not a blanket sweep.

   Own namespace: .tp-hero / .tp-split had zero prior hits in css, html and js.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* .tp-hero was REMOVED. The split hero it styled was replaced by the design
   system's existing .hero--photo full-bleed pattern (owner call, 2026-09-02),
   which brings its own dual-graduated .hero__scrim and the :has() relight for
   on-photo eyebrows. Leaving these rules behind would have been dead CSS that
   quietly competes with the real hero. */

/* OPTION A (owner pick, 2026-09-02): the copy and the bullets stack in one column and
   the photograph becomes the other, STRETCHED to whatever height the text needs.

   WHY: with the picture under the copy the left column ran far taller than the bullet
   list beside it — measured 271px + 308px + 167px = 746px of empty bottom-right across
   the three bands. Stretching the image into that column means the imbalance cannot
   exist: the picture absorbs exactly the leftover height, whatever it is. */
.tp-split > .container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.55fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  column-gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 52px);
  align-items: start;
}
.tp-split__copy { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
.tp-split__copy > .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 10px; }
.tp-split__copy > h2 { margin: 0 0 12px; }
.tp-split__copy > .lede { margin: 0; }
.tp-split__list { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; margin: 22px 0 0; }
/* the list keeps whatever component class it had; only placement is ours */

@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .tp-hero > .container,
  .tp-split > .container { grid-template-columns: 1fr; row-gap: 24px; }
  .tp-hero__copy, .tp-hero__media,
  .tp-split__copy, .tp-split__list { grid-column: 1; }
  .tp-hero__media img { max-width: 420px; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CK] FAQ BAND, TWO COLUMNS — market x type pages only
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner picked option A (2026-09-02).

   MEASURED on the live page before building: the band is 762px tall and the
   accordion renders only 820px wide inside a 1200px container, so 380px of the
   row sits empty beside six collapsed bars.

   ⚠ SCOPED TO .faq--2col, NEVER TO .faq__list. There are 56 .faq__list
   instances across 55 files and only 50 of them are the type-page band. The
   other six are faq.html (7 items, two lists), index.html (4 items) and three
   market pages. Restyling the shared class would have re-laid out all 56 to
   change 50, including the site's actual FAQ page. The generator stamps
   .faq--2col only on the bands it converts.

   TWO REAL COLUMNS, NOT A REFLOW. The six items are wrapped into two column
   divs of three, so opening an answer grows only its own column. A CSS
   `columns: 2` would have reflowed items ACROSS the two columns on every open
   and close, which is disorienting on a click.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.faq--2col {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: 0 clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
  align-items: start;
}
/* ⚠ THE ID SELECTOR BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING. `#faqs .faq__list` already claims
   max-width: 820px at specificity (1,1,0). A plain `.faq--2col` rule is (0,1,0)
   and LOSES, so the two columns were silently squeezed into 820px of a 1200px
   container — the layout looked applied and the 380px it was meant to reclaim
   stayed empty. MEASURED: computed max-width still read 820px until this rule
   existed. Match the weight, or the override is decoration. */
#faqs .faq--2col {
  max-width: none;
  margin-inline: 0;
}

/* TYPE SCALE FOR A HALF-WIDTH COLUMN. At full width the question sits in Fraunces
   19px/29px, which is right for one wide column. MEASURED in two columns: the same
   question wraps to THREE lines in a 548px column and every row grows to ~92px, which
   is what reads as boxy. The two-column variant therefore drops to the body face at
   15px so a question fits two lines. Scoped with #faqs .faq--2col so faq.html and the
   homepage — which keep the single wide column — are untouched. */
#faqs .faq--2col .faq-item__q {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body, system-ui, sans-serif);
  font-size: 15px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  padding: 14px 0;
  gap: 18px;
}
#faqs .faq--2col .faq-item__a {
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.55;
  padding-bottom: 15px;
}
.faq--2col > .faq__col { min-width: 0; }
/* each column carries its own top rule so the two read as a pair */
.faq--2col > .faq__col > .faq-item:first-child { border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .12); }

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .faq--2col { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  /* stacked, only the very first item needs the top rule */
  .faq--2col > .faq__col + .faq__col > .faq-item:first-child { border-top: 0; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [CL] IMAGERY IN THE TYPE-PAGE BANDS
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner asked for imagery in #authorizes / #fit / #how (2026-09-02).

   IT SITS UNDER THE COPY, NOT IN A THIRD COLUMN. Measured on the live page:
   the bullet column is 630px and the copy column 466px. Introducing a third
   column would have squeezed the bullets to roughly 420px and pushed most of
   them onto a second line, which is the opposite of the two-column band the
   owner just approved. Under the copy, the bullets keep their measure.

   IT ADDS HEIGHT, AND THAT IS UNAVOIDABLE. Only #how had real spare room
   (117px under the copy); #authorizes had 13px and #fit was already copy-driven
   at -24px. A picture in those rows makes them taller. 16/9 was chosen over
   4/3 to bound how much.

   The image is decorative-adjacent but NOT decorative: each one is chosen from
   the licence class the page is about (see the generator), so an off-sale page
   never shows on-premises drinking.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.tp-split__media {
  grid-column: 2;
  grid-row: 1 / span 2;      /* spans BOTH text rows — this is what kills the dead space */
  align-self: stretch;
  margin: 0;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  overflow: hidden;
  line-height: 0;
  min-height: 260px;         /* a floor, so a very short band still shows a real picture */
}
.tp-split__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;              /* NOT auto here: the image is sized by the row, not by its
                                own ratio, which is the whole point of option A */
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}

@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .tp-hero > .container,
  .tp-split > .container { grid-template-columns: 1fr; row-gap: 22px; }
  .tp-split__copy, .tp-split__list, .tp-split__media {
    grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto;
  }
  .tp-split__media { max-width: 460px; min-height: 0; }
  .tp-split__media img { height: auto; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
  .tp-split__list { margin-top: 18px; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CN] SERVICE PAGES -- H-A hero + C-A covers band (owner picked 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Both halves REUSE existing components, so this block is deliberately tiny:
     H-A  -> .hero--photo / .hero__media / .hero__scrim / .hero__inner  (already
             on 60 pages; see the hero-photo primitive further up this sheet)
     C-A  -> .tp-split / .tp-split__copy / .tp-split__list / .tp-split__media
             from [CL], including its full-height media column and its
             <=1000px single-column collapse. Nothing re-declared here.

   THE ONE RULE THAT IS ACTUALLY NEW. `.sv-list` runs its own responsive grid:
     .sv-list { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); }
   Inside a .tp-split__list the column is ~630px, so auto-fit would break the
   items into TWO sub-columns and re-create the ragged block the owner asked us
   to get away from. Forcing one column keeps the checklist reading as a list.
   Specificity: (0,2,0) here vs (0,1,0) for the base rule, so this wins without
   !important and without touching the 40-card .svc-split grid on services.html,
   which is a different container.
   ========================================================================== */
.tp-split__list .sv-list {
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  margin-top: 0;             /* the [CL] .tp-split__list already supplies the 22px */
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CO] "WHERE" BAND -- MARKETS AS PILLS (owner picked W-B, 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE DEFECT THIS FIXES. [CA] made the markets a real <ul> in two columns, and
   `.mkt-list a` is display:block with a border-bottom. Measured on the live page:
   the columns are 258px, so the rule under "Fresno" is exactly as long as the one
   under "San Bernardino County" -- thirteen identical rules that read as an empty
   table. 13 items over 2 columns also strands one item alone on the last row.

   A pill is sized by its own label, so a trailing empty rule is not something to
   tune -- it becomes structurally impossible. Wrapping also absorbs any list
   length (the band carries 13 markets on 21 pages and 8 on the other 4).

   SCOPED TO `.where--split .mkt-list`, NOT bare `.mkt-list`. They coincide 1:1
   today (25 pages each, asserted by the generator), but the scope keeps a market
   list dropped into some other band on the old treatment until that is a decision
   rather than a side effect. Same reasoning [CA] used for this band.

   NOT `.chip`. That component already exists but is built for the light ground
   (border var(--ds-line), color var(--ds-ink)); this band is .section--dark, so
   its colours would fail here.

   Hover moves colour and background only. The base rule animates `padding-left`,
   which on a pill would resize the box and shove every later pill sideways
   mid-transition, so it is explicitly reset to 0.
   ========================================================================== */
.where--split .mkt-list {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 9px;
}
.where--split .mkt-list > li { min-width: 0; }
.where--split .mkt-list a {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 8px 15px;
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-glass-border, rgba(230, 156, 78, .18));
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-pill, 999px);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .03);
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #eba459);
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: color .16s ease, background .16s ease, border-color .16s ease;
}
.where--split .mkt-list a:hover,
.where--split .mkt-list a:focus-visible {
  padding-left: 15px;                    /* cancels the base rule's 6px nudge */
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff);
  border-color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e) 14%, transparent);
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .where--split .mkt-list a { transition: none; }
}

/* THE UNDERLINE. An accessibility rule further up this sheet --
     .section :is(p, li, dd, td, blockquote, .lede, .data-proof__sub) a:not([class])
   -- underlines every classless link inside a .section, at specificity (0,3,1).
   `.where--split .mkt-list a` is only (0,2,1), so the pills inherited an underline
   INSIDE their border: two affordances doing one job. (This predates the pills --
   the two-column list was underlined too.)

   Removing it here is safe, and is not a defeat of that rule's purpose: the rule
   exists because a bare UA link on a dark band fails contrast on colour alone.
   The pill keeps that rule's exact colour (--ds-accent-ink) AND adds a border and
   background, which is a stronger non-colour affordance than an underline. Scope
   is these 25 bands only; every other classless link on the site keeps its underline.

   Specificity, deliberately, not !important: adding `li` and `:not([class])` makes
   this (0,3,2), which beats (0,3,1) outright rather than relying on source order. */
.where--split .mkt-list li a:not([class]) {
  text-decoration: none;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CP] THE CTA BANNER'S OVERLAP RESERVE, PAIRED TO THE BAND IT ACTUALLY FOLLOWS
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   `.cta-banner` lifts itself by clamp(140px, 12vw, 176px) so it straddles the seam into the band
   above. That lift is a CLASS, so it follows the suite wherever the suite goes.
   Its matching reserve was written as `#faq { padding-bottom: ... }` -- an ID,
   so it only ever applied to one band on one page.

   That held while the suite only followed the FAQ. Adding the suite to the 8
   service pages, where it follows #next, broke it: measured on
   service-new-business at 1440, the banner lifted -172.8px into a band reserving
   only 72px, hiding the "The other seven services" heading and all seven links
   behind the banner.

   Pairing the reserve to `:has(+ .cta-suite)` ties it to the same condition as
   the lift, so the two cannot drift apart again when the suite is placed
   somewhere new. The straddle itself is unchanged -- this only stops the band
   above from being shorter than the lift that eats into it.

   The original #faq rule is left in place: it is an ID (1,0,0) and wins over
   this (0,2,0), but computes the identical value, so it is now redundant rather
   than conflicting. Every band before the suite is a .section (asserted by the
   generator), so padding-bottom is the right lever.
   ========================================================================== */
.section:has(+ .cta-suite) {
  padding-bottom: calc(var(--ds-section-py) + clamp(140px, 12vw, 176px) + var(--ds-space-lg));
}
@media (max-width: 860px) {
  .section:has(+ .cta-suite) {
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--ds-section-py) + 58px + var(--ds-space-lg));
  }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CQ] CROSS-LINK RAILS AS CARDS (owner picked R-C, 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Replaces a centred, middot-separated, underlined run of links. Measured on
   the live page first: the seven-service rail was 106px, wrapped 5 + 2 leaving
   an orphan line, and its tap targets were 25px tall.

   R-C WON ON THE STRESS TEST, not on looks. These rails come in three shapes,
   and it is the only treatment that balances all of them:
       7 links  -> 4 + 3      (pills and columns both orphan)
       12 links -> 4 + 4 + 4
       4 links  -> one row    (every other option orphans 3 + 1, because the
                               classification labels are ~340px wide)
   Tap target 25px -> 94px. The cost is height: +147px on a seven-link rail.

   FLEX, NOT GRID, and that is the owner's centring request (2026-09-03). CSS Grid places
   leftover items from the start of the row, so seven cards in a four-column grid left the
   last three hard against the left edge with a card-sized hole on the right. Grid has no
   way to centre only the leftover items. Flex with `justify-content: center` does it for
   free, and because each item's basis still divides the row exactly, a FULL row is
   unchanged -- centring a row that already spans the container is a no-op. Equal heights
   survive: flex items stretch by default, and the inner <a> is height:100%.

   SCOPED BY MODIFIER, not by component. 58 rails exist site-wide; only the 31
   that are "other N things" lists were converted, and only those carry
   `--cards`. The other 27 keep the prose rail untouched.

   The underline override is the same (0,3,1) fight as [CO]: the sheet's a11y
   rule `.section :is(p,li,...) a:not([class])` underlines every classless link
   in a section and beats a plain `.cross-link-rail__cards a` (0,2,1). Adding
   `li` and `:not([class])` makes this (0,3,2) and wins outright. The card frame
   is the non-colour affordance in the underline's place, and the link keeps the
   same accent colour that rule sets.
   ========================================================================== */
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__label {
  margin: 0 0 18px;
  font-size: 11.5px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1;
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff) 50%, transparent);
}
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: flex;             /* NOT grid -- see the note above about centring short rows */
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 14px;
  text-align: left;          /* the band is .closing-cta (centred); the cards are not */
}
/* One card per "column", sized so a full row still spans the container exactly. The
   column counts and their breakpoints were MEASURED off the previous auto-fit grid
   (2-up below 830px, 3-up 830-1089, 4-up from 1090) so the layout is unchanged at every
   width -- the only difference is that a short final row now centres. */
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards > li {
  flex: 0 1 calc((100% - 14px) / 2);
  min-width: 0;
}
@media (min-width: 830px) {
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards > li { flex-basis: calc((100% - 2 * 14px) / 3); }
}
@media (min-width: 1090px) {
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards > li { flex-basis: calc((100% - 3 * 14px) / 4); }
}
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 10px;
  height: 100%;
  padding: 20px 22px;
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff) 10%, transparent);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff) 3%, transparent);
  transition: border-color .16s ease, background .16s ease;
}
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards li a:not([class]) { text-decoration: none; }
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a:hover,
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a:focus-visible {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e) 8%, transparent);
}
.cross-link-rail--cards .clc__name {
  color: var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff);
  font-size: 16px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.35;
}
.cross-link-rail--cards .clc__go {
  margin-top: auto;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #eba459);
  font-size: 13px;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a { transition: none; }
}

/* TWO-UP ON SMALL SCREENS (owner asked, 2026-09-03, after seeing the one-up cost).
   auto-fit with a 250px floor drops to a single column below ~560px, which made the
   twelve-market rail 1308px tall on a 375px phone and the seven-link rail 770px.
   Forcing two columns halves that.

   600px, and the number was MEASURED rather than assumed. My first attempt used 560px on
   the theory that auto-fit was already giving two columns there. It was not: at a 561px
   viewport the container is 513px and two 250px tracks plus the 14px gap need 514px, so
   auto-fit fell back to ONE column and the twelve-market rail sprang back to 1308px in a
   one-pixel-wide dead zone. 600px clears it with margin -- at 601px the container is 553px,
   comfortably over the 514px needed -- so the two-column run is continuous from 320px up.
   Padding and the name size step down with it, because a ~156px card cannot carry 22px
   of side padding and a 16px name without the longest labels -- "San Bernardino County",
   "Type 41 - On-Sale Beer & Wine, Eating Place" -- running to five or six lines. */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards { gap: 10px; }
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards > li { flex-basis: calc((100% - 10px) / 2); }
  .cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a { padding: 14px 15px; gap: 8px; }
  .cross-link-rail--cards .clc__name { font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.3; }
  .cross-link-rail--cards .clc__go   { font-size: 12px; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CR] NEW-BUSINESS "SERVICES AND MARKETS" BAND (owner picked N-A + T-3)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ONE band on ONE page, and it is the odd one out by construction: the other
   seven #covers bands carry 4-5 plain scope items, this one carries 13 LINKS
   and is really a navigation index sitting in the [CL] .tp-split shell.

   Measured before: 1016px tall -- nearly double every sibling -- with the 13
   links in a single 713px column and the heading breaking 268px / 417px.

   TWO LABELLED COLUMNS, because the split is true rather than decorative: the
   13 are 7 services (services.html#*) and 6 markets (locations.html#*), which
   the generator asserts by href rather than by reading the labels.

   THE 42px HEADING IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE, IT IS ARITHMETIC. "Services and
   markets on this site" needs 694px on one line at 44px and the copy column is
   669px, so it missed by 25px and broke to a short orphan first line. 42px
   clears it at 663px.

   DESKTOP-ONLY, DELIBERATELY. [CL] collapses .tp-split to a single column at
   <=1000px, where the heading is already responsive and the columns already
   stack. An unscoped 42px leaked into that layout and forced the phone heading
   to three lines, so everything here sits above the breakpoint and the mobile
   band is byte-for-byte what it was.
   ========================================================================== */
.covers__groups {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  row-gap: 22px;
}
.covers__gh {
  margin: 0 0 10px;
  font-size: 11.5px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1;
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ds-ink-on-dark, #fff) 50%, transparent);
}
/* .sv-list runs its own auto-fit grid; inside a group it must be one column --
   the same override [CN] makes for .tp-split__list, for the same reason. */
.covers__groups .sv-list.covers__one {
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  margin-top: 0;
}
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .covers__groups {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: 36px;
    row-gap: 0;
  }
  #covers.tp-split .tp-split__copy h2 { font-size: 42px; }
  #covers.tp-split .tp-split__copy .lede { margin-top: 14px; max-width: 58ch; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CT] A MEDIA SLOT FOR THE LINK CARD (owner asked for images, 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [CQ]'s card is text-only: a name and a "View ..." affordance. This adds an
   optional image above them. It is OPT-IN by markup -- a card with no
   .clc__media is untouched, so the 31 rails already shipped are unaffected.

   The card's anchor is display:flex/column with padding; the image has to sit
   flush to the card's top and side edges, so the media is pulled out by the
   anchor's own padding with negative margins rather than the padding being
   removed (which would break every text-only card sharing the rule).

   16/9 is fixed rather than intrinsic: the five assets are 900x562 up to
   1400x933, and without a fixed box the cards in a row would have different
   image heights and the names would stop aligning.
   ========================================================================== */
.cross-link-rail--cards .cross-link-rail__cards a:has(.clc__media) { padding-top: 0; }
.clc__media {
  display: block;
  margin: 0 -22px 4px;          /* cancels the card's 22px side padding */
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px) var(--ds-r-lg, 10px) 0 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  line-height: 0;
}
.clc__media img {
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  height: auto;                 /* aspect-ratio only derives the missing axis */
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .clc__media { margin-inline: -15px; }   /* the card's small-screen padding */
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CU] THE FIVE CLASSIFICATIONS ON ONE ROW (owner picked K-A, 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Five cards previously wrapped 4 + 1, leaving 875px of empty width beside the
   lone card on row two. The card was centred; the GAP was the imbalance. One row
   removes it by construction and takes the band 834px -> ~545px.

   THE RE-TUNE IS THE POINT, NOT THE COLUMN COUNT. [CQ]'s card is sized for a
   278px column -- 22px side padding, a 16px name at 1.35, a 10px gap. At 219px
   that leaves 175px of text measure, and the longest name
   ("Type 41 - On-Sale Beer & Wine, Eating Place") ran to five tight lines.
   Padding comes down to buy measure back, and the type steps down with it so the
   line count does not simply move the cramping somewhere else.

   SCOPED TO THIS BAND. The 31 link-card rails elsewhere stay on [CQ]'s sizing.
   DESKTOP ONLY: below 1001px this band is 2-up, where 219px columns do not exist.
   ========================================================================== */
/* THREE-UP THROUGH THE NARROW DESKTOP RANGE. Five across only holds while the
   card stays wide enough for a two-line name. Measured: 219px at 1440 and 215px at
   1180 give two lines on all five, but 199px at 1100 and 184px at 1024 break the
   longest names to three -- and unevenly (2/2/3/3/3), which reads as an accident
   rather than a rhythm. Between 1001 and 1179 the band therefore goes 3 + 2 on
   wider cards instead, which keeps every name at two lines. [CQ]'s 4-up would
   re-introduce the 4 + 1 gap this change exists to remove. */
@media (min-width: 1001px) and (max-width: 1179px) {
  #classifications .cross-link-rail__cards > li {
    flex-basis: calc((100% - 2 * 14px) / 3);
  }
}
@media (min-width: 1180px) {
  #classifications .cross-link-rail__cards > li {
    flex-basis: calc((100% - 4 * 14px) / 5);
  }
}
/* HEADING -> CARDS GAP. On the 31 other rails a .cross-link-rail__label sits above
   the cards carrying an 18px bottom margin. This band uses its section h2 instead and
   omits that label, so nothing separated the two: measured 0px, against 12px from the
   eyebrow to the h2 and 24px from the cards down to the note -- the only zero gap in
   the band. 24px matches the gap below the cards, so the grid now sits evenly between
   its heading and its note. (For reference the same page's #coverage gives its h2 40px
   before a full-width image block; a card row is a lighter payload than that.) */
#classifications .cross-link-rail {
  display: block;
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px);
}
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  #classifications .cross-link-rail__cards a {
    padding: 0 16px 16px;        /* the media is flush to the top, so no top pad */
    gap: 7px;
  }
  #classifications .clc__media {
    margin-inline: -16px;        /* re-cancel: the side padding changed */
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  #classifications .clc__name {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.32;
  }
  #classifications .clc__go {
    font-size: 12.5px;
    padding-top: 2px;
  }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CV] BODY-LENGTH PARAGRAPHS STOP USING LEDE SIZING
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Owner pointed at process.html #phases as the reference. Measured, both ends:

     reference  .feature-row__body p   16px / 24.8   rgb(200,198,197)
     was        .lede                  20px / 31     rgb(200,198,197)

   A lede is an intro. The paragraph this started from is 166 words in a 633px
   column, and thirteen others are 85-159 words -- including #about-<state> bands
   that stack FOUR consecutive "ledes", which is prose by any reading.

   ONLY SIZE AND LEADING MOVE. A plain <p> was measured first and rejected: it
   gives the right 16px/24.8 but also turns the soft grey to pure white and drops
   .lede's 633px max-width, so the lines would run the full column. Keeping .lede
   and adding a modifier preserves colour and measure, which is the difference
   between matching the reference and merely shrinking the text.

   Applied to 14 paragraphs on 5 pages, all outside a hero. The 44 long ledes that
   ARE in a hero keep 20px -- a hero lede is meant to be large.
   ========================================================================== */
.lede.lede--prose {
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.55;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CS] REMOVED 2026-09-03 -- .ca-cards__grid no longer exists
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [CS] existed to keep .ca-cards__grid honest as cards left it: auto-fit when it
   dropped from three to two, and a `:has(> .ca-card:only-child)` arm to stop the
   last card stretching the full 1152px. Coverage, Classifications and finally
   Corporate have all moved out to bands of their own, so the grid and its cards
   are gone from every page (verified: 0 occurrences of "ca-cards" site-wide) and
   the rules had nothing left to select. Recorded rather than silently deleted so
   the block letters stay a continuous history.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ==========================================================================
   [CW] ALTERNATE THE .ca-fig BANDS (owner asked, 2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Three .ca-fig bands now run consecutively on the CA services page and all put
   copy left, figure right -- the eye tracks one straight edge for three sections.
   The middle band flips.

   BY MODIFIER, NOT :nth-of-type. The three are not adjacent siblings
   (#classifications and #coverage sit between them), so an nth rule would count
   the wrong elements.

   THE TRACK RATIO SWAPS WITH THE COLUMNS. .ca-fig is 1.5fr / 1fr and the WIDE
   track carries the prose. Moving the copy to column 2 without reversing the
   ratio would put 200 words in the narrow track and stretch a square photo across
   the wide one.

   DESKTOP ONLY -- below 1000px .ca-fig already collapses to one column with the
   copy first, and the reading order there is heading, prose, picture regardless
   of which side the figure takes at full width.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .ca-fig--reverse > .container {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.5fr);
  }
  .ca-fig--reverse .ca-fig__copy   { grid-column: 2; }
  .ca-fig--reverse .ca-fig__figure { grid-column: 1; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CX] THE FOUR-CARD VARIANT OF THE CLASSIFICATION BAND (2026-09-03)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The 50 market type pages show the OTHER FOUR classifications -- their own is
   excluded, because a card linking to the page you are already on is not a link.

   [CU] sizes this band's cards for FIVE across and is scoped to #classifications,
   so four cards inherit a 1/5 basis and leave a fifth of the row empty on the
   right. That is the same 4 + 1 imbalance [CU] was written to remove, arriving
   from the other direction. This re-bases the variant so the row always divides
   evenly: 2 + 2 below 1180, 4 across above it.

   THE 1001-1179 RANGE IS THE REASON THE BASE RULE CARRIES NO MEDIA QUERY.
   [CU] puts five cards at 3-up there; four cards at 3-up is 3 + 1. The unqueried
   rule below therefore has to beat a rule that IS inside a media query. Both are
   (1,1,1), so source order decides and this block must stay after [CU] -- it is
   appended at the end of the file for exactly that reason. Moving it above [CU]
   silently reintroduces the gap.

   Sizing, padding and the media slot are all [CU]'s and [CT]'s -- deliberately
   untouched, so the four-card band and the five-card band are the same component.
   ========================================================================== */
#classifications .cross-link-rail__cards--four > li {
  flex-basis: calc((100% - 14px) / 2);
}
@media (min-width: 1180px) {
  #classifications .cross-link-rail__cards--four > li {
    flex-basis: calc((100% - 3 * 14px) / 4);
  }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CY] THE BUY PAGE MIRRORS THE CLIENT'S OWN /buy LAYOUT (2026-09-04)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Three pieces, all scoped so nothing else on the site moves:

   1. .hero--lead turns the buy hero into two columns -- the existing copy on the
      left, a lead-capture card on the right -- because the client's /buy opens with
      a form beside the masthead rather than under it. Below 1001px the card drops
      under the copy: a 320px form column beside a 320px copy column is two bad
      columns, not two good ones.

   2. .field--choice is the FIRST radio group in this design system (only
      locations.html had a radio before, and it is a filter, not a form field). It
      is built from the existing .field/.form tokens rather than a new look, so the
      two forms on this page read as one component.

   3. .buyclass is the three-column classification band. It mirrors the pairing on
      the client's page (20 & 21 / 41 & 47 / 48) but carries NO definitions -- those
      belong to licence-types.html per dedup-ledger rows C18-C20 -- so the columns
      are shorter than the client's and the grid is sized for a heading, a short
      paragraph and a button row rather than a full definition.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .hero--lead .hero__cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 0.85fr);
    gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .hero--lead .hero__lead { position: sticky; top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px); }
}
.hero--lead .hero__lead { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px); }
@media (min-width: 1001px) { .hero--lead .hero__lead { margin-top: 0; } }
.hero--lead .hero__leadh {
  font-size: 20px;
  line-height: 1.3;
  margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-sm, 12px);
  color: #1e1e1e;                 /* the card is .form--light, so it sits on white */
}
.hero--lead .cta-formcard { max-width: 460px; }

/* the radio group */
.form .field--choice { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--ds-space-md); min-inline-size: 0; }
.form .field--choice legend {
  padding: 0;
  font-size: 14px;
  margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-2xs, 6px);
}
.form--light .field--choice legend { color: #3a3a3a; }
.form .choice-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px 18px; }
.form .choice {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 15px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.form .choice input {
  width: auto;                    /* .form input is width:100% -- undo it here */
  margin: 0;
  accent-color: var(--ds-accent);
  flex: none;
}
.form .choice span { line-height: 1.2; }

/* the three-column classification band */
.buyclass {
  list-style: none;
  margin: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px) 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: grid;
  gap: 18px;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
}
@media (min-width: 700px)  { .buyclass { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
@media (min-width: 1001px) { .buyclass { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
.buyclass__col {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 10px;
  padding: 22px;
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .028);
}
.buyclass__col h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.25; }
.buyclass__col p  { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; }
.buyclass__go { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-top: auto; padding-top: 4px; }
/* SCOPED ON PURPOSE. The first draft styled a bare .btn-sm -- which is used in the
   markup of 106 pages and, measured, has NO rule anywhere in any stylesheet. Defining
   it here would have silently restyled a button across the whole site off the back of
   a single-page request. The dead class is left dead and reported instead. */
.buyclass__go .btn { padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 14px; }

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [CY] continued -- #buy-intro uses layout I-A (owner picked from four options).

   The band shipped first as a full-width h2 over a half-width paragraph: the
   heading spanned 96% of the container while the prose sat at 506px, so 694px --
   58% of the band -- was empty to its right. It read as a two-column layout with
   one column missing.

   I-A gives the heading its own column. Measured on the real page at 1440:
   463px -> 334px tall, unused right gutter 694px -> 24px, prose 9 lines -> 7.
   The paragraph's measure stays comfortable (60 -> 67 chars per line).

   DESKTOP ONLY. Below 1001px the two columns stack and the band is identical to
   the plain stacked version -- 557px, one 327px column -- so this rule buys
   nothing on a phone and is not asked to.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .buyintro {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 72px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .buyintro__head h2 { margin-top: 6px; }   /* optical align to the prose's first line */
}
.buyintro .lede--prose { max-width: none; }  /* the grid column is the measure now */

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   [CY] continued -- the lead column in the classification band.

   The client's own /buy and /sell put the middle column (Type 41 & 47) in a
   bordered card to mark it as the pair most businesses are working towards. That
   emphasis is mirrored here in this theme's terms -- a brighter border and a
   slightly lifted surface rather than the reference's white box on grey, which
   would punch a hole in a dark page.

   Emphasis only. The column keeps the same grid track, padding and type as its
   neighbours, so nothing reflows and the three stay the same height.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.buyclass__col--lead {
  border-color: var(--ds-accent, #e69c4e);
  background: rgba(230, 156, 78, .06);
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [CZ] THE STATE PAGES' COUNTIES/CITIES BAND (owner picked G-C, 2026-09-04)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   FIRST, A SCOPING BUG. [MRAIL] makes "#markets > .container" a two-column grid
   and pins .eyebrow to column 1 row 1 and h2 to column 1 row 2. It was written for
   locations.html's master/detail rail. The two state pages reuse the same #markets
   id for a different band, so their .loc-geo -- which has no placement -- auto-flowed
   into column 2 and pushed the heading BELOW the cards, into a 585px-tall row 2,
   leaving 698px of empty left column.

   THE OVERRIDE KEYS OFF .markets--geo, NOT #markets. A bare "#markets > .container
   { display:block }" would also hit locations.html and destroy the rail [MRAIL]
   exists to build. Only the two state sections carry this class.

   WHY ONE WIDE CARD. .loc-geo__list is capped at max-height:340px with
   overflow-y:auto, so any narrow layout hides items behind a scrollbar with no
   affordance. Measured on FLORIDA (66 counties), the real stress case: a
   heading-left/cards-right split gives the list ONE grid column and hides 54 of 66;
   two cards side by side give three columns and hide 30; one full-width card gives
   six columns, 302px, and hides none. Arizona hides only 2 under the narrow splits,
   which is exactly why they look fine there and fail here.
   ========================================================================== */
/* THE ID HAS TO BE IN THE SELECTOR. [MRAIL] writes "#markets > .container", which is
   (1,1,0). A ".markets--geo > .container" override is (0,2,0) and LOSES -- one id
   outranks any number of classes -- so the container stayed display:grid, the card
   stayed in a narrow column, and Florida still hid 24 counties behind the 340px cap.
   Measured before this line was corrected. Keeping #markets in the selector makes it
   (1,2,0), which wins, while .markets--geo keeps locations.html out of range. */
#markets.markets--geo > .container { display: block; }
#markets.markets--geo > .container > * { grid-column: auto; grid-row: auto; }

.geo--wide .lede--prose { max-width: 70ch; }
.geo--wide .loc-geo {
  display: block;                      /* one card, not a grid of cards */
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px);
  margin-bottom: 0;
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-shadow-card);
  padding: var(--ds-space-md) var(--ds-space-lg);
}
.geo--wide .loc-geo__col {             /* the columns stop being cards themselves */
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
  padding: 0;
}
.geo--wide .loc-geo__col + .loc-geo__col {
  margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px);
  padding-top: var(--ds-space-md);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
}
/* The list is a grid already; widening it is what keeps it under the 340px cap. */
@media (min-width: 700px)  { .geo--wide .loc-geo__list { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
@media (min-width: 1000px) { .geo--wide .loc-geo__list { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
@media (min-width: 1200px) { .geo--wide .loc-geo__list { grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }

/* ==========================================================================
   [DA] STATE PAGES: PAIRED MARKET LISTS, AND COPY-BESIDE-IMAGE BANDS
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Both state pages had ZERO images in <main> and three bands that ran a single
   narrow column with the right half of the container empty.

   .geo--pair -- ARIZONA ONLY. G-C stacks Counties above Cities inside one wide
   card, which is right for Florida's 66 counties but wasteful for Arizona's 15:
   at six internal columns the names wrapped ("Apache County" over two lines).
   Measured: stacked 6-up = 588px section; paired with 3 internal columns = a 245px
   card with 0 wrapped names. Florida is deliberately NOT paired -- 66 counties in
   half the width would push the list back under the max-height:340px cap that hid
   54 of them before G-C.

   .statefig -- copy left, image right. Used by #classifications on both pages and
   by Florida's #about-florida. The image column is deliberately the narrower one:
   these bands are read, not looked at.

   .statefig--cols -- Florida's #about-florida only. 300 words in six paragraphs ran
   1187px tall in one column. Running the prose in two text columns beside the image
   is what makes the band shorter rather than merely wider.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .geo--pair .loc-geo {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(24px, 3vw, 44px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* the stacked rule put a top border between the two; paired, they sit side by side */
  .geo--pair .loc-geo__col + .loc-geo__col {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    border-top: 0;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--ds-hairline);
    padding-left: clamp(24px, 3vw, 44px);
  }
  .geo--pair .loc-geo__list { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}

.statefig__media { margin: 0; }
.statefig__media img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
  object-fit: cover;
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  display: block;
}
.statefig__copy > :first-child { margin-top: 0; }
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .statefig {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.45fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
    align-items: center;
  }
  .statefig--cols { align-items: start; }
}
/* THREE TRACKS, NOT TWO. The first attempt kept the 1.45fr/1fr split and put the
   prose into two columns inside it: that gave 303px per text column -- about 38
   characters -- and with break-inside:avoid a 527px paragraph could not split, so
   the band got TALLER (1187px -> 1216px), the opposite of the ask. The copy now
   spans two full tracks and the image takes a third, so each text column is ~357px
   (~45 characters) and the paragraphs balance. break-inside:avoid is gone: it was
   what stopped the columns balancing at all. */
/* THREE THINGS WERE WRONG HERE AND ALL THREE WERE MEASURED, NOT GUESSED:
     · the prose ran at 20px/31px while every other paragraph on the page is 15px,
       so this band shouted;
     · the image was 240x180 in a 794px column -- 614px of void underneath it;
     · the h2 had margin-bottom:0 under a 44px heading, so the heading collided
       with the first line of body copy.
   The prose drops to 16px/1.6, this build's established long-form size ([CV]); the
   image column widens and STRETCHES to the copy's height with object-fit:cover, so
   there is no void to leave; and the heading gets real separation. */
.statefig--cols .statefig__copy p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; }
.statefig--cols .statefig__copy h2 { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-md, 16px); }
.statefig--cols .statefig__copy p:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }

@media (min-width: 1200px) {
  .statefig--cols {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, .78fr);
    align-items: stretch;              /* the image fills the column, not a corner */
  }
  .statefig--cols .statefig__media { align-self: stretch; }
  .statefig--cols .statefig__media img {
    height: 100%;
    min-height: 320px;
    aspect-ratio: auto;                /* height is driven by the copy now */
  }
  .statefig--cols .statefig__copy {
    grid-column: 1 / 3;
    columns: 2;
    column-gap: clamp(28px, 3vw, 44px);
  }
  .statefig--cols .statefig__copy h2,
  .statefig--cols .statefig__copy .eyebrow { column-span: all; }
  .statefig--cols .statefig__media { grid-column: 3; }
}
.statefig__media { margin-top: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px); }
@media (min-width: 1001px) { .statefig__media { margin-top: 0; } }

/* ==========================================================================
   [DB] CONTACT FORM, REBUILT TO THE CLIENT'S OWN /contact-us (owner, 2026-09-04)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The reference labels its inputs with PLACEHOLDERS only. A placeholder is not a
   label -- it disappears on focus and screen readers treat it as a hint -- so each
   field keeps a real <label> that is visually hidden. The look matches the
   reference; the accessibility does not regress to match it.

   .vh is defined here because .sr-only is used in this build's markup but has NO
   rule anywhere in any stylesheet (a pre-existing defect, reported separately).
   Rather than fix that silently under a different ticket, this block ships its own.
   ========================================================================== */
.vh {
  position: absolute !important;
  width: 1px; height: 1px;
  margin: -1px; padding: 0; border: 0;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  overflow: hidden;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.form--ref .field { margin-bottom: var(--ds-space-md); }
.form--ref input,
.form--ref textarea {
  width: 100%;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 15px;
  padding: var(--ds-space-sm) var(--ds-space-md);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-md);
  background: var(--ds-bg-surface);
  color: var(--ds-ink);
}
.form--ref textarea { min-height: 132px; resize: vertical; }
.form--ref input:focus,
.form--ref textarea:focus { outline: none; border-color: var(--ds-accent); }
/* the widget renders a 302x76 iframe; reserve it so the button does not jump when
   the Google script finishes loading */
.form--ref .g-recaptcha { min-height: 78px; }

/* ==========================================================================
   [DC] HERO LEAD FORM: GLASS (F-A), AND A MOBILE ORDER OF TITLE > FORM > LEDE
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE ORDER NEEDED MARKUP. The eyebrow, h1, lede and cta-row shared one
   .hero__inner with the form as its sibling, so no ordering property could put the
   form BETWEEN the h1 and the lede. .hero__inner is now split into .hero__head and
   .hero__body with the form between them, which means the DOM order already IS the
   mobile order -- mobile stacks and needs no rules here at all. Desktop is the
   case that needs placing.

   F-A, picked from four treatments rendered on the real page. The defect: the card
   was white and its inputs were WHITE, so the fields had almost no presence.
   Measured contrast after, composited over the hero's own ground: labels 9.79,
   input text 15.79, heading 18.32 -- against a 4.5 AA threshold.

   backdrop-filter is progressive: where it is unsupported the panel falls back to
   its own rgba ground, which is opaque enough to stay legible on its own.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 1001px) {
  .hero--lead .hero__cols {
    grid-template-rows: auto auto;
    column-gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px);
    row-gap: var(--ds-space-md, 16px);
  }
  .hero--lead .hero__head { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }
  .hero--lead .hero__body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; }
  .hero--lead .hero__lead { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; align-self: start; }
}

/* ---- THE PANEL IS WHITE ON DESKTOP, GLASS ON MOBILE (owner, 2026-09-07) ----
   The glass shipped at every width; the owner then asked for white on DESKTOP,
   keeping the gradient edge. So the glass rules below are scoped to the SMALL
   end only, and desktop simply falls through to the base .cta-formcard white
   card and .form--light inputs -- nothing is overridden back to white, which
   would leave two competing rule sets fighting over the same properties.

   1000px is deliberate: it is one pixel under the 1001px the hero already uses
   to go two-column, so the panel turns glass at exactly the width it stops
   sitting beside the copy and starts sitting over the photo full-bleed.

   The gradient edge is OUTSIDE this query on purpose -- it was asked for at both
   breakpoints, and it is the one thing both treatments share. */
@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .hero--lead .cta-formcard {
    background: rgba(28, 20, 16, .62);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(1.2);
    backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(1.2);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .14);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, .45), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, .10);
  }
  .hero--lead .hero__leadh { color: #fff; }
  .hero--lead .form label,
  .hero--lead .form legend { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .72); }
  .hero--lead .form input:not([type="radio"]) {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .16);
    color: #fff;
  }
  .hero--lead .form input:not([type="radio"])::placeholder { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .4); }
  .hero--lead .form input:not([type="radio"]):focus {
    border-color: var(--ds-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(230, 156, 78, .25);
    outline: none;
  }
  .hero--lead .form .choice span { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, .86); }
}
/* the accent dot reads on both grounds, so it is not scoped */
.hero--lead .form .choice input { accent-color: var(--ds-accent); }

/* ==========================================================================
   [DD] LEAD FORM: A GRADIENT EDGE, AND ONE PAIRED ROW (owner, 2026-09-07)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE GRADIENT EDGE. The glass panel had no edge of its own against a dark photo,
   so it read as a soft rectangle. A 3px gold-to-red rule along its top gives it a
   defined start. It is drawn as a clipped ::before rather than a border-top so it
   follows the card's radius and curves into the corners, which is what a border-top
   would not do. Scoped to .hero--lead so the #contact card, which shares the
   .cta-formcard class, is untouched.

   THE PAIRED ROW. Operating state and County are two short fields that each had a
   full-width row. They now share one. The owner declined the OTHER available pair --
   the two radio sets -- because "Type of business" carries three choices that would
   wrap to two lines in half the width, trading a row of height for a wrapped list.
   That trade was offered, measured and turned down; only this pair ships.

   TARGETED BY CLASS, NOT POSITION. The card's existing pairing uses
   :nth-child(2)/(3) for email and phone. Adding a third and fourth nth-child rule
   would break the moment a field is inserted, so these two carry .field--half.
   Same (0,3,0) specificity as the `.form > .field` full-width default, so this
   block must stay after it -- it is appended at the end of the file for that reason.

   It collapses at the same 600px the existing pairing collapses at, so a phone
   never puts two inputs side by side.
   ========================================================================== */
.hero--lead .cta-formcard { position: relative; overflow: hidden; }
.hero--lead .cta-formcard::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0 0 auto 0;
  height: 3px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--ds-accent), #ad1e1e);
  pointer-events: none;
}

.cta-formcard .form > .field--half { grid-column: span 1; }
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .cta-formcard .form > .field--half { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [DE] HERO COPY CENTRES ON MOBILE (owner, 2026-09-07)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Below the two-column breakpoint the hero is one stacked column, and the owner
   asked for the eyebrow + h1 block and the lede block to centre there.

   BOTH properties are needed, not one. .hero__inner is a flex column with
   align-items:flex-start, so its children shrink to their content width and sit
   left; text-align alone would centre the text INSIDE a block still pinned left.
   align-items centres the blocks, text-align centres their lines.

   The CTA buttons are already full-width at this size -- measured 327px in a 327px
   column -- so they are unaffected, and there is no risk of centred copy sitting
   above left-aligned buttons.

   1000px is the width at which this hero stops being two-column, so the copy
   centres at exactly the moment it stops sitting beside the form.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (max-width: 1000px) {
  .hero--lead .hero__head,
  .hero--lead .hero__body {
    text-align: center;
    align-items: center;
  }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   [DF] #where CARRIES THREE STATE CARDS ON THE BUY AND SELL PAGES (2026-09-07)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The band listed 13 California market pills. The owner asked for three cards --
   California, Florida, Arizona -- IN ONE LINE, then picked treatment C: a
   monochrome photograph inside each card.

   PLACEMENT IS THE FIRST HALF OF THIS BLOCK, and it is not decoration. [CH]
   pins .eyebrow / h2 / .lede to column 1 rows 1-3 and .mkt-list to column 2
   spanning rows 1..3. A list WITHOUT a placement rule auto-places into row 1 of
   column 2, which pushes the h2 down to row 2 -- and row 2 begins below the
   cards. That produced a tall empty gap between the eyebrow and the heading.
   .statecards therefore takes the same placement .mkt-list has.

   ONE LINE IS THE REQUIREMENT, so the desktop rule is an explicit
   repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)): three equal tracks that cannot wrap, rather than an
   auto-fit that would silently drop to 2 + 1 the moment the container narrowed.
   That is the same class of failure the counties band hit -- a grid reflowing on
   CONTAINER width while the breakpoint fires on VIEWPORT width -- so the count is
   pinned instead of derived.

   THE PHOTOGRAPH IS NOT CROPPED. All three files are 3:2 and the card box is
   near square (171x173 at 1440), so object-fit:cover would have discarded about
   two thirds of every frame. The image is laid in at its own aspect ratio --
   full card width, height derived -- and anchored to the bottom edge, where the
   upward fade reveals it. Nothing is cut off on any side.

   COLOUR IS PINNED ON BOTH THE ANCHOR AND THE NAME. Without it .statecard__name
   inherits the anchor colour and renders :visited purple once a reader has
   opened a state page.

   Below 700px the three stack, because three cards across a phone would give
   each about 100px and the sublines would wrap to three lines.

   Scoped to .statecards, which appears on exactly two pages; the other 23 pages
   carrying this band keep their pills untouched.
   ========================================================================== */
@media (min-width: 900px) {
  .where--split > .container > .statecards {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1 / span 3;
    margin-top: 6px;
    align-self: start;
  }
}
.statecards {
  list-style: none;
  margin: var(--ds-space-lg, 24px) 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  gap: 14px;
}
@media (min-width: 700px) {
  .statecards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}
.statecard {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  isolation: isolate;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 5px;
  height: 100%;
  padding: 18px 20px 16px;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-line);
  border-radius: var(--ds-r-lg, 10px);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .028);
  transition: border-color var(--ds-dur, .2s) var(--ds-ease, ease);
}
.statecard:visited { color: var(--ds-ink); }
.statecard:hover,
.statecard:focus-visible { border-color: var(--ds-accent); }
.statecard__ph {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: -1;
  line-height: 0;
}
/* object-fit COVER, so the photograph occupies the WHOLE card. This reverses an
   earlier choice, deliberately, because the two requests are geometrically
   incompatible and the later one wins: a 3:2 photograph cannot both fill a
   171x173 near-square box and keep its whole frame. Contain kept every pixel but
   letterboxed to 169x112, leaving the top 35% of the card bare -- which is the
   gap in the owner's screenshot. Cover fills the card and crops instead:
     desktop 1440  card 171x173  image scales to 260x173  -> 34% of WIDTH cropped
     mobile  375   card 327x173  image scales to 327x218  -> 21% of HEIGHT cropped
   Measured, not estimated. object-position is centred so the crop is taken evenly
   from both edges rather than lopping one side off.

   THE MASK NO LONGER REACHES FULL TRANSPARENCY. It used to fade to 0 at the top,
   so even a covering image would leave the card's upper third looking empty --
   the whole point of this change. It now bottoms out at 40%, so the image reads
   across the entire card while the heading keeps enough separation to stay
   legible against it. */
.statecard__ph img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  object-position: center;
  display: block;
  filter: grayscale(1) sepia(.62) saturate(1.5) contrast(1.05) brightness(.85);
  opacity: .32;
  transition: opacity var(--ds-dur-slow, .25s) var(--ds-ease, ease);
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to top, #000 55%, rgba(0, 0, 0, .4) 100%);
  mask-image: linear-gradient(to top, #000 55%, rgba(0, 0, 0, .4) 100%);
}
.statecard:hover .statecard__ph img,
.statecard:focus-visible .statecard__ph img { opacity: .44; }
.statecard__mark {
  font-size: 11.5px;
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #e69c4e);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.statecard__name {
  font-family: var(--ds-font-display);
  font-size: 23px;
  line-height: 1.15;
  color: var(--ds-ink);
  margin-top: 8px;
}
.statecard__sub {
  font-size: 13.5px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
  min-height: 2.9em;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}
.statecard__go {
  margin-top: auto;
  padding-top: 10px;
  font-size: 13px;
  color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #e69c4e);
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .statecard,
  .statecard__ph img { transition: none; }
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [DG] LOCATIONS MEGA MENU: HEIGHT CAPPED TO THE RAIL (2026-09-07)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The owner asked for the panel to stop "just below Arizona 15 counties".

   THE MARKUP DID MOST OF THE WORK, not this block. Cutting each pane from
   seven or eight rows to three took the panel from 547px to 351px on its own.
   What is left is the gap between 351px and the rail: measured with the menu
   open, the Arizona button's bottom edge sits 185px below the panel's top, and
   the rail carries 16px of bottom padding, so the requested stop line is 201px.
   The panel is set to 219px, not 201px, and the extra 18px is not slack: the panes
   column needs 219px for its three rows (168px of rows plus 14px of padding top and
   bottom, plus the row gaps). At 201px the third row -- "All 14 California markets"
   -- was clipped by exactly 18px, which defeats the point of cutting to three rows.
   Measured with the menu open, not derived on paper. The panel therefore ends 36px
   below the Arizona button rather than 18px.

   THE HEIGHT IS SET ON THE GRID, not on the panel, because the panel's own
   max-height is a viewport fraction (76vh) and would go on winning at any tall
   viewport. Setting height on .mm-casc sizes the single grid row, and the three
   columns stretch into it.

   THE DETAIL COLUMN THEN SCROLLS, and that is a real consequence rather than a
   free win: the California services panel wants about 245px, so roughly 60px of
   it sits below the fold and is reached by scrolling. The counties panel (52
   entries) scrolls considerably more. Both columns already carried
   overflow-y:auto before this change, so nothing is clipped without a way to
   reach it -- but the trade is height for scrolling, and it was asked for
   explicitly.

   min-height:0 is required on the scrolling children. Without it a grid item's
   automatic minimum size is its CONTENT size, so the row would refuse to shrink
   to 201px and the cap would silently do nothing.
   ========================================================================== */
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc {
  height: 232px;
}
/* The detail column's own padding was 18px top / 20px bottom -- 38px of the 232px
   budget spent on air. Trimmed to 12/12 HERE ONLY, which buys back 14px and is
   what lets the classifications row and the footnote sit above the fold instead of
   just below it. Scoped to this mega so nothing else inherits a tighter panel. */
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__details {
  padding-top: 12px;
  padding-bottom: 12px;
}
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__rail,
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__panes,
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__details {
  min-height: 0;
}
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__panes,
.mm-mega--locations .mm-casc__details {
  overflow-y: auto;
}

/* The services panel stacks two lists, so its second heading needs air above it
   that a first-child heading never needed. */
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="2"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="6"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(6, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A SUBTLE GRID ON THE DETAIL LISTS, and smaller type.
   Sixty-six county names set at 13px in three gapped columns read as a wall --
   the eye has no rule to travel along, so the columns blur into each other.
   Hairlines at 7% white give the rows and columns an edge without becoming
   furniture, and the 14px column gap becomes cell padding so the rules sit
   flush instead of floating.

   The left rule is suppressed on the first cell of each row. That has to be
   done per column count, because :nth-child cannot read grid-template-columns
   -- hence one selector per data-cols value in use (1, 2, 3 and 6, all four
   confirmed present in the rendered menu).
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.mm-casc__dlist {
  gap: 0;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .07);
}
.mm-casc__dlist li {
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .07);
  border-left: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, .07);
}
/* A filler cell exists only to carry the grid's hairlines where the content ran
   out. It is empty, aria-hidden, and matched to a real cell's height so the last
   row lines up with the rest. */
.mm-casc__dlist__pad {
  min-height: 27px;
}
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="4"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="5"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.mm-casc__dlist:not([data-cols]) li:nth-child(2n + 1),
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="1"] li,
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="2"] li:nth-child(2n + 1),
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="3"] li:nth-child(3n + 1),
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="4"] li:nth-child(4n + 1),
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="5"] li:nth-child(5n + 1),
.mm-casc__dlist[data-cols="6"] li:nth-child(6n + 1) {
  border-left: 0;
}
.mm-casc__dhead--sub {
  margin-top: 8px;
  font-size: 13px;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
/* Carried forward from the old pane footers, which this rebuild removed. */
.mm-casc__dfoot {
  margin: 8px 0 0;
  font-family: var(--ds-font-body);
  font-size: 12px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  color: var(--ds-ink-soft);
}
.mm-casc__dfoot a { color: var(--ds-accent-ink, #e69c4e); }


/* ==========================================================================
   [DH] MEGA PANELS NO LONGER STICK OPEN AFTER A MOUSE CLICK (2026-09-08)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reported as "the Locations tab drops down, stays for a while and does not go
   back up". It was not Locations and it was not a delay -- it affected ALL
   THREE panels and the panel never closed at all until the reader clicked
   somewhere else. Measured trace, at 1440:

     hover the trigger    is-open true   aria-expanded true    visible   correct
     CLICK the trigger    is-open FALSE  aria-expanded FALSE   VISIBLE   <-- wrong
     pointer far away     is-open false  aria-expanded false   VISIBLE   after 5.5s
     click elsewhere      is-open false  aria-expanded false   hidden

   The JS was right the whole time: clicking a trigger toggles the group shut
   and sets aria-expanded="false". The CSS then kept painting it, because
   :focus-within was in the visibility selector and CLICKING A <button> FOCUSES
   IT. So the panel stayed lit while the markup said closed -- and only a click
   elsewhere, which moved focus to <body>, released it.

   :focus-within is there for KEYBOARD users, and that still has to work. The
   fix keeps the intent and drops the mouse case:

     - :focus-visible instead of :focus -- a mouse click does not set it, a
       keyboard tab does;
     - scoped INSIDE .mm-panel, not to the group, so focus on the TRIGGER does
       not hold the panel open. That matters for Escape, which closes the group
       and returns focus to the trigger; had this been :has(:focus-visible) on
       the group, Escape would have re-lit the panel it just closed.

   IT IS A SEPARATE RULE, NOT A THIRD CLAUSE ON THE LINE ABOVE, and that is
   deliberate. One unsupported selector invalidates the WHOLE comma-separated
   group it sits in. Folding :has() into that line would mean that on any engine
   without :has() the panels would stop opening on hover AND on click -- the
   navigation would simply not work. Split, the worst case is that keyboard
   focus alone no longer holds a panel open, while hover and .is-open keep
   working. Verified supported here: CSS.supports('selector(:has(a))') and
   ('selector(:focus-visible)') both true.
   ========================================================================== */
.mm-has-panel:has(.mm-panel :focus-visible) > .mm-panel {
  opacity: 1;
  visibility: visible;
  transform: translateY(0);
  pointer-events: auto;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   [DI] THE LOCATIONS MEGA GETS THE SAME NAV CLEARANCE AS THE OTHER TWO (2026-09-08)
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The owner: the Locations dropdown "does not have the same gap as the others.
   It should be below the nav bar and NEVER overlapping the nav bar."

   Measured at 1600, all three panels open:

     #mm-mega-services    top 88px    clears the header
     #mm-dd-licensing     top 88px    clears the header
     #mm-mega-locations   top 68px    OVERLAPS the header by 14px

   #mm-mega-locations had NO id-scoped rules at all -- 0 in this sheet, and 0 at
   4830cc5 either, so it has never had them -- and therefore fell back to the base
   `.mm-panel { top: calc(100% + 4px) }`. That resolves against the nav <li>, whose
   bottom is 59px, not against the 82px header, so the panel opened INSIDE the bar.

   This is not a new rule, it is a rule that was never copied across. The comment
   above #mm-mega-services already diagnoses exactly this: "68 against a real 80 is
   why the panel currently opens 12px INSIDE the nav (measured navGap -12)". The
   Locations mega was built later and never picked it up.

   Same idiom, same literal, same edge as the other two, deliberately -- so all
   three panels share one top edge and there is one number to change if the header
   height ever moves. 80px stays a measured literal because there is still no
   header-height token in tokens.css.
   ========================================================================== */
#mm-mega-locations { top: calc(80px + var(--ds-space-xs)); }
